/* In-course Quiz Centre bank: the same quizzes as the QTI packages (Teacher's Version) */
window.COURSE_QUIZBANK = [{"pkg":"Section Tests","quizzes":[{"id":"test_s1","title":"Section 1 Test","desc":"<p>Section 1: Computer Fundamentals and Information Processing. 30 multiple-choice items. Pass mark 70%.</p>","minutes":0,"items":[{"t":"mc","title":"Item 1","q":"<p>A student explains to her Form 4 class that a supermarket in Gros Islet uses computers and telecommunications to capture, store, process and transmit sales data. Which term BEST describes this use of technology?</p>","options":["Computer engineering","Process control","Information Technology","Data logging"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. IT is the use of computers (and telecommunications) to store, retrieve, transmit and manipulate data. B is hardware design; C controls physical processes; D is automatic sensor recording.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 2","q":"<p>At Bridge Street Phone Hub, the rule 'Back up the sales file every evening' is part of the ICT system. Which level of the ICT Components Hierarchy does this rule belong to?</p>","options":["Procedures","Hardware","Software","Telecommunications"],"correct":0,"why":"Key: A. A rule people follow is a procedure. A is physical equipment; C is programs; D is communication links.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 3","q":"<p>A regional weather service needs to model the path of a hurricane approaching the Leeward Islands using trillions of calculations. Which type of computer is MOST suitable?</p>","options":["Supercomputer","Embedded computer","Mainframe","Tablet"],"correct":0,"why":"Key: A. Supercomputers are built for massive calculation speed. A suits many transactions; B and D lack the power.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 4","q":"<p>A bank with branches on six islands must process millions of card transactions each day, reliably, for thousands of users at once. Which type of computer should it use?</p>","options":["Game console","Supercomputer","Mainframe","Laptop"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. Mainframes handle huge numbers of simultaneous transactions reliably. A is for scientific calculation; B and C are personal devices.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 5","q":"<p>A LUCELEC clerk types a meter reading of 95,000 instead of 950, and the customer receives a huge bill. The computer calculated correctly. This situation illustrates:</p>","options":["Time-sharing","Hardware failure","GIGO","A virus"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. Garbage In, Garbage Out: wrong input gives wrong output. Nothing suggests malware (B), failure (C) or a processing mode (D).","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 6","q":"<p>A juice factory in Trinidad keeps its pasteuriser at exactly 72 °C with no operator. Which feature is ESSENTIAL for this process control application?</p>","options":["A laser printer","A graphical user interface","Human-readable documents","Sensor input"],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. Process control needs sensor input, real-time processing and output to control devices. A, C and D are not essential.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 7","q":"<p>Tiana is typing her SBA when a power cut switches off her desktop. Work she had not saved is lost. In which type of memory was the unsaved work held?</p>","options":["EPROM","RAM","Hard disk","ROM"],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. RAM is volatile. ROM and EPROM are non-volatile (A, D); the hard disk holds only saved work (B).","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 8","q":"<p>While a grade-book program runs, one part of the CPU decides whether each student's mark is greater than or equal to 50. Which part performs this comparison?</p>","options":["Arithmetic logic unit","Hard disk","Cache","Control unit"],"correct":0,"why":"Key: A. The ALU performs arithmetic and logical comparisons. A coordinates; C stores frequently used data; B is secondary storage.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 9","q":"<p>Each promotional photo for a Rodney Bay hotel is 4 MB. How many photos fit on a 2 GB memory card? (Use 1 GB = 1,024 MB.)</p>","options":["2,048","512","500","8"],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. 2 × 1,024 = 2,048 MB; 2,048 ÷ 4 = 512. B uses 1,000; C forgets to divide; D is a wrong operation.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 10","q":"<p>A technician says a chip in an old machine can be wiped by exposing it to ultraviolet light and then reprogrammed. Which type of memory is this?</p>","options":["EPROM","Cache","PROM","SRAM"],"correct":0,"why":"Key: A. EPROM is erased with ultraviolet light. A can be written only once; C and D are volatile RAM types.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 11","q":"<p>A regional bank keeps a copy of all its records for ten years in a vault in another country, and rarely needs to read individual records. Which medium is MOST suitable?</p>","options":["USB flash drive","CD-RW","RAM","Magnetic tape"],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. Tape has huge capacity, low cost and long shelf life for archives. A and B have small capacity; D is volatile.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 12","q":"<p>A small business stores all its files only in cloud storage. After a storm, power returns in Gros Islet but there is no Internet for three days. What is the MAIN problem?</p>","options":["The files cannot be accessed","The files are encrypted by the storm","The files become read-only","The files are deleted"],"correct":0,"why":"Key: A. Cloud storage depends on an Internet connection. The files still exist (A); C and B are not caused by an outage.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 13","q":"<p>Your CXC Paper 01 answer sheet is marked by a machine that detects which bubbles have been shaded. Which input method is used?</p>","options":["MICR","OCR","OMR","Barcode reader"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. OMR detects marks in set positions. C reads characters; B reads magnetic ink; D reads bars.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 14","q":"<p>Island Auto Repairs in Gros Islet must print job cards on three-part carbon forms in a dusty workshop. Which printer should it buy?</p>","options":["Dot-matrix","Laser","Inkjet","Thermal"],"correct":0,"why":"Key: A. Only an impact printer presses through carbon copies. A, D and C are non-impact.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 15","q":"<p>Aaliyah, a Form 4 student, is blind. Which pair of devices would BEST allow her to use a computer for her coursework?</p>","options":["Large monitor and light pen","Screen reader with headphones and a refreshable braille display","Projector and plotter","Graphics tablet and mouse"],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. Sound and touch replace sight. A, B and D all depend on seeing the screen or output.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 16","q":"<p>A hotel pool system uses a sensor to measure the water level and a motorised valve to let in more water when the level is low. What is the motorised valve?</p>","options":["An actuator","A sensor","An input device","A storage device"],"correct":0,"why":"Key: A. An actuator turns a signal into physical action. The sensor (B) is the input device (A); the valve does not store data (B).","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 17","q":"<p>Kyle edits a document while another document prints and music plays. Which operating system feature allows one user to do several tasks at the same time?</p>","options":["Defragmentation","Time-sharing","Multitasking","Batch processing"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. Multitasking lets one user run and switch between several tasks. B processes saved-up jobs; C serves many users; D is a utility.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 18","q":"<p>LUCELEC collects meter readings over a month and then produces every customer's bill in a single run. Which processing mode is this?</p>","options":["Batch","Online","Real-time","Interactive"],"correct":0,"why":"Key: A. Data accumulated over a period and processed together is batch processing. A and C process immediately; D is not one of the three modes.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 19","q":"<p>Shanice downloads a free 'Carnival filter' app that secretly sends her passwords to criminals. Which type of malware is this?</p>","options":["Trojan horse","Defragmenter","Worm","Compiler"],"correct":0,"why":"Key: A. A Trojan horse is disguised as something useful or fun. A spreads by itself; B and D are not malware.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 20","q":"<p>A student's Pascal program for the SBA is translated as a whole into machine code, producing a file that can run on its own. Which translator was used?</p>","options":["Scan disk","Compiler","Interpreter","Assembler"],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. A compiler translates the whole program at once. A translates line by line; B translates assembly language; C is a utility.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 21","q":"<p>A two-person tour company in Soufrière needs invoicing software within a week and has a budget of US$300. Which type of software is MOST appropriate?</p>","options":["Custom-written software","A new operating system","Off-the-shelf software","A device driver"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. Off-the-shelf software is cheap and available immediately. B is costly and slow; C and D do not provide invoicing.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 22","q":"<p>Tourists collecting hire cars at a Hewanorra Airport self-service kiosk need to find their booking, sign and collect a key. Which user interface is MOST suitable?</p>","options":["Touch-screen menu-driven","Voice only","Programming interface","Command line"],"correct":0,"why":"Key: A. Simple guided choices suit first-time users. A needs expert knowledge; C is for developers; D is unreliable in a noisy airport.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 23","q":"<p>Tamika, a Form 5 student who travels by bus, compares laptops. One has a 10-hour battery and weighs 1.4 kg; the other has a 3.5-hour battery and weighs 2.9 kg. Which specifications matter MOST for her?</p>","options":["Battery life and weight","Dedicated graphics card","Number of HDMI ports","17-inch screen"],"correct":0,"why":"Key: A. Portability depends on battery life and weight. B, A and D do not address her daily travel.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 24","q":"<p>In the Mahoe Grove High School lab, a monitor's power light is on and the computer is running, but the screen is black. What should a student check NEXT?</p>","options":["The wall socket","The printer paper","The amount of RAM","The video cable and the monitor's input source"],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. Both devices have power, so the signal path is next. A is already proven; C and B are unrelated to the display.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 25","q":"<p>A Form 5 class records 45, 78, 62, 90 and 51 as test marks. The teacher then states, 'The class average is 65.2.' Which statement is correct?</p>","options":["The marks are data; the average is information","The marks are information; the average is data","Both are data","Both are information"],"correct":0,"why":"Key: A. Raw marks are data; the processed average is meaningful information.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 26","q":"<p>A cable company prints each bill with a payment slip showing the customer's account number. The customer returns the slip with payment and it is scanned back into the same system. The slip is a:</p>","options":["Report file","Source document only","Turnaround document","Data logger"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. It is output by the system, completed and returned as input. A is incomplete; C and D are not documents of this kind.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 27","q":"<p>Kemar is researching coral reefs around St. Lucia. He finds a dive shop's page that says 'Our reefs are perfect, book your dive now!' Which criterion is MOST in question?</p>","options":["Objectivity","Coverage","Currency","Accuracy of spelling"],"correct":0,"why":"Key: A. A business selling dives has a reason to be biased. A, B and D are not the main concern here.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 28","q":"<p>A website asks a new user to type her password twice so that the two entries can be compared. This is an example of:</p>","options":["A presence check","Validation by range check","Verification by double entry","A check digit"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. Entering data twice and comparing is double-entry verification. A, C and B are validation checks.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 29","q":"<p>At the tuck shop, prices range from US$0.50 to US$6.00. A patty was rung up as US$250 instead of US$2.50. Which validation check would BEST prevent this?</p>","options":["Consistency check","Presence check","Proofreading","Range check"],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. 250 is outside 0.50–6.00. B only checks for blanks; C compares two fields; D is verification, not validation.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 30","q":"<p>A supermarket till must find a product's price the instant its barcode is scanned, from a file of 20,000 products. Which file organisation is MOST suitable?</p>","options":["None, prices should be typed","Serial","Sequential on magnetic tape","Random (direct)"],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. Random organisation calculates the record's address for instant access. A and C require reading from the start; D causes errors.","fb":[]}]},{"id":"test_s2","title":"Section 2 Test","desc":"<p>Section 2: Computer Networks and Web Technologies. 30 multiple-choice items. Pass mark 70%.</p>","minutes":0,"items":[{"t":"mc","title":"Item 1","q":"<p>A hotel group links the reservation systems of its hotels in Montserrat, Antigua and Barbados so all front desks share one booking database. What type of network is this?</p>","options":["LAN","PAN","MAN","WAN"],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. The hotels are on different islands, so the network covers a wide area. A LAN covers one site, a MAN one city, and a PAN the space around one person.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 2","q":"<p>Jada's smartwatch sends her step count to her phone, which plays music through her Bluetooth earbuds while she jogs along the Santo Domingo boardwalk. These devices form a</p>","options":["WAN","PAN","MAN","LAN"],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. Devices around one person, linked by Bluetooth within a few metres, form a Personal Area Network. The other options cover much larger areas.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 3","q":"<p>At a secondary school, students can log in at any of the 90 computers and find their own saved documents, and the technician backs up all work every night from one machine. Which network model is in use?</p>","options":["Peer-to-peer","Extranet","PAN","Client–server"],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. Roaming log-ins and central backup depend on a central server. Peer-to-peer has no server; PAN and extranet describe area and access, not this model.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 4","q":"<p>A resort in Grenville lets approved travel agents in the USA log in to its booking system to reserve rooms for their clients. The agents are using the resort's</p>","options":["Internet","intranet","extranet","LAN"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. Selected outsiders with controlled log-ins use an extranet. An intranet is for the resort's own staff only; the public Internet requires no special access; a LAN is defined by area.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 5","q":"<p>In a computer lab arranged in a star topology, the central switch suddenly fails. What is the MOST likely result?</p>","options":["All computers in the lab lose network access","Only one computer loses its connection","Data is rerouted through the backbone cable","The printer becomes the new switch"],"correct":0,"why":"Key: A. Every device in a star connects through the central switch, so its failure stops the whole network. One computer failing would affect only that computer; rerouting is a mesh feature; a printer cannot act as a switch.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 6","q":"<p>A school replaces cables in its library with Wi-Fi so students can use laptops anywhere. Which is a disadvantage of this WLAN compared with the old cabled LAN?</p>","options":["New devices are easier to add","Signals can be intercepted, so strong encryption is needed","Students can move around the library","No cables need to be laid"],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. Radio signals travel through the air and can be intercepted, so WPA2/WPA3 encryption is needed. The other three options are advantages of a WLAN.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 7","q":"<p>An emergency operations centre wants its network links to keep working even if one connection is cut by a falling tree during a hurricane. Which topology BEST meets this need?</p>","options":["Star","Mesh","Bus","Ring"],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. Mesh provides several routes, so data can avoid a broken link. A bus fails if the backbone breaks, a star if the centre fails, and a single ring if one link breaks.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 8","q":"<p>A three-person tour desk at the cruise pier shares one printer and a few files between laptops. They have no IT budget and little technical skill. Which is the MAIN advantage of a peer-to-peer network for them?</p>","options":["Central backup on a server","Cheap and simple to set up without a dedicated server","Strong central security","Scales easily to hundreds of users"],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. Peer-to-peer uses existing computers and needs no server or specialist. Central security, central backup and scalability are client–server advantages.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 9","q":"<p>Ms Hodge is on a call while a bus carries her from Speightstown to Bridgetown. Her phone moves from one cell tower's area to the next without the call dropping. This process is called</p>","options":["tethering","roaming","pairing","a handover"],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. Passing a call between cells is a handover. Roaming means using another operator's network; tethering shares data; pairing links Bluetooth devices.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 10","q":"<p>A lab of 30 PCs connected through an old hub slows down whenever students save work at the same time. The technician replaces the hub with a switch. Why does this help?</p>","options":["A switch converts digital signals to analogue","A switch sends data only to the port of the intended device","A switch connects the lab directly to the Internet","A switch boosts the Wi-Fi signal"],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. A switch uses MAC addresses to deliver data only where it is needed, reducing collisions. Converting signals is a modem's job, connecting to the Internet is a router's, and boosting is a repeater's.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 11","q":"<p>Which device connects the LAN in a Kaya Grandi jewellery store to its ISP's network and forwards data packets between the two networks?</p>","options":["Hub","Switch","Network interface card","Router"],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. Routers connect different networks using IP addresses. Switches and hubs connect devices within one LAN; a NIC connects one device.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 12","q":"<p>A family in a rural area still connects over a telephone line. Which device converts the computer's digital signals into analogue signals for the line, and back again?</p>","options":["Switch","Wireless access point","Modem","Router"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. Modem stands for modulator–demodulator. A router links networks, a switch links LAN devices and a WAP lets wireless devices join a LAN.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 13","q":"<p>A school wants students' tablets in every classroom to connect to its existing cabled network. Which device should be installed in each block?</p>","options":["Hub","Bridge","Wireless access point","Modem"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. A wireless access point connects wireless devices to a wired LAN. A modem converts signals for an ISP line, a bridge joins LAN segments, and a hub is a wired connector.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 14","q":"<p>The Sint Maarten Water &amp; Light company needs a high-bandwidth link to a power station 2 km away, past high-voltage equipment and in an area with frequent lightning. Which medium is MOST suitable?</p>","options":["Infrared","Fibre-optic cable","Bluetooth","Unshielded twisted pair"],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. Fibre carries light, so it is immune to electrical interference and lightning, and it handles long distances at high bandwidth. UTP is limited to about 100 m and affected by interference; infrared and Bluetooth have tiny ranges.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 15","q":"<p>A cruise ship sailing between Haiti and Barbados wants to offer Internet access to passengers while far from land. Which transmission medium makes this possible?</p>","options":["Satellite","Coaxial cable","Infrared","Twisted pair"],"correct":0,"why":"Key: A. Only satellite covers ships in the open ocean. Cables cannot reach a moving ship, and infrared works only over a few metres.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 16","q":"<p>Andre downloads a 500 MB video for his SBA on a 25 Mbps connection. Ignoring overheads, how long will the download take?</p>","options":["125 seconds","40 seconds","20 seconds","160 seconds"],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. 500 MB × 8 = 4,000 Mb; 4,000 ÷ 25 = 160 s. 20 s comes from forgetting to multiply by 8; the other values come from arithmetic slips.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 17","q":"<p>Security officers at Carnival Village use two-way radios. Only one person can speak at a time and each says 'Over' when finished. Which transmission mode is this?</p>","options":["Full-duplex","Simplex","Half-duplex","Broadcast-only"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. Both directions, but one at a time, is half-duplex. Simplex is one-way only; full-duplex allows both at once, as in a phone call.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 18","q":"<p>Rohan's TV remote stops working whenever his little brother sits between the remote and the TV. Which medium does the remote MOST likely use?</p>","options":["Wi-Fi","Satellite","Bluetooth","Infrared"],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. Infrared needs a clear line of sight and is blocked by people and objects. Bluetooth and Wi-Fi radio pass around and through obstacles; satellites are not used by remotes.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 19","q":"<p>An engineer in Paramaribo wants to control a port crane remotely in real time, with almost no delay between her command and the crane's movement. Which feature of 5G is MOST important here?</p>","options":["Very low latency","SMS text messaging","Longer battery life of 2G phones","Infrared support"],"correct":0,"why":"Key: A. Low latency is what allows real-time remote control. SMS arrived with 2G; infrared and 2G batteries are irrelevant.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 20","q":"<p>At a store on Domineestraat, Tamara holds her phone about 2 cm from the card terminal and the payment is approved after she scans her fingerprint. Which technology sent the payment details?</p>","options":["Infrared","NFC","Bluetooth","GPS"],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. Tap-to-pay over a few centimetres with no pairing is Near Field Communication. GPS gives location, Bluetooth needs pairing over about 10 m, and infrared is used by remotes.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 21","q":"<p>Kemar is hiking where there is no mobile signal, yet his phone still shows his exact position on a map he downloaded earlier. Why is this possible?</p>","options":["NFC tags along the trail send his position","The GPS receiver uses satellite signals and does not need mobile data","The phone uses Bluetooth from nearby hikers","The phone guesses from his last Wi-Fi network"],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. GPS is receive-only from satellites; the downloaded map provides the background. Bluetooth and NFC do not provide location in this way, and guessing would not be exact.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 22","q":"<p>Dante's laptop has no Internet connection, but his phone has a strong 4G signal. He turns on a setting so the laptop can use the phone's data over Wi-Fi. This is called</p>","options":["a personal hotspot (tethering)","a handover","pairing","roaming"],"correct":0,"why":"Key: A. Sharing a phone's mobile data with other devices is tethering via a personal hotspot. Roaming uses another operator's network; pairing is Bluetooth; handover is moving between cells.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 23","q":"<p>At Toussaint Louverture Airport, Rhea sees two networks: the airport's official one and 'Free_Airport_WiFi2' with no password. What is the MAIN risk of logging in to her bank on the second network?</p>","options":["It may be a fake (evil twin) hotspot used to capture her data","Her battery will drain instantly","Bank apps cannot work on any Wi-Fi","It will use up her mobile data bundle"],"correct":0,"why":"Key: A. An open hotspot imitating the real network can be used to intercept log-ins. Battery and data use are not the main risk, and bank apps do work on Wi-Fi.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 24","q":"<p>Mr Lake, who lives in Dutch Quarter, sends his daily blood-pressure readings from a home monitor through his phone to a heart specialist overseas and has monthly video consultations. This is an example of</p>","options":["telemedicine","e-commerce","roaming","cloud gaming"],"correct":0,"why":"Key: A. Health care delivered at a distance using telecommunications is telemedicine. The other options are unrelated uses.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 25","q":"<p>Kayla says that a WhatsApp video call to her cousin in Antigua and reading the CXC website are both uses of the World Wide Web. Which statement is correct?</p>","options":["Only the video call uses the Internet","Neither uses the Internet","Both use the Internet, but only the website is part of the World Wide Web","Both are part of the World Wide Web"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. The Web is linked pages accessed with HTTP; the call is a VoIP service that uses the Internet but not the Web. The other options misunderstand the Internet–Web relationship.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 26","q":"<p>Keisha is booking a snorkelling trip on the site https://www.tobagotours.co.tt/trips/reef.html and wants to know which country's domain it uses. Which part of the URL is the top-level domain?</p>","options":["https","/trips/","reef.html",".tt"],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. The TLD is the last part of the domain name: .tt (Trinidad and Tobago). https is the protocol, /trips/ the path and reef.html the file name.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 27","q":"<p>Ashley types www.coralreefcafe.gd into her browser. Before the page can load, which system converts that name into the web server's IP address?</p>","options":["HTML","VoIP","FTP","DNS"],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. The Domain Name System translates domain names into IP addresses. HTML writes pages, FTP transfers files and VoIP carries calls.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 28","q":"<p>Ms Hodge is about to pay school fees online. The address starts with https:// and shows a padlock. What does the HTTPS protocol guarantee?</p>","options":["The site contains no errors","The page will load faster","Data sent between her browser and the server is encrypted","The organisation running the site is honest"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. HTTPS encrypts data in transit. It does not prove a site is honest (scammers can use HTTPS), error-free or faster.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 29","q":"<p>A web designer finishes a website for a guavaberry shop on her laptop. To make it available to the world, she logs in to the hosting server and transfers the files. What is she doing, and with which protocol?</p>","options":["Downloading, using HTTP","Downloading, using VoIP","Uploading, using DNS","Uploading, using FTP/SFTP"],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. Sending files from her laptop to the server is uploading, and FTP/SFTP is the file transfer protocol. DNS translates names, VoIP carries calls, and downloading is the opposite direction.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 30","q":"<p>A small business in Spanish Town stores all its records in cloud storage. During a two-day Internet outage after a storm, what problem is it MOST likely to face?</p>","options":["Its records are permanently deleted","Staff cannot access the records until the connection returns","The cloud provider loses all data on the island","Its computers stop turning on"],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. Cloud data is safe off-site but needs an Internet connection to reach it. An outage does not delete cloud data, stop computers starting or affect data held overseas.","fb":[]}]},{"id":"test_s3","title":"Section 3 Test","desc":"<p>Section 3: Social and Economic Impact of ICT. 30 multiple-choice items. Pass mark 70%.</p>","minutes":0,"items":[{"t":"mc","title":"Item 1","q":"<p>A hacker breaks into a Barbados school's system and changes several students' final marks. The system stays online and nothing is copied. Which security goal has been broken?</p>","options":["Confidentiality","Portability","Availability","Integrity"],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. The marks are now inaccurate (integrity). Nothing was disclosed (A) and the system stayed online (C); portability is not a security goal.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 2","q":"<p>An audit of a Willemstad law office finds every PC still uses the factory password 'admin'. This password is BEST described as a</p>","options":["threat","attack","vulnerability","countermeasure"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. It is a weakness. A threat is something that could exploit it, an attack is the actual attempt, a countermeasure reduces risk.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 3","q":"<p>Within an hour of one infected laptop joining a hotel network in Ocho Rios, every unpatched PC is infected, though no one opened a file. The malware is MOST likely a</p>","options":["virus","trojan","worm","adware program"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. Self-spreading without user action is a worm. A virus needs a host file to be opened; a trojan relies on disguise; adware shows adverts.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 4","q":"<p>Shania downloads a free movie app that works normally but secretly records her passwords. The app is BEST described as a</p>","options":["firewall","denial-of-service attack","trojan carrying spyware","worm"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. It is disguised as useful (trojan) and spies (spyware). It did not self-replicate (A). C is a protection; D floods servers.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 5","q":"<p>Customers of a Trinidad bank type the bank's correct web address but arrive at a convincing fake site. This attack is</p>","options":["plagiarism","vishing","phishing","pharming"],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. Correct address, wrong site = pharming (e.g. poisoned DNS). Phishing needs a fake link; vishing is by phone; plagiarism is unrelated.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 6","q":"<p>A resident of Nevis receives a text saying she has won a cruise and must reply with her card number. This is</p>","options":["vishing","spear phishing by e-mail","smishing","pharming"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. Phishing by SMS = smishing. Vishing is by voice call; C uses e-mail; pharming redirects web traffic.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 7","q":"<p>A ferry company's booking website in Anguilla goes offline for six hours because thousands of infected devices flood it with requests. This is a</p>","options":["pharming attack","case of industrial espionage","DDoS attack","software piracy"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. Many devices flooding a server = distributed denial of service. The others do not involve flooding.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 8","q":"<p>Kerry copies an 1850 poem, which is out of copyright, and enters it in a school competition as her own. This is</p>","options":["plagiarism only","copyright infringement only","both plagiarism and infringement","neither"],"correct":0,"why":"Key: A. No copyright exists, so it is not infringement, but she claimed the work as hers: plagiarism.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 9","q":"<p>A Grenada business buys one licence for accounting software and installs it on six office PCs. This breaks the</p>","options":["Data Protection Act's accuracy principle","rules of e-mail netiquette","End User Licence Agreement, so it is software piracy","acceptable use policy of the bank"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. Installing beyond the licence terms in the EULA is piracy. The other options are unrelated documents or principles.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 10","q":"<p>Over three weeks, a Form 4 student in Road Town is mocked daily in group chats, stops sleeping well and misses classes. The MAIN impact on him is</p>","options":["financial","legal","national security","psychological, affecting his education"],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. Anxiety and sleep loss are psychological, with knock-on effects on school. There is no evidence of A, C or D.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 11","q":"<p>Ransomware shuts down the computer systems of a company that unloads containers at the Belize City tender port for a week. The MOST likely knock-on impact is that</p>","options":["the airport runway closes","mobile phones stop working","students cannot use Google Classroom","supermarkets and hardware stores run short of stock"],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. Imports cannot be processed, so businesses run short. B, C and D are not linked to the port's systems.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 12","q":"<p>A small Antigua hotel refused to pay a ransom and restored its data from backups but still lost money. Its LARGEST hidden cost was MOST likely</p>","options":["printer ink","lost bookings while systems were down","its antivirus subscription","the ransom note"],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. Downtime and lost revenue are usually the biggest hidden cost. The ransom was not paid; C and D are trivial.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 13","q":"<p>Why is the leak of citizens' fingerprints from a national ID database MORE serious in the long term than the theft of their bank cards?</p>","options":["Bank cards cannot be replaced","Fingerprints are not personal data","Fingerprints cannot be changed","Fingerprints are worth more money"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. Biometric data is permanent; cards can be cancelled. C and D are false; B misses the point.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 14","q":"<p>Hackers try to disrupt the computers that control water pumping for an entire Caribbean island. This is especially serious for the government because</p>","options":["the water company loses advertising","it is a form of plagiarism","water supply is critical national infrastructure","water companies do not use computers"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. Critical infrastructure attacks threaten public health and safety. B and C are irrelevant; D is false.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 15","q":"<p>The Port of Spain power supply often cuts out for a few minutes. An office wants staff to be able to save their work and shut down safely. It should install</p>","options":["an IDS","a surge protector","a UPS","a firewall"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. A UPS provides short-term battery power. A surge protector handles spikes; C and D are network protections.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 16","q":"<p>A Portsmouth shop kept its only backup drive in the same desk as its PC, and a flood destroyed both. The BEST improvement is to</p>","options":["back up more carefully on Fridays","buy a more expensive drive","install antivirus","keep an automatic backup in the cloud or another location"],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. Off-site/cloud copies survive local disasters. A and D keep the same location problem; C does not stop floods.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 17","q":"<p>The IT technician at a St. Lucia credit union is choosing a login method for staff who approve loans. Which combination is a TRUE example of two-factor authentication?</p>","options":["Password and PIN","Two different passwords","Username and password","Password and a code from an authenticator app"],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. Something you know plus something you have. A and C are both 'know'; D is single-factor.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 18","q":"<p>A laptop holding a St. Kitts clinic's patient records is stolen from a car. Which countermeasure would mean the thief CANNOT read the records?</p>","options":["A screen saver","A larger hard drive","A surge protector","Full-disk encryption"],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. Encrypted data is unreadable without the key. The others do not protect stored data.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 19","q":"<p>At a Jamaican hospital, porters can view full patient histories. Which countermeasure BEST fixes this?</p>","options":["A UPS","Turning off the firewall","Access privileges based on least privilege","Faster computers"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. Give each role only the access it needs. B and C are irrelevant; D weakens security.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 20","q":"<p>A teacher's grade was changed overnight in a school system. Which countermeasure shows WHO made the change and WHEN?</p>","options":["Hidden SSID","Audit trail","Surge protector","Cable lock"],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. An audit trail logs actions with user and time. The others do not record activity.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 21","q":"<p>Mr. Hodge receives an e-mail from support@rnicrosoft.com asking him to reset his password. Which habit would reveal the trick MOST directly?</p>","options":["Forwarding it to colleagues","Replying to ask if it is genuine","Inspecting the address for substituted characters","Clicking the link to check"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. 'rn' replaces 'm'. Replying, clicking or forwarding all engage with the attacker or spread the risk.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 22","q":"<p>A café in Cable Beach that lets customers use its Wi-Fi still protects the network with WEP encryption, and the owner worries about eavesdropping. The technician should advise</p>","options":["keeping WEP because it is strongest","removing the password","only hiding the network name","upgrading to WPA2 or WPA3 with a strong passphrase"],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. WEP is easily cracked. C makes it open; D is only a speed bump.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 23","q":"<p>A Trinidad supermarket replaces 8 of 12 cashier tills with self-checkouts. Which plan BEST balances automation's benefits with its impact on staff?</p>","options":["Introduce them gradually and retrain cashiers for new roles","Cancel the self-checkouts","Dismiss eight cashiers immediately","Install them without telling staff"],"correct":0,"why":"Key: A. Benefits are kept and workers are redeployed. A ignores human cost; B gives up the benefits; D damages trust.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 24","q":"<p>Ms. Gonsalves, an insurance clerk, works from home in Mesopotamia using a laptop, video calls and cloud storage. Which is a DISADVANTAGE for her?</p>","options":["More time with family","Feeling isolated from colleagues","No commuting costs","Flexible hours"],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. Isolation is a key disadvantage; A, B and D are advantages.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 25","q":"<p>Who in a Guyanese bank would back up the customer database, set which staff can access it and tune slow queries?</p>","options":["Computer engineer","Web designer","Trainer","Database administrator"],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. These are core DBA duties. B designs websites, C teaches users, D designs hardware.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 26","q":"<p>A Jamaican hospital hires someone to interview staff, evaluate the current paper process and specify a new computerised system for programmers. This person is a</p>","options":["graphic artist","programmer","systems analyst","IT technician"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. Defining problems and specifying solutions is the analyst's role. The programmer then codes it.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 27","q":"<p>A small hospital in Dominica sends a CT scan at night to a radiologist in Jamaica, who returns a report within an hour. A drawback of this approach is that</p>","options":["it is always slower than a local radiologist","it requires the patient to fly to Jamaica","CT scans cannot be sent electronically","the remote radiologist cannot examine the patient and it fails if the Internet is down"],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. Teleradiology depends on connectivity and limits follow-up. B, C and D are false.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 28","q":"<p>A St. John’s supermarket's tills scan barcodes and automatically reduce the stock count for each item sold. This is a function of</p>","options":["teleradiology","an IDS","EPOS","CAD"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. Electronic point of sale records sales and updates stock. The others belong to medicine, design and security.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 29","q":"<p>Tourists point their phones at Brimstone Hill Fortress and see historical images and labels appear over the live camera view. This technology is</p>","options":["virtual reality","cloud computing","process control","augmented reality"],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. AR adds digital content to the real world. VR replaces the view entirely; C and D are unrelated.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 30","q":"<p>After a hurricane, the Caymanian government wants close-up images of roof damage across several districts quickly and safely. The MOST suitable technology is</p>","options":["e-readers","drones with cameras","3-D printers","EPOS systems"],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. Drones survey damage quickly without risking people. The others do not capture images.","fb":[]}]},{"id":"test_s4","title":"Section 4 Test","desc":"<p>Section 4: Word-Processing and Web Page Design. 30 multiple-choice items. Pass mark 70%.</p>","minutes":0,"items":[{"t":"mc","title":"Item 1","q":"<p>Tamara is typing a report on Liberta Marina and needs to show the area of a berth as 45 m². Which feature formats the 2 correctly?</p>","options":["Subscript","Strikethrough","Small caps","Superscript"],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. The 2 in m² is raised above the line: superscript. Subscript (A) lowers text, as in H₂O. Strikethrough (B) crosses text out, and small caps (D) changes letter shapes, not position.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 2","q":"<p>A 12-page SBA report on the San Nicolaas Community Garden needs one wide table printed sideways on page 8 while all other pages stay upright. What must the student insert before and after the table?</p>","options":["Text wrapping breaks","Section breaks (Next Page)","Column breaks","Page breaks"],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. Orientation belongs to a section, so page 8 needs its own section. Page breaks (A) start a new page without a new section; column breaks (B) are for newsletter columns; text wrapping breaks (C) affect text around objects.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 3","q":"<p>The secretary of the Kaya Grandi Traders Association wants the association's name to appear at the top of every page of a 20-page report automatically. Which feature should she use?</p>","options":["Header","Footnote","Heading 1 style","Title page"],"correct":0,"why":"Key: A. A header repeats in the top margin of every page. A footnote (A) sits at the bottom of one page; a heading (C) is a section title, not repeated text; a title page (D) appears once.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 4","q":"<p>Andre formatted ten headings in a 15-page report by hand. His teacher asks him to change all headings to green. Which feature would have let him change all ten in one step?</p>","options":["Format Painter","Word count","A heading style","Find and Replace"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. Modifying a style updates every heading using it. Format Painter (C) copies formatting but painted copies do not update later; Find and Replace (B) works on text; word count (D) only counts.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 5","q":"<p>Keisha copies a paragraph from a utility company's web page into her newsletter, but it arrives in a large blue font with a grey background. Which paste option makes it match the newsletter's own formatting?</p>","options":["Keep Text Only","Paste link","Picture","Keep Source Formatting"],"correct":0,"why":"Key: A. Keep Text Only drops the web page's formatting. Keep Source Formatting (A) keeps the problem; Picture (C) makes it uneditable; Paste link (D) keeps a link to a source file and does not fix formatting.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 6","q":"<p>In the bibliography of an SBA report on Caribbean fisheries, the first line of each reference starts at the margin and the remaining lines are indented. This is a</p>","options":["right indent","first-line indent","fully justified paragraph","hanging indent"],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. Everything except the first line indented is a hanging indent. A first-line indent (A) indents only line 1; a right indent (C) moves the right edge; justification (B) is alignment, not indentation.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 7","q":"<p>The menu of the Guavaberry Grove Café lists prices such as $4.50, $12.75 and $105.00. Which tab stop lines up the prices on their decimal points?</p>","options":["Centre","Left","Right","Decimal"],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. A decimal tab aligns numbers on the point. Left (A) aligns starts, centre (B) centres text, and right (D) aligns the ends, which only works if every price has the same number of decimal places and is a weaker answer.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 8","q":"<p>Kevin replaces every “Manager” with “Supervisor” in a 30-page pharmacy handbook using Replace All with no options set. Afterwards “Management” reads “Supervisorment”. Which option would have prevented this?</p>","options":["Use wildcards","Match case","Sounds like","Find whole words only"],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. Whole words only stops matches inside longer words. Match case (B) checks capitals, which does not help here; wildcards (C) and Sounds like (D) widen the search rather than narrowing it.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 9","q":"<p>Jordan's letter says “Please reply buy Friday” and the spelling checker found no errors. Why?</p>","options":["The letter is too short to check","The checker was switched off","“buy” is a real word, so the dictionary check accepts it","Spelling checkers only check names"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. Spell checkers compare words with a dictionary, so real words used wrongly (buy/by) pass. That is why proofreading is needed. The other options are not how checkers work.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 10","q":"<p>Three teachers at the Sea-Almond Secondary School must suggest edits to the hurricane plan so the principal can accept or reject each one. Which feature should they use?</p>","options":["Mark as Final","Comments only","Track Changes","Format Painter"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. Track Changes records edits for accept/reject. Comments (A) suggest without editing; Mark as Final (C) discourages editing; Format Painter (D) copies formatting.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 11","q":"<p>The office manager of the Otrobanda Hotel wants to make sure front-desk staff cannot OPEN a document listing staff salaries. Which feature is MOST suitable?</p>","options":["Always Open Read-Only","Mark as Final","Encrypt with Password","Restrict Editing ▸ Comments"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. Only encryption with a password stops the file being opened. Mark as Final (C) and read-only (B) still let anyone read it; Restrict Editing (D) limits changes, not reading.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 12","q":"<p>A permission slip from the Breadfruit Valley Secondary PTA must let parents tick boxes and type names but not change any wording. Which setting achieves this?</p>","options":["Mark as Final","Restrict Editing ▸ Filling in forms","Track Changes","Encrypt with Password"],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. Filling in forms allows input only in form controls. Mark as Final (B) blocks normal editing including filling in; Track Changes (A) records but allows any change; encryption (D) controls opening, not editing.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 13","q":"<p>A power cut hits while Dario is typing his SBA. When he restarts Word, it offers a recovered version of his document. Which safety feature saved his work?</p>","options":["AutoRecover","Thesaurus","AutoCorrect","Format Painter"],"correct":0,"why":"Key: A. AutoRecover saves recovery information at intervals (10 minutes by default). AutoCorrect (A) fixes typing errors; the others are unrelated.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 14","q":"<p>Simone wants Word to generate a table of contents for her report on the Dominican Republic Fisheries Cooperative. What must she do FIRST?</p>","options":["Type the headings in bold","Insert page numbers by hand","Apply heading styles to the headings","Put the headings in a table"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. Word builds a TOC from heading styles. Bold (A) changes appearance only; typed page numbers (C) are unreliable; a table (D) is irrelevant.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 15","q":"<p>After generating a TOC, Marlon adds a new section called “Interviews with Fishers”. Which option should he choose when updating the TOC?</p>","options":["Update entire table","No update is needed","Delete and retype the TOC","Update page numbers only"],"correct":0,"why":"Key: A. New or renamed headings need an entire-table update. Page numbers only (A) misses the new heading; retyping (C) is unnecessary; the TOC does not update itself (B).","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 16","q":"<p>In a mail merge of 640 Sports Day letters for Coralstone PTA, the Excel file containing parents' names, addresses and children's forms is called the</p>","options":["merge field","data source","merged document","primary document"],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. The list of variable details is the data source (data file). The primary document (B) holds the standard letter; a merge field (C) is a placeholder; the merged document (C) is the output.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 17","q":"<p>The Island Water Sports filters its recipient list with: District Equal to Punda AND Balance Greater than 0. Which customer receives a letter?</p>","options":["Otrobanda, balance 140.50","Willemstad, balance 85.00","Punda, balance 40.00","Punda, balance 0.00"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. With AND, both conditions must be true: Punda and a balance above 0. Options (B) and (A) fail the district condition; option (D) fails the balance condition.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 18","q":"<p>Kezia has set up the first label of a sheet of mailing labels with an Address Block. The other labels show only «Next Record». What should she click next?</p>","options":["Preview Results","Finish & Merge","Update Labels","Greeting Line"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. Update Labels copies the first label's layout to every label. Preview (A) would show mostly blank labels; Greeting Line (C) is for letters; finishing now (B) would print empty labels.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 19","q":"<p>A registration form for the Trinidad and Tobago Youth Sports Camp asks for T-shirt size: S, M, L or XL. Which form control is BEST?</p>","options":["Drop-down list","Date picker","Plain text","Picture"],"correct":0,"why":"Key: A. A drop-down limits answers to valid choices. Plain text (A) allows misspellings; date picker (B) is for dates; picture (D) is for images.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 20","q":"<p>Tanya draws a diagram showing that the home page of the Grenada Steel Pan Orchestra site links to About, Concerts, Join and Contact. This planning tool is a</p>","options":["style sheet","wireframe","site map","web host"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. A site map shows pages and links. A wireframe (B) shows one page's layout; a style sheet (C) holds formatting; a web host (D) provides server space.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 21","q":"<p>The Brades Jubilee Library is planning a website for adults over 60. Which design decision BEST suits this intended audience?</p>","options":["Small decorative fonts to fit more on the page","Flashing animations on every page","Large, clear, high-contrast text and simple layouts","Navigation hidden behind icons with no labels"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. Older readers benefit from large, clear text and simple layouts. The other options reduce readability and usability for this audience.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 22","q":"<p>On the Hellshire Beach Eco Club site, which HTML correctly displays a photo with a suitable text alternative?</p>","options":["&lt;image file=\"cleanup.jpg\" text=\"photo\"&gt;","&lt;img src=\"cleanup.jpg\" alt=\"Students collecting plastic on Hellshire beach\"&gt;","&lt;a src=\"cleanup.jpg\"&gt;Photo&lt;/a&gt;","&lt;img alt=\"cleanup.jpg\"&gt;"],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. img needs src (the file) and alt (a meaningful description). Option (D) has no source; option (C) uses the anchor tag wrongly; option (A) uses invalid tag and attribute names.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 23","q":"<p>A student's page shows the words “Cruise ships at Port Zante” instead of the photo after uploading. On her laptop the photo appeared. What is the MOST likely cause?</p>","options":["The alt text is too long","The image file was not uploaded or its name/path does not match exactly on the server","The page has no title","JPEG images are not allowed on the Web"],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. Alt text appears when the browser cannot load the image, usually a missing upload or a case/path mismatch. Alt length (A) never hides images; JPEG (B) is standard; the title (D) affects only the tab.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 24","q":"<p>A student in Charlestown is researching local news for a Nevis heritage project and finds the page http://www.nevispages.com/news.htm. In this URL, what is the domain name?</p>","options":["http://www.nevispages.com","news.htm",".com","nevispages.com"],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. The domain name is nevispages.com; www is not part of it. Option (A) includes the protocol and server name; option (B) is the page file; option (C) is the extension.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 25","q":"<p>Keon finishes the Montserrat Volcano Tours site on his laptop and copies the files to his hosting company's server. Which protocol is normally used for this upload?</p>","options":["DNS","SMTP","FTP","HTTP"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. FTP (File Transfer Protocol) transfers files to the web server. HTTP (A) is used to download pages into a browser; SMTP (C) sends e-mail; DNS (B) translates domain names to IP addresses.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 26","q":"<p>Barbados Reef Divers wants a link that opens a new e-mail to bookings@reefdivers.example.bb. Which href is correct?</p>","options":["href=\"https://bookings@reefdivers.example.bb\"","href=\"bookings@reefdivers.example.bb\"","href=\"#bookings\"","href=\"mailto:bookings@reefdivers.example.bb\""],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. E-mail links need mailto:. Option (B) is treated as a file path; option (C) jumps to a bookmark; option (A) tries to open a web page.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 27","q":"<p>A long FAQ page for the St. Lucia Jazz Festival has a heading with id=\"tickets\". A link at the top uses href=\"#Tickets\" and does nothing. Why?</p>","options":["Bookmarks only work in Word","The link needs mailto:","The bookmark name must match exactly, including capital letters","Headings cannot be bookmarks"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. Fragment identifiers are case-sensitive: #Tickets does not match tickets. Bookmarks work on the Web (B); mailto (C) is for e-mail; headings are common bookmark targets (D).","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 28","q":"<p>The Grenada Spice Co-op website links to a PDF price list that the co-op created. Which type of hyperlink is this?</p>","options":["A link to a user-created file","A link to another web page","A link to an e-mail address","A link to a bookmark"],"correct":0,"why":"Key: A. A PDF the organisation made is a user-created file. The other types go to pages, positions on a page or e-mail.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 29","q":"<p>While evaluating Trinidad Carnival Visitor Guide, a student finds that the fete calendar still shows last year's dates. Which evaluation criterion is MOST affected?</p>","options":["Accessibility","Display on devices","Load time","Accuracy"],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. Out-of-date information is an accuracy (currency) problem. It does not affect loading speed (B), layout on devices (C) or access for people with disabilities (D).","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 30","q":"<p>The home page of the TCI Island Ferry Express site has a 9 MB photo and takes 25 seconds to load on a phone. Which action BEST improves the page?</p>","options":["Resize and compress the photo before uploading","Underline all the links","Add alt text to the photo","Change the domain extension to .net"],"correct":0,"why":"Key: A. Smaller files load faster. Alt text (A) improves accessibility, not speed; the domain (C) and underlining (B) do not affect load time.","fb":[]}]},{"id":"test_s5","title":"Section 5 Test","desc":"<p>Section 5: Spreadsheets. 30 multiple-choice items. Pass mark 70%.</p>","minutes":0,"items":[{"t":"mc","title":"Item 1","q":"<p>Mr. Hodge, treasurer of a Belize City Carnival troupe, wants totals to update automatically whenever a costume price changes. Which feature of a spreadsheet makes this possible?</p>","options":["Mail merge","Spell check","Print preview","Automatic recalculation"],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. Formulas recalculate when their input cells change. A and C are word-processing features; D only previews printing.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 2","q":"<p>A Form 4 student opens a worksheet and sees the Name Box showing K12. What does K12 identify?</p>","options":["Column K, row 12","Row K, column 12","A range of 12 cells","The 12th worksheet"],"correct":0,"why":"Key: A. A cell address is the column letter followed by the row number. A reverses them; B and D misread the address.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 3","q":"<p>A teacher at Palmetto Point Secondary selects the range C3:F10 to format test marks. How many cells are selected?</p>","options":["32","40","28","24"],"correct":0,"why":"Key: A. C–F is 4 columns and rows 3–10 is 8 rows: 4 × 8 = 32. A and B miscount the rows; C adds an extra row and column.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 4","q":"<p>In a tuck-shop sheet, cell D4 displays 37.50 but the formula bar shows =B4*C4. Which statement is correct?</p>","options":["D4 contains a label","The formula bar is faulty","D4 contains a value typed by the user","D4 contains a formula and displays its result"],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. The formula bar shows the true contents; the cell shows the result. C and B ignore the formula; D is not a real fault.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 5","q":"<p>A school stores student ID numbers such as 00427 in a spreadsheet, but they display as 427. How should the IDs be entered so the zeros remain?</p>","options":["Add a decimal point","Type an apostrophe before each ID or format the cells as Text","Use the SUM function","Widen the column"],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. An apostrophe or Text format stores the ID as a label. A, B and D do not stop leading zeros being dropped.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 6","q":"<p>A Grenada farmer's spreadsheet has B2 = 12, C2 = 4 and D2 = 2. What does =B2+C2*D2 display?</p>","options":["18","24","32","20"],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. Multiplication first: 4 × 2 = 8, then 12 + 8 = 20. B adds first; C and D are arithmetic slips.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 7","q":"<p>A student's three test marks are in B5, C5 and D5. Which formula correctly calculates her average?</p>","options":["=SUM(B5:D5)*3","=AVERAGE(B5,D5)","=(B5+C5+D5)/3","=B5+C5+D5/3"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. Brackets make the addition happen before division. A divides only D5; C multiplies; B omits C5.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 8","q":"<p>A ferry office lists passenger NAMES in A2:A41. Which formula counts how many passengers are listed?</p>","options":["=COUNTIF(A2:A41,\">0\")","=SUM(A2:A41)","=COUNTA(A2:A41)","=COUNT(A2:A41)"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. COUNTA counts non-empty cells including text. COUNT counts only numbers (0 here); SUM adds numbers; the COUNTIF criterion tests numbers.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 9","q":"<p>A grade book has averages in F2:F30. Which formula counts the students with an average of 50 or more?</p>","options":["=COUNT(F2:F30>=50)","=COUNTIF(F2:F30,>=50)","=IF(F2:F30>=50)","=COUNTIF(F2:F30,\">=50\")"],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. COUNTIF needs the range, then the criterion in quotation marks. B lacks quotes; C and D are not valid ways to count.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 10","q":"<p>A credit union pays a $25 bonus to members whose savings in C4 are at least $1,000; otherwise nothing. Which formula is correct?</p>","options":["=IF(C4>=1000,25,0)","=IF(C4>1000,25,0)","=IF(C4<=1000,25,0)","=IF(C4>=1000,\"25\",\"0\")"],"correct":0,"why":"Key: A. 'At least' means >=. A excludes exactly $1,000; C returns text instead of numbers; B reverses the test.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 11","q":"<p>A Little Bay hardware store's order form uses =VLOOKUP(E2,$A$2:$C$50,3,FALSE). A cashier types a code that is not in the price list. What appears?</p>","options":["0","#REF!","The nearest code's price","#N/A"],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. With FALSE (exact match), a missing code returns #N/A. D would happen only with TRUE; A and C are not VLOOKUP's response.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 12","q":"<p>Swim times are in B2:B9; the fastest (smallest) time should be ranked 1st. Which formula in C2 can be copied down?</p>","options":["=RANK($B$2,B2:B9,1)","=RANK(B2,$B$2:$B$9)","=RANK(B2,B2:B9,1)","=RANK(B2,$B$2:$B$9,1)"],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. Order 1 ranks ascending, and the list is locked. A ranks the slowest first; B's range slides when copied; D always ranks the first swimmer.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 13","q":"<p>A librarian's sheet has a due date in C2 and today's date in D2. Which formula shows how many days the book is overdue?</p>","options":["=C2-D2","=TODAY","=D2+C2","=D2-C2"],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. Today minus the due date gives days late. A gives a negative number for overdue books; C is meaningless; B lacks brackets and a calculation.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 14","q":"<p>Kayla is calculating the average rainfall for a Barbados weather project and types =AVERGE(B2:B12) into cell B13. What will the cell display?</p>","options":["#NAME?","#VALUE!","#REF!","#DIV/0!"],"correct":0,"why":"Key: A. A misspelt function name gives #NAME?. The other errors have different causes.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 15","q":"<p>In a New Amsterdam car-rental sheet, cell E2 contains =C2*D2 (days × daily rate). The clerk copies it to E7. What does E7 contain?</p>","options":["=C2*D2","=C7*D7","=E7*D7","=C5*D5"],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. Relative references move five rows down with the formula. B would need absolute references; C and D miscount.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 16","q":"<p>A duty-free shop stores the exchange rate in B1. Prices are in A4:A30. Which formula in B4 converts a price and can be copied down?</p>","options":["=$A$4*$B$1","=$A$4*B1","=A4*B1","=A4*$B$1"],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. The price moves; the rate is locked. A's rate slides to empty cells; B and D lock the price.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 17","q":"<p>Ravi is building a two-way ticket-price table for a Carnival fete and needs a reference in which only the COLUMN is locked. Which reference should he use?</p>","options":["$D$9","D9","$D9","D$9"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. The $ sits before the letter D. A locks the row; C locks both; D locks neither.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 18","q":"<p>A sales sheet's total in B12 is =SUM(B2:B11). A new row is inserted above row 6. What is the total now?</p>","options":["B12: =SUM(B2:B11)","B13: =SUM(B2:B12)","B13: =SUM(B2:B11)","B12: =SUM(B2:B12)"],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. The total moves down a row and the range grows to include the new row.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 19","q":"<p>A roster of 400 volunteers has headings in row 1 and names in column A. Which cell should be selected before View ▸ Freeze Panes to keep both visible?</p>","options":["A1","B2","B1","A2"],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. Freeze Panes freezes rows above and columns to the left of the active cell.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 20","q":"<p>A commission rate of 15% must be stored so formulas work. A cell holds the number 15 and the % button is clicked. What is displayed?</p>","options":["15%","150%","1500%","0.15%"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. Percentage format multiplies the display by 100: 15 becomes 1500%. Type 15% or 0.15 instead.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 21","q":"<p>A Trinidad bakery's spreadsheet has 2.456 stored in A1, formatted to show 2 decimal places. The baker enters =A1*100 in B1. What does B1 return?</p>","options":["245.60","2.46","246","245.6"],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. Formulas use the stored value 2.456, not the displayed 2.46.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 22","q":"<p>The Road Town cruise pier tour list is sorted by Tour (A to Z) and then by Price (largest to smallest). What is Price in this sort?</p>","options":["The primary key","The filter","The secondary key","The criteria range"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. The second sort field orders records within each tour.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 23","q":"<p>Leon selects only the Scores column of a class list and sorts it, ignoring Excel's warning. What is the result?</p>","options":["Excel sorts the names instead","The names are deleted","Scores no longer match the students' names","The list sorts correctly"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. Only one column moves, splitting the records apart.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 24","q":"<p>A stock list has an AutoFilter showing Warehouse = Sandy Ground and Qty less than 20. Which records are displayed?</p>","options":["All items, sorted by quantity","Sandy Ground items that also have fewer than 20","Only the first 20 Sandy Ground items","Sandy Ground items OR items with fewer than 20"],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. Filters on different fields are combined with AND.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 25","q":"<p>In an Advanced Filter criteria range, 'Paint' is typed under Category on one row and '<10' under Qty on the NEXT row. Which records are extracted?</p>","options":["No records","Paint items OR any item with fewer than 10","Only items named exactly <10","Paint items with fewer than 10"],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. Criteria on different rows are joined with OR.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 26","q":"<p>A supermarket chain wants total sales for each store and each month from 40,000 sales records, and to rearrange the summary easily. Which tool is MOST suitable?</p>","options":["Pivot table","Find and Replace","Merge & Center","Freeze Panes"],"correct":0,"why":"Key: A. A pivot table groups and summarises large lists and can be rearranged by dragging fields.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 27","q":"<p>The Meteorological Department wants to show how rainfall changed each month from June to November. Which chart is MOST suitable?</p>","options":["Bar chart of one month","Pie chart","Line chart","Doughnut chart"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. Change over time is best shown by a line chart.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 28","q":"<p>A family wants a chart showing what share of their income goes to rent, food, transport and savings. Which chart is MOST suitable?</p>","options":["Pie chart","Area chart","Line chart","Scatter chart"],"correct":0,"why":"Key: A. Parts of one whole: a pie chart.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 29","q":"<p>A summary sheet must show cell B5 from a sheet named Feb. Which formula is correct?</p>","options":["=Feb!B5","=B5!Feb","=Feb:B5","=Feb(B5)"],"correct":0,"why":"Key: A. The sheet name, an exclamation mark, then the cell.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 30","q":"<p>A workbook has sheets Jan, Feb, Mar and Apr in that order, each with total sales in D10. Which formula on a Summary sheet adds all four?</p>","options":["=SUM(D10:D10)","=SUM(Jan!D10:Apr!D10)","=SUM(Jan:Apr!D10)","=Jan:Apr+D10"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. A 3-D reference: first sheet:last sheet!cell.","fb":[]}]},{"id":"test_s6","title":"Section 6 Test","desc":"<p>Section 6: Database Management. 30 multiple-choice items. Pass mark 70%.</p>","minutes":0,"items":[{"t":"mc","title":"Item 1","q":"<p>The Turks and Caicos Islands Ferry Line keeps passenger names, sailings and seat bookings in organised, related tables that staff can search and update. What is this collection of data called?</p>","options":["A DBMS","A spreadsheet","A word-processed file","A database"],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. An organised collection of related data is a database; a DBMS is the software that manages it.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 2","q":"<p>A clinic in Rincon installs Microsoft Access to build tables, run queries and print reports. Microsoft Access is BEST described as:</p>","options":["a database management system","a database","a utility program","an operating system"],"correct":0,"why":"Key: A. Access is DBMS software; the clinic's records are the database.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 3","q":"<p>Before computers, the Island Reads Library kept a drawer of cards, one per book, sorted by title. This drawer is an example of:</p>","options":["a query","a manual database","a computerised database","a relational DBMS"],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. Paper-based organised records are a manual database.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 4","q":"<p>A Carnival troupe stores each member's phone number on every costume row of one spreadsheet. When a member changes her number, some rows are updated and others are not. This problem is called:</p>","options":["data verification","data encryption","data redundancy only","data inconsistency"],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. Redundant copies that now disagree show data inconsistency.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 5","q":"<p>In the tblGuest at a La Romana hotel there are 7 columns and 40 rows of guest data. Each row is called a:</p>","options":["key","field","record","table"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. A row holding all the data about one guest is a record (tuple).","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 6","q":"<p>A school database table has columns StudentID, FirstName, LastName and Form. The column LastName is BEST described as a:</p>","options":["field","record","table","tuple"],"correct":0,"why":"Key: A. A column is a field (attribute).","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 7","q":"<p>Island Power, the island’s electricity company, stores customer telephone numbers such as 542-1187. Which data type should the Phone field have?</p>","options":["Date/Time","Text","Number","Currency"],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. Phone numbers contain dashes and are never calculated, so Text.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 8","q":"<p>The Wallblake Road Pharmacy records whether each prescription has been collected. Which data type is MOST suitable for the Collected field?</p>","options":["Number","Long Text","Text","Yes/No"],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. Only two possible values: logical Yes/No.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 9","q":"<p>The Anguilla Ferry Line's PASSENGER table includes Name, DateOfBirth, Nationality and PassportNo. Which field is the MOST suitable primary key?</p>","options":["Name","Nationality","DateOfBirth","PassportNo"],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. Passport numbers are unique to each traveller; the others repeat.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 10","q":"<p>In a CXC results table, StudentNo and SubjectCode together uniquely identify each record. This primary key is called a:</p>","options":["candidate key only","composite key","foreign key","secondary key"],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. Two or more fields forming the primary key together make a composite key.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 11","q":"<p>In Brades Youth League, PLAYER (PlayerID, Name, TeamID) and TEAM (TeamID, TeamName, Coach). In PLAYER, TeamID is a:</p>","options":["primary key","foreign key","candidate key","composite key"],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. TeamID is TEAM's primary key placed in PLAYER to link the tables.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 12","q":"<p>In the PLAYER and TEAM tables of a youth league, each team has many players and each player plays for one team. The relationship from TEAM to PLAYER is:</p>","options":["one-to-many","one-to-one","many-to-many","many-to-one from TEAM"],"correct":0,"why":"Key: A. One team, many players: one-to-many with TEAM on the one side.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 13","q":"<p>Students at Seagrape Bay Secondary School can join many clubs and each club has many students. How should this be represented in a relational database?</p>","options":["Put ClubID in the STUDENT table","Create a linking table with StudentID and ClubID","Put StudentID in the CLUB table","Store everything in one flat file"],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. Many-to-many relationships are resolved with a linking (junction) table.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 14","q":"<p>A Grenada swim club's validation rule for Age must allow members from 8 to 17 years old inclusive. Which rule is correct?</p>","options":["8 Or 17","Between 8 And 17",">=8 Or <=17",">8 And <17"],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. Between includes both ends; the Or version accepts every number.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 15","q":"<p>A school's tblStudent has a House field that must contain Red, Blue or Gold only. Which validation rule is correct?</p>","options":["Like \"R*\"","=\"Red, Blue, Gold\"","\"Red\" And \"Blue\" And \"Gold\"","In (\"Red\",\"Blue\",\"Gold\")"],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. In checks against a list of allowed values.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 16","q":"<p>The Speightstown Car Rentals uses the input mask 000\\-0000 on its Phone field. What does the 0 mean in this mask?</p>","options":["A letter is required","Any character is allowed","A digit is required","A digit is optional"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. 0 = digit required; 9 = digit optional; L = letter required.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 17","q":"<p>Referential integrity is enforced between tblMember and tblLoan at a library with no cascade options. Which action will Access REFUSE?</p>","options":["Changing a member's phone number","Adding a new member with no loans","Adding a loan for a MemberID that does not exist","Deleting a member who has no loans"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. A foreign key value must match an existing primary key: no orphan records.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 18","q":"<p>Cascade Delete Related Records is ticked between tblCustomer and tblRental. A clerk deletes a customer who has six rentals. What happens?</p>","options":["Only the customer is deleted","The rentals are moved to another customer","The deletion is refused","The customer and the six rentals are deleted"],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. Cascade Delete removes the related child records too.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 19","q":"<p>A volunteer reduces the Field Size of a Short Text field District from 20 to 5. The field holds Georgetown. After accepting the warning, what is stored?</p>","options":["etown","Georgetown","Nothing; the record is deleted","Georg"],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. Longer values are truncated to the new field size.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 20","q":"<p>Using the Island Reads tblBook, a query has Genre: \"Fiction\" and Price: >20 on the same Criteria row. Which books are returned?</p>","options":["Fiction books costing more than $20","Every Fiction book","Books over $20 only","Fiction books or books over $20"],"correct":0,"why":"Key: A. Criteria on the same row are joined with AND.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 21","q":"<p>A Trinidad bookshop query has \"Poetry\" in the Criteria row under Genre and \"Drama\" in the or row under Genre. Which records are returned?</p>","options":["Books that are both Poetry and Drama","Only Poetry books","No books","Poetry books and Drama books"],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. Separate rows are joined with OR.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 22","q":"<p>Island Reads books cost $24.50, $45.00, $18.75, $32.00, $15.00, $28.00, $21.00, $39.99, $19.50, $26.00, $35.50 and $12.00. How many does the criterion Between 20 And 30 return?</p>","options":["6","5","3","4"],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. $24.50, $28.00, $21.00 and $26.00: four prices from 20 to 30.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 23","q":"<p>A music shop in Barbados wants every song title containing the word Island anywhere. Which criterion should be entered under Title?</p>","options":["Like \"Island*\"","Like \"*Island*\"","\"Island\"","Like \"?Island?\""],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. Stars on both sides match the word anywhere in the title.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 24","q":"<p>A Jamaican tuck shop query has the fields Quantity and UnitPrice. Which entry in the Field row correctly calculates each line's total?</p>","options":["LineTotal: [Quantity]*[UnitPrice]","=SUM(Quantity, UnitPrice)","LineTotal = Quantity*UnitPrice","[LineTotal]: Quantity x UnitPrice"],"correct":0,"why":"Key: A. Name, colon, then the expression with field names in square brackets.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 25","q":"<p>The librarian wants a query that asks \"Which genre?\" each time it runs. What should be typed in the Criteria row under Genre?</p>","options":["\"Which genre?\"","[Which genre?]","#Which genre?#","Like Which genre?"],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. A prompt in square brackets creates a parameter query.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 26","q":"<p>A teacher shows the class this statement: SELECT Title FROM tblBook WHERE Price < 20 ORDER BY Title; Which part sets the condition the records must meet?</p>","options":["WHERE Price < 20","SELECT Title","FROM tblBook","ORDER BY Title"],"correct":0,"why":"Key: A. WHERE holds the criteria; ORDER BY sorts.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 27","q":"<p>A dive shop receptionist enters new customers one at a time on a screen with labels, drop-down lists and a Save button. This Access object is a:</p>","options":["form","query","table","report"],"correct":0,"why":"Key: A. Forms are designed for entering and viewing data.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 28","q":"<p>A school secretary wants one screen showing a form class at the top and a list of all its students underneath, changing as she moves between classes. She needs:</p>","options":["a report grouped by class","a form with a subform","a parameter query","two unrelated tables"],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. A main form (one side) with a subform (many side) shows related records.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 29","q":"<p>In a Carnival committee report grouped by Section, where should =Sum([Fee]) be placed to show the total fees for EACH section?</p>","options":["Section (group) Footer","Detail","Page Footer","Report Header"],"correct":0,"why":"Key: A. Per-group totals go in the group footer; a grand total goes in the Report Footer.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 30","q":"<p>Kayla's SBA report about the Galibi Hotel is titled \"Report1\". According to the SBA mark scheme, what should she do to earn full marks for the title?</p>","options":["Use the query name as the title","Leave it, since any title earns full marks","Replace it with a specific title such as Galibi Hotel: Bookings by Room Type, October 2026","Delete the title"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. A correct, specific title earns 2 marks; a generic title earns at most 1.","fb":[]}]},{"id":"test_s7","title":"Section 7 Test","desc":"<p>Section 7: Problem-Solving and Program Design. 30 multiple-choice items. Pass mark 70%.</p>","minutes":0,"items":[{"t":"mc","title":"Item 1","q":"<p>Before building a lunch ordering app for Hummingbird Ridge High School, Kayla interviews the tuck shop manager to find out exactly what is needed. Which problem-solving step is she carrying out?</p>","options":["Test and validate","Select the optimum solution","Develop the algorithm","Define the problem"],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. Understanding what is needed is defining the problem. B and C come later; A is the final step.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 2","q":"<p>A Form 4 class breaks the problem 'clean the dirty classroom' into Input (broom, mop), Processing (sweep, mop) and Output (clean classroom). Which step is this?</p>","options":["Validate the solution","Propose solutions","Implement the program","Analyse the problem"],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. Breaking a problem into IPO is analysis. A suggests alternatives; C checks the final solution; D codes it.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 3","q":"<p>An Anguilla ferry company compares a paper list, an offline app and a cloud website, then picks the offline app because the terminal often has no Internet. This choice is called:</p>","options":["Selecting the optimum solution","Desk checking","Stepwise refinement","Defining the problem"],"correct":0,"why":"Key: A. Choosing the best solution with a justification. D is the first step; C tests algorithms; D adds detail.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 4","q":"<p>A team designing a Carnival band app draws a diagram with 'Band Registration System' in the top box and 'Register member', 'Take deposit' and 'Print list' underneath. What is this diagram?</p>","options":["Structure chart","Trace table","Flowchart","IPO table"],"correct":0,"why":"Key: A. Modules in a hierarchy. A uses symbols and arrows; B records variable values; D has three columns.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 5","q":"<p>Your teacher asks for an IPO table to accept the price of an item and display a 17% VAT. Which entry belongs in the PROCESSING column?</p>","options":["VAT","The shop's name","Price","Calculate VAT (Price * 0.17)"],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. Calculations are processing. A is input; B is output; D is irrelevant.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 6","q":"<p>A program for a Holetown marina uses RATE ← 3.25 throughout, and the value never changes while the program runs. RATE is best described as a:</p>","options":["Constant","Counter","Variable","Sentinel"],"correct":0,"why":"Key: A. A fixed value during the run is a constant. A can change; C ends input; D counts.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 7","q":"<p>A Barbados dive shop stores each customer's phone number, such as +1 246 555 0187. Which data type is most suitable?</p>","options":["Integer","Real","Boolean","String"],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. It contains + and spaces and is never used in arithmetic. A and B would lose symbols; D holds only TRUE/FALSE.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 8","q":"<p>In an algorithm for a Point Fortin shop, the value '335' is written in single quotation marks. What data type is it?</p>","options":["Real","Character","Integer","String"],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. Several characters in quotes form a string. A and B would be unquoted numbers; C is one character.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 9","q":"<p>Jaheim is choosing variable names for a cricket scoring program in Pascal. Which identifier is INVALID?</p>","options":["totalRuns","2ndInnings","overs_2","Wickets"],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. Identifiers cannot start with a digit. A, B and D follow the rules.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 10","q":"<p>A student lists these as characteristics of a good algorithm for a Waitukubuli Power & Water bill program. Which is NOT a characteristic?</p>","options":["It can be interpreted in many different ways","Its steps are unambiguous","It has a finite number of steps","It terminates"],"correct":0,"why":"Key: A. Steps must have exactly one meaning. A, B and D are all characteristics.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 11","q":"<p>In the pseudocode, which statement is a PROMPTING statement for a library fines program?</p>","options":["Fine ← DaysLate * 0.50","Read DaysLate","IF DaysLate > 0 THEN","Write \"Enter the number of days late\""],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. A prompt uses Write to ask for data. A is input; C is assignment; D is selection.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 12","q":"<p>Which symbol is used for assignment in Pascal, as in the SBA programs written at Calabash Point Secondary?</p>","options":["==","<>",":=","←"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. Pascal uses :=. A is pseudocode; C is Python comparison; D means not equal.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 13","q":"<p>During a desk check of a calculation for a Jamaican hardware store, what is stored in Total by the line Total ← 6 + 4 * 2 ^ 2?</p>","options":["38","40","22","100"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. Powers first: 2 ^ 2 = 4; then 4 * 4 = 16; then 6 + 16 = 22. B ignores BODMAS; C and D apply the operations in the wrong order.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 14","q":"<p>A Weather Club program tests IF rainfall > 20 AND wind > 50 THEN. On a day with 25 mm of rain and wind of 40 km/h, the condition is:</p>","options":["TRUE, because one part is enough","Impossible to tell","TRUE, because rainfall > 20","FALSE, because both parts must be TRUE for AND"],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. 40 > 50 is FALSE, so the AND is FALSE. A and C describe OR; D: both values are given.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 15","q":"<p>Using Boolean logic, a student evaluates NOT (TRUE OR FALSE) for a Paper 01 revision question. What is the result?</p>","options":["TRUE","FALSE","1","Cannot be evaluated"],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. TRUE OR FALSE = TRUE; NOT TRUE = FALSE. A and C are the value before NOT; D: it can be evaluated.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 16","q":"<p>A raffle program at Coralstone College uses FOR NUM = 10 TO 100 STEP 10 DO. How many times does the loop body run?</p>","options":["11","100","9","10"],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. 10, 20, …, 100 is ten values. A misses one end; C adds one; D ignores STEP.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 17","q":"<p>A cruise passenger program reads counts until the operator types 0, and some days there are no ships. Which loop is most suitable?</p>","options":["WHILE … ENDWHILE","FOR … ENDFOR","REPEAT … UNTIL","IF … ENDIF"],"correct":0,"why":"Key: A. Unknown number of repetitions, possibly zero: a pre-test WHILE loop. A needs a known count; C always runs once; D is not a loop.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 18","q":"<p>What is the minimum number of times the body of a REPEAT … UNTIL loop runs in a PIN-checking program for an ATM on King Street?</p>","options":["0","2","1","It depends on a FOR counter"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. The condition is tested after the body. A describes WHILE; C and D are wrong.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 19","q":"<p>Trace this algorithm from a Sea-Almond Secondary School worksheet: x ← 4; y ← x * 3; x ← y − x; Write x, y. What is output?</p>","options":["8, 12","12, 8","8, 4","4, 12"],"correct":0,"why":"Key: A. y = 12; x = 12 − 4 = 8. A uses the old x; C swaps them; D uses the wrong y.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 20","q":"<p>Trace: t ← 0; FOR i = 2 TO 6 STEP 2 DO t ← t + i ENDFOR; Write t. This is from a fundraiser tally for a Trinidad school. What is output?</p>","options":["20","30","12","6"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. i = 2, 4, 6; t = 12. A stops early; C adds all numbers 2–6; B is wrong.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 21","q":"<p>Trace: n ← 20; c ← 0; WHILE n > 3 DO n ← n DIV 2; c ← c + 1 ENDWHILE; Write c, n. What is output?</p>","options":["3, 5","4, 1","2, 5","3, 2"],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. n: 20 → 10 → 5 → 2 (three passes); 2 > 3 is FALSE. A stops one pass early; C goes one too far; D mixes values.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 22","q":"<p>A Grand Anse Beach swim club accepts members aged 8 to 16 inclusive. Which pair of values are BOTH boundary test data?</p>","options":["-3 and 'ten'","10 and 12","8 and 17","12 and 30"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. 8 is the lower edge and 17 is just above the upper edge. A is normal; C is erroneous; D mixes normal and erroneous.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 23","q":"<p>A commission algorithm should pay 5% on sales MORE THAN $50,000 but uses IF sales >= 50000 THEN. The program runs without crashing. What type of error is this?</p>","options":["Run-time error","Logic error","No error","Syntax error"],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. It runs but gives a wrong result for exactly $50,000. A stops translation; B crashes; D: it is wrong.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 24","q":"<p>Which flowchart symbol should contain 'Read Gallons' in a flowchart for a water company?</p>","options":["Oval","Rhombus","Parallelogram","Rectangle"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. Input uses the parallelogram. A is Start/Stop; C is processing; D is a decision.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 25","q":"<p>In a flowchart for a Providenciales Airport baggage robot, a diamond 'Is weight <> 0?' sits above the loop body, and an arrow from the end of the body returns to it. Which pseudocode structure does this show?</p>","options":["FOR … ENDFOR with STEP","REPEAT … UNTIL","WHILE … ENDWHILE","IF … THEN … ELSE"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. A decision before the body with a loop-back is a pre-test WHILE loop. A has no loop-back; C tests after the body; D needs a counter.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 26","q":"<p>Kaya the Robo Lab rover faces East at (0, 0) and runs [drive forward (3)] [turn left (90)] [drive forward (2)]. Where does she finish?</p>","options":["(3, 2) facing East","(3, 2) facing North","(2, 3) facing North","(3, -2) facing South"],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. East 3 to (3, 0); left turn faces North; up 2 to (3, 2). B swaps coordinates; C turns the wrong way; D forgets the turn.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 27","q":"<p>A beach clean-up robot program uses the block [repeat (10)]. Which CXC pseudocode structure matches it?</p>","options":["FOR i = 1 TO 10 DO … ENDFOR","REPEAT … UNTIL FALSE","WHILE TRUE DO","IF … THEN"],"correct":0,"why":"Key: A. A fixed number of repeats is a bounded FOR loop. A is selection; C and D never stop.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 28","q":"<p>A Scratch-style program for a baggage robot at John A. Osborne Airport uses [repeat until &lt;belt empty?&gt;]. Because Scratch tests this condition BEFORE each pass, the matching pseudocode is:</p>","options":["WHILE NOT beltEmpty DO … ENDWHILE","IF beltEmpty THEN","FOR i = 1 TO beltEmpty","REPEAT … UNTIL beltEmpty"],"correct":0,"why":"Key: A. A pre-test loop that continues while the condition is FALSE. A always runs once; C and D are not suitable.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 29","q":"<p>A relief robot team defines a custom block [define deliver supplies (packs)] and uses it at three shelters in Moengo. Which is the main benefit?</p>","options":["It stores data permanently","It removes the need for testing","The robot drives faster","The routine is written once and reused, so fixes are made in one place"],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. Modules give reuse and easier maintenance. A is unrelated; C is false; D describes files.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 30","q":"<p>Trace: p ← 3; q ← 5; p ← p + q; q ← p − q; p ← p − q; Write p, q. This swaps values without a temporary variable. What is output?</p>","options":["5, 3","8, 3","3, 5","8, 5"],"correct":0,"why":"Key: A. p = 8; q = 8 − 5 = 3; p = 8 − 3 = 5. C is the original; C and D stop part-way.","fb":[]}]},{"id":"test_s8","title":"Section 8 Test","desc":"<p>Section 8: Program Implementation. 30 multiple-choice items. Pass mark 70%.</p>","minutes":0,"items":[{"t":"mc","title":"Item 1","q":"<p>A technician at the Bay Volt Engineering writes instructions such as LDA 10 and ADD 11 for a meter chip. Which type of language is he using?</p>","options":["Assembly language","Machine code","A natural language","A fourth-generation language"],"correct":0,"why":"Key: A. LDA and ADD are mnemonics, used in assembly language (2GL). Machine code is binary; 4GLs and natural languages are high-level.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 2","q":"<p>Kenya says her Python program will run on both her Windows laptop and her friend's Mac after translation. Which feature of high-level languages does this describe?</p>","options":["Speed","Machine dependence","Portability","Direct hardware control"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. High-level source code is portable: it can be translated for different machines. The other options describe low-level languages.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 3","q":"<p>The Sauteurs Credit Union needs a list of all members whose loans are overdue, produced by stating WHAT data is wanted. Which generation of language is MOST suitable?</p>","options":["1GL","2GL","4GL","3GL"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. A 4GL such as SQL states the result wanted; 3GLs need step-by-step instructions; 1GL and 2GL are low-level.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 4","q":"<p>Island Byte Studios sells a game as a single .exe file so that customers never see its source code. Which translator produced the file?</p>","options":["Linker only","Compiler","Interpreter","Assembler"],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. A compiler translates the whole program into object code, which is linked into an executable. An interpreter produces no executable; an assembler handles assembly language.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 5","q":"<p>When Jaden runs his Python program, lines 1 to 6 print their results and then the program stops on line 7 with an error. Which translator behaviour explains this?</p>","options":["An interpreter translates and runs one statement at a time","The program was compiled into an executable first","The linker skipped line 7","An assembler converted the program"],"correct":0,"why":"Key: A. Interpreters execute statements as they translate them, so earlier lines run before the faulty one is reached.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 6","q":"<p>Which of the following is the CORRECT order of the stages in implementing a program?</p>","options":["Testing and executing, create source code, linking, interpreting and compiling, maintaining","Create source code, interpreting and compiling, linking, testing and executing, maintaining","Create source code, linking, interpreting and compiling, testing and executing, maintaining","Interpreting and compiling, create source code, linking, maintaining, testing and executing"],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. Source code is created, translated, linked into an executable, run and tested, then maintained.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 7","q":"<p>Shanice's Pascal program compiles, but every exam mark of exactly 50 is shown as Fail. The code says If mark > 50 Then WriteLn('Pass'). What type of error is this?</p>","options":["Run-time error","Linking error","Syntax error","Logic error"],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. The program runs and finishes but gives a wrong result: a logic error caused by > instead of >=.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 8","q":"<p>A program for the Te Amo Beach Grill divides the night's tips by the number of staff. On a night when 0 staff are entered, it crashes. This is BEST described as a:</p>","options":["logic error that the compiler detected","run-time error","syntax error","maintenance error"],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. Division by zero happens while the program is running: a run-time error.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 9","q":"<p>A program accepts the number of tickets for a ferry booking, from 1 to 20. Which set contains ONLY boundary test data?</p>","options":["1, 20","0, -5","\"two\", 35","7, 12"],"correct":0,"why":"Key: A. 1 and 20 are the edges of the valid range. 7 and 12 are normal; the others are erroneous.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 10","q":"<p>Before typing his program, Rohan works through his algorithm by hand, recording the value of each variable after every step. Which manual test is he performing?</p>","options":["Computerised testing","A dry run using a trace table","Debugging with breakpoints","Linking"],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. Working through an algorithm by hand with a trace table is a dry run (desk check).","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 11","q":"<p>A school's program must store whether each student has returned a permission slip. The MOST suitable data type is:</p>","options":["Boolean","Integer","String","Real"],"correct":0,"why":"Key: A. Returned or not returned: only two values, so Boolean.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 12","q":"<p>Malik is naming the variables in his Pascal program for the Freeport library's book-loan system. Which of these is a VALID Pascal identifier?</p>","options":["total marks","begin","2nd_term","Total_Marks"],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. Identifiers start with a letter or underscore, contain no spaces and are not reserved words; begin is reserved.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 13","q":"<p>The Sunrise Bakery sells each coconut tart for $3.50. Which Pascal statement correctly assigns 3.50 to the variable Unit_Price?</p>","options":["Unit_Price := 3.50;","Unit_Price == 3.50","3.50 := Unit_Price;","Unit_Price = 3.50;"],"correct":0,"why":"Key: A. Pascal's assignment operator is := and the statement ends with a semicolon.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 14","q":"<p>Devon's Python program reads two scores with input() and adds them. Entering 5 and 3 prints 53. What is the cause?</p>","options":["The computer's ALU is faulty","There is a syntax error","Python cannot add","The inputs are strings and + joins them; int() is needed"],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. input() returns text; + concatenates strings. Converting with int() fixes the logic error.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 15","q":"<p>What is displayed by the Pascal statement WriteLn(17 Mod 5, ' ', 17 Div 5); ?</p>","options":["3.4 2","2 3","3 2","2 3.4"],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. 17 Mod 5 is the remainder 2; 17 Div 5 is the whole-number quotient 3.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 16","q":"<p>Kenya's Pascal program will not compile: If mark >= 50 Then WriteLn('Pass'); Else WriteLn('Fail'); What must she change?</p>","options":["Replace >= with =>","Use double quotes","Put Else in capitals","Remove the semicolon before Else"],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. In Pascal a semicolon ends the If statement, so an Else after it is a syntax error.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 17","q":"<p>The hurricane program tests wind >= 74 FIRST, then wind >= 96, wind >= 111 and so on. What category does it report for a 140 mph storm?</p>","options":["5","4","1","3"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. The first true condition wins; 140 >= 74 is true, so it reports Category 1. The tests must start with the highest threshold.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 18","q":"<p>At the Heerenstraat Phones shop, a customer's record holds age = 15 and member = 'Y'. Which condition is TRUE for this customer?</p>","options":["(age > 15) Or (member = 'N')","(age >= 18) Or (member = 'Y')","(age >= 18) And (member = 'Y')","Not (member = 'Y')"],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. OR needs only one true part; member = 'Y' is true. The others are all false.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 19","q":"<p>A program for the Corozal Market must repeat a set of instructions exactly 25 times, once for each stall. Which loop construct is MOST appropriate?</p>","options":["WHILE … DO","FOR … DO","IF … THEN","REPEAT … UNTIL"],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. A known number of repetitions is bounded iteration: the FOR loop.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 20","q":"<p>A menu at the Kingstown ferry office must be displayed at least once and repeated until the user chooses Exit. Which construct is BEST?</p>","options":["WHILE, tested at the top","REPEAT … UNTIL, tested at the bottom","CASE","FOR"],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. REPEAT … UNTIL always runs its body at least once.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 21","q":"<p>count ← 0; FOR i ← 1 TO 10 DO IF i MOD 3 = 0 THEN count ← count + 1 ENDIF ENDFOR; Write count. What is written?</p>","options":["0","3","4","10"],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. i = 3, 6 and 9 are multiples of 3, so count is 3.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 22","q":"<p>Tamara's program should total five prices but prints only the last one. Inside her FOR loop she has total ← 0 followed by total ← total + price. What is wrong?</p>","options":["The prices are strings","The output is inside the loop","total is re-initialised on every pass; it should be set to 0 before the loop","The loop runs too few times"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. Initialising inside the loop resets the accumulator each time.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 23","q":"<p>A WHILE loop adds ages until 0 is entered. The inputs are 15, 16, 14 and 0. How many times is the loop body executed?</p>","options":["0","4","3","2"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. 15, 16 and 14 are processed; 0 fails the test at the top, so the body runs 3 times.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 24","q":"<p>Mr. Hodge stores the test scores of his Form 5 class at Sandbox Tree College using the Pascal declaration scores : Array[1..30] Of Integer; This declaration creates:</p>","options":["one integer with the value 30","an array of 1 element","30 string elements","30 integer elements numbered 1 to 30"],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. The index range 1..30 gives thirty elements, all of type Integer.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 25","q":"<p>A program searches an UNSORTED list of 200 employee numbers for one number. Which search method can be used?</p>","options":["Binary search after deleting half the list","Binary search only","Linear search","Neither"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. Binary search requires sorted data; linear search works on any order.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 26","q":"<p>In Excel VBA, a teacher writes Do While Cells(r, 2) &lt;> \"\" … r = r + 1 … Loop, starting at r = 2. What does the loop do?</p>","options":["Runs exactly twice","Deletes every cell in column B","Reads column B from row 2 down until an empty cell","Reads row 2 from column B across"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. Cells(r, 2) is column B; r increases each pass until an empty cell is reached.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 27","q":"<p>Shanice is preparing the documentation for her SBA program, the Troupe Registration system for a Carnival band. Which item is an example of EXTERNAL documentation?</p>","options":["A comment beside the fee calculation","A user guide explaining how to register a member","A procedure-head comment","The variable name totalFees"],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. External documentation is for users; the others are internal documentation for programmers.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 28","q":"<p>A procedure-head comment in Mr. Hodge's SBA example lists the purpose, author, date created and date modified. Which category of internal documentation is this?</p>","options":["Complex segments of code","Declarations","User manual","Heads of procedures"],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. Author, dates and purpose belong in the procedure (program) head.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 29","q":"<p>The beach clean-up algorithm is rewritten from Pascal into JavaScript. Which of the following changes?</p>","options":["The keywords and punctuation","The order of the steps","The 50 kg target test","The loop's stopping condition"],"correct":0,"why":"Key: A. Logic stays the same; syntax such as keywords and punctuation changes.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 30","q":"<p>Rohan is converting the beach clean-up pseudocode into a flowchart for his SBA. For the statement Read weight, which flowchart symbol must he use?</p>","options":["Terminator","Rectangle","Parallelogram","Diamond"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. Input and output are shown with parallelograms; rectangles are processes, diamonds decisions, terminators Start/Stop.","fb":[]}]}]},{"pkg":"Topic Quizzes · Section 1","quizzes":[{"id":"q_s1u1","title":"Section 1 · What is IT? Types of computers quiz","desc":"<p>Topic quiz for Section 1: What is IT? Types of computers. 25 items.</p>","minutes":0,"items":[{"t":"mc","title":"Defining IT","q":"<p>A careers officer visiting Flamboyant Heights College asks students to define IT. Which answer is MOST complete?</p>","options":["Any machine that uses electricity","Using computers to play games and watch videos","Using computers and telecommunications to capture, store, process, retrieve and transmit information","The repair of computers and printers"],"correct":2,"why":"IT combines the technology (computers and telecommunications) with what it does to data and information. The other answers are either one small use or far too broad.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"ICT vs IT","q":"<p>Your teacher writes ICT on the board instead of IT. What does the extra C emphasise?</p>","options":["Communication","Calculation","Cables","Computing"],"correct":0,"why":"ICT stands for Information and Communication Technology. It stresses networks, phones, the Internet and messaging.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Software component","q":"<p>At a King George V Street jewellery shop, which item is SOFTWARE in its IT system?</p>","options":["The shop assistant","The stock-control program","The list of item prices","The receipt printer"],"correct":1,"why":"A program is software. The printer is hardware, the assistant is people and the prices are data.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Procedure component","q":"<p>The rule \"Back up the sales file to the cloud every evening before closing\" is an example of which level of the ICT Components Hierarchy?</p>","options":["Telecommunications","Software","Hardware","Procedures"],"correct":3,"why":"A rule or step that people follow is a procedure.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"GIGO","q":"<p>A teacher's spreadsheet shows a student averaging 245% because one test score was typed as 950 instead of 95. Which term describes this?</p>","options":["Convergence","GIGO","Embedded system","Obsolescence"],"correct":1,"why":"Garbage In, Garbage Out: the computer processed incorrect input correctly and produced incorrect output.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Strength of computers","q":"<p>The CXC multiple-choice answer sheets for thousands of candidates are marked by machine in a fraction of the time humans would take. Which strength of computers does this MOST show?</p>","options":["Portability","Judgement","Speed","Creativity"],"correct":2,"why":"Processing large volumes quickly is speed. Computers do not have human judgement or creativity.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Limitation of computers","q":"<p>During a power cut in Portsmouth, a supermarket cannot use its tills even though the software is working. Which limitation of computers does this show?</p>","options":["Lack of storage","Too much accuracy","GIGO","Dependence on electricity"],"correct":3,"why":"Computers cannot work without power. A UPS or generator reduces this risk.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Supercomputer use","q":"<p>A regional weather service wants to model the path and strength of a hurricane over the next five days. Which type of computer is BEST suited?</p>","options":["Supercomputer","Laptop","Mainframe","Tablet"],"correct":0,"why":"Weather modelling needs enormous numbers of calculations: a supercomputer.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Mainframe use","q":"<p>A bank with branches on six Caribbean islands must process millions of card transactions daily with near-zero downtime. Which type of computer is MOST suitable?</p>","options":["Mainframe","Desktop","Supercomputer","Smartphone"],"correct":0,"why":"Huge volumes of transactions and many simultaneous users, with high reliability, is the job of a mainframe.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Measuring supercomputers","q":"<p>A science article says a new supercomputer can perform over a billion billion floating-point operations each second. Which unit measures this?</p>","options":["GB","Hz only","FLOPS","MIPS"],"correct":2,"why":"Supercomputer speed is measured in FLOPS (floating-point operations per second). An exaflop is 10<sup>18</sup> FLOPS.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Embedded system","q":"<p>Which device in a hotel room in Canefield is MOST likely to contain an embedded system?</p>","options":["The glass mirror","The digital safe with a keypad","The paper welcome card","The wooden wardrobe"],"correct":1,"why":"A digital safe has a small dedicated computer that checks the code and controls the lock.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Feature of embedded systems","q":"<p>Which statement about an embedded system is TRUE?</p>","options":["It is always larger than a desktop","It can run any program the user installs","It is a dedicated computer that controls one device or task","It must be connected to a monitor and keyboard"],"correct":2,"why":"Embedded systems do a dedicated job inside another device and usually have no general user interface.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Wearables","q":"<p>A coach at a Caribbean football academy wants to track each player's heart rate and distance run during training. Which device is MOST suitable?</p>","options":["Mainframe","Fitness band or smartwatch","Desktop computer","Plotter"],"correct":1,"why":"Wearables use sensors on the body to track heart rate and movement.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Laptop vs desktop","q":"<p>Which is a disadvantage of a laptop compared with a desktop of the same price?</p>","options":["It has no screen","It cannot connect to Wi-Fi","It is usually less powerful and harder to upgrade","It cannot run on a battery"],"correct":2,"why":"To stay thin and cool, laptops use lower-power parts that are harder to upgrade.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Portability","q":"<p>Which computer type has the GREATEST portability?</p>","options":["All-in-one PC","Desktop","Mainframe","Smartphone"],"correct":3,"why":"A smartphone fits in a pocket and runs on a battery. Of these, it is the most portable.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Game console","q":"<p>Which component in a game console is MOST responsible for producing detailed 3D graphics?</p>","options":["The power cable","The controller","The HDMI port","The graphics processing unit (GPU)"],"correct":3,"why":"The GPU is a specialised processor for rendering graphics.","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"Strengths of computers","q":"<p>Select EVERY statement that is a genuine strength of computers.</p>","options":["They always know when data is wrong","They work at very high speed","They can store huge amounts of data","They can make moral judgements","They do repetitive tasks without getting tired"],"correct":[1, 2, 4],"why":"Speed, storage and consistency are strengths. Computers cannot detect nonsense data on their own (GIGO) and have no moral judgement.","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"Mobile devices","q":"<p>A tour operator in Mahaut needs a device to use while walking with tourists. Select EVERY feature that makes a smartphone suitable.</p>","options":["GPS","Built-in camera","Mobile data","Needs mains power","Very large storage expansion bays"],"correct":[0, 1, 2],"why":"Camera, GPS and mobile data suit a guide on the move. Phones have no drive bays and run on batteries.","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"Embedded devices","q":"<p>Select EVERY item that contains an embedded system.</p>","options":["A printed bus timetable","A smart electricity meter","A steel cutlass","Anti-lock brakes in a car","A washing machine with programmes"],"correct":[1, 3, 4],"why":"ABS, smart meters and modern washing machines all contain dedicated controllers.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Manual vs automated","q":"<p>A Carnival committee used to add up thousands of ticket sales by hand. Which is the MAIN advantage of switching to automated information processing?</p>","options":["It is faster and more accurate for large volumes of data","It can use human judgement for unusual cases","It never needs electricity","It costs nothing to set up"],"correct":0,"why":"Automated processing handles large volumes quickly and consistently. It needs power, costs money to set up and cannot use human judgement.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Process control","q":"<p>A juice factory in Trinidad keeps its pasteuriser at exactly 72 °C with no operator present. Which feature is ESSENTIAL for this process control system?</p>","options":["A graphical user interface","A laser printer","Sensor input","Human-readable documents"],"correct":2,"why":"Process control needs sensor input, real-time processing and output to control devices.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Components","q":"<p>Match each component of an IT system to the example from a school library.</p>","left":["Hardware","Software","Telecommunications","People","Procedures"],"right":["Barcode scanner","Library management program","Wi-Fi linking the desk PC to the school server","The librarian","Overdue books must be returned before borrowing again"],"why":"These are the five levels of the ICT Components Hierarchy: touchable things are hardware, programs are software, links are telecommunications, users are people and rules are procedures.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"IT words","q":"<p>Match each term to its meaning.</p>","left":["Information Technology","IT system","ICT","ICT Components Hierarchy"],"right":["Using computers to store, retrieve, transmit and manipulate data or information","A computer system with its hardware, software and peripherals, used by a limited group of users","IT extended to stress unified communications and telecommunications","People, procedures, software, hardware and telecommunications in logical order"],"why":"These four definitions come up again and again in Paper 02.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Computer types","q":"<p>Match each computer type to its BEST use.</p>","left":["Supercomputer","Mainframe","Laptop","Embedded device"],"right":["Climate change modelling","Airline reservation system","Student writing an essay on the ferry","Controlling a microwave oven"],"why":"Each type is matched to its design strength: calculation, transactions, portable personal use or dedicated control.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Measures","q":"<p>Match each measure to what it describes.</p>","left":["FLOPS","MIPS","GHz","TB"],"right":["Supercomputer calculating speed","Mainframe instruction speed","Processor clock speed","Storage capacity"],"why":"FLOPS and MIPS measure work done per second; GHz is clock speed; TB is a unit of storage.","fb":[]}]},{"id":"q_s1u2","title":"Section 1 · Hardware and the IPOS cycle quiz","desc":"<p>Topic quiz for Section 1: Hardware and the IPOS cycle. 29 items.</p>","minutes":0,"items":[{"t":"mc","title":"CPU part","q":"<p>A spreadsheet at the Sunrise Bakery checks whether each day's sales are greater than US$500. Which part of the CPU carries out this comparison?</p>","options":["Control Unit","Arithmetic Logic Unit","Hard disk","ROM"],"correct":1,"why":"Comparisons and calculations are done by the ALU.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Control unit","q":"<p>Which part of the CPU fetches instructions from memory, decodes them and coordinates the other components?</p>","options":["Registers","Control Unit","ALU","Cache"],"correct":1,"why":"The Control Unit is the CPU's manager.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Registers","q":"<p>Where does the CPU hold the data it is working on at this very instant?</p>","options":["Hard disk","Cloud storage","Optical disc","Registers"],"correct":3,"why":"Registers are tiny, very fast storage locations inside the CPU.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Volatile memory","q":"<p>Tiana is typing her SBA in Word when a Barbados Light and Power power cut switches off her desktop. She had not saved for 20 minutes. Why is that work lost?</p>","options":["It was in RAM, which is volatile","The hard disk was full","It was in ROM, which is read-only","The CPU deleted it"],"correct":0,"why":"Unsaved work is held in RAM, which loses its contents when power is lost.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"ROM","q":"<p>Which of these is MOST likely to be stored in ROM?</p>","options":["A downloaded music video","A student's homework file","The instructions that start up the computer","Today's browser history"],"correct":2,"why":"ROM holds firmware such as the boot instructions.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Cache","q":"<p>A laptop advert says the processor has '24 MB cache'. What is the purpose of cache memory?</p>","options":["To hold frequently used instructions and data close to the CPU so it works faster","To store files permanently when the power is off","To connect to the Internet","To cool down the processor"],"correct":0,"why":"Cache reduces the time the CPU waits for data from slower RAM.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Byte","q":"<p>How many bits are in one byte?</p>","options":["1,024","16","4","8"],"correct":3,"why":"1 byte = 8 bits.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Conversion","q":"<p>Jamal's game download is 3 GB. How many megabytes is that? (Use 1 GB = 1,024 MB.)</p>","options":["307 MB","3,000 MB","0.003 MB","3,072 MB"],"correct":3,"why":"3 × 1,024 = 3,072 MB.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Calculation","q":"<p>A tour company's promo photos are 5 MB each. How many fit on a 1 GB memory card? (1 GB = 1,024 MB; give the whole number.)</p>","options":["200","1,024","204","5,120"],"correct":2,"why":"1,024 ÷ 5 = 204.8, so 204 complete photos fit.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Largest unit","q":"<p>Which of these is the LARGEST unit of storage?</p>","options":["Petabyte","Gigabyte","Terabyte","Kilobyte"],"correct":0,"why":"KB < MB < GB < TB < PB.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Tape","q":"<p>A regional bank keeps a copy of all its records for 10 years in a vault in another country. Which medium is MOST suitable for this archive?</p>","options":["CD-R","Magnetic tape","RAM","USB flash drives"],"correct":1,"why":"Tape has very high capacity, low cost per GB and long shelf life, ideal for archives.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"SSD advantage","q":"<p>Why do most new laptops use an SSD rather than an HDD?</p>","options":["SSDs are cheaper per gigabyte","SSDs are faster and have no moving parts","SSDs need a laser to read them","SSDs are volatile"],"correct":1,"why":"Speed, low power and robustness are the main advantages; SSDs cost more per GB.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Optical capacity","q":"<p>Roughly how much can a single-layer DVD hold?</p>","options":["25 GB","1 TB","4.7 GB","700 MB"],"correct":2,"why":"CD ≈ 700 MB, DVD ≈ 4.7 GB, single-layer Blu-ray ≈ 25 GB.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"IPOS","q":"<p>At a GAIA self check-in kiosk, a passenger's boarding pass is printed. Which stage of the IPOS cycle is this?</p>","options":["Output","Input","Storage","Process"],"correct":0,"why":"Printing presents information to the user: output.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"IPOS storage","q":"<p>In the IPOS cycle, why is the arrow between processing and storage drawn in BOTH directions?</p>","options":["Because the CPU is inside the hard drive","Because storage is part of the output","Because storage devices are also input devices","Because data is saved to storage and also retrieved from it for processing"],"correct":3,"why":"Data is saved to storage and later retrieved to be processed again.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Cloud dependence","q":"<p>After a storm, Holetown has electricity but no Internet for three days. Which business is MOST affected?</p>","options":["One that stores files on its office PC's SSD","One that stores all its files only in the cloud","One that uses an external HDD for backups","One that keeps paper records"],"correct":1,"why":"Cloud-only storage cannot be reached without an Internet connection.","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"Cloud advantages","q":"<p>Select EVERY advantage of cloud storage over local storage.</p>","options":["Files can be accessed from any device with Internet","There are never any ongoing costs","Easy to share and collaborate on files","Files are safe off-site if the office floods","It always works without the Internet"],"correct":[0, 2, 3],"why":"Access, off-site safety and sharing are cloud advantages. Cloud needs the Internet and usually has ongoing fees above a free allowance.","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"Non-volatile","q":"<p>Select EVERY type of memory or storage that keeps its data when the power is switched off.</p>","options":["Hard disk drive","SSD","RAM","Registers","ROM"],"correct":[0, 1, 4],"why":"ROM and all secondary storage are non-volatile. RAM and registers lose their contents.","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"Solid-state","q":"<p>Select EVERY solid-state storage device.</p>","options":["SD memory card","Magnetic tape","SSD","Blu-ray disc","USB flash drive"],"correct":[0, 2, 4],"why":"Flash drives, memory cards and SSDs use flash memory chips with no moving parts.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"ROM variations","q":"<p>A technician says the firmware chip in an old machine can be wiped by shining ultraviolet light on it and then reprogrammed. What type of memory is this?</p>","options":["Cache","RAM","EPROM","PROM"],"correct":2,"why":"EPROM is Erasable Programmable ROM, wiped with ultraviolet light. PROM can be programmed only once.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Buffer","q":"<p>Keisha sends a 40-page document to print and immediately carries on typing while the printer works. What makes this possible?</p>","options":["The ALU","A buffer that temporarily holds the print data","A faster mouse","A larger monitor"],"correct":1,"why":"The buffer holds data waiting to be printed, so the CPU is free to continue.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Ports","q":"<p>The Holetown Youth Centre wants to connect a laptop to a projector to show a video with sound. Which port is MOST suitable?</p>","options":["Audio jack only","Ethernet","HDMI","SATA"],"correct":2,"why":"HDMI carries both video and sound to displays and projectors.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Sequential access on tape","q":"<p>A backup tape holds records with track numbers 16, 25, 34, 45, 59, 60 and 78 in that order. What must happen to read the record on track 34?</p>","options":["The tape must be formatted first","Track 34 cannot be read from tape","The drive jumps straight to track 34","The drive must wind past the records on tracks 16 and 25 first"],"correct":3,"why":"Tape uses sequential access: data is retrieved in the order it was stored, so earlier records must be passed first.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Cylinder","q":"<p>Which term describes a set of matched tracks, for example track 3, on all the platters of a hard disk?</p>","options":["Buffer","Cylinder","Spindle","Sector"],"correct":1,"why":"A cylinder is the same track on every platter; storing a file on one cylinder avoids moving the heads.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"PROM","q":"<p>A factory receives blank memory chips and programs its machine instructions onto them once, using a special burner. The contents can never be changed. Which type of memory is this?</p>","options":["RAM","Cache","PROM","EEPROM"],"correct":2,"why":"PROM is manufactured blank and written once using a PROM programmer (burner).","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"Cloud risks","q":"<p>Select EVERY risk of storing an organisation's data in the cloud.</p>","options":["Lack of standardisation","Government intrusion","Ransomware locking data","Insider threats at the provider","Data can never be accessed remotely"],"correct":[0, 1, 2, 3],"why":"Insider threats, ransomware, government intrusion and lack of standardisation are on the list; remote access is a cloud advantage.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Storage types","q":"<p>Match each storage medium to its type.</p>","left":["Hard disk drive","DVD","USB flash drive","Magnetic tape"],"right":["Magnetic, direct access","Optical","Solid-state","Magnetic, sequential access"],"why":"HDDs and tape are magnetic (direct vs sequential access); DVDs are optical; flash drives are solid-state.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"IPOS hardware","q":"<p>Match each device to its stage in the IPOS cycle.</p>","left":["Barcode scanner","CPU","Receipt printer","Solid-state drive"],"right":["Input","Process","Output","Storage"],"why":"Scanners capture data, the CPU processes it, printers output it and drives store it.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Memory facts","q":"<p>Match each memory type to its description.</p>","left":["RAM","ROM","Cache","Registers"],"right":["Volatile working memory for open programs","Non-volatile memory holding boot firmware","Small fast memory between CPU and RAM","Tiny storage locations inside the CPU"],"why":"These form the memory hierarchy from main memory to the CPU itself.","fb":[]}]},{"id":"q_s1u3","title":"Section 1 · Input and output devices quiz","desc":"<p>Topic quiz for Section 1: Input and output devices. 28 items.</p>","minutes":0,"items":[{"t":"mc","title":"CXC answer sheets","q":"<p>Your CXC Paper 01 answer sheet is marked by a machine that detects which bubble you shaded. Which technology is this?</p>","options":["MICR","OMR","OCR","RFID"],"correct":1,"why":"OMR detects marks in fixed positions.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Cheques","q":"<p>A bank in Georgetown processes cheques. The numbers at the bottom are printed in special ink so they can be read even if stamped over. Which technology reads them?</p>","options":["Light pen","OMR","MICR","Barcode reader"],"correct":2,"why":"Magnetic Ink Character Recognition reads magnetic ink characters on cheques.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"OCR","q":"<p>A newspaper archive wants to turn scanned pages of 1990s editions into searchable text. Which technology is needed?</p>","options":["MICR","OMR","Biometrics","OCR"],"correct":3,"why":"OCR converts images of printed characters into editable text.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"QR codes","q":"<p>A restaurant in Hopkins puts a square black-and-white code on each table. Diners point their phone camera at it to open the menu. What is this?</p>","options":["Barcode","MICR line","Magnetic stripe","QR code"],"correct":3,"why":"A QR code is a 2-D code read by a camera; it often holds a web address.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Graphics tablet","q":"<p>A Carnival troupe designer wants to draw costume designs naturally on the computer, with lines that get thicker when she presses harder. What should she use?</p>","options":["Barcode reader","Trackball","Joystick","Graphics tablet with pressure-sensitive stylus"],"correct":3,"why":"Graphics tablets detect stylus pressure for natural drawing.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Sensors","q":"<p>A weather station at Hato Airport must record air pressure every minute, day and night. What kind of input device is MOST suitable?</p>","options":["Sensor","OMR reader","Keyboard","Webcam"],"correct":0,"why":"Sensors measure physical quantities automatically and continuously.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"ADC","q":"<p>Why does a temperature sensor connected to a computer often need an analogue-to-digital converter?</p>","options":["To print the temperature","Because the sensor's signal is analogue but the computer processes digital data","To make the sensor waterproof","To store the readings in the cloud"],"correct":1,"why":"Computers process digital values, so the continuous analogue signal must be converted.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Chip and PIN","q":"<p>Why is chip-and-PIN more secure than a magnetic stripe card?</p>","options":["Magnetic stripes cannot be read","The chip creates unique transaction codes and the PIN confirms the cardholder","Chip cards never need a network","The chip is bigger"],"correct":1,"why":"Unique codes make cloning hard and the PIN proves identity.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Biometric","q":"<p>A clinic wants only its pharmacist to open the medicine cabinet. Staff keep lending key cards. Which device is BEST?</p>","options":["Fingerprint scanner","Barcode reader","Magnetic stripe reader","Microphone"],"correct":0,"why":"A fingerprint cannot be lent to another person.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Multi-part forms","q":"<p>A courier company prints delivery notes with two carbon copies underneath. Which printer must it use?</p>","options":["Dot-matrix","Thermal","Laser","Inkjet"],"correct":0,"why":"Only an impact printer can press through carbon copies.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Receipts","q":"<p>Which printer is used in most supermarket tills and ATMs to print receipts?</p>","options":["Dot-matrix","Plotter","3D printer","Thermal"],"correct":3,"why":"Thermal printers are fast, quiet and need no ink.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"High-volume office printing","q":"<p>A school office prints about 4,000 black-and-white pages each month. Which printer is MOST cost-effective?</p>","options":["Colour inkjet","Plotter","Monochrome laser","3D printer"],"correct":2,"why":"Lasers are fast with a low cost per page for high-volume text.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Plotter","q":"<p>An architect in St. John’s must print large, accurate building plans on A0 paper. Which device should she use?</p>","options":["Thermal printer","Dot-matrix printer","Projector","Plotter"],"correct":3,"why":"Plotters produce large, precise drawings.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"3D printer","q":"<p>A hospital in Barbados wants to make a custom-fitted plastic hand brace for a patient. Which output device can do this?</p>","options":["Laser printer","Braille embosser","3D printer","Plotter"],"correct":2,"why":"3D printers build solid objects layer by layer.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Actuator","q":"<p>In an automatic gate at a Sandy Point villa, which device physically opens the gate after the processor receives the correct code?</p>","options":["Sensor","Actuator (motor)","Speaker","Keypad"],"correct":1,"why":"Actuators turn signals into movement.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Accessibility","q":"<p>Which pair of devices would BEST help a student who is deaf follow an online lesson?</p>","options":["Live captions on the screen and visual alerts","Speakers and a microphone","A louder speaker","A braille embosser and headphones"],"correct":0,"why":"Information must be visual for a deaf user.","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"Direct data capture","q":"<p>Select EVERY direct data capture method.</p>","options":["OMR","MICR","Barcode reader","Keyboard","Mouse"],"correct":[0, 1, 2],"why":"These read data from the source automatically; keyboards and mice need manual input.","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"Both input and output","q":"<p>Select EVERY device that is BOTH an input and an output device.</p>","options":["Multifunction printer that scans","Headset with microphone","Plotter","Touch screen","Scanner"],"correct":[0, 1, 3],"why":"Each of these combines an input and an output part.","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"Blind user","q":"<p>Select EVERY device that would help a blind person use a computer.</p>","options":["High-resolution monitor","Screen reader with headphones","Refreshable braille display","Speech recognition","Light pen"],"correct":[1, 2, 3],"why":"Sound and touch replace sight; monitors and light pens need sight.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Machine-readable output","q":"<p>A courier company's computer prints a barcode label that is later scanned at every depot. What kind of output is the barcode label?</p>","options":["Soft copy","Machine-readable output","Human-readable output only","A control signal"],"correct":1,"why":"A barcode is printed so that another machine can read it: machine-readable output.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Keyboard keys","q":"<p>Which group of keys includes F1 to F12?</p>","options":["Typing keys","Control keys","Function keys","Numeric keypad"],"correct":2,"why":"The twelve function keys run along the top of the keyboard.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Mouse limitation","q":"<p>Which statement about a mouse is TRUE?</p>","options":["It is an output device","It is used to enter text","It controls the pointer but cannot enter text","It always has three scroll wheels"],"correct":2,"why":"A mouse moves the pointer and selects; it cannot type text.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Daisy-wheel","q":"<p>Which early impact printer struck characters on a rotating wheel against an ink ribbon?</p>","options":["Thermal","Laser","Inkjet","Daisy-wheel"],"correct":3,"why":"The daisy-wheel printer used a wheel of characters struck by a hammer.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Microfilm","q":"<p>A government archive wants a compact, low-cost way to keep 50 years of printed gazettes for the long term. Which output medium is traditionally used?</p>","options":["A projector","RAM","Microfilm","Thermal paper"],"correct":2,"why":"Microfilm is compact, cheap to store and a good archival form.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Headset","q":"<p>A call-centre agent in West Bay needs both hands free to type while talking to customers. Which device is MOST suitable?</p>","options":["Speakers","Light pen","Plotter","Headset with microphone"],"correct":3,"why":"A headset gives hands-free listening and speaking.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Printers","q":"<p>Match each printer to its BEST use.</p>","left":["Laser","Inkjet","Dot-matrix","Thermal"],"right":["Thousands of office letters a month","A few colour photos at home","Invoices with carbon copies","Airline boarding passes and bag tags"],"why":"Each printer's strengths suit a different job.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Sensors","q":"<p>Match each sensor to its use.</p>","left":["Temperature","Light","Motion (PIR)","Moisture"],"right":["Coral reef bleaching monitor","Street lights switching on at dusk","Burglar alarm","Automatic farm irrigation"],"why":"Each sensor measures a different physical quantity.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Readers","q":"<p>Match each reader to what it reads.</p>","left":["OCR","OMR","MICR","Magnetic stripe reader"],"right":["Printed characters","Shaded marks","Magnetic ink on cheques","The strip on an older card"],"why":"Characters, marks, magnetic ink and magnetic stripes each need a different reader.","fb":[]}]},{"id":"q_s1u4","title":"Section 1 · Software and user interfaces quiz","desc":"<p>Topic quiz for Section 1: Software and user interfaces. 33 items.</p>","minutes":0,"items":[{"t":"mc","title":"System software","q":"<p>Which of these is system software?</p>","options":["Instagram","A payroll program","Microsoft Word","Android"],"correct":3,"why":"Android is an operating system, so it is system software.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Application software","q":"<p>Shanice uses a program to edit her Carnival TikTok videos. What type of software is this?</p>","options":["Operating system","Application software","Utility","Device driver"],"correct":1,"why":"Video editing is a task for the user: application software.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"OS function","q":"<p>While Kyle types his essay, Spotify plays music and a game updates in the background. Which OS function makes this possible?</p>","options":["Multitasking / processor management","Data validation","File management","Compression"],"correct":0,"why":"The OS shares the processor between programs.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"File management","q":"<p>Which OS function lets you create a folder called 'IT SBA' and move files into it?</p>","options":["Interrupt handling","Device management","File management","Memory management"],"correct":2,"why":"Creating, naming, moving and deleting files and folders is file management.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Device driver","q":"<p>Malia plugs in a new printer but it will not print. A message says software is missing. What is MOST likely needed?</p>","options":["A spreadsheet","More RAM","A new operating system","The printer's device driver"],"correct":3,"why":"A driver lets the OS communicate with a specific device.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Utility","q":"<p>Which utility program would help after a computer is infected by a worm?</p>","options":["Disk defragmenter","Screen saver","Antivirus / anti-malware","File compression"],"correct":2,"why":"Anti-malware detects and removes malware such as worms.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Compression","q":"<p>A student needs to e-mail 40 photos, but the attachment limit is 25 MB. Which utility helps MOST?</p>","options":["File compression","Defragmenter","Firewall","Backup"],"correct":0,"why":"Compression reduces file size.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"General-purpose","q":"<p>Which of these is general-purpose application software?</p>","options":["Airline reservation system","Hotel booking system","Spreadsheet program","Payroll program"],"correct":2,"why":"A spreadsheet can be used for many unrelated tasks.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Custom software","q":"<p>The government of Aruba commissions programmers to build a system for its unique work-permit rules. This is:</p>","options":["A utility","Off-the-shelf software","Custom-written (bespoke) software","Shareware"],"correct":2,"why":"Written specially for one organisation's needs.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Off-the-shelf advantage","q":"<p>Which is an advantage of off-the-shelf software over custom software?</p>","options":["The buyer owns the source code","It always meets every need exactly","It is cheaper and available immediately","Competitors cannot use it"],"correct":2,"why":"Development costs are shared among many buyers, and it is ready now.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Open source","q":"<p>What makes software open source?</p>","options":["Its source code is available to view and modify","It has no licence at all","It only runs on Linux","It is sold in a shop"],"correct":0,"why":"Open source means the code is published under a licence that allows modification.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Shareware","q":"<p>Devon downloads a game editor that works free for 30 days and then asks him to pay. This is:</p>","options":["Shareware","Open source","Public domain","Freeware"],"correct":0,"why":"Shareware is 'try before you buy'.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"CLI","q":"<p>A network technician types ipconfig to check a computer's address. Which interface is she using?</p>","options":["Menu-driven","Command line","Touch","GUI"],"correct":1,"why":"Typed commands at a prompt: command line interface.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"CLI advantage","q":"<p>Which is an advantage of a command line interface?</p>","options":["Uses little memory and is fast for experts","Easy for beginners","Shows lots of pictures","Needs no typing"],"correct":0,"why":"CLIs are efficient for experts and need few resources.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Menu-driven","q":"<p>An ATM on Wallblake Road offers 'Withdraw / Balance / Deposit / Exit'. Which interface is this?</p>","options":["Natural language","Menu-driven","WIMP desktop","Command line"],"correct":1,"why":"Choosing from a list of options is menu-driven.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"WIMP","q":"<p>In GUI terms, what does WIMP stand for?</p>","options":["Words, Images, Music, Programs","Windows, Icons, Menus, Pointer","Windows, Internet, Mouse, Printer","Wireless, Internet, Mobile, Phone"],"correct":1,"why":"WIMP describes the parts of a GUI.","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"OS functions","q":"<p>Select EVERY function of an operating system.</p>","options":["Providing a user interface","Managing input and output devices","Managing memory","Calculating staff wages","Writing business letters"],"correct":[0, 1, 2],"why":"Letters and wages are tasks done by application software.","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"Voice interface","q":"<p>Select EVERY genuine disadvantage of a voice interface.</p>","options":["Affected by background noise","Privacy concerns","Requires a mouse","May misunderstand accents","Cannot be used hands-free"],"correct":[0, 1, 3],"why":"Voice is hands-free and needs no mouse; noise, accents and privacy are real issues.","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"Free software","q":"<p>Select EVERY type of software that can be used free of charge (at least for some time).</p>","options":["Freeware","Shareware","Open source","Proprietary commercial software with no trial"],"correct":[0, 1, 2],"why":"Freeware and most open source are free; shareware is free during the trial.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Customised vs custom-written","q":"<p>A school cannot afford new software, so it pays a technician to add templates, formulas and simplified menus to Excel to create its grade book. This is an example of:</p>","options":["Custom-written software","A device driver","Customised general-purpose software","Shareware"],"correct":2,"why":"Adapting a general-purpose package is customisation. Custom-written software is created from scratch for one client.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Worm","q":"<p>Malware spreads across a company network overnight, copying itself from computer to computer with nobody opening any file. What is it MOST likely to be?</p>","options":["Spreadsheet virus","Worm","Trojan horse","Adware"],"correct":1,"why":"A worm replicates without human interaction and does not need to attach to a program.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Trojan horse","q":"<p>Shanice downloads a free 'Carnival filter' app that secretly steals her passwords. This is an example of a:</p>","options":["Trojan horse","Defragmenter","Compiler","Worm"],"correct":0,"why":"A Trojan horse is disguised as something useful or fun.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Defragmenter","q":"<p>Which utility gathers the scattered pieces of each file on a hard disk into one place and puts the free space together?</p>","options":["Disk defragmenter","Compiler","Antivirus","Scan disk"],"correct":0,"why":"Defragmenting rearranges file fragments without changing their contents.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Compiler","q":"<p>Which translator converts a whole high-level program into machine code at once, producing a file that can run on its own?</p>","options":["Assembler","Compiler","Interpreter","Scan disk"],"correct":1,"why":"A compiler translates the entire program; an interpreter works line by line.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Batch processing","q":"<p>Virgin Power & Water collects meter readings all month and then produces every customer's bill in one run. Which processing mode is this?</p>","options":["Real-time","Time-sharing","Batch","Online"],"correct":2,"why":"Data accumulated over a period and processed together is batch processing.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Real-time","q":"<p>Which task MOST needs real-time processing?</p>","options":["Controlling an aircraft autopilot","Printing end-of-term report cards","Monthly payroll","Producing annual tax statements"],"correct":0,"why":"Life-critical control systems need instant, continuous processing.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Time-sharing","q":"<p>A university mainframe lets 300 students log on at once, each receiving slots of CPU time and memory. This is called:</p>","options":["Defragmenting","Batch processing","Multiprocessing","Time-sharing"],"correct":3,"why":"Time-sharing allocates computer resources in time slots to many users simultaneously.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Kernel","q":"<p>Which component of an operating system provides basic-level control over all the hardware, reading and writing memory and handling devices?</p>","options":["Desktop wallpaper","API documentation","Web browser","Kernel"],"correct":3,"why":"The kernel is the OS core.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Translators","q":"<p>Match each translator to how it works.</p>","left":["Assembler","Compiler","Interpreter"],"right":["Converts assembly language into machine code","Translates the whole high-level program at once","Translates and runs one line at a time"],"why":"These are the three main types of translator.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Multi- words","q":"<p>Match each term to its meaning.</p>","left":["Multitasking","Multiprogramming","Multiprocessing","Time-sharing"],"right":["One user does several tasks at once","Several programs share a single processor","Several programs run on multiple processors","Many users share one computer's resources"],"why":"Users, programs and processors are the keys to telling them apart.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Interfaces","q":"<p>Match each interface to the BEST user.</p>","left":["Command line","Menu-driven","Touch","Voice"],"right":["IT technician automating tasks","First-time user at a ticket machine","Young child on a tablet","Driver who needs hands-free control"],"why":"Each interface suits users with different skills and situations.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Licences","q":"<p>Match each licence type to its description.</p>","left":["Proprietary","Open source","Freeware","Shareware"],"right":["Paid, source code secret","Source code available to modify","Free to use, code secret","Free trial, then pay"],"why":"Price and access to source code define the categories.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Utilities","q":"<p>Match each utility to its job.</p>","left":["Antivirus","Backup","Disk cleanup","Task Manager"],"right":["Detects and removes malware","Copies files so they can be restored","Deletes temporary files to free space","Shows running programs and ends frozen ones"],"why":"Each utility maintains or protects the computer in a different way.","fb":[]}]},{"id":"q_s1u5","title":"Section 1 · Choosing and troubleshooting a computer system quiz","desc":"<p>Topic quiz for Section 1: Choosing and troubleshooting a computer system. 28 items.</p>","minutes":0,"items":[{"t":"mc","title":"Clock speed","q":"<p>A laptop advert says its processor runs at 3.2 GHz. What does GHz measure?</p>","options":["Battery life","Screen resolution","Storage capacity","The number of clock cycles per second"],"correct":3,"why":"GHz (gigahertz) = billions of clock cycles per second.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Cores","q":"<p>Why does a quad-core processor help when Rhea streams music, video-calls and edits a document at the same time?</p>","options":["Four cores can work on several tasks at once","It increases battery capacity","It makes the screen brighter","It has more storage"],"correct":0,"why":"Multiple cores share the work of several programs.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Graphics card","q":"<p>Which upgrade would MOST improve performance in 3-D games and video rendering?</p>","options":["A larger mouse mat","A bigger hard drive","A new keyboard","A dedicated graphics card"],"correct":3,"why":"A graphics card has its own processor and memory for graphics.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"RAM","q":"<p>Darnell's laptop slows down badly whenever he has many browser tabs and a game open. Task Manager shows memory at 97%. What would help MOST?</p>","options":["More RAM","A new mouse","A larger monitor","A faster printer"],"correct":0,"why":"Memory is the bottleneck; more RAM (or closing programs) helps.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"SSD","q":"<p>Which is the MAIN advantage of an SSD over an HDD in a laptop?</p>","options":["It uses magnetic platters","Faster start-up and loading, and no moving parts","Larger capacity at the same price","Cheaper per gigabyte"],"correct":1,"why":"SSDs are faster and more robust, though dearer per GB.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Requirements","q":"<p>A program lists '8 GB RAM minimum, 16 GB recommended'. What does 'recommended' mean?</p>","options":["The program will run well with this amount","The program will not install with less","The program needs exactly this amount","The program needs a printer"],"correct":0,"why":"Minimum = it runs; recommended = it runs well.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Portable user","q":"<p>Which spec matters MOST to a sales rep travelling around the islands all day?</p>","options":["Dedicated GPU","Long battery life and light weight","Four HDMI ports","A 17-inch screen"],"correct":1,"why":"Portability depends on battery and weight.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Software compatibility","q":"<p>A bakery's accounting program only runs on Windows. Which computer should NOT be bought?</p>","options":["A Windows all-in-one","A ChromeOS Chromebook","A Windows laptop","A Windows desktop"],"correct":1,"why":"The software cannot run on ChromeOS.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Safety","q":"<p>Before unplugging a desktop's power cable, what should you do FIRST?</p>","options":["Spray it with water to cool it","Switch off at the wall socket","Pull the cable firmly","Open the case"],"correct":1,"why":"Always switch off at the wall before handling mains cables.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Will not switch on","q":"<p>A desktop shows no lights after a storm. The cable is firmly connected. What is the NEXT sensible check?</p>","options":["Buy a new computer","Open the power supply","Test the wall socket with a lamp that is known to work","Reinstall the operating system"],"correct":2,"why":"Test the socket before suspecting the computer.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Blank monitor","q":"<p>The computer and monitor both have power lights on, but the screen is black. Which is the MOST likely fix?</p>","options":["Replace the keyboard","Defragment the drive","Install more RAM","Reconnect the video cable and check the input source"],"correct":3,"why":"The signal path between computer and monitor is the next thing to check.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Frozen program","q":"<p>Excel has stopped responding. Which keyboard shortcut opens Task Manager directly so you can end it?</p>","options":["Ctrl + C","Ctrl + Shift + Esc","Alt + Tab","Ctrl + P"],"correct":1,"why":"Ctrl + Shift + Esc opens Task Manager.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Paper jam","q":"<p>How should you remove jammed paper from a printer?</p>","options":["Use a knife to cut it out","Pull it quickly backwards while printing","Switch off, then pull it gently in the direction the paper normally travels","Shake the printer"],"correct":2,"why":"Gentle pulling in the feed direction avoids tearing and damage.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Overheating","q":"<p>A laptop used on a bed keeps shutting down. What is the MOST likely cause?</p>","options":["Blocked air vents causing overheating","A virus in the printer","Low screen brightness","Too much RAM"],"correct":0,"why":"Soft surfaces block vents, trapping heat.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"No Internet","q":"<p>A phone and a tablet are online, but one laptop cannot connect to the same Wi-Fi. Where is the fault MOST likely?</p>","options":["The power station","The router","The Internet provider","The laptop itself (e.g. airplane mode or Wi-Fi off)"],"correct":3,"why":"Other devices work, so the problem is on the laptop.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"UPS","q":"<p>A shop in Providenciales loses unsaved sales data whenever the power flickers. Which device would BEST prevent this?</p>","options":["A graphics card","An uninterruptible power supply (UPS)","A larger monitor","A barcode scanner"],"correct":1,"why":"A UPS keeps the computer running long enough to save and shut down.","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"Evaluation factors","q":"<p>Select EVERY factor you should consider when evaluating a computer for a user.</p>","options":["Processing speed","RAM","The user's special needs","Software requirements","The colour of the box it came in"],"correct":[0, 1, 2, 3],"why":"Speed, memory, user needs and software requirements all matter; packaging does not.","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"Slow PC fixes","q":"<p>Select EVERY sensible step for a computer that has become slow.</p>","options":["Pour water on the fan","Close unused programs","Free up storage space","Run an antivirus scan","Restart it"],"correct":[1, 2, 3, 4],"why":"Restarting, closing programs, scanning and freeing space are all standard steps.","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"Escalate","q":"<p>Select EVERY situation where you should stop and call a technician.</p>","options":["The fix requires opening the case","The hard drive makes loud clicking noises","The mouse batteries are flat","A browser tab is frozen","You smell burning from the computer"],"correct":[0, 1, 4],"why":"Burning, failing drives and anything needing the case opened are for technicians.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Megahertz","q":"<p>A processor runs at 200 MHz. How many cycles does it complete each second?</p>","options":["200 million","200","200 thousand","200 billion"],"correct":0,"why":"1 MHz = one million cycles per second.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"RAM upgrade","q":"<p>Kayla's motherboard supports a maximum of 16 GB of DDR4 RAM and has two slots. Which upgrade is correct?</p>","options":["Two 16 GB DDR5 modules","Two 8 GB DDR4 modules","Four 4 GB DDR3 modules","One 32 GB DDR4 module"],"correct":1,"why":"Match the type (DDR4), respect the maximum (16 GB) and the number of slots (two).","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Expansion card","q":"<p>A desktop has no sound output. Which component could be added to an expansion slot to fix this?</p>","options":["Daisy wheel","ISA cable","Sound card","Scan disk"],"correct":2,"why":"Sound, network and video cards are expansion cards.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Monitor LED","q":"<p>A monitor's power light is orange and flashing, and the screen is black. What does this usually mean?</p>","options":["The monitor is in suspend (sleep) mode","The monitor has exploded","The printer is out of ink","The hard disk is full"],"correct":0,"why":"Wake the computer by moving the mouse or pressing a key.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Printer ghosting","q":"<p>Laser prints show a faint copy of the image repeated down the page. What is the BEST fix?</p>","options":["Add more RAM","Replace the ageing drum or maintenance kit","Clean the inkjet nozzles","Install a Wi-Fi repeater"],"correct":1,"why":"Ghosting on laser output points to the drum.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Li-ion battery","q":"<p>Which is the BEST modern advice for a lithium-ion laptop battery?</p>","options":["Fully drain it to 0% every day","Keep it cool and avoid regularly running it completely flat","Keep charging it after it swells","Store it in a hot car"],"correct":1,"why":"Lithium-ion batteries have no memory effect; heat and deep discharges shorten their life.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Spec meanings","q":"<p>Match each specification to what it tells you.</p>","left":["4.2 GHz","16 GB RAM","1 TB SSD","1920 × 1080"],"right":["Processor clock speed","Memory for programs in use","Fast storage capacity","Screen resolution (Full HD)"],"why":"Each line of a spec sheet answers a different question.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Symptom to check","q":"<p>Match each symptom to the BEST first check.</p>","left":["Computer has no lights","Monitor black, power light on","Wireless mouse dead","Printer says 'Offline'"],"right":["Power cable and socket","Video cable and input source","Batteries and USB receiver","Printer connection (USB/Wi-Fi) and printer selected"],"why":"Start with the check most directly linked to the symptom.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Prevention","q":"<p>Match each risk to its preventive measure.</p>","left":["Power surges","Data loss","Overheating","Security holes"],"right":["Surge protector or UPS","Regular backups","Clear vents and remove dust","Install updates"],"why":"Each measure targets a specific cause of faults.","fb":[]}]},{"id":"q_s1u6","title":"Section 1 · Data, information and online reliability quiz","desc":"<p>Topic quiz for Section 1: Data, information and online reliability. 22 items.</p>","minutes":0,"items":[{"t":"mc","title":"Data","q":"<p>Which of these is DATA rather than information?</p>","options":["The average mark of Form 5B is 68%","Sales rose 10% in Carnival week","Saturday is the busiest day","45, 78, 62, 90, 51"],"correct":3,"why":"A list of raw numbers with no context is data.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Information","q":"<p>Which statement BEST defines information?</p>","options":["Raw facts and figures","Any number stored on a computer","Data that has been processed so that it is meaningful and useful","A type of input device"],"correct":2,"why":"Information is processed, meaningful data.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Data processing","q":"<p>A shop owner organises a week's receipts to find which items to reorder. This activity is called:</p>","options":["Proofreading","Data processing","Data logging","Defragmenting"],"correct":1,"why":"Turning disorganised data into useful information is data processing.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Source document","q":"<p>A student fills in a printed application form, and a clerk later types the data into the school's database. The application form is a:</p>","options":["Turnaround document","Machine-readable document","Source document","Report"],"correct":2,"why":"It contains data that will be input into the system.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Keeping source documents","q":"<p>Why should source documents be filed away after the data has been entered?</p>","options":["They can be sold","The data may need to be checked again","They become machine-readable","Computers need paper to run"],"correct":1,"why":"Keeping them allows the entered data to be verified later.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Turnaround","q":"<p>Which is an example of a turnaround document?</p>","options":["A sensor reading","A blank piece of lined paper","An Internet bill with a payment slip pre-printed with your account number, returned with payment","A novel from the library"],"correct":2,"why":"Printed by the system, completed and returned to the same system.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Machine-readable","q":"<p>Which document is MACHINE-readable?</p>","options":["A diary","A poster","A handwritten letter","A multiple-choice answer sheet marked by OMR"],"correct":3,"why":"OMR reads the marks directly.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Form design","q":"<p>Why do many forms ask you to write one CAPITAL letter in each box?</p>","options":["Because computers cannot store lowercase letters","To make handwriting easier to read and reduce errors","To save paper","To make the form look nice"],"correct":1,"why":"Separated capital letters are much easier to read accurately.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Data logging","q":"<p>A weather station at Hewanorra Airport records wind speed automatically every minute. This method of data capture is:</p>","options":["Proofreading","A turnaround document","A source document","Data logging"],"correct":3,"why":"Sensors recording at set intervals is data logging.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Information management","q":"<p>Which is NOT one of the qualities stored data needs for good information management?</p>","options":["Accurate","Structured for searching","Up to date","Written in pencil"],"correct":3,"why":"Data should be accurate, up to date, structured and on a suitable medium.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Retrieval","q":"<p>When an insurance company searches its stored data to find a customer's policy, this is called:</p>","options":["Information retrieval","Encryption","Data logging","Batch printing"],"correct":0,"why":"Searching stored data to find relevant information is retrieval.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Regulation","q":"<p>Why must users evaluate online information more carefully than a TV news broadcast?</p>","options":["Internet information is not regulated for quality or accuracy, and anyone can publish","Websites are always older","The Internet has no images","TV is always wrong"],"correct":0,"why":"Almost anyone can publish anything online.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Currency","q":"<p>Kezia is researching smartphone features and finds an article from 2012. Which criterion is the MAIN concern?</p>","options":["Authority","Currency","Objectivity","Coverage"],"correct":1,"why":"Currency is about how up to date the information is.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Objectivity","q":"<p>A website selling solar panels says solar panels are the best investment anyone can make. Which criterion is MOST in question?</p>","options":["Authority","Objectivity","Currency","Coverage"],"correct":1,"why":"A seller has an interest in promoting its product: bias.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Authority","q":"<p>Which source has the strongest AUTHORITY for information about a hurricane warning?</p>","options":["The national meteorological service","A celebrity's Instagram","An anonymous voice note","A travel blog"],"correct":0,"why":"Official weather agencies are the authority on warnings.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"HTTPS","q":"<p>A website shows a padlock and uses HTTPS. What does this tell you?</p>","options":["The information is true","The author is an expert","The connection is encrypted","The site is government-run"],"correct":2,"why":"HTTPS is about a secure connection, not accuracy.","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"Warning signs","q":"<p>Select EVERY warning sign that a website may be unreliable.</p>","options":["No date","Trying to sell a product","Many spelling errors","Citations to university studies that work","No author named"],"correct":[0, 1, 2, 4],"why":"Named sources with working citations are a good sign; the others are warnings.","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"Machine-readable advantages","q":"<p>Select EVERY advantage of machine-readable documents.</p>","options":["No need to read handwriting","Cheaper per item for large volumes","Fewer errors than typing","They work for every type of data","Fast input of large amounts of data"],"correct":[0, 1, 2, 4],"why":"They only work for particular types of data.","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"Fake news checks","q":"<p>Select EVERY sensible step before sharing a shocking post.</p>","options":["Check who posted it","See whether reliable news or official sources report it","Do a reverse image search on the photo","Share first and check later","Trace the claim to its original source"],"correct":[0, 1, 2, 4],"why":"Check before sharing, never after.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Documents","q":"<p>Match each document type to its description.</p>","left":["Source document","Turnaround document","Machine-readable document","Human-readable document"],"right":["Contains data to be input into the system","Output by the system, completed, then input again","Can be read directly by an input device","Needs to be read by a person"],"why":"These are the four document types in the syllabus.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Five criteria","q":"<p>Match each criterion to the question it asks.</p>","left":["Authority","Currency","Accuracy","Objectivity","Coverage"],"right":["Who wrote it and are they qualified?","How recent is it?","Can the facts be verified?","Is it balanced or biased?","Is the topic covered in enough depth?"],"why":"These five criteria evaluate any source.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Domains","q":"<p>Match each domain ending to what it usually indicates.</p>","left":[".gov",".edu",".com",".lc"],"right":["Government agency","Educational institution","Commercial company","St. Lucia country code"],"why":"Domain endings are clues, not proof of reliability.","fb":[]}]},{"id":"q_s1u7","title":"Section 1 · Validation, verification and file organisation quiz","desc":"<p>Topic quiz for Section 1: Validation, verification and file organisation. 24 items.</p>","minutes":0,"items":[{"t":"mc","title":"Verification","q":"<p>A website asks Jaden to type his new password twice. What is this an example of?</p>","options":["A range check","Verification by double entry","Proofreading","Validation"],"correct":1,"why":"Entering data twice and comparing is double-entry verification.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Validation","q":"<p>Which statement BEST describes validation?</p>","options":["Software automatically checks that data is reasonable","A human compares data with the source document","Files are sorted into order","Data is backed up to tape"],"correct":0,"why":"Validation is automatic and checks reasonableness.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Transposition","q":"<p>A clerk types the phone number 5428130 as 5421830. What type of error is this?</p>","options":["Format error","Transposition error","Range error","Presence error"],"correct":1,"why":"Two digits have been swapped.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Range check","q":"<p>A program accepts exam marks only from 0 to 100. Which check is this?</p>","options":["Presence check","Length check","Range check","Check digit"],"correct":2,"why":"Lower and upper boundaries: range check.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Presence check","q":"<p>An online form will not submit until the 'Emergency contact' field is filled. Which check is this?</p>","options":["Consistency check","Presence check","Format check","Data type check"],"correct":1,"why":"A presence check ensures a field is not left blank.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Data type","q":"<p>A Quantity field rejects 'six' but accepts 6. Which check is this?</p>","options":["Data type check","Length check","Check digit","Consistency check"],"correct":0,"why":"It checks the data is a number.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Format check","q":"<p>A student ID must be two letters followed by four digits, e.g. SM1234. Which check enforces this pattern?</p>","options":["Range check","Format check","Presence check","Reasonableness check"],"correct":1,"why":"A format check enforces a pattern.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Consistency","q":"<p>A system rejects an order where the delivery date is before the order date. Which check is this?</p>","options":["Presence check","Consistency check","Data type check","Length check"],"correct":1,"why":"Comparing two related fields is a consistency check.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Check digit purpose","q":"<p>Why do barcodes include a check digit?</p>","options":["To make the barcode longer","To store the price","To encrypt the product name","To detect errors when the code is read or typed"],"correct":3,"why":"The check digit is recalculated and compared to detect errors.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Check digit calculation","q":"<p>Using weights 5, 4, 3, 2 and modulus 11 (check digit = 11 − remainder), what is the check digit for 3142?</p>","options":["0","2","9","5"],"correct":2,"why":"3×5+1×4+4×3+2×2 = 35; 35 ÷ 11 = 3 r 2; 11 − 2 = 9.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Limits of validation","q":"<p>A clerk types a valid but wrong date of birth (15/03/2009 instead of 15/03/2008). Which technique would catch it?</p>","options":["Presence check","Verification (double entry or proofreading)","Range check","Format check"],"correct":1,"why":"The value is reasonable, so only comparing with the source catches it.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Record","q":"<p>In a club membership file, all the data about ONE member is called a:</p>","options":["Byte","Field","Record","File"],"correct":2,"why":"A record is all the data about one item.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Field","q":"<p>In a table of students, the column 'Surname' is a:</p>","options":["Record","Field","Key file","File"],"correct":1,"why":"Each column is a field.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Serial vs sequential","q":"<p>Records in a file are stored in the order they were added, with no sorting. This is:</p>","options":["Index-sequential organisation","Random organisation","Serial organisation","Sequential organisation"],"correct":2,"why":"Serial = unsorted, in the order added.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Index-sequential","q":"<p>Why can an index-sequential file NOT be stored on magnetic tape?</p>","options":["Tape is too small","Tape cannot store numbers","Index files are always on CDs","Tape only allows sequential access, but the index requires jumping to a position"],"correct":3,"why":"Index-sequential needs direct access to jump to positions.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Random file","q":"<p>In random file organisation, how is a record's location found?</p>","options":["By a formula calculated from data in the record, such as the key","By reading every record from the start","By asking the user","By alphabetical order only"],"correct":0,"why":"A hashing formula calculates the address.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Payroll","q":"<p>Which file organisation suits a monthly payroll that processes every employee in turn?</p>","options":["Serial","Random","Sequential","None"],"correct":2,"why":"Every record in order: sequential.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Transaction file","q":"<p>A supermarket records each day's sales in a file used overnight to update stock levels. This is a:</p>","options":["Master file","Report file","Transaction file","Reference file"],"correct":2,"why":"Transaction files hold temporary data used to update the master.","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"Validation checks","q":"<p>Select EVERY item that is a validation check.</p>","options":["Proofreading","Presence check","Double entry","Range check","Format check"],"correct":[1, 3, 4],"why":"Double entry and proofreading are verification methods.","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"Direct access media","q":"<p>Select EVERY medium that allows direct access.</p>","options":["Cassette tape","Magnetic tape","Hard disk","Flash drive","DVD"],"correct":[2, 3, 4],"why":"Tapes allow only sequential access.","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"Grandfather–father–son","q":"<p>Select EVERY true statement about the grandfather–father–son principle.</p>","options":["It means storing all files in the cloud","It is closely linked to batch updating of master files","Three generations of master files are kept","A lost son file can be recreated from the father and its transaction file","Only one copy of the master file is ever kept"],"correct":[1, 2, 3],"why":"Three generations with their transaction files allow recovery.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Checks and examples","q":"<p>Match each check to its example.</p>","left":["Range","Length","Format","Consistency"],"right":["Month from 1 to 12","Password at least 8 characters","Date as DD/MM/YYYY","Title Mrs only if gender is F"],"why":"Each check enforces a different kind of rule.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Access methods","q":"<p>Match each access method to its description.</p>","left":["Serial","Sequential","Direct","Index-sequential"],"right":["Unsorted, read from the start","Sorted by key, read in order","Jump straight to the record","Sorted, with an index to jump close"],"why":"Serial vs sequential is the classic exam confusion.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Applications","q":"<p>Match each application to the BEST file organisation.</p>","left":["Payroll run","Supermarket price look-up","Library catalogue by ISBN","Log of door-card swipes"],"right":["Sequential","Random (direct)","Index-sequential","Serial"],"why":"Match organisation to how records are used.","fb":[]}]}]},{"pkg":"Topic Quizzes · Section 2","quizzes":[{"id":"q_s2u1","title":"Section 2 · Types of networks quiz","desc":"<p>Topic quiz for Section 2: Types of networks. 26 items.</p>","minutes":0,"items":[{"t":"mc","title":"Define a network","q":"<p>A Form 4 student writes: 'A network is when computers are near each other.' Which definition should she use instead in her exam answer?</p>","options":["A group of computers in the same room","Two or more computers or devices linked together so they can communicate and share resources","Software that lets you browse the Internet","Any computer that is connected to a printer"],"correct":1,"why":"A network needs devices LINKED (cable or wireless) for communicating and sharing resources. Being near each other is not enough.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Lab advantage","q":"<p>The principal of a school in Puerto Plata asks why the 25 lab computers should be networked. Which is a valid advantage?</p>","options":["All computers can share one printer and the Internet connection","Each computer will run twice as fast","Viruses can no longer infect the computers","Students will no longer need passwords"],"correct":0,"why":"Sharing hardware and the Internet connection is a main reason to network. Networking does not speed up each PC or stop viruses (it can actually spread them).","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Network disadvantage","q":"<p>After the lab computers at a school are networked, a virus on one student's USB stick spreads to every PC. This illustrates which disadvantage of networks?</p>","options":["Malware can spread easily between connected computers","Higher printing costs","Networks reduce storage space","Networks cannot share files"],"correct":0,"why":"Connected computers give malware a path to spread. This is a classic network disadvantage.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Island hotel chain","q":"<p>A hotel group links the reservation systems of its hotels in Haiti, St. Kitts and Antigua. What type of network is this?</p>","options":["LAN","MAN","WAN","PAN"],"correct":2,"why":"Several islands apart means a wide area: WAN.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Beach speaker","q":"<p>Jaylen connects his phone to a Bluetooth speaker and his smartwatch while liming at Negril Beach. These devices form a</p>","options":["PAN","MAN","WAN","intranet"],"correct":0,"why":"Devices around one person within a few metres, usually via Bluetooth, form a PAN.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"City network","q":"<p>A government links its offices, libraries and fire stations across one capital city such as Port of Spain. This is BEST described as a</p>","options":["LAN","MAN","WLAN","PAN"],"correct":1,"why":"A network covering a town or city is a MAN.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"W for wireless","q":"<p>A café on Young Street lets customers connect laptops and phones by Wi-Fi inside the café. Which network type is this?</p>","options":["WLAN","MAN","PAN","WAN"],"correct":0,"why":"Wi-Fi inside one building is a Wireless LAN. WAN would mean a wide area.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Server's job","q":"<p>In the client–server network at a secondary school, which computer checks students' usernames and passwords when they log in?</p>","options":["The server","Any client computer","The modem","The printer"],"correct":0,"why":"Authentication is a service provided centrally by a server.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Peer-to-peer feature","q":"<p>A family business in Warrens has three laptops that share files and a printer with no central server. Which statement about this network is TRUE?</p>","options":["It cannot share a printer","Each computer has equal status and can share its own resources","One laptop must control all log-ins","It is a WAN"],"correct":1,"why":"In peer-to-peer every computer is a peer with equal status.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Best for a clinic","q":"<p>A medical clinic must keep 5,000 patient records confidential and back them up every night. Which network model is MOST suitable?</p>","options":["A PAN, because it is personal","No network, because networks are unsafe","Client–server, because security and backup are central","Peer-to-peer, because it is cheapest"],"correct":2,"why":"Sensitive data and central backup point to client–server.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Staff portal","q":"<p>Employees of a cruise line log in to a private website to see rosters and company policies. The public cannot access it. This is an</p>","options":["extranet","PAN","intranet","Internet"],"correct":2,"why":"Private, staff-only access = intranet.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Supplier access","q":"<p>A supermarket in Little Bay lets its wholesaler log in to view stock levels so it can deliver automatically. The wholesaler is using the supermarket's</p>","options":["PAN","intranet","extranet","MAN"],"correct":2,"why":"Selected outsiders with controlled access = extranet.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Intranet from home","q":"<p>A bank manager uses a VPN from home to reach the bank's private staff portal. The portal is still an intranet because</p>","options":["anyone can see it","it uses no web technology","it is inside one building","access is restricted to members of the organisation"],"correct":3,"why":"Access, not location, defines an intranet. A VPN only provides secure remote access.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Star failure","q":"<p>In a school lab using a star topology, the central switch fails. What happens?</p>","options":["Only the printer stops","Only one computer stops working","The whole lab loses network access","Nothing, data takes another route"],"correct":2,"why":"Everything connects through the central switch, so its failure stops the whole star.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Mesh reliability","q":"<p>An emergency operations centre wants its network to keep working even if one link is cut during a hurricane. Which topology offers this?</p>","options":["Mesh","Bus","Star","Ring"],"correct":0,"why":"Mesh offers multiple routes, so data can avoid a broken link.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Handover","q":"<p>Ms Thielman is on a video call while riding from Kralendijk to Antriol. Her call stays connected as her phone switches from one tower's area to the next. This is called</p>","options":["a handover","peer-to-peer","roaming","a firewall"],"correct":0,"why":"Moving between cells without dropping the call is a handover. Roaming means using another operator's network.","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"Client–server advantages","q":"<p>A school is choosing a client–server network. Select EVERY genuine advantage of client–server over peer-to-peer.</p>","options":["Central backup of users' files","Users can log in at any computer and reach their files","Cheaper, because no server is needed","If the server fails, nobody is affected","Central control of passwords and access rights"],"correct":[0, 1, 4],"why":"Central backup, central security and roaming log-ins are client–server benefits. It costs MORE, and server failure affects everyone.","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"WLAN drawbacks","q":"<p>A school is replacing cables with Wi-Fi. Select EVERY genuine disadvantage of a WLAN.</p>","options":["No Internet access is possible","Interference from other wireless networks","Devices cannot move around","Signals can be intercepted if not encrypted","Thick concrete walls can weaken signals"],"correct":[1, 3, 4],"why":"Security, walls and interference are WLAN drawbacks. Mobility is an advantage, and WLANs do give Internet access.","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"Bus topology","q":"<p>Select EVERY statement that is true of a bus topology.</p>","options":["All devices share one backbone cable","A break in the backbone can stop the whole network","It uses relatively little cable","Every device links to every other device"],"correct":[0, 1, 2],"why":"Bus uses one shared cable (cheap), but a break stops everyone. 'Every device to every other' is full mesh.","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"Intranet pros","q":"<p>A bank in Paramaribo is considering an intranet for its staff. Select EVERY advantage of an intranet listed in your notes.</p>","options":["Commercial or confidential data is kept secure within the organisation","Anyone in the world can read the bank's policies","E-mails stay private and may be encrypted","It never needs upgrades"],"correct":[0, 2],"why":"Security of confidential data and private, encryptable e-mail are advantages. Intranets are not public, and they do need upgrades (a disadvantage).","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Intranet con","q":"<p>The IT manager of a hotel group warns that its intranet needs regular upgrades and some old staff software no longer works with it. Which disadvantage of an intranet is this?</p>","options":["It cannot store documents","It replaces the Internet","It is open to the public","Upgrade costs and software incompatibility problems"],"correct":3,"why":"Upgrades and software incompatibility are listed disadvantages of intranets.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"WAN links","q":"<p>A shipping company joins its LANs in Kingston and Port of Spain into a WAN. Which of these is a typical way WAN links are provided?</p>","options":["Leased lines, the public telephone network or satellite links","Bluetooth pairing","A USB cable","An infrared remote"],"correct":0,"why":"WANs use public networks, leased lines, radio, fibre or satellites; the others are very short-range.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Who uses what?","q":"<p>Match each organisation to the network it is MOST associated with in your notes.</p>","left":["A bank sharing confidential staff information","A call centre accessing part of a client company's system","A business selling to the public online"],"right":["Intranet","Extranet","Internet"],"why":"Banks keep data inside (intranet); call centres and partners get limited outside access (extranet); public selling uses the Internet.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Match the network","q":"<p>Match each network type to its example.</p>","left":["PAN","LAN","MAN","WAN"],"right":["Smartwatch paired with a phone","Computers in one school building","Offices across one city","Branches on different islands"],"why":"Size order: PAN < LAN < MAN < WAN.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Who can get in?","q":"<p>Match each network to who can access it.</p>","left":["Internet","Intranet","Extranet"],"right":["Anyone with a connection","Members of the organisation only","The organisation plus approved outsiders"],"why":"Access is what distinguishes these three.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Topology weak spots","q":"<p>Match each topology to its main weakness.</p>","left":["Star","Bus","Ring","Mesh"],"right":["The central switch fails","The backbone cable breaks","One link in the loop breaks","High cost and complex cabling"],"why":"Each layout has a different failure point; mesh's problem is cost rather than a single point of failure.","fb":[]}]},{"id":"q_s2u2","title":"Section 2 · Network components and transmission media quiz","desc":"<p>Topic quiz for Section 2: Network components and transmission media. 31 items.</p>","minutes":0,"items":[{"t":"mc","title":"No network port","q":"<p>Mr Hodge buys an old desktop for his shop in Port of Spain but it has no way to connect to the office network. Which component must he add?</p>","options":["A printer","A modem","A network interface card","A hub"],"correct":2,"why":"A NIC lets the device connect to a network.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Unique hardware address","q":"<p>A technician at a school in All Saints checks the physical address 3C:52:82:1A:9F:04 on a laptop. This is the laptop's</p>","options":["MAC address","URL","IP address","domain name"],"correct":0,"why":"A MAC address is a unique hardware address written in hexadecimal, belonging to the NIC.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Wi-Fi in classrooms","q":"<p>A secondary school wants tablets in every classroom to connect to the school's cabled LAN. Which device should be installed in each block?</p>","options":["Modem","Wireless access point","Bridge","Hub"],"correct":1,"why":"A WAP connects wireless devices to a wired LAN.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"The slow lab","q":"<p>A lab of 30 PCs connected through a hub slows down badly when everyone saves work at once. Why?</p>","options":["The hub only works with fibre","The hub sends data to every port, causing collisions","The hub has too much bandwidth","The hub encrypts every message"],"correct":1,"why":"Hubs broadcast to all ports, so traffic collides as devices increase.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Switch advantage","q":"<p>Which statement BEST explains why switches replaced hubs in school labs?</p>","options":["Switches are wireless","Switches connect to satellites","Switches send data only to the intended device using MAC addresses","Switches convert digital to analogue"],"correct":2,"why":"Targeted forwarding reduces traffic and collisions.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Between networks","q":"<p>Which device connects a home LAN in Sandy Point to the ISP's network and forwards data packets between them?</p>","options":["Router","NIC","Switch","Repeater"],"correct":0,"why":"Routers connect different networks.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Modem's job","q":"<p>A family still uses a DSL connection over the telephone line. The device that changes the computer's digital signals into analogue signals for the line is the</p>","options":["modem","access point","switch","router"],"correct":0,"why":"MOdulator–DEModulator.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Weak Wi-Fi","q":"<p>The Wi-Fi is very weak in the back rooms of a large concrete villa in Bartica. Which device would MOST likely fix this?</p>","options":["Hub","Bridge","Repeater / Wi-Fi extender","Modem"],"correct":2,"why":"A repeater regenerates the signal to extend coverage.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Lightning-proof link","q":"<p>A hotel at Starfish Point needs a link between two buildings 600 m apart. The area suffers frequent lightning. Which medium is BEST?</p>","options":["Fibre-optic","Twisted pair","Infrared","Coaxial"],"correct":0,"why":"Fibre carries light, not electricity, so lightning and interference do not affect it, and it covers long distances.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Cable TV line","q":"<p>The cable that brings cable TV and cable Internet into many homes, with a copper core and a braided metal shield, is</p>","options":["fibre-optic","twisted pair","Bluetooth","coaxial"],"correct":3,"why":"That description is coaxial cable.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"TV remote","q":"<p>A TV remote control stops working when a bag is placed between it and the TV. Which medium does it use?</p>","options":["Satellite","Bluetooth","Infrared","Fibre-optic"],"correct":2,"why":"Infrared needs a clear line of sight.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Ships at sea","q":"<p>A cruise ship sailing between Dominica and St. Lucia needs Internet access for passengers far from land. Which medium is used?</p>","options":["Infrared","Twisted pair","Satellite","Bluetooth"],"correct":2,"why":"Only satellite covers ships in the open sea.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Download time","q":"<p>Shanice downloads a 400 MB file on a 40 Mbps connection. Ideally, how long will it take?</p>","options":["80 s","10 s","3,200 s","160 s"],"correct":0,"why":"400 × 8 = 3,200 Mb; 3,200 ÷ 40 = 80 s.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Bandwidth meaning","q":"<p>An ISP advertises a 100 Mbps plan. The 100 Mbps describes the connection's</p>","options":["MAC address","latency","bandwidth","storage capacity"],"correct":2,"why":"Bandwidth is data per second.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Walkie-talkies","q":"<p>Carnival marshals use radios where only one person can speak at a time, saying 'Over' to hand over. This transmission mode is</p>","options":["simplex","broadband","half-duplex","full-duplex"],"correct":2,"why":"Both directions, one at a time = half-duplex.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Radio station","q":"<p>A radio station in Nassau broadcasts to thousands of car radios that cannot reply. The mode is</p>","options":["full-duplex","peer-to-peer","half-duplex","simplex"],"correct":3,"why":"One direction only = simplex.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"What is a protocol?","q":"<p>In a Paper 02 answer, Shanice must define a network protocol. Which definition is correct?</p>","options":["An agreed set of rules that controls how data is sent and received","A cable that carries light","A device that connects two networks","A program that removes viruses"],"correct":0,"why":"A protocol is a set of rules; routers are devices, fibre is a medium, antivirus is software.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Example protocol","q":"<p>A technician lists items in a school network: a router, a wireless access point, a web server and Ethernet. Which one is a protocol?</p>","options":["Router","Wireless access point","Ethernet","Web server"],"correct":2,"why":"Ethernet is a set of rules for wired LANs; the others are hardware or servers.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Admin tools","q":"<p>The network administrator at a college uses software to add new student accounts, monitor network use and push an update to all 200 PCs at once. This is</p>","options":["a web browser","a modem","a search engine","network management software"],"correct":3,"why":"Network management software sets up users, monitors use, fixes problems and distributes software.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Broadcast or point-to-point?","q":"<p>A radio station in Kingstown sends its signal so that anyone with a radio can listen, while two friends on a phone call hear only each other. These are examples of</p>","options":["both point-to-point","point-to-point and broadcast","both broadcast","broadcast and point-to-point"],"correct":3,"why":"The radio station is broadcast; the private call is point-to-point.","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"Fibre vs copper (notes)","q":"<p>A telephone company is replacing copper lines with fibre-optic cable. Select EVERY advantage of fibre listed in your notes.</p>","options":["Better security during transmission","Easy to install and modify","Faster data transmission","Smaller and lighter","Carries significantly more signals","Cheaper than twisted pair"],"correct":[0, 2, 3, 4],"why":"Fibre carries more signals, is faster, more secure and thinner/lighter; it costs more and is difficult to install and modify.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Voice band","q":"<p>An old dial-up connection over an ordinary telephone line transmits at about 100 to 9,600 bps. This bandwidth category is</p>","options":["voice band","baseband","broadband","narrowband"],"correct":0,"why":"Telephone-line speeds of 100–9,600 bps are voice band; narrowband is 40–300 bps; broadband is millions of bps.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Communication channel","q":"<p>Your teacher asks the class for another name for a transmission medium, the pathway that carries information from sender to receiver. The answer is</p>","options":["protocol","communication channel","router","bandwidth"],"correct":1,"why":"A transmission medium is also called a communication channel.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Duplex","q":"<p>In the notes, the three communication modes are simplex, half-duplex and duplex. A video call where both people talk and hear at once is</p>","options":["half-duplex","duplex","simplex","broadcast"],"correct":1,"why":"Duplex (full-duplex) means both directions at the same time.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Wireless advantage","q":"<p>An events company sets up a network at a different beach venue every weekend. Which advantage of wireless networks matters MOST?</p>","options":["Needs more cables","Immune to interference","Fastest possible speeds","Easy to set up and dismantle, mobile and versatile"],"correct":3,"why":"Wireless networks are quick to set up and take down, ideal for temporary sites.","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"Fibre advantages","q":"<p>Select EVERY advantage of fibre-optic cable over copper cable.</p>","options":["Carries signals further with less loss","Cheapest to install","Immune to electrical interference","Easiest to join (splice)","Higher bandwidth"],"correct":[0, 2, 4],"why":"Fibre wins on bandwidth, distance and interference; it is more expensive and harder to splice.","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"Line of sight","q":"<p>Select EVERY wireless medium that needs a clear line of sight.</p>","options":["Microwave","Wi-Fi","Bluetooth","Infrared"],"correct":[0, 3],"why":"Infrared and point-to-point microwave need line of sight; Wi-Fi and Bluetooth can pass through some walls.","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"Inside a home router","q":"<p>A typical ISP 'router' box in a home combines several devices. Select EVERY function it usually includes.</p>","options":["Switch ports","Laser printer","Modem or fibre terminal","Router","Wireless access point"],"correct":[0, 2, 3, 4],"why":"Router, switch, WAP and modem/ONT are often combined; a printer is not.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Device and function","q":"<p>Match each device to its function.</p>","left":["NIC","Switch","Router","Modem"],"right":["Connects a device to the network","Forwards data to one device in a LAN","Connects different networks","Converts digital and analogue signals"],"why":"Each device is defined by its job.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Medium and use","q":"<p>Match each medium to a typical use.</p>","left":["Twisted pair","Fibre-optic","Bluetooth","Satellite"],"right":["Office Ethernet LAN","Undersea Internet cable","Wireless earbuds","Ship at sea"],"why":"Choose media by distance, bandwidth and mobility.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Transmission modes","q":"<p>Match each mode to its description.</p>","left":["Simplex","Half-duplex","Full-duplex"],"right":["One direction only","Both directions, one at a time","Both directions at the same time"],"why":"Direction and timing define the modes.","fb":[]}]},{"id":"q_s2u3","title":"Section 2 · Mobile communication technologies quiz","desc":"<p>Topic quiz for Section 2: Mobile communication technologies. 28 items.</p>","minutes":0,"items":[{"t":"mc","title":"First generation","q":"<p>Mrs Usher's first mobile phone in the 1980s could only make voice calls, with no texts or Internet. Which generation was it?</p>","options":["4G","3G","1G","2G"],"correct":2,"why":"1G was analogue and voice-only; 2G added digital voice and SMS.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"M-commerce","q":"<p>Kerry-Ann orders sneakers on a shopping app while waiting in line at a supermarket in Spanish Town. This is an example of</p>","options":["citizen journalism","telemedicine","m-commerce","tethering"],"correct":2,"why":"Buying and selling using mobile devices is mobile commerce.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Mojo","q":"<p>A reporter covering a press briefing films, edits and sends a news story using only her smartphone. This is called</p>","options":["roaming","e-learning","citizen journalism","mobile journalism"],"correct":3,"why":"Professional reporting done entirely on a phone is mobile journalism (mojo).","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Citizen journalism risk","q":"<p>During a storm, a resident posts a dramatic flooding video on social media that turns out to be from another country, years earlier. Which drawback of citizen journalism does this show?</p>","options":["Content may be unverified or false","It needs expensive cameras","It is too slow","It cannot be shared"],"correct":0,"why":"Citizen reports can be unverified, out of date or false.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Hotspot equipment","q":"<p>A café owner on the Brades main road wants to create a Wi-Fi hotspot for customers. According to your notes, which two devices create a hotspot?</p>","options":["A GPS receiver and NFC tag","A modem and a wireless router","A hub and a projector","A printer and a scanner"],"correct":1,"why":"A hotspot network mainly includes a modem and a wireless router.","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"Mobile in education","q":"<p>Select EVERY benefit of mobile technology in education from your notes.</p>","options":["An alternative to heavy textbooks","Preparing students for the future","Learning can continue outside the classroom","Up-to-date learning","Guaranteed free data for every student"],"correct":[0, 1, 2, 3],"why":"The four benefits are future readiness, up-to-date learning, digital textbooks and learning beyond the classroom; free data is not guaranteed.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Bluetooth origin","q":"<p>Bluetooth was introduced in 1994 as a wireless replacement for which type of connection?</p>","options":["RS-232 serial cables","Fibre-optic backbone","Coaxial TV cable","Satellite dishes"],"correct":0,"why":"Bluetooth was designed to replace RS-232 serial cables between nearby devices.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"SMS generation","q":"<p>Grandpa remembers when mobile phones first became digital and he could send SMS text messages. Which generation introduced this?</p>","options":["4G","5G","2G","1G"],"correct":2,"why":"2G brought digital voice and SMS.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Mobile broadband","q":"<p>A content creator live-streams HD video of a beach clean-up from her phone. The generation that first made mobile HD streaming common was</p>","options":["2G","4G","3G","1G"],"correct":1,"why":"4G (LTE) delivered mobile broadband for HD streaming.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Low latency","q":"<p>An engineer wants to control a crane remotely in real time with almost no delay. Which feature of 5G matters MOST?</p>","options":["Lower cost of phones","SMS texting","Very low latency","Infrared support"],"correct":2,"why":"Low latency allows real-time control.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Tethering","q":"<p>Dante's laptop has no Internet, so he shares his phone's 4G data with it over Wi-Fi. This is called</p>","options":["a personal hotspot (tethering)","NFC","roaming","a handover"],"correct":0,"why":"Sharing phone data with other devices is tethering / personal hotspot.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Roaming","q":"<p>A Curaçaoan resident visiting Aruba sees a message that her phone is now on an Aruban network and charges may apply. This is</p>","options":["tethering","roaming","pairing","streaming"],"correct":1,"why":"Using another operator's network outside your home network is roaming.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Pairing","q":"<p>Before using new wireless earbuds, Leah must confirm a 'Pair' request on her phone. Which technology needs this step?</p>","options":["GPS","Satellite","Bluetooth","NFC"],"correct":2,"why":"Bluetooth devices must be paired.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"NFC range","q":"<p>Why is NFC suitable for contactless payment?</p>","options":["Its very short range (about 4 cm) means payments need a deliberate tap","It works over several kilometres","It uses GPS satellites","It needs no electricity at all"],"correct":0,"why":"The very short range prevents accidental or remote payments.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"GPS without data","q":"<p>Kemar has no mobile signal on a hike but his phone still shows his position on a map he downloaded earlier. Why?</p>","options":["Wi-Fi from the trail","GPS receives satellite signals and does not need mobile data","The phone guesses","Bluetooth sends the position"],"correct":1,"why":"GPS is receive-only from satellites; offline maps provide the background.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Minimum satellites","q":"<p>How many GPS satellites does a receiver normally need to calculate a 3D position and correct its clock?</p>","options":["At least four","Three","One","Two"],"correct":0,"why":"At least four satellites are needed.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Open Wi-Fi danger","q":"<p>At an airport, Rhea sees 'Free_Airport_WiFi' with no password. What is the main risk of online banking on it?</p>","options":["Others may intercept data or it may be a fake (evil twin) hotspot","Banking apps never work on Wi-Fi","The battery will explode","It uses too much mobile data"],"correct":0,"why":"Open and fake hotspots allow eavesdropping.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Telemedicine meaning","q":"<p>A patient in The Valley has a video consultation with a specialist in another country. This is an example of</p>","options":["telemedicine","e-commerce","roaming","tethering"],"correct":0,"why":"Health care at a distance is telemedicine.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"After the storm","q":"<p>All local cell towers are down after a hurricane. Which device can a rescue coordinator still use to call overseas?</p>","options":["Satellite phone","Ordinary smartphone on 4G","NFC card","Bluetooth speaker"],"correct":0,"why":"Satellite phones do not depend on local towers.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Smishing","q":"<p>Jason receives a text: 'Your bank card is blocked. Click this link to unblock it now.' This is MOST likely</p>","options":["a genuine alert","smishing","roaming","tethering"],"correct":1,"why":"Phishing by SMS is smishing.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Remote wipe","q":"<p>A sales rep's phone holding customer data is stolen. Which measure lets the company erase the data from a distance?</p>","options":["Bluetooth pairing","Remote wipe","GPS offline maps","Roaming"],"correct":1,"why":"Remote wipe erases data on a lost device.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Dead spots","q":"<p>A house in a valley behind San Nicolaas has no mobile signal, while the hilltop does. This coverage problem is mainly caused by</p>","options":["NFC interference","SMS limits","terrain blocking signals","too many satellites"],"correct":2,"why":"Hills block radio signals, creating dead spots.","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"5G over 4G","q":"<p>Select EVERY genuine advantage of 5G compared with 4G.</p>","options":["Works without any towers","Higher frequencies travel further through walls","Faster data speeds","Supports more connected devices per area","Lower latency"],"correct":[2, 3, 4],"why":"5G is faster, lower latency and higher capacity. It still needs towers, and higher frequencies travel LESS well through walls.","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"Business uses","q":"<p>A small business in Grace Bay goes mobile. Select EVERY genuine business benefit.</p>","options":["Taking orders on WhatsApp Business","Accepting card and phone payments anywhere","No need for any security","Sharing its location with customers","Guaranteed coverage everywhere at sea"],"correct":[0, 1, 3],"why":"Payments, orders and location sharing are benefits; coverage is never guaranteed and security is always needed.","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"Safe habits","q":"<p>Select EVERY good security habit for a smartphone.</p>","options":["Leave Bluetooth discoverable everywhere","Use a screen lock","Install updates promptly","Install apps only from official stores","Tap links in unexpected messages"],"correct":[1, 2, 3],"why":"Lock, official apps and updates protect you.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Technology and use","q":"<p>Match each mobile technology to a typical use.</p>","left":["GPS","NFC","Bluetooth","Personal hotspot"],"right":["Showing your position on a map","Tapping to pay","Wireless earbuds","Sharing phone data with a laptop"],"why":"Each technology has a signature use.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Generation and feature","q":"<p>Match each generation to its key feature.</p>","left":["2G","3G","4G","5G"],"right":["Digital calls and SMS","Usable mobile Internet","Mobile broadband and HD streaming","Very low latency and massive IoT"],"why":"Each generation added a major capability.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Issue and fix","q":"<p>Match each mobile issue to a sensible countermeasure.</p>","left":["Stolen phone","Open public Wi-Fi","SIM-swap fraud","High roaming fees"],"right":["Screen lock and remote wipe","Use mobile data or a VPN","Authenticator app instead of SMS codes","Local SIM/eSIM or Wi-Fi"],"why":"Name the risk, then the measure that targets it.","fb":[]}]},{"id":"q_s2u4","title":"Section 2 · The Web and web technologies quiz","desc":"<p>Topic quiz for Section 2: The Web and web technologies. 29 items.</p>","minutes":0,"items":[{"t":"mc","title":"Internet defined","q":"<p>For a Paper 02 answer, Kemar must define the Internet. Which statement BEST describes the Internet?</p>","options":["A search engine","A collection of linked web pages","A global network of interconnected computer networks","A web browser made by Microsoft"],"correct":2,"why":"The Internet is the network infrastructure; linked pages are the Web.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Not the Web","q":"<p>Jamal makes a WhatsApp call to his uncle in Barbados. Which statement is correct?</p>","options":["He is using neither","He is using the Web, not the Internet","He is using FTP","He is using the Internet, but not the World Wide Web"],"correct":3,"why":"VoIP is an Internet service that is not part of the Web.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Browser","q":"<p>Ms Campbell tells her Form 4 class to open the program they use to view web pages. Which of these is a web browser?</p>","options":["Microsoft Edge","HTML","DNS","Google Search"],"correct":0,"why":"Edge is a browser. Google Search is a search engine.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Web server","q":"<p>A café's website files are stored on a computer at a hosting company that sends pages to visitors on request. That computer is a</p>","options":["web browser","modem","web server","search engine"],"correct":2,"why":"Web servers store and serve pages.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"TLD","q":"<p>In the URL https://www.tobagotours.co.tt/trips.html, what is the top-level domain?</p>","options":[".co","trips.html",".tt","www"],"correct":2,"why":"The TLD is the last part of the domain: .tt.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"File name","q":"<p>In the URL https://www.gndschools.gd/forms/registration.pdf, the file name is</p>","options":["gndschools","https","forms","registration.pdf"],"correct":3,"why":"The last item after the final slash is the file.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Path","q":"<p>In https://www.islandnews.bb/sports/cricket/scores.html, which part is the path?</p>","options":["islandnews.bb","https","/sports/cricket/","scores.html"],"correct":2,"why":"Folders between the domain and the file form the path.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"DNS role","q":"<p>When Kerry-Ann types www.coralreefcafe.tt, which system finds the matching IP address?</p>","options":["DNS","VoIP","HTML","FTP"],"correct":0,"why":"DNS translates names into IP addresses.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"IP address","q":"<p>A technician at a Roseau office is typing a printer's IPv4 address into its settings. Which of these is a valid IPv4 address?</p>","options":["192.0.2.300","www.192.dm","192.0.2.45","192-0-2-45"],"correct":2,"why":"IPv4 uses four numbers 0–255 separated by dots; 300 is out of range.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"HTML","q":"<p>Tyrese opens a web page's source code and sees tags such as &lt;h1&gt; and &lt;p&gt; marking headings and paragraphs. The language he is looking at is</p>","options":["FTP","URL","HTML","HTTP"],"correct":2,"why":"HyperText Markup Language.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"HTTPS","q":"<p>A banking site's address starts with https:// and shows a padlock. This means</p>","options":["the site cannot contain mistakes","the site uses FTP","the site is free","data sent between the browser and the site is encrypted"],"correct":3,"why":"HTTPS encrypts data in transit.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"ISP","q":"<p>The company that the Richardson family pays each month to connect their home to the Internet is their</p>","options":["web browser","DNS","search engine","ISP"],"correct":3,"why":"An ISP provides Internet access.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Uploading","q":"<p>Nadia sends her completed SBA from her laptop to Microsoft Teams. She is</p>","options":["downloading","streaming","browsing","uploading"],"correct":3,"why":"Sending data from your device to a server is uploading.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"FTP use","q":"<p>A web designer needs to move the finished website files from her laptop onto the hosting server. Which protocol is designed for this?</p>","options":["SMS","FTP (or SFTP)","VoIP","DNS"],"correct":1,"why":"FTP/SFTP transfers files to servers.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Wiki","q":"<p>Students in a Form 5 class create revision pages that every class member can edit. This website is a</p>","options":["wiki","ISP","blog","search engine"],"correct":0,"why":"A wiki is collaboratively editable.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Who owns it?","q":"<p>During a class debate, Jaden claims that one American company owns and controls the whole Internet. Which response is correct?</p>","options":["No single person, company or government owns or controls the Internet; it works through agreed protocols","The Internet is owned by the United Nations","Each ISP owns the whole Internet","He is right: one company controls it"],"correct":0,"why":"The Internet is a network of networks owned by many parties, held together by shared protocols such as TCP/IP.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Podcast","q":"<p>The Hummingbird Ridge High School media club records a series of audio episodes interviewing local musicians, which listeners download to their phones. This is a</p>","options":["wiki","newsgroup","podcast","search engine"],"correct":2,"why":"Audio (or video) episodes for streaming or download are podcasts.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Dial-up","q":"<p>Grandpa remembers his modem dialling the ISP's number and screeching before he typed his username and password to get online. This connection method is</p>","options":["WiMAX","DSL","dial-up","fibre"],"correct":2,"why":"Dial-up is the oldest method: the modem dials the ISP over the phone line.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"WWW inventor","q":"<p>For a Carnival-week quiz night at school, Team Soca must answer: which statement about the origin of the World Wide Web is correct?</p>","options":["It is owned by one government","It was designed in 1991 by Tim Berners-Lee at CERN","It existed before the Internet","It was invented in 2010 by a social media company"],"correct":1,"why":"Tim Berners-Lee designed the Web at CERN around 1991, and CERN gave it away free; the Internet came first.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"TCP","q":"<p>When Andre downloads a photo, it is split into packets that travel separately and are put back together in order at his device. Which protocol makes this transfer reliable?</p>","options":["DNS","HTML","Wi-Fi","TCP"],"correct":3,"why":"TCP controls reliable transfer by checking and reassembling packets.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"XHTML","q":"<p>A web designer is told to use XHTML instead of older HTML. What is the main difference?</p>","options":["XHTML is a search engine","XHTML is used to send e-mail","XHTML encrypts pages","XHTML is stricter, with more rules, so browsers handle the code consistently"],"correct":3,"why":"XHTML (2000) is a stricter version of HTML.","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"Internet drawbacks","q":"<p>Select EVERY disadvantage of the Internet listed in your notes.</p>","options":["Card details used online can be stolen","E-mail is delivered instantly","Inappropriate content is easy for children to find","Personal information can be accessed by others"],"correct":[0, 2, 3],"why":"Instant e-mail is an advantage; the others are disadvantages.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Blog","q":"<p>A Paramaribo chef posts recipes regularly; the newest post appears at the top and readers comment. This is a</p>","options":["wiki","FTP site","web browser","blog"],"correct":3,"why":"Reverse-date posts with comments = blog.","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"Cloud advantages","q":"<p>A small business in Santiago de los Caballeros moves its files to cloud storage. Select EVERY genuine advantage.</p>","options":["Works with no Internet connection at all","Easy file sharing and collaboration","No privacy concerns ever","Off-site backup if the office is damaged","Access from any device with Internet"],"correct":[1, 3, 4],"why":"Cloud gives access anywhere, off-site backup and collaboration; it needs Internet and raises privacy questions.","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"Search tips","q":"<p>Select EVERY search technique that narrows results correctly.</p>","options":["Typing the whole question in capital letters","site:cxc.org to search one website","\"Hurricane Irma\" in quotes for the exact phrase","Using a minus sign to exclude a word"],"correct":[1, 2, 3],"why":"Quotes, minus and site: are real operators; capitals change nothing.","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"Internet services","q":"<p>Select EVERY service that uses the Internet but is NOT itself the World Wide Web.</p>","options":["VoIP calls","FTP file transfer","E-mail","Reading a web page in a browser"],"correct":[0, 1, 2],"why":"E-mail, VoIP and FTP are Internet services; web pages are the Web.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Acronyms","q":"<p>Match each acronym to its meaning.</p>","left":["URL","HTML","HTTP","DNS","ISP"],"right":["Uniform Resource Locator","HyperText Markup Language","HyperText Transfer Protocol","Domain Name System","Internet Service Provider"],"why":"Know the full forms: examiners sometimes ask for them.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"URL parts","q":"<p>For https://www.reefdive.ht/trips/night.html, match each part to its label.</p>","left":["https",".ht","/trips/","night.html"],"right":["Protocol","Top-level domain","Path","File name"],"why":"Read left to right: protocol, domain, path, file.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Tool for the job","q":"<p>Match each need to the best tool.</p>","left":["Free call to relatives abroad","Formal letter with a CV attached","Find pages about 5G in the Caribbean","Store SBA files to open at school and home"],"right":["VoIP","E-mail","Search engine","Cloud storage"],"why":"Choose the tool by purpose.","fb":[]}]}]},{"pkg":"Topic Quizzes · Section 3","quizzes":[{"id":"q_s3u1","title":"Section 3 · Computer security, cybersecurity and misuse quiz","desc":"<p>Topic quiz for Section 3: Computer security, cybersecurity and misuse. 43 items.</p>","minutes":0,"items":[{"t":"mc","title":"Undeniableness","q":"<p>A manager at a Guyana firm authorises a large payment, then claims he never did. The system keeps no record of who approved it. Which kind of system security is missing?</p>","options":["Reliability","Portability","Undeniableness (non-repudiation)","Usability"],"correct":2,"why":"Undeniableness means people cannot deny their actions; a record (audit trail or digital signature) provides it.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Vulnerability definition","q":"<p>An IT auditor at a Nevis hotel writes: 'The procedure for issuing staff keycards has no check on whether the person still works here.' In security terms this is a</p>","options":["denial-of-service attack","countermeasure","vulnerability in a set of procedures","piece of malware"],"correct":2,"why":"A vulnerability can be a weakness in procedures, not just software.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Drive-by download","q":"<p>Shania visits a hacked recipe website and, without clicking any download button, malware is installed on her laptop. This distribution channel is called</p>","options":["physical media","self-propagation","whaling","a drive-by download"],"correct":3,"why":"Unintended download just by visiting a site = drive-by download.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Malware vs bugs","q":"<p>A Barbados school's timetable program sometimes shows the wrong room because of a coding mistake made by its developer. This program is BEST described as</p>","options":["a trojan","ransomware","spyware","defective software with a bug, not malware"],"correct":3,"why":"Malware is deliberately harmful; a legitimate program with accidental errors is defective software.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"EULA","q":"<p>When the Regent Street Accounting buys a software package, it receives a document stating that the program may be installed on three computers only. This document is the</p>","options":["acceptable use policy","End User Licence Agreement (EULA)","privacy policy","audit trail"],"correct":1,"why":"The EULA sets out how the software may be used; breaking it is piracy.","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"Pirated software risks","q":"<p>Select EVERY risk faced by a business that uses pirated software.</p>","options":["No access to updates or bug fixes","No warranty or customer support","Fines for copyright infringement","Higher risk of viruses","Guaranteed free upgrades forever"],"correct":[0, 1, 2, 3],"why":"Pirated copies get no support, updates or warranty, often carry malware and can lead to fines.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Computer Misuse Act","q":"<p>The two journalists in the Prestel case could not be convicted of fraud. Why?</p>","options":["They had permission to use the system","Hacking was already illegal","The password was fake","Fraud required a person to be deceived, and a computer cannot be deceived"],"correct":3,"why":"This gap led to the Computer Misuse Act 1990, which created specific offences such as unauthorised access.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Privacy policy","q":"<p>A fitness app collects users' location 'to map their runs' but later sells the location history to advertisers without telling them. This is MAINLY</p>","options":["a worm","software piracy","plagiarism","a violation of privacy (data used for a different purpose)"],"correct":3,"why":"Collecting for one purpose and using for another without consent violates privacy.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Surveillance types","q":"<p>Investigators hide a small microphone in an office that transmits conversations to a nearby receiver. This type of electronic surveillance is</p>","options":["video surveillance","bugging","wiretapping","pharming"],"correct":1,"why":"A hidden transmitting microphone = bugging; wiretapping physically taps phone lines.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Financial abuse","q":"<p>A carer has her elderly client's online-banking password. The client's bills go unpaid and there are unexplained withdrawals. This MOST likely indicates</p>","options":["fair use","financial abuse","adware","a DDoS attack"],"correct":1,"why":"Unauthorised use of someone's money, shown by unpaid bills and unexplained withdrawals, is financial abuse.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Cyberbullying concerns","q":"<p>Which feature of cyberbullying means a student can be targeted even at midnight in her own bedroom?</p>","options":["It only happens at school","It is always anonymous","It is legal","It is persistent: devices allow 24-hour contact"],"correct":3,"why":"Persistence (24/7 contact) is one of the special concerns, with permanence and being hard to notice.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Five kinds of system security","q":"<p>Match each kind of system security to its meaning.</p>","left":["Reliability","Entirety","Usability","Undeniableness"],"right":["The system operates correctly without false alarms","Data is not forged, tampered with or deleted","Users can enjoy the services offered","People are held responsible for their actions"],"why":"These extend the CIA triad.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Phishing types","q":"<p>Match each phishing type to its description.</p>","left":["Spear phishing","Whaling","Search engine phishing","Vishing"],"right":["Targets a specific person with a message from an apparently known sender","Targets senior executives and people in powerful positions","Fake sites placed in search results","Phishing by phone call"],"why":"Phishing is classified by channel and target.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"CIA: integrity","q":"<p>A hacker gets into a Barbados school's grading system and changes several students' final marks. No data is deleted and the system stays online. Which security goal has been MOST directly broken?</p>","options":["Availability","Authentication","Integrity","Portability"],"correct":2,"why":"The data is now inaccurate: integrity. The system is still available, and portability is not a security goal.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"CIA: availability","q":"<p>On the morning of the tax deadline, a government e-services portal cannot be reached because its servers are overwhelmed by fake requests. Which goal is broken?</p>","options":["Integrity","Availability","Confidentiality","Non-repudiation"],"correct":1,"why":"Legitimate users cannot use the service: availability.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Vulnerability","q":"<p>An IT audit of a Nassau law office finds that all computers still use the factory-default password 'admin'. In security terms, the default password is BEST described as a</p>","options":["threat","attack","vulnerability","countermeasure"],"correct":2,"why":"It is a weakness that could be exploited: a vulnerability.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Threat vs attack","q":"<p>The manager of a Grenada credit union says: 'Criminals in the region have started targeting small banks with ransomware.' Nothing has happened at his credit union yet. What he describes is a</p>","options":["vulnerability","countermeasure","threat","attack"],"correct":2,"why":"Something that could cause harm but has not happened yet is a threat.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Worm","q":"<p>Within one hour of a single infected laptop joining the network at a hotel in Ocho Rios, Jamaica, every unpatched PC on the network is infected, though no one opened any file. This malware is MOST likely a</p>","options":["adware","trojan","virus","worm"],"correct":3,"why":"Spreading by itself across a network without user action is the defining feature of a worm.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Virus","q":"<p>A Word document on the shared drive at a St. Lucia school infects each computer that opens it, and each infected computer then infects other documents. This is a</p>","options":["denial-of-service attack","firewall","virus","pharming attack"],"correct":2,"why":"Malware that attaches to host files and spreads when they are opened is a virus.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Ransomware","q":"<p>Staff at a Georgetown, Guyana, accounting firm arrive to find every file renamed with the extension .locked and a note demanding payment in Bitcoin for the decryption key. This is</p>","options":["ransomware","spyware","adware","a worm"],"correct":0,"why":"Encrypting files and demanding payment is ransomware.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Spyware","q":"<p>A student's online banking password is stolen, even though she never shared it. Investigators find a small program on her laptop that recorded every key she pressed. This program is a type of</p>","options":["spyware (keylogger)","worm","firewall","adware"],"correct":0,"why":"A keylogger secretly records keystrokes: spyware.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Smishing","q":"<p>A resident of Nevis receives an SMS saying she has won a Carnival cruise and must text back her ID number and card details to claim it. This is an example of</p>","options":["vishing","smishing","pharming","eavesdropping"],"correct":1,"why":"Phishing via SMS text message is smishing.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Pharming","q":"<p>Customers of a Trinidad bank type the bank's correct web address into their browsers but are taken to a convincing fake site, where their logins are stolen. This attack is called</p>","options":["hacking","phishing","pharming","plagiarism"],"correct":2,"why":"Redirecting users who typed the correct address (e.g. by poisoned DNS) is pharming.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Vishing","q":"<p>Mr. Hodge in Anguilla receives a phone call from someone claiming to be from his internet provider, who asks for his account password to 'fix a fault'. This is</p>","options":["smishing","spam","vishing","a DDoS attack"],"correct":2,"why":"Phishing by voice call is vishing.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Industrial espionage","q":"<p>An employee of a Barbados rum distillery is secretly paid by a foreign competitor to copy the company's new blending formula onto a flash drive. This is BEST described as</p>","options":["industrial espionage","cyberbullying","denial of service","adware"],"correct":0,"why":"Stealing a competitor's trade secrets = industrial espionage.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Eavesdropping","q":"<p>At a busy café in Layou with free open Wi-Fi, a person uses software to capture unencrypted messages and logins sent by other customers. This misuse is</p>","options":["plagiarism","pharming","software piracy","electronic eavesdropping"],"correct":3,"why":"Intercepting communications in transit = electronic eavesdropping.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Piracy","q":"<p>A small business in Antigua buys one licence for a design program and installs it on all six office computers. This is</p>","options":["fair use","software piracy","identity theft","phishing"],"correct":1,"why":"Using software beyond the terms of its licence is software piracy.","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"Signs of phishing","q":"<p>A Form 5 student receives an e-mail claiming to be from the school's Microsoft 365 administrator. Select EVERY feature that suggests it is a phishing e-mail.</p>","options":["It says the account will be deleted in 1 hour unless he logs in through the link","It asks for his password and date of birth","It uses the school's correct name","It begins 'Dear user'","The sender address is admin@micr0soft-support.co"],"correct":[0, 1, 3, 4],"why":"Urgency, a fake look-alike domain, requests for credentials and a generic greeting are red flags. Using the correct school name proves nothing.","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"Availability attacks","q":"<p>Select EVERY incident that mainly attacks the AVAILABILITY of a system.</p>","options":["A student editing marks in a spreadsheet","A hacker reading staff e-mails","Ransomware encrypting a clinic's files","A worm using up all the network bandwidth","A DDoS attack on a ferry booking site"],"correct":[2, 3, 4],"why":"DDoS, ransomware and bandwidth-hungry worms stop users from using systems. Reading e-mails is confidentiality; editing marks is integrity.","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"Identity theft sources","q":"<p>A police officer in St. Vincent explains how criminals gather information for identity theft. Select EVERY method she would mention.</p>","options":["Installing a firewall","Phishing e-mails","Searching rubbish bins for bank statements","Card skimming devices on ATMs","Reading details people overshare on social media"],"correct":[1, 2, 3, 4],"why":"Phishing, dumpster diving, skimming and oversharing all leak personal data. A firewall is a countermeasure.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Malware match","q":"<p>Match each malware type to its key feature.</p>","left":["Virus","Worm","Trojan","Adware"],"right":["Needs a host file to spread","Spreads across networks by itself","Disguised as useful software","Displays unwanted advertisements"],"why":"Spreading method and behaviour identify the type.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Misuse match","q":"<p>Match each misuse to its example from the Caribbean news.</p>","left":["Cyberbullying","Propaganda","Plagiarism","Violation of privacy"],"right":["A fake account repeatedly mocks a Form 2 student","A one-sided campaign of edited videos to sway voters","A student submits a blog post as his own SBA write-up","A clinic posts patients' names and diagnoses online"],"why":"Each misuse harms in a different way: a person, public opinion, academic honesty, personal data.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Security vocabulary","q":"<p>Match each term to its meaning.</p>","left":["Vulnerability","Threat","Attack","Countermeasure"],"right":["A weakness that could be exploited","Something that could cause harm","An actual attempt to cause harm","An action or device that reduces risk"],"why":"Weakness, possible danger, real event, protection.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"DoS vs DDoS","q":"<p>A news report says a Jamaican government website was taken offline by traffic from over 20,000 infected devices around the world. The word that BEST describes this attack is</p>","options":["DoS from a single computer","industrial espionage","pharming","DDoS using a botnet"],"correct":3,"why":"Many infected devices acting together = distributed denial of service via a botnet.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Plagiarism vs copyright","q":"<p>Kerry copies a poem from 1850, which is out of copyright, and enters it in a school poetry competition as her own work. This is</p>","options":["neither","both plagiarism and copyright infringement","copyright infringement only","plagiarism only"],"correct":3,"why":"No copyright exists any more, but she still claimed someone else's work as her own: plagiarism.","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"Computer fraud","q":"<p>Select EVERY example of computer fraud.</p>","options":["Using stolen card details to buy phones online","A student saves her SBA to the cloud","A website owner sells 'investment' in a business that does not exist","An employee alters payroll records to pay herself extra","A fake online shop takes payment for Carnival costumes and never delivers"],"correct":[0, 2, 3, 4],"why":"Each uses computers to deceive people for money. Saving to the cloud is normal use.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"More misuse terms","q":"<p>Match each term to its meaning.</p>","left":["Cybercrime","Bias","Propaganda","Surveillance"],"right":["Crime carried out using computers and the Internet","A point of view that affects what is said, not always deliberately","A deliberately one-sided message to persuade people","Monitoring people's activities by electronic means"],"why":"Know the precise meaning of each term.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Bias or propaganda?","q":"<p>A government ministry in an island state deliberately posts only flattering statistics and edited photos about a new road project to persuade citizens to support it before an election. This is BEST described as</p>","options":["propaganda","unintentional bias","plagiarism","pharming"],"correct":0,"why":"A deliberate, one-sided message designed to persuade = propaganda. Bias can be unintentional; this was planned.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Bias","q":"<p>A Barbados cricket fan writes a blog about a Test match. Without meaning to, he praises every decision by his own team and criticises every decision by the visitors. His blog shows</p>","options":["denial of service","a worm","pharming","bias"],"correct":3,"why":"His point of view affects what he writes, though not deliberately: bias.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Credit card fraud","q":"<p>Mrs. Hodge in St. John’s notices purchases on her card statement from an online electronics store she has never used. Her card is still in her purse. What has MOST likely happened?</p>","options":["Electronic publishing","A denial-of-service attack","Software piracy","Credit card fraud using her stolen card details"],"correct":3,"why":"Criminals used her card details without the physical card: credit card fraud, a form of identity theft.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Surveillance","q":"<p>A government installs cameras on main roads and software that reads car number plates, and stores the records for years. Critics say this could be misused. This debate is about</p>","options":["adware","software piracy","surveillance and privacy","plagiarism"],"correct":2,"why":"Electronic monitoring of people's activities = surveillance, which raises privacy concerns.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Cybercrime","q":"<p>Which of these is the BEST example of cybercrime?</p>","options":["A student forgets her password","A criminal hacks into a Trinidad shop's system and transfers money to his own account","A teacher deletes an old file by mistake","A printer runs out of ink"],"correct":1,"why":"Cybercrime is crime carried out using computers and the Internet, such as hacking to steal money.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Fake news type","q":"<p>During a tropical storm, a resident of Charlestown forwards a voice note claiming Liamuiga Power & Water will cut all power for a week. She genuinely believes it is true, but it is false. Her forwarding is BEST described as spreading</p>","options":["misinformation","pharming","propaganda created by her","disinformation"],"correct":0,"why":"False information shared by someone who believes it = misinformation. Disinformation is deliberate.","fb":[]}]},{"id":"q_s3u2","title":"Section 3 · The impact of computer misuse quiz","desc":"<p>Topic quiz for Section 3: The impact of computer misuse. 22 items.</p>","minutes":0,"items":[{"t":"mc","title":"Individual impact","q":"<p>After her e-mail is hacked, a nurse in Dominica finds that someone has opened two credit cards in her name and run up debts. Which impact on the individual does this BEST show?</p>","options":["Loss of national security","Financial loss through identity theft","Copyright infringement","Lost competitive advantage"],"correct":1,"why":"Debts in her name are a financial impact of identity theft.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Psychological impact","q":"<p>A school counsellor in Barbados notices that a student targeted by months of online harassment is sleeping badly, avoiding friends and missing school. This is MAINLY a</p>","options":["financial impact","national-security impact","psychological impact","legal impact"],"correct":2,"why":"Effects on sleep, mood and social life are psychological.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Reputational impact","q":"<p>After news reports that a Caribbean airline 'lost passenger data to hackers', bookings drop sharply even though the system is working again. This is an example of</p>","options":["software piracy","denial of service","integrity loss","reputational damage"],"correct":3,"why":"Customers avoid the airline because they no longer trust it.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Legal impact","q":"<p>A clinic in Jamaica is investigated and fined after patient records were left unprotected and leaked. The fine is MAINLY</p>","options":["a psychological impact","an impact on national security","an intangible impact","a legal / regulatory impact"],"correct":3,"why":"Investigation and fines by a regulator are legal impacts (and tangible).","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Government impact","q":"<p>Hackers disrupt the computer systems that control water pumping for an entire island. Why is this especially serious for the government?</p>","options":["It affects one person's privacy","It is a form of plagiarism","Water companies do not use computers","Water supply is critical national infrastructure that affects public health and safety"],"correct":3,"why":"Critical infrastructure attacks endanger the whole population.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Elections","q":"<p>Days before an election, thousands of WhatsApp messages falsely tell voters in one district that their polling station has moved. What is the MAIN impact on democracy?</p>","options":["Voters' phones are infected with worms","The election officials lose money","Voters may be misled into not voting, reducing fairness and trust in the result","It improves turnout"],"correct":2,"why":"False voting information can suppress votes and damage trust.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Hidden costs","q":"<p>A small Antigua hotel paid no ransom but still lost money after a ransomware attack. Which was MOST likely its largest hidden cost?</p>","options":["The hotel's electricity bill","The price of the antivirus it already had","Lost bookings while systems were down","The cost of the ransom note paper"],"correct":2,"why":"Downtime and lost revenue are usually the biggest hidden cost.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Tangible impact","q":"<p>Which of these impacts on a Trinidad firm after a cyberattack is TANGIBLE?</p>","options":["Staff feeling embarrassed","Customers feeling uneasy","Loss of the brand's 'friendly' image","A US$8,000 invoice for IT recovery"],"correct":3,"why":"A specific invoice can be measured: tangible.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Long-term privacy","q":"<p>Why is the leak of citizens' fingerprints from a national ID database MORE serious in the long term than the theft of their bank cards?</p>","options":["Bank cards cannot be replaced","Fingerprints are not personal data","Fingerprints cannot be changed, so the risk lasts for life","Fingerprints are worth more money"],"correct":2,"why":"Biometric data is permanent; bank cards can be cancelled and reissued.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Supply chain","q":"<p>Ransomware shuts down the computer system of the company that manages container unloading at the Willemstad cruise terminal for a week. Which is the MOST likely knock-on impact?</p>","options":["Smartphones stop receiving calls","The airport runway closes","Students cannot log into Google Classroom","Supermarkets and hardware stores run short of stock"],"correct":3,"why":"If containers cannot be processed, businesses that depend on imports run short.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Small business","q":"<p>Why are small businesses, such as a family garage in West Bay, often hit HARDER by a cyberattack than large banks?</p>","options":["Small businesses do not use computers","They usually have fewer resources, backups and IT staff to recover","Large banks are never attacked","Hackers are not interested in banks"],"correct":1,"why":"Fewer resources make recovery harder; some small firms never reopen.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Espionage impact","q":"<p>A Guyanese engineering firm loses a major contract after a competitor obtained its tender price through hacking. This impact is BEST described as</p>","options":["plagiarism","loss of competitive advantage","cyberbullying","loss of availability"],"correct":1,"why":"Stolen trade secrets let a rival win: lost competitive advantage.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Piracy impact","q":"<p>A young soca producer in Trinidad finds her new album shared free on download sites the day it is released. The MOST direct impact on her is</p>","options":["her computer is infected","her identity is stolen","loss of national security","loss of income from sales and streams"],"correct":3,"why":"Piracy reduces the creator's legitimate income.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Command words","q":"<p>A question asks: 'Explain ONE impact of a DDoS attack on an online store. (2 marks)'. Which answer earns BOTH marks?</p>","options":["Customers cannot access the site to buy, so the store loses sales during the attack.","DDoS stands for distributed denial of service.","Loss of money.","It is bad."],"correct":0,"why":"It states the impact and explains how the attack causes it.","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"Impacts on organisations","q":"<p>Island Fresh Supermarkets suffers a data breach. Select EVERY likely impact on the organisation.</p>","options":["Downtime at the tills","Cost of hiring IT experts","Loss of customer trust","Customers' dates of birth change","Possible fines under data protection law"],"correct":[0, 1, 2, 4],"why":"Recovery costs, trust, fines and downtime all hit the business. Dates of birth do not change.","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"National level","q":"<p>Select EVERY impact that is a concern at a NATIONAL (government) level.</p>","options":["Attack on the electricity grid","Disinformation campaign during an election","A teenager's game account is hacked","Theft of confidential trade-negotiation documents","A café's Wi-Fi password is guessed"],"correct":[0, 1, 3],"why":"Critical infrastructure, state secrets and elections are national concerns.","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"Individual harms","q":"<p>Select EVERY impact a victim of identity theft might suffer.</p>","options":["Debts in their name","Friends receiving scam messages 'from them'","Stress and time spent sorting it out","Improved credit rating","Difficulty getting a loan later"],"correct":[0, 1, 2, 4],"why":"All except an improved credit rating are typical harms.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Impact categories","q":"<p>Match each effect to its impact category.</p>","left":["Paying an IT consultant","Customers no longer trusting the store","Tills not working all day","Being sued by a customer"],"right":["Financial","Reputational","Operational","Legal"],"why":"Money, image, ability to work, courts.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Three levels","q":"<p>Match each impact to the level it MAINLY affects.</p>","left":["A student is harassed online","A hotel loses a week of bookings","Citizens lose trust in election results","A pensioner loses savings to a scam call"],"right":["Individual","Organisation","Government / nation","Individual (financial)"],"why":"Who carries the main loss?","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Time scale","q":"<p>Match each impact to how long it typically lasts.</p>","left":["Website offline during a DDoS","Recovery from ransomware using backups","Regulator's investigation","Leaked fingerprints"],"right":["Hours to days","Days to weeks","Months","A lifetime"],"why":"Operational impacts are short; legal and privacy impacts last much longer.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Ransom decision","q":"<p>The owner of an Orange Walk dive shop asks whether to pay a ransom after an attack. Which is the STRONGEST reason against paying?</p>","options":["There is no guarantee of recovery, and paying funds crime and may lead to repeat attacks","It is illegal to have backups","Ransoms are always very small","Paying guarantees the data is returned"],"correct":0,"why":"Criminals may not deliver, and payers become targets.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Evaluate","q":"<p>Which sentence shows EVALUATION rather than description in an answer about a data breach at a school?</p>","options":["The privacy impact on students is the most serious because their data can be misused for years, whereas the school's costs are one-off.","Student data was stolen.","Hackers broke in.","The school lost money."],"correct":0,"why":"It makes a judgement and justifies it with a comparison.","fb":[]}]},{"id":"q_s3u3","title":"Section 3 · Countermeasures: protecting data and systems quiz","desc":"<p>Topic quiz for Section 3: Countermeasures: protecting data and systems. 38 items.</p>","minutes":0,"items":[{"t":"mc","title":"Hardware vs software firewall","q":"<p>A Barbados company wants ONE firewall that protects all 60 computers on its network before traffic reaches any of them. It should choose</p>","options":["a UPS","an antivirus program","a software firewall on one PC","a hardware firewall"],"correct":3,"why":"A hardware firewall sits between the network and the Internet and protects every device.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"IDS types","q":"<p>A system monitors the important operating-system files on one server and alerts the administrator if they change. This is a</p>","options":["honeypot","host-based IDS (HIDS)","network IDS (NIDS)","surge protector"],"correct":1,"why":"Monitoring a single host's files = HIDS.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Dictionary attack","q":"<p>A hacker's program tries every word in an English dictionary, with common number endings, as a student's password. This is a</p>","options":["denial-of-service attack","drive-by download","dictionary attack","pharming attack"],"correct":2,"why":"Trying dictionary words and variations = dictionary attack.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Authentication vs authorisation","q":"<p>Kayla logs into the school system with her username and password (step 1). The system then lets her see her own grades but not her classmates' (step 2). Step 2 is</p>","options":["authentication","authorisation","encryption","phishing"],"correct":1,"why":"Authentication proves who you are; authorisation decides what you may access.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Differential backup","q":"<p>A Trinidad firm does a full backup on Sunday. Each night it copies everything that has changed since Sunday's full backup. These nightly copies are</p>","options":["archives","mirrors","incremental backups","differential backups"],"correct":3,"why":"Changes since the last FULL backup = differential.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"WEP","q":"<p>A café in Albina still uses WEP encryption on its Wi-Fi. What should the IT technician advise?</p>","options":["Keep WEP, it is the strongest","Remove the password completely","Upgrade to WPA2 or WPA3, because WEP is easily cracked","Only hide the SSID"],"correct":2,"why":"WEP is the weakest; WPA2 is a minimum and WPA3 is best.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Verify sender","q":"<p>An e-mail 'from your principal' asks you to buy gift cards urgently. What is the BEST way to verify it?</p>","options":["Click the link to check","Reply to the e-mail and ask if it is real","Forward it to all your friends","Phone or speak to the principal using contact details you already have"],"correct":3,"why":"Verify through a channel you already trust, never through the suspicious message.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Substitution trick","q":"<p>An e-mail comes from support@rnicrosoft.com. What trick is being used?</p>","options":["A drive-by download","Encryption","Mirroring","Substitution: 'rn' replaces 'm' to look like microsoft"],"correct":3,"why":"Attackers swap characters that look alike.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Behavioural biometric","q":"<p>A bank's app recognises its customers partly by the rhythm in which they type their PIN. This is an example of</p>","options":["a token","a behavioural biometric","a physiological biometric","a firewall"],"correct":1,"why":"Typing rhythm, gait and voice are behavioural biometrics.","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"Website checks","q":"<p>Ms. Pérez is unsure whether an online furniture shop is genuine. Select EVERY sensible check.</p>","options":["Look for poor spelling and grammar","Test the phone number on the Contact page","Check the URL carefully for misspellings","Search the shop's name for reviews","Trust it because it has nice photos"],"correct":[0, 1, 2, 3],"why":"Appearance is the weakest test; independent checks matter.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Social media safety","q":"<p>Match each STOP. THINK. CONNECT. tip to its meaning.</p>","left":["When in doubt, throw it out","Own your online presence","Unique account, unique passphrase","Post only about others as you would have them post about you"],"right":["Delete suspicious links even from people you know","Set privacy settings to your comfort level","A different passphrase for every account","The Golden Rule online"],"why":"Good social media habits.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"UPS","q":"<p>The power in The Valley goes off for a few minutes several times a week. Wallblake Road Travel office wants staff to be able to save work and shut down safely when this happens. Which device should they buy?</p>","options":["Surge protector","Scanner","UPS","Router"],"correct":2,"why":"A UPS provides battery power for a short time so users can save and shut down.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Surge protector","q":"<p>After the power returns following an outage in Spanish Town, a spike in voltage damages a shop's computer. Which device would have BEST prevented this?</p>","options":["Surge protector","Antivirus","Keycard","Firewall"],"correct":0,"why":"Surge protectors absorb voltage spikes.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Off-site backup","q":"<p>A Barbados accounting firm keeps its only backup drive in the same room as its server. Why is this a poor arrangement?</p>","options":["A fire, flood or theft could destroy both the server and the backup","It breaks copyright law","The backup will run too slowly","Backups must always be on paper"],"correct":0,"why":"Backups must be kept separately, ideally off-site or in the cloud.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Encryption","q":"<p>A laptop containing a St. Kitts clinic's patient records is stolen from a car. Which countermeasure would mean the thief CANNOT read the records?</p>","options":["A larger hard drive","A screen saver","Full-disk encryption","A surge protector"],"correct":2,"why":"Encrypted data is unreadable without the key.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"2FA factors","q":"<p>Which of these combinations is a TRUE example of two-factor authentication?</p>","options":["Two different passwords","Username and password","Password and a code from an authenticator app on your phone","Password and PIN"],"correct":2,"why":"Two different factor types: something you know + something you have.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Firewall","q":"<p>A school in Guyana wants to block unknown computers on the Internet from connecting to its network, and stop students reaching certain websites. Which countermeasure does BOTH?</p>","options":["Firewall","CCTV","UPS","Audit trail"],"correct":0,"why":"A firewall filters traffic in and out according to rules.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Antivirus updates","q":"<p>Why must antivirus software at a Trinidad bank be updated regularly?</p>","options":["To make the screen brighter","So it can recognise newly discovered malware","So it can replace the firewall","To speed up the printer"],"correct":1,"why":"Signature databases must be updated to detect new malware.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Least privilege","q":"<p>At a Jamaican hospital, porters can view patients' full medical histories on the system. Which countermeasure would BEST fix this?</p>","options":["Install a UPS","Buy faster computers","Turn off the firewall","Set access privileges so porters see only what their job needs"],"correct":3,"why":"Least privilege limits access to what each role needs.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Audit trail","q":"<p>A teacher's grade in the Oranjestad school system was changed overnight. Which countermeasure would show WHO made the change and WHEN?</p>","options":["Surge protector","Cable lock","Encryption","Audit trail"],"correct":3,"why":"An audit trail logs who did what and when.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Patches","q":"<p>Ransomware spreads through a network in Antigua by exploiting a flaw for which the software maker released a fix two months ago. Which countermeasure would have prevented this?</p>","options":["Using a dot-matrix printer","Installing security updates/patches promptly","Buying a bigger monitor","Writing the password down"],"correct":1,"why":"Patches fix known vulnerabilities.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Biometrics","q":"<p>A Turks and Caicos bank wants only specific managers to enter the vault room, and wants entry that cannot be lent to someone else like a key. Which is MOST suitable?</p>","options":["A shared PIN","A sign on the door","A surge protector","A biometric fingerprint scanner"],"correct":3,"why":"Biometrics are tied to the person and cannot be lent.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Fire","q":"<p>Which is the MOST suitable way to put out a fire in a server room?</p>","options":["Opening the windows","Sand from the beach","Water hose","CO₂ or gas-based extinguisher"],"correct":3,"why":"CO₂ and clean-agent gases extinguish fire without damaging electronics; water conducts electricity and damages equipment.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"AUP","q":"<p>New students at a Grenada secondary school must agree to rules about using school computers before receiving a login. This document is a(n)</p>","options":["firewall rule","data protection law","audit trail","acceptable use policy"],"correct":3,"why":"An AUP is an organisation's rules for using its systems.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Passphrase","q":"<p>Which password for a Form 5 student's school e-mail account is the STRONGEST?</p>","options":["qwerty123","P@ssword1","Keon2009","Coconut-Drum-Ferry-Orbit-73"],"correct":3,"why":"A long passphrase of random words with numbers and symbols is strongest.","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"Physical countermeasures","q":"<p>Select EVERY physical countermeasure.</p>","options":["CCTV cameras","Antivirus software","Encryption","Locked server room","Security guard"],"correct":[0, 3, 4],"why":"CCTV, locks and guards protect the premises and hardware.","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"Hurricane prep","q":"<p>A dive shop in Salem is preparing its office computers for an approaching hurricane. Select EVERY sensible action.</p>","options":["Confirm a recent cloud/off-site backup exists","Cover equipment with plastic","Leave the server on the floor near the door","Move equipment off the floor and away from windows","Unplug equipment"],"correct":[0, 1, 3, 4],"why":"Back up off-site, raise, unplug and protect from water.","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"Data protection principles","q":"<p>Select EVERY principle commonly found in data protection laws.</p>","options":["Keep data secure","Collect only the data you need","Keep data forever just in case","Let people see data held about them","Keep personal data accurate"],"correct":[0, 1, 3, 4],"why":"Data should not be kept longer than necessary.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Countermeasure match","q":"<p>Match each threat to the BEST countermeasure.</p>","left":["Stolen password","Intercepted Wi-Fi traffic","Malware in a download","Short power cut"],"right":["Multi-factor authentication","Encryption","Antivirus software","UPS"],"why":"Each threat has a targeted defence.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Authentication factors","q":"<p>Match each example to its authentication factor.</p>","left":["PIN","Bank card","Fingerprint","Authenticator app on a phone"],"right":["Something you know","Something you have","Something you are","Something you have (device)"],"why":"Know, have, are.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Countermeasures and CIA","q":"<p>Match each countermeasure to the CIA goal it MAINLY protects.</p>","left":["Encryption","Read-only access to prices","Generator"],"right":["Confidentiality","Integrity","Availability"],"why":"Seeing, changing, using.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Digital citizenship","q":"<p>Tia sees a cruel meme about a classmate being shared in her year-group chat. Which response BEST shows digital citizenship?</p>","options":["Reply with an insult to whoever posted it","Don't share it, support the classmate, report it and tell a trusted adult","Ignore it completely","Share it to her own story"],"correct":1,"why":"Being an upstander: support, report and tell.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Intrusion detection","q":"<p>A bank in Kingston wants a system that watches its network and immediately warns the security team when someone appears to be accessing systems without authorisation. It needs a(n)</p>","options":["UPS","word processor","intrusion detection system","scanner"],"correct":2,"why":"An IDS monitors for unauthorised access and raises alerts.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Archiving","q":"<p>At the end of each school year, the Calabash Point Secondary moves the records of students who have left onto separate storage, in case they are needed in future, and removes them from the everyday system. This is</p>","options":["archiving","phishing","a firewall","encryption"],"correct":0,"why":"Storing data no longer in daily use for future reference = archiving (not the same as a backup).","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Data integrity","q":"<p>A clerk at a Barbados clinic accidentally overwrites a patient's blood group with the wrong value. Which aspect of data has been damaged?</p>","options":["Bandwidth","Data portability","Data integrity","Data compression"],"correct":2,"why":"Integrity means data is accurate and not wrongly changed.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Memory sticks","q":"<p>Why do many organisations, such as a St. Lucia insurance company, discourage staff from moving files on memory sticks?</p>","options":["Memory sticks are illegal","Memory sticks make computers faster","Memory sticks can carry viruses and are easily lost along with sensitive data","Memory sticks cannot store documents"],"correct":2,"why":"They can introduce malware and, if lost, expose the data stored on them.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Security terms","q":"<p>Match each term to its meaning.</p>","left":["Recovery","Archive","Data security","Intrusion detection system"],"right":["Bringing data back from a backup","Stored copy of data no longer in everyday use","Keeping data safe and private from unauthorised access","Warns when unauthorised access is detected"],"why":"Four terms from the study of computer security.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Cloud sync vs backup","q":"<p>A small Antigua business syncs its files to a cloud folder instantly. Ransomware encrypts the office PC and the encrypted files sync to the cloud. What should the business use instead?</p>","options":["A bigger monitor","A second sync folder","A faster Internet connection","Backups that keep previous versions or an offline copy"],"correct":3,"why":"Versioned or offline backups let you restore files from before the attack.","fb":[]}]},{"id":"q_s3u4","title":"Section 3 · Automation, jobs and computer professionals quiz","desc":"<p>Topic quiz for Section 3: Automation, jobs and computer professionals. 35 items.</p>","minutes":0,"items":[{"t":"mc","title":"Automation advantage","q":"<p>A Jamaican factory installs robots that apply paint in a sealed booth, so workers no longer breathe fumes. Which advantage of automation does this show?</p>","options":["Worker displacement","Lower flexibility","Improved safety","Higher capital cost"],"correct":2,"why":"Removing workers from hazards = improved safety.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Automation disadvantage","q":"<p>After its packing line is automated, a Barbados company finds it takes weeks to switch the line to a new product. This shows that automated systems</p>","options":["use more materials","always reduce maintenance","are less flexible than manual systems","never need workers"],"correct":2,"why":"Lower flexibility is a known disadvantage.","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"White-collar automation","q":"<p>Select EVERY office task that AI and automation can now partly perform.</p>","options":["Reviewing legal documents for key clauses","Negotiating a sensitive business deal","Watching security camera feeds for unusual movement","Processing standard insurance claims","Comforting a grieving family"],"correct":[0, 2, 3],"why":"Routine analysis tasks can be automated; empathy and negotiation remain human strengths.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Social media specialist","q":"<p>A Chaguanas resort hires someone to grow its Instagram presence, respond to online reviews and analyse engagement figures. The job title is</p>","options":["database administrator","social media specialist","network engineer","computer engineer"],"correct":1,"why":"Strategy, content, reviews and metrics = social media specialist.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Network engineer vs administrator","q":"<p>Which BEST describes the difference between a network engineer and a network administrator?</p>","options":["The engineer only fixes printers","The administrator designs processors","They are identical jobs","The engineer plans and designs networks; the administrator keeps an existing network running"],"correct":3,"why":"Engineers design and implement; administrators maintain and operate.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Systems analyst duty","q":"<p>Which is a duty of a computer systems analyst?</p>","options":["Replacing toner in printers","Monitoring competitors' Instagram","Defining the problem by conferring with clients and evaluating procedures","Designing the company logo"],"correct":2,"why":"Analysts define problems and design solutions.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Human strengths","q":"<p>Match each human strength to an example machines struggle with.</p>","left":["Social interaction","Creativity","Moral judgement"],"right":["Persuading an upset customer to stay","Designing a new Carnival costume theme","Deciding whether a refund request is fair"],"why":"Areas where humans keep the upper hand.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Software tester","q":"<p>Before a Jamaican bank releases its new mobile app, a team runs it with planned test data to find bugs and report them to the programmers. These professionals are</p>","options":["graphic artists","file librarians","software testers","network administrators"],"correct":2,"why":"Finding bugs using test data = software tester.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Systems programmer","q":"<p>A programmer writes device drivers and parts of an operating system so that new hardware works correctly. This person is a</p>","options":["data analyst","systems programmer","webmaster","trainer"],"correct":1,"why":"Writing system software = systems programmer.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Webmaster","q":"<p>Dominica tourism office employs someone to keep its website running, update events, fix broken links, answer visitor e-mails and check traffic statistics. This person is the</p>","options":["file librarian","systems analyst","computer engineer","webmaster (website administrator)"],"correct":3,"why":"Maintaining a live website = webmaster.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"File librarian","q":"<p>In a large government office in Trinidad, one person is responsible for keeping track of all the organisation's files and storage media, organising backups and restoring lost files. This role is the</p>","options":["multimedia artist","web designer","file librarian","software tester"],"correct":2,"why":"Tracking files and managing backups/restores = file librarian.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Paperless office","q":"<p>A Grenada law firm scans its old files, stores all new documents electronically and shares them by e-mail and the cloud. It is moving towards a</p>","options":["gig economy","paperless office","denial of service","botnet"],"correct":1,"why":"Storing and sharing documents electronically = paperless office.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"More roles","q":"<p>Match each job to its duty.</p>","left":["Multimedia artist","SysAdmin","Applications programmer","Software tester"],"right":["Combines animation, video, images and sound","Keeps the organisation's systems running day to day","Writes useful software such as apps and games","Finds bugs using planned test data"],"why":"Specialised roles in technology.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Automation definition","q":"<p>A supermarket in Montego Bay installs machines that let customers scan and pay for their own shopping without a cashier. This is an example of</p>","options":["automation","telecommuting","the gig economy","plagiarism"],"correct":0,"why":"Machines doing tasks people did = automation.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Job at risk","q":"<p>Which job at a Barbados utility company is MOST likely to disappear when smart meters are installed in every home?</p>","options":["Trainer","Cybersecurity analyst","Network administrator","Meter reader"],"correct":3,"why":"Smart meters send readings automatically, replacing manual reading.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Job creation","q":"<p>A Trinidad bank introduces mobile banking. Which new job is MOST likely to be created?</p>","options":["Mobile app developer","Cheque sorter","Typist","Switchboard operator"],"correct":0,"why":"Building and maintaining the app creates developer jobs.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Retraining","q":"<p>The Caribbean Coastal Bank sends tellers on a course so they can work as digital-services advisers after its branches automate. This is an example of</p>","options":["retraining","telecommuting","deskilling","hacking"],"correct":0,"why":"Learning skills for a new role = retraining (reskilling).","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Telecommuting","q":"<p>Ms. Toussaint, an insurance clerk, works from her home in Saint-Marc using a laptop, video calls and cloud storage. This is called</p>","options":["industrial espionage","telecommuting","outsourcing","automation"],"correct":1,"why":"Working remotely using ICT = telecommuting.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Telecommuting disadvantage","q":"<p>Which is a DISADVANTAGE of telecommuting for the employee?</p>","options":["No commuting costs","Feeling isolated from colleagues","Flexible hours","More time with family"],"correct":1,"why":"Isolation is a common disadvantage; the others are advantages.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Gig economy","q":"<p>Tevin earns money in Jamaica by accepting delivery jobs through an app whenever he chooses, with no fixed employer. He is part of the</p>","options":["systems life cycle","public sector","BPO industry","gig economy"],"correct":3,"why":"Short-term, app-based, flexible work = gig economy.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Gig disadvantage","q":"<p>Which is the MAIN disadvantage of gig work for someone like Tevin?</p>","options":["He needs a smartphone","He can choose his hours","He usually has no sick pay, paid holidays or guaranteed income","He can work for several apps"],"correct":2,"why":"Insecure income and lack of benefits are the key disadvantages.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Systems analyst","q":"<p>A Jamaican hospital hires someone to interview staff, study the current paper-based process and design a new computerised system. This person is a</p>","options":["IT technician","programmer","systems analyst","graphic artist"],"correct":2,"why":"Investigating needs and designing systems = systems analyst.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"DBA","q":"<p>Who in a Guyanese bank would be responsible for backing up the customer database and controlling which staff can access it?</p>","options":["Computer engineer","Web designer","Trainer","Database administrator"],"correct":3,"why":"Backups and access control for databases = DBA.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Network administrator","q":"<p>At Frangipani Hill Academy, who would set up user accounts for new students and keep the school's Wi-Fi and switches working?</p>","options":["Graphic artist","Data analyst","Systems analyst","Network administrator"],"correct":3,"why":"Network and user account management = network administrator.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Cybersecurity analyst","q":"<p>An airline in the Caribbean hires someone to monitor its systems for attacks, investigate suspicious logins and test for vulnerabilities. The job title is</p>","options":["IT manager","cybersecurity analyst","trainer","web designer"],"correct":1,"why":"Monitoring and investigating threats = cybersecurity analyst.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Computer engineer","q":"<p>A company in Barbados needs someone to design the circuit board for a new hurricane weather-sensor device. It should hire a</p>","options":["database administrator","computer engineer","graphic artist","trainer"],"correct":1,"why":"Designing hardware = computer engineer.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Data analyst","q":"<p>The Barbadian tourism office wants someone to study visitor statistics and produce charts showing trends by month and country. Who should it hire?</p>","options":["IT technician","Network administrator","Data analyst","Programmer"],"correct":2,"why":"Analysing data and reporting trends = data analyst.","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"Jobs at risk","q":"<p>Select EVERY job that is at HIGH risk from automation because its tasks are mostly routine.</p>","options":["Cybersecurity analyst","Data entry clerk","Toll booth attendant","Social worker","Supermarket cashier"],"correct":[1, 2, 4],"why":"Data entry, scanning goods and collecting tolls are routine; security analysis and social work need judgement.","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"Telecommuting needs","q":"<p>Select EVERY item a company should provide or require so its staff can telecommute SECURELY.</p>","options":["Reliable Internet connection","Sharing one password among staff","VPN","Encrypted laptops","Multi-factor authentication"],"correct":[0, 2, 3, 4],"why":"VPN, MFA, encryption and connectivity support secure remote work; shared passwords are unsafe.","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"IT manager duties","q":"<p>Select EVERY duty of an IT manager / CIO.</p>","options":["Making sure technology supports business goals","Repairing every jammed printer personally","Setting the IT budget","Managing IT staff","Planning the IT strategy"],"correct":[0, 2, 3, 4],"why":"IT managers plan, budget, lead and align IT with goals; technicians repair printers.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Roles and duties","q":"<p>Match each professional to a key duty.</p>","left":["Programmer","Web designer","IT technician","Trainer"],"right":["Writes and debugs code","Plans website layout and appearance","Repairs hardware and supports users","Teaches users to use new systems"],"why":"Code, look, fix, teach.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Work terms","q":"<p>Match each term to its meaning.</p>","left":["Automation","Telecommuting","Gig economy","Upskilling"],"right":["Machines doing tasks people used to do","Working away from the office using ICT","Short-term jobs found through online platforms","Improving skills for your current job"],"why":"Four key ideas from SO 3.4.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Who to call","q":"<p>Match each problem at a hotel to the professional who should handle it.</p>","left":["Guest database is slow and has duplicates","Wi-Fi drops on the third floor","Suspicious login from overseas","New logo needed for the hotel's website"],"right":["Database administrator","Network administrator","Cybersecurity analyst","Graphic artist"],"why":"Route the problem to the right specialist.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Deskilling","q":"<p>After a factory in Trinidad installs robots, the remaining workers only press start and stop buttons and watch the machines. Their job has become less skilled. This is called</p>","options":["encryption","upskilling","deskilling","telecommuting"],"correct":2,"why":"Jobs that need less skill after automation = deskilling.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Remote work opportunity","q":"<p>Which BEST explains why remote work can be an opportunity for small Caribbean islands?</p>","options":["It removes the need for Internet access","It ends all unemployment","Islanders can work for clients and employers anywhere in the world without emigrating","It guarantees higher pay for everyone"],"correct":2,"why":"Remote work opens global job markets to island residents.","fb":[]}]},{"id":"q_s3u5","title":"Section 3 · ICT in different fields and emerging technologies quiz","desc":"<p>Topic quiz for Section 3: ICT in different fields and emerging technologies. 27 items.</p>","minutes":0,"items":[{"t":"mc","title":"Flipped classroom","q":"<p>At a Grenada secondary school, students watch a short lesson video at home and use class time for group problem-solving with the teacher's help. This approach is called</p>","options":["telecommuting","teleradiology","EPOS","the flipped classroom"],"correct":3,"why":"Lesson at home, practice in class = flipped classroom.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Digital literacy","q":"<p>A Form 4 student can search for information, judge whether a website is reliable and produce a well-referenced presentation. She is showing</p>","options":["the digital divide","biometrics","software piracy","digital literacy"],"correct":3,"why":"Finding, evaluating and producing information = digital literacy.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Education drawback","q":"<p>A teacher in Trinidad notices several essays contain identical paragraphs copied from the same website. Which drawback of ICT in education does this show?</p>","options":["Augmented reality","Process control","Plagiarism","Telemonitoring"],"correct":2,"why":"Easy copying has increased plagiarism.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"EHR","q":"<p>When an unconscious tourist is brought into a Guyanese hospital, doctors can see his allergy list from his home clinic. This benefit comes from</p>","options":["a firewall","EPOS","CAD","electronic health records"],"correct":3,"why":"EHRs can be accessed across facilities.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"MRI","q":"<p>Which medical imaging technology uses strong magnets and radio waves to show soft tissue?</p>","options":["CT","MRI","ANPR","Barcode scanning"],"correct":1,"why":"MRI = magnetic resonance imaging.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Teleradiology","q":"<p>A small hospital in Dominica sends a CT scan to a radiologist in Jamaica at night, who returns a report within the hour. This is</p>","options":["a drive-by download","teleradiology","e-commerce","telecommuting"],"correct":1,"why":"Sending images to a remote radiologist = teleradiology.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Self-diagnosis risk","q":"<p>A man uses an online symptom checker, which suggests a mild virus, so he stays home; he actually has a serious infection. This illustrates which risk of self-diagnosis tools?</p>","options":["Denial of service","Software piracy","Industrial espionage","False reassurance leading to delayed treatment"],"correct":3,"why":"False results can delay proper care.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"B2B","q":"<p>A Barbados hotel orders bulk linen through a supplier's online trade portal. This is</p>","options":["EFT only","telemedicine","C2C e-commerce","B2B e-commerce"],"correct":3,"why":"Business buying from business = B2B.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"EPOS benefit","q":"<p>Which is a key BUSINESS benefit of an EPOS system in a George Town supermarket?</p>","options":["Customers get free groceries","It replaces the need for electricity","Stock levels update automatically with each sale","It diagnoses illnesses"],"correct":2,"why":"Automatic stock control and accurate reporting.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Fingerprinting","q":"<p>Why are fingerprints useful to police as long-term markers of identity?</p>","options":["They can only be taken by doctors","They change every year","They are detailed, nearly unique, hard to alter and last a lifetime","Everyone has the same fingerprints"],"correct":2,"why":"These properties make fingerprints reliable identifiers.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Bugging","q":"<p>Investigators place a tiny microphone in a suspect's car that transmits conversations to a nearby receiver. This type of electronic surveillance is</p>","options":["phishing","bugging","telemonitoring","wiretapping"],"correct":1,"why":"A hidden transmitting microphone = bugging.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Citizen journalism","q":"<p>During flooding in Nassau, residents post videos of the rising water before any reporter arrives. This is</p>","options":["teleradiology","citizen journalism","propaganda","desktop publishing"],"correct":1,"why":"Ordinary people reporting events with their phones.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Music piracy","q":"<p>Which development made music piracy grow rapidly?</p>","options":["Digital formats such as MP3 that are easy to copy and share","Radio broadcasts","Vinyl records","Live concerts"],"correct":0,"why":"Digitisation made copying easy.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Motion sensors","q":"<p>A game lets players in Antigua swing their arms to play virtual cricket without a controller. Which gaming technology is used?</p>","options":["Pharming","EPOS","Motion sensors","Teleradiology"],"correct":2,"why":"Motion sensors detect body movement.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Process control","q":"<p>A Jamaican factory's cold store has sensors that send temperature readings to a computer, which switches the cooling on or off to keep it at 4 °C. This is a</p>","options":["drive-by download","C2C platform","process control system","word processor"],"correct":2,"why":"Sensor feedback controlling a process = process control.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"CAD/CAM","q":"<p>An engineer designs a part on screen and sends the design directly to a machine that cuts it from metal. The design and making stages are</p>","options":["AR and VR","CAD and CAM","HIDS and NIDS","EPOS and EFT"],"correct":1,"why":"Computer-aided design, then computer-aided manufacturing.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Hurricane forecasting","q":"<p>Which combination of technologies is MOST important for predicting a hurricane's track?</p>","options":["EPOS tills and barcode scanners","E-readers and tablets","Satellites, hurricane hunter aircraft data and supercomputer models","3-D printers"],"correct":2,"why":"Observation data plus forecast models.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Expert system","q":"<p>A garage in Georgetown enters a car's symptoms into software that compares them with stored rules and suggests the most likely fault. This software is</p>","options":["a firewall","an expert system","a social media app","a spreadsheet only"],"correct":1,"why":"Diagnosis from stored knowledge and rules = expert system.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"AR vs VR","q":"<p>Tourists point their phones at Fort James and see historical information and images appear over the live camera view. This is</p>","options":["virtual reality","augmented reality","cloud computing","teleradiology"],"correct":1,"why":"AR adds digital content to the real world.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"IoT","q":"<p>Smart water meters across an island send readings automatically over the network and alert the utility to leaks. They are part of</p>","options":["desktop publishing","the Internet of Things","software piracy","the flipped classroom"],"correct":1,"why":"Connected everyday objects with sensors = IoT.","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"Cloud computing","q":"<p>Select EVERY advantage of cloud computing for a small business in Sandy Point.</p>","options":["Access files from any device anywhere","Easy collaboration on shared documents","Little local storage required","Automatic off-site backup","No Internet connection needed"],"correct":[0, 1, 2, 3],"why":"Cloud computing needs an Internet connection.","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"Telemedicine benefits","q":"<p>Select EVERY benefit of telemedicine for island communities.</p>","options":["Fewer people in waiting rooms","Shorter waiting times","Works perfectly without Internet","Lower costs for patients","Access to overseas specialists without travel"],"correct":[0, 1, 3, 4],"why":"Telemedicine depends on connectivity.","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"Drone uses","q":"<p>Select EVERY realistic use of drones in the Turks and Caicos Islands.</p>","options":["Searching for a missing swimmer","Filming a tourism advert of Sapodilla Bay","Replacing all doctors","Surveying roof damage after a hurricane","Inspecting power lines"],"correct":[0, 1, 3, 4],"why":"Drones survey, film, inspect and search.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"E-commerce types","q":"<p>Match each e-commerce type to its example.</p>","left":["B2C","B2B","C2C","C2B"],"right":["A souvenir shop sells T-shirts online to tourists","A restaurant orders from a wholesaler's portal","A student sells a used phone to another person online","A photographer sells photos to a hotel"],"why":"Seller first, buyer second.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Fields and technology","q":"<p>Match each field to a technology it uses.</p>","left":["Medicine","Law enforcement","Education","Business"],"right":["Electronic health records","AFIS fingerprint matching","Interactive whiteboards","EPOS systems"],"why":"Each field has signature technologies.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Emerging technologies","q":"<p>Match each technology to its definition.</p>","left":["3-D printing","Wearable technology","Artificial intelligence","Virtual reality"],"right":["Building objects layer by layer from a digital design","Clothing or accessories with built-in computers","Machines that mimic human problem-solving and learn","A computer-generated world viewed through a headset"],"why":"Key definitions for 3.5.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Digital divide","q":"<p>The government moves all business registrations online. Older shop owners in rural areas without Internet struggle to register. This problem is an example of</p>","options":["e-publishing","the digital divide","pharming","process control"],"correct":1,"why":"Unequal access to ICT and skills = digital divide.","fb":[]}]}]},{"pkg":"Topic Quizzes · Section 4","quizzes":[{"id":"q_s4u1","title":"Section 4 · Creating and formatting documents quiz","desc":"<p>Topic quiz for Section 4: Creating and formatting documents. 25 items.</p>","minutes":0,"items":[{"t":"mc","title":"Text from File","q":"<p>Jalen has typed his SBA introduction and wants to combine his partner's three-page Word file into it without opening it and copying. Which command should he use?</p>","options":["Home ▸ Paste ▸ Picture","File ▸ Open","Insert ▸ Object ▸ Text from File","Insert ▸ Pictures"],"correct":2,"why":"Text from File inserts the whole content of another document at the cursor, combining documents in one step.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Subscript","q":"<p>A Form 5 chemistry teacher at Breadfruit Valley Secondary is typing a worksheet that includes the formula for carbon dioxide. Which feature formats the 2 in CO2 correctly?</p>","options":["Subscript","Strikethrough","Superscript","Small caps"],"correct":0,"why":"The 2 in CO₂ sits below the line, which is subscript (Ctrl+=).","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Superscript","q":"<p>The Oranjestad Realty advertises an apartment of 85 m2. Which formatting should be applied to the 2?</p>","options":["Subscript","Highlight","Bold","Superscript"],"correct":3,"why":"Square metres are written m² with the 2 raised: superscript (Ctrl+Shift+=).","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Orientation","q":"<p>The Turks and Caicos Islands Schools Athletics Association needs to print an A4 certificate that is wider than it is tall. Which page setting should be changed?</p>","options":["Line spacing","Orientation to landscape","Columns","Margins"],"correct":1,"why":"Landscape orientation makes the page wider than it is tall.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Header","q":"<p>Every page of the Kaya Grandi Traders Association report must show the association's name at the top. Which feature is BEST?</p>","options":["Header","Title style on page 1","Footnote","Text box on each page"],"correct":0,"why":"A header appears in the top margin of every page automatically.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Page numbering","q":"<p>Rhea typed “Page 1”, “Page 2” and so on at the bottom of each page of her 12-page report. Then she inserted a table on page 3. What is the MOST likely result?</p>","options":["The table is moved to the end","Word converts them to page number fields","The numbers update automatically","The typed numbers are now in the wrong places"],"correct":3,"why":"Typed numbers are ordinary text and move with the content. Use Insert ▸ Page Number instead.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Hanging indent","q":"<p>In the bibliography of her SBA, Tamika wants the first line of each reference to start at the margin and the following lines to be indented. Which feature does she need?</p>","options":["First-line indent","Hanging indent","Right indent","Justify"],"correct":1,"why":"A hanging indent keeps line 1 at the margin and indents the rest.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Columns or table?","q":"<p>The Liberta Marina must show boat names, lengths and berth numbers so that each boat's details line up across the page. Which feature is MOST appropriate?</p>","options":["Two newspaper columns","A table","Bullets","A page border"],"correct":1,"why":"Data that must line up in rows and columns belongs in a table.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Line spacing","q":"<p>A teacher at Sea-Almond Secondary School asks for essays with a gap the height of a full line between every line, so she can write corrections. Which setting meets this request?</p>","options":["Spacing after 6 pt","Line spacing 1.0","Line spacing 1.15","Line spacing 2.0"],"correct":3,"why":"Double spacing (2.0) leaves a full line's gap between lines.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Paste option","q":"<p>Marcus copies a table of ferry times from a web page and wants it to look exactly as it does on the website, including its colours. Which paste option should he choose?</p>","options":["Picture","Merge Formatting","Keep Text Only","Keep Source Formatting"],"correct":3,"why":"Keep Source Formatting keeps the original fonts, colours and layout.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Endnote","q":"<p>The Caribbean History Society wants all its notes and sources listed together at the very end of its booklet rather than on each page. Which feature should it use?</p>","options":["Endnotes","Comments","Footer","Footnotes"],"correct":0,"why":"Endnotes collect all notes at the end of the document or section.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Styles","q":"<p>Andre formatted 10 headings by hand in 16 pt blue. His teacher asks for green headings. Which feature would have let him change all 10 in one step?</p>","options":["Find and Replace","Format Painter","Page colour","A heading style"],"correct":3,"why":"Modify the style once and every heading using it updates.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Page break","q":"<p>Kemar wants Chapter 2 of his report to always start at the top of a new page, even if he edits Chapter 1. What should he insert before the Chapter 2 heading?</p>","options":["A column break","Several blank lines","A footnote","A page break (Ctrl+Enter)"],"correct":3,"why":"A page break forces a new page however the text above changes.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Scanning","q":"<p>Dangriga Church wants to include its typed 1970 history in a new booklet AND edit the wording. After scanning the pages, what technology turns them into editable text?</p>","options":["Barcode reader","OCR","OMR","MICR"],"correct":1,"why":"Optical Character Recognition converts images of text into editable characters.","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"Paragraph formatting","q":"<p>Select EVERY option that is <b>paragraph</b> formatting.</p>","options":["Centre alignment","Line spacing 1.5","Bold","Font colour","Bullets","First-line indent"],"correct":[0, 1, 4, 5],"why":"Line spacing, alignment, indents and bullets apply to whole paragraphs. Bold and font colour are character formatting.","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"Page formatting","q":"<p>The Dushi Power & Water customer newsletter is being set up. Select EVERY option that is <b>page</b> (layout) formatting.</p>","options":["Margins","Orientation","Paper size","Subscript","Hanging indent","Columns"],"correct":[0, 1, 2, 5],"why":"Margins, orientation, size and columns are set on the Layout tab and apply to a page or section.","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"Inserting content","q":"<p>Select EVERY item that can be added through the Insert tab in Word.</p>","options":["A table","A chart","A picture from the computer","A header","Justify","Line spacing"],"correct":[0, 1, 2, 3],"why":"Charts, pictures, headers and tables are on Insert. Line spacing and justify are on Home ▸ Paragraph.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Shortcuts","q":"<p>Match each keyboard shortcut to what it does in Word.</p>","left":["Ctrl+Enter","Ctrl+E","Ctrl+=","Ctrl+Shift+=","Ctrl+J"],"right":["Page break","Centre alignment","Subscript","Superscript","Justify"],"why":"These are the standard Word shortcuts for breaks, alignment and raised/lowered text.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Breaks","q":"<p>Match each break to its best use.</p>","left":["Page break","Column break","Section break (Next Page)","Section break (Continuous)"],"right":["Start the conclusion on a new page","Move text to the top of the next newsletter column","Make one page landscape in a portrait report","Put a full-width headline above two columns on the same page"],"why":"Only section breaks allow different layout settings; continuous keeps you on the same page.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Formatting layers","q":"<p>Match each feature to its formatting layer.</p>","left":["Italic","Hanging indent","Margins","Heading 1"],"right":["Character formatting","Paragraph formatting","Page formatting","Style (saved formatting)"],"why":"Character = letters, paragraph = blocks, page = layout, style = a named bundle of formatting.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Merge cells","q":"<p>St. Kitts–Nevis Ferry timetable table needs the title “Weekend Sailings” across the full width of its top row of four cells. What should be done?</p>","options":["Merge cells","AutoFit contents","Insert column","Split cells"],"correct":0,"why":"Merging the four top cells makes one wide cell for the title.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Decimal tab","q":"<p>The Wallblake Road Café menu lists prices such as $4.50, $12.75 and $105.00 down the right of the page. Which tab stop lines the prices up on the decimal point?</p>","options":["Centre tab","Decimal tab","Left tab","Bar tab"],"correct":1,"why":"A decimal tab stop aligns numbers on their decimal point.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Hard return","q":"<p>Kayla presses Enter at the end of every line while typing a paragraph. When she later changes the margins, her lines break in strange places. What should she have done?</p>","options":["Typed in Overtype mode","Used a page break after each line","Let word wrap move text to the next line and pressed Enter only at the end of the paragraph","Pressed Tab at the end of each line"],"correct":2,"why":"Word wrap adjusts automatically; hard returns at the end of every line do not.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Fully justified","q":"<p>A newsletter column for the Barbados Youth Choir has straight left AND right edges. This alignment is called</p>","options":["left-justified","centred","right-justified","fully justified"],"correct":3,"why":"Fully justified text adjusts word spacing so both margins are straight.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Appropriate formatting","q":"<p>Which formatting choice is MOST appropriate for a formal letter from Coralstone College's principal to parents?</p>","options":["Three decorative fonts and clip art","All text centred in bold","Yellow text on a white background","One clear font at 11–12 pt with the school letterhead in the header"],"correct":3,"why":"Formal letters need clear, consistent, professional formatting.","fb":[]}]},{"id":"q_s4u2","title":"Section 4 · Editing, reviewing and protecting documents quiz","desc":"<p>Topic quiz for Section 4: Editing, reviewing and protecting documents. 24 items.</p>","minutes":0,"items":[{"t":"mc","title":"Cut vs delete","q":"<p>Sasha selects a paragraph in her report on Corozal Market and presses the Delete key, intending to paste it on page 4. What happens when she presses Ctrl+V on page 4?</p>","options":["Word asks where the paragraph should go","Nothing from that paragraph is pasted, because Delete does not place text on the clipboard","The paragraph appears on page 4","The paragraph is restored to its original place"],"correct":1,"why":"Delete removes text without copying it to the clipboard. She should have used Cut (Ctrl+X).","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Drag to copy","q":"<p>Tyrese wants a COPY of a table of ferry prices further down the same page, using the mouse only. What should he do?</p>","options":["Hold Shift while clicking the table","Hold Ctrl while dragging the selected table","Double-click the table","Drag the selected table to the new place"],"correct":1,"why":"Dragging moves; holding Ctrl while dragging copies.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Format Painter","q":"<p>The Anguilla Ferry Co. timetable has one destination formatted perfectly. Nia wants to apply the same formatting to nine other destinations scattered around the page without clicking the button nine times. What should she do?</p>","options":["Copy and paste the formatted destination nine times","Double-click Format Painter, paint all nine, then press Esc","Use Find and Replace","Single-click Format Painter"],"correct":1,"why":"Double-clicking locks the Format Painter on until you press Esc.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Find whole words","q":"<p>A teacher replaces every “art” with “visual arts” in a timetable. Now the timetable says “visual artsists” and “stvisual arts”. Which option would have prevented this?</p>","options":["Sounds like","Match case","Find whole words only","Use wildcards"],"correct":2,"why":"Whole words only stops matches inside longer words such as “artists” and “start”.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Undo","q":"<p>Devon accidentally applies bold to his entire 10-page SBA report. What is the quickest fix?</p>","options":["Retype the report","Press Ctrl+Z","Press Ctrl+Y","Close without saving and lose today's work"],"correct":1,"why":"Undo (Ctrl+Z) reverses the last action.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Word count","q":"<p>An essay competition run by the Caribbean Youth Writers' Network has a 750-word limit. Where can Keisha find the exact number of words in her essay?</p>","options":["Layout ▸ Columns","Home ▸ Paragraph","Review ▸ Word Count or the status bar","Insert ▸ Header"],"correct":2,"why":"Word count is on the status bar and in Review ▸ Word Count.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Homophones","q":"<p>Which error would a spelling checker MOST likely fail to detect?</p>","options":["Teh ferry leaves at 7 a.m. from Marigot.","The ferry leaves at 7 a.m. frm Marigot.","The ferry leavs at 7 a.m. from Marigot.","The ferry leaves at 7 a.m. form Marigot."],"correct":3,"why":"“form” is a real word used wrongly, so it passes the dictionary check.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Thesaurus","q":"<p>In a speech for Curaçao Youth Parliament, Jaden used “important” seven times. Which tool helps him find alternatives?</p>","options":["Word Count","Thesaurus (Shift+F7)","Find and Replace","Track Changes"],"correct":1,"why":"The thesaurus suggests synonyms such as vital, essential or significant.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Comment","q":"<p>Ms. Hodge wants to ask a student whether a statistic in his report is from a reliable source, without changing his text. What should she use?</p>","options":["Mark as Final","Find and Replace","A comment","Track Changes"],"correct":2,"why":"A comment adds a note in the margin without altering the text.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Track Changes display","q":"<p>With Track Changes on, Kerry-Ann deletes the word “Friday” and types “Saturday”. In All Markup view, how do these changes usually appear?</p>","options":["Both words disappear","Both words are highlighted yellow","Friday is struck through; Saturday is underlined","Friday is underlined; Saturday is struck through"],"correct":2,"why":"Deletions are struck through (or in a balloon); insertions are underlined.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Reject","q":"<p>A reviewer changed “Brades” to “Braddes” in a tracked document. What should the author do with this change?</p>","options":["Reject it","Add it to the dictionary","Mark the document as final","Accept it"],"correct":0,"why":"The change introduces a spelling error, so reject it to restore the original.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Password to open","q":"<p>The Lelydorp Construction keeps a document of staff salaries. Only the director should be able to open it. Which feature is MOST suitable?</p>","options":["Mark as Final","Read-only recommended","Encrypt with Password","Track Changes"],"correct":2,"why":"Encryption with a password prevents anyone without it from opening the file.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Filling in forms","q":"<p>The Mahoe Grove High School PTA sends a Word form where parents tick boxes and type names but must not alter the questions. Which protection should be used?</p>","options":["Mark as Final","Encrypt with Password","Restrict Editing ▸ Filling in forms","Restrict Editing ▸ Comments"],"correct":2,"why":"Filling in forms allows input only in form fields/controls.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Mark as Final","q":"<p>Which statement about Mark as Final is TRUE?</p>","options":["It stops anyone from opening the document","It records every edit made","It discourages editing, but users can choose Edit Anyway","It encrypts the document"],"correct":2,"why":"Mark as Final is a weak, advisory protection.","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"Block operations","q":"<p>Select EVERY action that places the selected text on the clipboard.</p>","options":["Ctrl+C","Backspace","Delete","Copy","Cut","Ctrl+X"],"correct":[0, 3, 4, 5],"why":"Cut and Copy (and their shortcuts) place text on the clipboard; Delete and Backspace do not.","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"Review tab","q":"<p>Select EVERY feature found on Word's Review tab.</p>","options":["Page Borders","Columns","Track Changes","New Comment","Thesaurus","Spelling & Grammar"],"correct":[2, 3, 4, 5],"why":"Page Borders is on Design; Columns is on Layout.","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"Protect Document","q":"<p>Select EVERY option found under File ▸ Info ▸ Protect Document.</p>","options":["Mark as Final","Word Count","Restrict Editing","Encrypt with Password","Always Open Read-Only","Format Painter"],"correct":[0, 2, 3, 4],"why":"Format Painter and Word Count are editing/review tools, not protection.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Shortcuts","q":"<p>Match each shortcut to its action.</p>","left":["Ctrl+H","Ctrl+Z","Ctrl+Y","F7","Shift+F7"],"right":["Replace","Undo","Redo","Spelling & Grammar","Thesaurus"],"why":"Standard Word editing and proofing shortcuts.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Protection strength","q":"<p>Match each protection feature to what it controls.</p>","left":["Encrypt with Password","Password to modify","Restrict Editing ▸ Comments","Mark as Final"],"right":["Who can open the file at all","Who can save changes to the file","Readers can only add comments","Only discourages editing"],"why":"Each feature controls a different level of access.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Review features","q":"<p>Match each review feature to its purpose.</p>","left":["Comment","Track Changes","Accept","Reject"],"right":["A note that does not change the text","Records insertions and deletions","Makes a suggested change permanent","Removes a suggested change and restores the original"],"why":"Comments suggest; Track Changes records; Accept and Reject decide.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Overtype","q":"<p>While correcting “Paramaribo” in a letter, Ana notices that every letter she types replaces the letter to the right of the cursor. Which mode is on?</p>","options":["Overtype mode","Track Changes","Insert mode","Caps Lock"],"correct":0,"why":"In Overtype mode new characters replace existing ones; Insert mode pushes text along.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"AutoRecover","q":"<p>A power cut hits the Santiago de los Caballeros Library while Dario is typing. When he restarts Word, it offers a recovered version of his document. Which feature made this possible?</p>","options":["Format Painter","Restrict Editing","AutoRecover","Mark as Final"],"correct":2,"why":"AutoRecover saves recovery information at intervals (10 minutes by default).","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Backspace vs Delete","q":"<p>The cursor is between the letters “Ma” and “rigot”. Which key removes the letter “a”?</p>","options":["Tab","Delete","Backspace","Insert"],"correct":2,"why":"Backspace deletes to the left of the cursor; Delete removes to the right.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Order of work","q":"<p>A student is about to e-mail her finished SBA report. Which should she do LAST?</p>","options":["Run the spelling check","Protect the document","Proofread the document","Accept or reject all tracked changes"],"correct":1,"why":"Protection comes last; you cannot easily review or edit a protected document.","fb":[]}]},{"id":"q_s4u3","title":"Section 4 · Table of contents, mail merge and fillable forms quiz","desc":"<p>Topic quiz for Section 4: Table of contents, mail merge and fillable forms. 21 items.</p>","minutes":0,"items":[{"t":"mc","title":"TOC source","q":"<p>Simone wants Word to generate a TOC for her 18-page report on the English Harbour Fish Fry. What must she do to her headings first?</p>","options":["Put them in a table","Make them bold and 16 pt","Apply heading styles such as Heading 1 and Heading 2","Number them by hand"],"correct":2,"why":"Word builds a TOC from heading styles, not from appearance.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Update entire table","q":"<p>After generating a TOC, Marlon added a new section called “Interviews”. Which TOC update option should he choose?</p>","options":["No update is needed","Update entire table","Delete the TOC","Update page numbers only"],"correct":1,"why":"Adding or renaming headings requires the entire table to be updated.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"TOC location","q":"<p>Where is the Table of Contents command found in Microsoft Word?</p>","options":["Mailings tab","References tab","Insert tab","Review tab"],"correct":1,"why":"References ▸ Table of Contents.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Primary document","q":"<p>In a mail merge for the Jolly Harbour Yacht Club's annual dinner invitations, what is the primary document?</p>","options":["The printed invitations","The envelope labels","The Excel list of members","The invitation containing standard text and merge fields"],"correct":3,"why":"The primary (main) document holds the standard text and the merge fields.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Data source","q":"<p>The Falmouth Dental Clinic keeps patient names and addresses in an Access table that it uses for appointment reminder letters. In the mail merge, the Access table is the</p>","options":["data source","template","primary document","merge field"],"correct":0,"why":"The table of variable details is the data source.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Record","q":"<p>In a data source of 500 APUA-style utility customers, one row containing a single customer's details is called a</p>","options":["field","header","record","merge field"],"correct":2,"why":"One row = one record.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Separate fields","q":"<p>A data source stores full names in one field (“Ms. Shanice Hodge”). Why is this a problem for the greeting “Dear Ms. Hodge,”?</p>","options":["The letter will be too long","Mail merge only works with numbers","Word cannot read names","The title and surname cannot be inserted separately"],"correct":3,"why":"Separate Title, FirstName and LastName fields give flexible greetings and sorting.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Filter AND","q":"<p>A filter is set to District Equal to English Harbour AND Balance Greater than 0. Which customer is included?</p>","options":["English Harbour, balance 0.00","English Harbour, balance 25.00","St. John’s, balance 0.00","Jolly Harbour, balance 50.00"],"correct":1,"why":"With AND, both conditions must be true.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Filter OR","q":"<p>The Dickenson Bay Ferry Co. wants letters for passengers from Barbuda or Montserrat. Which filter is correct?</p>","options":["Island Not equal to Barbuda","Island Is blank","Island = Barbuda OR Island = Montserrat","Island = Barbuda AND Island = Montserrat"],"correct":2,"why":"A passenger can only have one home island, so OR is needed.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Labels","q":"<p>Kezia set up the first mailing label with Address Block. The other labels are still empty apart from «Next Record». What should she click?</p>","options":["Greeting Line","Update Labels","Finish & Merge","Preview Results"],"correct":1,"why":"Update Labels copies the first label's layout to all other labels.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Preview","q":"<p>Before printing 400 letters, why should the PTA secretary use Preview Results?</p>","options":["It sends the letters by e-mail","It protects the document","It sorts the recipients","It shows actual data in the letter so errors can be fixed before merging"],"correct":3,"why":"Preview shows real records so problems such as missing spaces are caught.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Developer tab","q":"<p>Andre cannot find the Check Box and Drop-Down List controls to build a club sign-up form. What should he do?</p>","options":["Insert ▸ Table","Mailings ▸ Start Mail Merge","File ▸ Options ▸ Customize Ribbon ▸ tick Developer","Review ▸ Track Changes"],"correct":2,"why":"Form controls are on the Developer tab, which is hidden by default.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Drop-down","q":"<p>A sign-up form for Antiguan Science Fair asks students to choose their school from five options. Which control is BEST?</p>","options":["Plain Text","Picture","Drop-Down List","Date Picker"],"correct":2,"why":"A drop-down limits answers to valid choices and prevents spelling variations.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Form protection","q":"<p>Which setting allows users to complete form controls but not change any other part of the document?</p>","options":["Restrict Editing ▸ Filling in forms","Restrict Editing ▸ Comments","Mark as Final","Encrypt with Password"],"correct":0,"why":"Filling in forms locks everything except the controls.","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"Mail merge outputs","q":"<p>Select EVERY item that Word's mail merge can produce.</p>","options":["Envelopes","A database query","Mailing labels","A pivot table","E-mail messages","Personalised letters"],"correct":[0, 2, 4, 5],"why":"Word merges to letters, labels, envelopes, e-mails and directories.","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"Data sources","q":"<p>Select EVERY file type or list that can be used as a mail merge data source.</p>","options":["An Excel worksheet","An MP3 file","A JPEG image","An Access table","A CSV file","A Word table"],"correct":[0, 3, 4, 5],"why":"Any structured table of records can be a data source; audio and images cannot.","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"Good data source","q":"<p>Select EVERY feature of a well-prepared data source.</p>","options":["Blank rows between records","Consistent spelling of districts","Separate fields for first and last names","Field names in the first row","One record per row"],"correct":[1, 2, 3, 4],"why":"Blank rows break the list; everything else helps the merge.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Form controls","q":"<p>Match each form control to its best use.</p>","left":["Check Box","Drop-Down List","Date Picker","Combo Box","Plain Text"],"right":["Yes/No: needs transport?","Choose one T-shirt size","Date of birth","Choose a school or type your own","Full name"],"why":"Each control suits a type of answer and prevents certain errors.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Merge steps","q":"<p>Match each Mailings command to what it does.</p>","left":["Start Mail Merge","Select Recipients","Insert Merge Field","Finish & Merge"],"right":["Choose letters, labels or envelopes","Connect the data source","Place a placeholder in the document","Produce the merged documents"],"why":"These follow the left-to-right order of the Mailings tab.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Unit tools","q":"<p>Match each problem to the tool that solves it.</p>","left":["Navigate a long report","Personalise 300 letters","Collect consistent answers","Copy first label to all"],"right":["Table of contents","Mail merge","Fillable form","Update Labels"],"why":"Each feature solves a different repetitive task.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Template","q":"<p>The St. John’s Library wants every member to start with a fresh, blank copy of its protected form. How should it save the form?</p>","options":["As a Word template (.dotx)","As a PDF","As a plain text file","Marked as final"],"correct":0,"why":"Opening a template creates a new document based on it, leaving the original blank.","fb":[]}]},{"id":"q_s4u4","title":"Section 4 · Planning and creating web pages quiz","desc":"<p>Topic quiz for Section 4: Planning and creating web pages. 33 items.</p>","minutes":0,"items":[{"t":"mc","title":"Planning first","q":"<p>Before building a website for St. Kitts Heritage Trust, which TWO things should be decided first?</p>","options":["The number of images","The font and background colour","The purpose and target audience","The domain name and hosting company"],"correct":2,"why":"Purpose and audience drive every other design decision.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Site map","q":"<p>Which planning tool shows all the pages of a website and how they are linked?</p>","options":["Content list","Wireframe","Style sheet","Site map"],"correct":3,"why":"A site map shows pages and links; a wireframe shows one page's layout.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Wireframe","q":"<p>Jamal sketches boxes labelled Logo, Menu, Main text, Photo and Footer for the Contact page of the Barbados Surf School site. What has he produced?</p>","options":["A wireframe (storyboard)","A site map","An HTML file","A domain name"],"correct":0,"why":"A wireframe sketches one page's layout.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Home page file","q":"<p>Which file name should be used for the home page of a website so the server opens it automatically?</p>","options":["home page.HTML","main page.htm","Start.doc","index.html"],"correct":3,"why":"Servers look for index.html by default; lowercase, no spaces.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Hierarchical","q":"<p>The Antigua Sailing Week site has a home page linking to Races, Results, Parties and Contact, and Results links to one page per day. Which navigation structure is this?</p>","options":["Random","Linear","Hierarchical","Peer-to-peer"],"correct":2,"why":"A tree from the home page down is hierarchical.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Linear","q":"<p>An online tutorial for the Guyana Coding Club moves from Lesson 1 to Lesson 2 to Lesson 3 using Next and Back buttons only. Which structure is this?</p>","options":["Networked","Star","Hierarchical","Linear"],"correct":3,"why":"Sequential pages with Next/Back are linear.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Consistency","q":"<p>Why should the navigation bar appear in the same place on every page of the Trinidad Steelband Festival site?</p>","options":["It makes the files smaller","It makes the site load faster","Search engines require it","Visitors always know where to find links and do not get lost"],"correct":3,"why":"Consistent navigation improves usability.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Alt text purpose","q":"<p>What is the MAIN purpose of alt text on an image?</p>","options":["To compress the image","To link the image to another page","To describe the image for screen readers and when it fails to load","To make the image larger"],"correct":2,"why":"Alt text is the text alternative to the image.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Best alt text","q":"<p>Which is the BEST alt text for a photo on the Jamaica Coffee Farm site?</p>","options":["Image of an image of coffee","IMG_4471.jpg","photo","Farm workers picking ripe red coffee cherries on a hillside"],"correct":3,"why":"Good alt text briefly describes what matters in the image.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Title element","q":"<p>The text “Hellshire Beach Eco Club” appears on the browser tab. Which HTML element sets this?</p>","options":["body","title","h1","header"],"correct":1,"why":"The title element in the head sets the tab text.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Image format","q":"<p>Which image format is MOST suitable for a photograph of a Carnival parade on a web page?</p>","options":["TXT","GIF","BMP","JPEG"],"correct":3,"why":"JPEG compresses photographs efficiently.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Logo format","q":"<p>The Les Cayes Bakery logo has a transparent background and sharp edges. Which format suits it best?</p>","options":["CSV","MP4","JPEG","PNG"],"correct":3,"why":"PNG supports transparency and keeps edges sharp (SVG also suits logos).","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Optimise images","q":"<p>A page for the Liberta Marina loads slowly on phones because of its photos. What is the BEST fix?</p>","options":["Resize and compress the photos before uploading","Remove all text","Use a longer domain name","Add more photos"],"correct":0,"why":"Smaller image files download faster.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Builder vs code","q":"<p>The Palmetto Point Secondary PTA has no budget and no one who can code, and needs a site this week. Which approach is MOST suitable?</p>","options":["Hire a software engineering firm","Save a spreadsheet as a web page","Hand-code HTML and CSS","A free online website builder with a template"],"correct":3,"why":"A builder needs no coding and includes hosting and templates.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Website vs web page","q":"<p>Which statement BEST distinguishes a website from a web page, using Antigua Carnival Committee as an example?</p>","options":["A web page is one document shown in a browser; the website is the collection of linked pages under the committee's domain","A website is one document; a web page is a collection of documents","A website is stored on your computer; a web page is stored on a server","They mean exactly the same thing"],"correct":0,"why":"One page versus a collection of related pages owned by one organisation under one domain.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Upload","q":"<p>Keon copies his finished pages from his laptop to the hosting company's server so the public can see them. This is called</p>","options":["uploading","browsing","streaming","downloading"],"correct":0,"why":"Sending files from your computer to a server is uploading.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Domain names","q":"<p>Which organisation oversees the system of domain names and top-level domains such as .gd and .com worldwide?</p>","options":["The World Health Organization","ICANN","CXC","Camerhogne Power & Water"],"correct":1,"why":"ICANN coordinates domain names and delegates top-level domains to registries.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Home vs index page","q":"<p>On the Nevis Heritage Trust site, the browser first downloads index.html, which immediately redirects to welcome.html, the main page with the introduction and links. Which statement is correct?</p>","options":["index.html is the index page (first page downloaded); welcome.html is the home page (main page)","Neither is a home page","Both are index pages","index.html is the home page and welcome.html is the index page"],"correct":0,"why":"The index page is the first page downloaded; the home page is the main page. Usually they are the same, but not here.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Web host","q":"<p>Which BEST defines a web host?</p>","options":["A protocol for uploading files","Software that displays web pages","A person or company that provides space on a web server for your website","The first page of a website"],"correct":2,"why":"The host is the provider; the server is the computer.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"URL dissection","q":"<p>Consider the URL http://www.nevispages.com/news.htm. What is the domain name?</p>","options":["www.nevispages.com/news.htm","http://www.nevispages.com","nevispages.com","news.htm"],"correct":2,"why":"The domain name is nevispages.com; www names the server and is not part of the domain name; news.htm is the page file; .com is the extension.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"FTP vs HTTP","q":"<p>When publishing and viewing the Montserrat Volcano Tours site, which protocols are normally used?</p>","options":["FTP to upload pages to the server, HTTP/HTTPS to download pages into a browser","SMTP for both","FTP for both","HTTP to upload pages, FTP to view them"],"correct":0,"why":"FTP transfers files up to the server; HTTP fetches pages for viewing.","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"Reasons for a website","q":"<p>Select EVERY main reason, from the course notes, for creating a website.</p>","options":["To inform","To create an online community","To defragment the hard disk","To sell or promote","To increase the computer's RAM"],"correct":[0, 1, 3],"why":"Inform, sell/promote and create an online community.","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"Uploading","q":"<p>Select EVERY item that MUST be uploaded when publishing the Grenada Chocolate Festival website.</p>","options":["The authoring software","The images on the pages","The pages that were created","The web browser","Any files visitors can download"],"correct":[1, 2, 4],"why":"Pages, images and downloadable files go to the server; software stays on your computer.","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"Server access","q":"<p>Select EVERY piece of information you MUST have before gaining access to your web server.</p>","options":["Your username","Your password","The visitor's IP address","The domain name (server address)","The colour scheme"],"correct":[0, 1, 3],"why":"Domain name, username and password.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Domain extensions","q":"<p>Match each domain name extension to the type of organisation it traditionally indicates.</p>","left":[".com",".org",".edu",".gov",".int"],"right":["A commercial business","A non-commercial organisation","An educational institution","A government agency","An international organisation"],"why":"Traditional meanings of top-level domains.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Publishing steps","q":"<p>Match each step number to the correct step in publishing a website.</p>","left":["Step 1","Step 2","Step 3","Step 4"],"right":["Create the website","Register a domain name","Select a web host","Upload the files"],"why":"Create, register, select a host, upload.","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"Page areas","q":"<p>Select EVERY item that usually belongs in a web page's footer.</p>","options":["Copyright notice","The large logo","The main article","Social media links","Contact e-mail","Date last updated"],"correct":[0, 3, 4, 5],"why":"The main article goes in the content area; the main logo in the header.","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"Planning considerations","q":"<p>Select EVERY item that is part of planning a website.</p>","options":["Running a virus scan","Identifying the purpose","Sketching wireframes","Identifying the target audience","Drawing a site map"],"correct":[1, 2, 3, 4],"why":"A virus scan is a security task, not website planning.","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"Good design","q":"<p>Select EVERY good web design practice.</p>","options":["Seven different fonts","High contrast between text and background","Alt text on images","Autoplaying music that cannot be stopped","Two or three consistent colours"],"correct":[1, 2, 4],"why":"Many fonts and autoplay audio harm usability.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"HTML tags","q":"<p>Match each HTML element to its purpose.</p>","left":["p","ul","li","img","td"],"right":["A paragraph","A bulleted (unordered) list","One item in a list","An image","A data cell in a table"],"why":"Basic HTML elements.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Planning tools","q":"<p>Match each planning item to what it shows.</p>","left":["Site map","Wireframe","Style plan","Content list"],"right":["How all the pages link together","The layout of one page","Colours, fonts and logo use","The text and media for each page"],"why":"Each planning document answers a different question.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Publishing terms","q":"<p>Match each term to its meaning.</p>","left":["Web hosting","Domain name","FTP","Maintenance"],"right":["Space on a server always connected to the Internet","A readable address such as carlislebayeco.bb","A protocol for uploading files to a server","Updating content and fixing links after publishing"],"why":"Publishing vocabulary.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Heading tag","q":"<p>Which line of HTML creates the MOST important heading on a page about Montserrat Volcano Tours?</p><p>A) h1 element  B) h6 element  C) head element  D) title element</p>","options":["A","B","C","D"],"correct":0,"why":"h1 is the top-level heading; head and title are not visible headings.","fb":[]}]},{"id":"q_s4u5","title":"Section 4 · Hyperlinks and evaluating websites quiz","desc":"<p>Topic quiz for Section 4: Hyperlinks and evaluating websites. 26 items.</p>","minutes":0,"items":[{"t":"mc","title":"Anchor element","q":"<p>Which HTML element is used to create every type of hyperlink on the Guyana Rainforest Tours website?</p>","options":["the table element","the title element","the img element","the a (anchor) element"],"correct":3,"why":"Links use the anchor element with an href attribute.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"href","q":"<p>In the anchor element, what does the href attribute specify?</p>","options":["The colour of the link","The text the visitor sees","Where the link goes","The size of the page"],"correct":2,"why":"href is the hypertext reference: the link's destination.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"mailto","q":"<p>The Antigua Sailing Club wants a link that opens a new e-mail to its secretary. The href must start with</p>","options":["ftp://","mailto:","https://","#"],"correct":1,"why":"mailto: opens the visitor's e-mail program.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Bookmark","q":"<p>A long FAQ page for the St. Lucia Jazz Festival has a heading with id=\"tickets\". Which href jumps to it from the top of the same page?</p>","options":["files/tickets","#tickets","mailto:tickets","tickets.html"],"correct":1,"why":"A hash followed by the id targets a bookmark on the same page.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Case sensitivity","q":"<p>A bookmark link uses href=\"#Contact\" but the target has id=\"contact\". What is the MOST likely result?</p>","options":["The link works perfectly","The link does not jump to the section","The page is deleted","An e-mail opens"],"correct":1,"why":"Fragment identifiers must match exactly, including case.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Word links","q":"<p>In Microsoft Word, which shortcut opens the Insert Link dialog?</p>","options":["Ctrl+B","Ctrl+K","Ctrl+H","Ctrl+L"],"correct":1,"why":"Ctrl+K opens Insert ▸ Link.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Word bookmark","q":"<p>In Word's Insert Link dialog, which option links to a bookmark or heading in the same document?</p>","options":["E-mail Address","Place in This Document","Existing File or Web Page","Create New Document"],"correct":1,"why":"Place in This Document lists bookmarks and headings.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"User-created file","q":"<p>Grenada Spice Co-op links its price list, which it made in Excel, from its website. This is a link to</p>","options":["another web page","a user-created file","a bookmark","an e-mail address"],"correct":1,"why":"A file the organisation created is a user-created file.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Thumbnail","q":"<p>On the Dominica Hiking Club gallery page, each small picture opens a large version when tapped. The small pictures are called</p>","options":["headers","favicons","thumbnails","bookmarks"],"correct":2,"why":"Thumbnails are small images that act as links.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"404 after upload","q":"<p>A link to files/menu.pdf worked on Tia's laptop but gives “404 Not Found” on the published site. What is the MOST likely cause?</p>","options":["The browser does not support PDF links","The PDF was not uploaded to the files folder on the server","The link text is too long","The page has no title"],"correct":1,"why":"The file must be uploaded to the location the link points to.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Link text","q":"<p>Which link text is BEST for accessibility on the Belize Reef Trust site?</p>","options":["Download the 2026 reef survey report (PDF)","https://example.org/x7f2","More","Click here"],"correct":0,"why":"Link text should describe the destination even when read on its own.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Evaluation: accuracy","q":"<p>While evaluating the Bahamas Weather Watch site, a student notes that the page shows when it was last updated and names the meteorological office as its source. Which criterion does this support?</p>","options":["Display on devices","Accuracy","Load time","Accessibility"],"correct":1,"why":"Date and source help judge whether information is accurate and current.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Evaluation: devices","q":"<p>The Curaçao Dive Centre site needs sideways scrolling on a phone and its text is tiny. Which criterion is weak?</p>","options":["Copyright","Authority","Accuracy","Display on devices"],"correct":3,"why":"The layout does not adapt to small screens.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Load time","q":"<p>Which change would MOST improve the load time of a home page containing eight 5 MB photos?</p>","options":["Using a longer domain name","Adding background music","Resizing and compressing the photos","Adding alt text"],"correct":2,"why":"Smaller image files download faster.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Testing","q":"<p>When should a website be tested?</p>","options":["Never, if a builder is used","Only before it is built","During building, before publishing and again after publishing","Only after it is published"],"correct":2,"why":"Some problems only appear after upload, so test at every stage.","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"Four link types","q":"<p>Select EVERY hyperlink type named in the CSEC syllabus.</p>","options":["To another web page","To a user-created file","To a printer","To a location on the same page","To the computer's RAM","To an e-mail address"],"correct":[0, 1, 3, 5],"why":"The four required types.","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"Accessibility checks","q":"<p>Select EVERY check that evaluates a website's accessibility.</p>","options":["Images have meaningful alt text","The domain ends in .com","Videos have captions","Text contrasts strongly with the background","All links can be reached with the Tab key"],"correct":[0, 2, 3, 4],"why":"The domain has nothing to do with accessibility.","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"Load time factors","q":"<p>Select EVERY factor that can slow a page's load time.</p>","options":["Good alt text","A slow mobile connection","Large uncompressed images","Many separate files to download","Autoplaying video"],"correct":[1, 2, 3, 4],"why":"Alt text adds only a few bytes.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Anchor text","q":"<p>On the Tobago Turtle Trust page, the words “Adopt a turtle” are underlined and link to adopt.html. What are these clickable words called?</p>","options":["The URL","The domain name","The anchor text","The index page"],"correct":2,"why":"The text that is linked from is the anchor text.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Checking before upload","q":"<p>Which is the BEST reason to view a new page in a web browser on your own computer before uploading it?</p>","options":["It makes the page load faster online","It registers the domain name","Some features only become apparent when the page is viewed in a browser","Browsers fix spelling errors"],"correct":2,"why":"Previewing in a browser reveals problems before visitors see them.","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"Ways to check a page","q":"<p>Select EVERY way of checking what a page will look like on the Web.</p>","options":["Upload it and view it in a browser","Open it in a browser on your own computer","Print the HTML code","Use the authoring tool's Preview tab","Run a virus scan"],"correct":[0, 1, 3],"why":"Preview, local browser and the final online check.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Traditional link colours","q":"<p>Traditionally, which two colours did browsers use for unvisited and visited hyperlinks?</p>","options":["Black and grey","Yellow and orange","Red and green","Blue and magenta (purple)"],"correct":3,"why":"Blue for links and magenta/purple for visited links, usually underlined.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Test audience","q":"<p>How can the Barbados Senior Citizens' Club check that its new website suits its intended audience?</p>","options":["Invite some club members to try the site and observe their reactions","Ask the web designer's teenage friends","Change the domain extension","Count the number of pages"],"correct":0,"why":"Test with people who fit the intended audience and watch how they use it.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Link code","q":"<p>Match each href to its link type.</p>","left":["about.html","#faq","mailto:info@example.tt","files/rota.docx"],"right":["Another web page","A bookmark on the same page","An e-mail address","A user-created file"],"why":"The start and end of the href reveal the type.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Evaluation criteria","q":"<p>Match each evaluation criterion to the question it asks.</p>","left":["Accuracy","Usability","Display on devices","Accessibility","Load time"],"right":["Is the information correct and current?","Can visitors find what they need easily?","Does it work on phones and laptops?","Can people with disabilities use it?","How quickly does it appear?"],"why":"The five evaluation criteria.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Testing","q":"<p>Match each test to what it checks.</p>","left":["Click every link","View on a phone","Use Tab and Enter only","Open in two browsers"],"right":["Links reach the right destination","Layout adapts to small screens","Keyboard accessibility","Compatibility across browsers"],"why":"Each test targets a different quality.","fb":[]}]}]},{"pkg":"Topic Quizzes · Section 5","quizzes":[{"id":"q_s5u1","title":"Section 5 · Spreadsheet purpose and terminology quiz","desc":"<p>Topic quiz for Section 5: Spreadsheet purpose and terminology. 30 items.</p>","minutes":0,"items":[{"t":"mc","title":"Spreadsheet definition","q":"<p>Kemar tells his classmate that a spreadsheet is 'just a table in Word'. Which statement BEST explains how a spreadsheet differs?</p>","options":["A spreadsheet recalculates formulas automatically when data changes","A spreadsheet can store pictures","A spreadsheet can only hold numbers","A spreadsheet has more fonts"],"correct":0,"why":"Automatic calculation and recalculation is what makes a spreadsheet different from a word-processed table.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Cell address","q":"<p>The active cell in a grade-book sheet is where column F meets row 12. What does the Name Box show?</p>","options":["12F","F12","F:12","R12C6F"],"correct":1,"why":"Column letter first, then row number: F12.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Counting a range","q":"<p>A teacher at Sandbox Tree College selects the range B3:E10 to format test scores. How many cells are selected?</p>","options":["40","28","24","32"],"correct":3,"why":"B–E is 4 columns; rows 3–10 is 8 rows; 4 × 8 = 32.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Formula bar","q":"<p>Cell H7 in a hotel's booking sheet displays 1,575. When the receptionist clicks H7, the formula bar shows =F7*G7. Which statement is TRUE?</p>","options":["The formula bar is showing the wrong cell","H7 contains the value 1575","H7 contains a formula and displays its result","H7 contains a label"],"correct":2,"why":"The formula bar shows the true contents (a formula); the cell displays the result.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Workbook vs worksheet","q":"<p>The St. John’s cruise port tour operator keeps January, February and March sales on three tabs in one file called Tours2026.xlsx. What is Tours2026.xlsx?</p>","options":["A workbook","A worksheet","A range","A cell"],"correct":0,"why":"The file is the workbook; each tab is a worksheet.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Label or value","q":"<p>In a school canteen sheet, which entry is a LABEL?</p>","options":["45","Chicken roti","3.50","=B4*C4"],"correct":1,"why":"Chicken roti is text describing data.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"What-if","q":"<p>A Grenada farmer changes the price of nutmeg in cell B2 to see how her income in B10 would change. This is an example of…</p>","options":["what-if analysis","mail merge","validation","sorting"],"correct":0,"why":"Changing inputs to see the effect on results is what-if analysis (modelling).","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Hash marks","q":"<p>After typing 1250000 into a narrow column, Shaquille sees ####### in the cell. What is the BEST action?</p>","options":["Change it to a label","Retype the number","Widen the column (AutoFit)","Delete the column"],"correct":2,"why":"##### means the column is too narrow to show the number. Widen it.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Fill handle","q":"<p>Daniella types 'Mon' in A2 and drags the fill handle down to A8. What appears in A8?</p>","options":["Sat","Mon","Sun","Mon8"],"correct":2,"why":"AutoFill continues the days: Mon, Tue, Wed, Thu, Fri, Sat, Sun (A2–A8).","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Starts with","q":"<p>Which entry will a spreadsheet treat as a formula?</p>","options":["SUM(A1:A5)","A1+A5","'=A1+A5","=A1+A5"],"correct":3,"why":"A formula must begin with =. An apostrophe forces text.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Best use","q":"<p>Which task is BEST suited to a spreadsheet rather than a word processor?</p>","options":["Calculating monthly loan repayments for different interest rates","Typing minutes of a meeting","Creating a newsletter","Writing a cover letter for a job at Douglas-Charles Airport"],"correct":0,"why":"Calculating and comparing repayments for different rates is numeric what-if modelling.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Leading zeros","q":"<p>A Barbados school's student IDs start with zeros, e.g. 00321. When typed into a cell they show as 321. How should the IDs be entered?</p>","options":["Format the cells as Text or type an apostrophe first","Add .00 after them","Use the SUM function","Make the column wider"],"correct":0,"why":"Text format or a leading apostrophe stores the ID as a label and keeps the zeros.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Status bar","q":"<p>Without typing a formula, Andre wants a quick total of the selected cells D2:D40. Where can he look?</p>","options":["Title bar","Sheet tab","Name Box","Status bar"],"correct":3,"why":"The status bar shows Sum, Average and Count for the selection.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Limitation","q":"<p>A bakery in Trinidad typed 2000 instead of 200 for flour costs and the sheet showed a huge loss. This illustrates…</p>","options":["automatic recalculation","replication","garbage in, garbage out","what-if analysis"],"correct":2,"why":"Wrong input gives wrong output, however accurately calculated.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Range notation","q":"<p>Which correctly refers to the cells from A2 down to A20?</p>","options":["A2 to A20","A2;A20","A2:A20","A2-A20"],"correct":2,"why":"A colon separates the first and last cells of a range.","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"Advantages","q":"<p>The manager of a Freeport supermarket is deciding whether to replace paper stock records with a spreadsheet. Select EVERY genuine advantage of the spreadsheet.</p>","options":["Charts of sales can be produced quickly","Totals update automatically when stock figures change","Calculations are accurate once formulas are correct","It prevents all data-entry mistakes","It works during power cuts"],"correct":[0, 1, 2],"why":"Recalculation, accuracy and charts are advantages. It cannot prevent wrong input and it needs power.","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"Window parts","q":"<p>Select ALL the items that are parts of the spreadsheet window.</p>","options":["Mail merge toolbar","Name Box","Fill handle","Formula bar","Sheet tabs"],"correct":[1, 2, 3, 4],"why":"Mail merge is a word-processing feature.","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"Values","q":"<p>A sheet for a Dominica track meet contains these entries. Select EVERY entry that is stored as a VALUE.</p>","options":["200","Runner name","15/03/2026","Lane 4","12.45"],"correct":[0, 2, 4],"why":"Numbers and dates are values. 'Lane 4' and 'Runner name' are labels.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Terms to definitions","q":"<p>Match each term to its definition.</p>","left":["Cell","Range","Active cell","Function"],"right":["Intersection of a row and a column","A block of adjacent cells such as B2:D9","The selected cell where typing appears","A predefined formula such as SUM or AVERAGE"],"why":"Precise definitions earn Paper 01 and Paper 02 marks.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Parts of the window","q":"<p>Match each part of the window to what it shows.</p>","left":["Name Box","Formula bar","Status bar","Sheet tab"],"right":["The address of the active cell","The real contents of the active cell","Quick Sum, Average and Count of a selection","The name of a worksheet"],"why":"Each part has a specific job.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Spreadsheet terms","q":"<p>Match each description to the correct spreadsheet term.</p>","left":["A calculation that begins with =","A group of adjacent cells","A numerical entry","An intersection of a row with a column"],"right":["Formula","Range","Value","Cell"],"why":"These are the terms for the unit test.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Uses of spreadsheets","q":"<p>Match each user to a typical spreadsheet use.</p>","left":["School teacher","Small business","Family","Cricket coach"],"right":["Grade book with averages","Sales and stock records","Household budget","Batting averages"],"why":"Spreadsheets serve school, business, home and sport.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Recalculation chain","q":"<p>In a sheet, B4 contains =B2*B3 and B7 contains =B4-B5. B2 is changed. Which cells recalculate?</p>","options":["Only B2","Every cell in the sheet","B4 only","B4 and B7"],"correct":3,"why":"B4 depends on B2, and B7 depends on B4.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Locate a cell","q":"<p>In which column and which row does the cell P64 lie?</p>","options":["Column 64, row P","Row 16, column 64","Column P, row 64","Column P, row 6"],"correct":2,"why":"A cell address is the column letter followed by the row number.","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"Cells in a range","q":"<p>Select EVERY cell in the range A2:B3.</p>","options":["B3","A2","B2","A3","A1","C3"],"correct":[0, 1, 2, 3],"why":"A2:B3 covers A2, A3, B2 and B3: four cells.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Template","q":"<p>Mrs. Hodge gives her Form 4 class a spreadsheet that already has the formulas, formatting and layout for a weekly budget; students only add their own figures. What is this called?</p>","options":["A macro","A range","A pivot table","A template"],"correct":3,"why":"A template is pre-prepared with formulas, formatting and layout.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Year as a label","q":"<p>Jamal wants 2026 in A1 to be treated as a label so it is never used in a calculation. What should he type?</p>","options":["'2026","2026%","#2026","=2026"],"correct":0,"why":"An apostrophe in front makes the entry a label.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Go To","q":"<p>A teacher's grade book has 900 rows and she needs cell K850 quickly. Which key opens the Go To box?</p>","options":["F2","F5","F11","F4"],"correct":1,"why":"F5 (or Ctrl + G) opens Go To.","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"Entering data","q":"<p>Select BOTH ways of entering data into a cell.</p>","options":["Typing in the formula bar after selecting the cell","Typing in the active cell","Typing in the status bar","Typing in the sheet tab"],"correct":[0, 1],"why":"Data goes into the active cell directly or through the formula bar.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Range size challenge","q":"<p>How many cells are in the range D4:G9?</p>","options":["28","20","18","24"],"correct":3,"why":"D–G is 4 columns; 4–9 is 6 rows; 4 × 6 = 24.","fb":[]}]},{"id":"q_s5u2","title":"Section 5 · Functions and formulae quiz","desc":"<p>Topic quiz for Section 5: Functions and formulae. 30 items.</p>","minutes":0,"items":[{"t":"mc","title":"Multiply symbol","q":"<p>Javon wants C2 to show the cost of 6 tickets at the price in B2. Which formula is correct?</p>","options":["=B2x6","=B2×6","=B2*6","B2*6"],"correct":2,"why":"The asterisk * is the multiplication operator, and formulas start with =.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Precedence","q":"<p>A cell contains =10+6/2. What does it display?</p>","options":["13","8","5","16"],"correct":0,"why":"Division first: 6/2 = 3, then 10 + 3 = 13.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Average formula","q":"<p>Test scores for a Montserrat student are in B5, C5 and D5. Which formula correctly calculates her average?</p>","options":["=B5+C5+D5/3","=SUM(B5:D5)*3","=(B5+C5+D5)/3","=AVERAGE(B5,D5)"],"correct":2,"why":"Brackets force the addition first. =AVERAGE(B5,D5) would ignore C5.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"COUNT","q":"<p>A tuck-shop sheet has prices in B2:B20. Three cells say 'TBA' and two are empty; the rest are numbers. What does =COUNT(B2:B20) return?</p>","options":["14","19","17","16"],"correct":0,"why":"19 cells − 3 text − 2 empty = 14 numbers.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"COUNTIF syntax","q":"<p>Which formula counts how many runners in C2:C40 finished in under 60 seconds?</p>","options":["=COUNTIF(C2:C40,<60)","=COUNT(C2:C40<60)","=COUNTIF(C2:C40,\"<60\")","=COUNTIF(\"<60\",C2:C40)"],"correct":2,"why":"Range first, then the criteria in quotation marks.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"IF result","q":"<p>D4 contains 49.5. What does =IF(D4>=50,\"Merit\",\"Keep going\") display?</p>","options":["Merit","Keep going","TRUE","#VALUE!"],"correct":1,"why":"49.5 is not ≥ 50, so the value-if-false is shown.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Not equal","q":"<p>Which operator means 'not equal to' in a spreadsheet?</p>","options":["=/=","!=","<>","><"],"correct":2,"why":"<> means not equal to.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"VLOOKUP column","q":"<p>A tour operator's table in A2:D30 has Tour code, Tour name, Duration, Price. Which formula returns the PRICE of the code in G2?</p>","options":["=VLOOKUP(G2,$A$2:$D$30,4,FALSE)","=VLOOKUP(G2,$A$2:$D$30,3,FALSE)","=VLOOKUP($A$2:$D$30,G2,4,FALSE)","=VLOOKUP(G2,$A$2:$D$30,4)"],"correct":0,"why":"Price is the 4th column of the table; FALSE for an exact code match.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"#N/A","q":"<p>A cashier types code X999 into a VLOOKUP order form and sees #N/A. Why?</p>","options":["X999 is not in the first column of the lookup table","The function is misspelt","The formula divides by zero","The column is too narrow"],"correct":0,"why":"#N/A means the lookup value was not found.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"RANK order","q":"<p>Golf scores are in B2:B12; the LOWEST score wins. Which formula ranks the player in row 2 correctly?</p>","options":["=RANK(B2,B2:B12,0)","=RANK(B2,$B$2:$B$12)","=RANK($B$2:$B$12,B2)","=RANK(B2,$B$2:$B$12,1)"],"correct":3,"why":"Order 1 ranks ascending, so the smallest score is 1st.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"TODAY","q":"<p>Which formula always shows the current date?</p>","options":["TODAY()","=TODAY()","=DATE()","=TODAY"],"correct":1,"why":"TODAY needs empty brackets and an equals sign.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Date subtraction","q":"<p>B2 holds 01/12/2026 and C2 holds 25/12/2026. What does =C2-B2 display when formatted as a number?</p>","options":["23","12","24","25"],"correct":2,"why":"25 − 1 = 24 days between the dates.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"#NAME?","q":"<p>Kayla typed =AVERGE(B2:B9). What will the cell show?</p>","options":["0","#VALUE!","#NAME?","#REF!"],"correct":2,"why":"A misspelt function name gives #NAME?.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"#DIV/0!","q":"<p>The formula =B2/C2 shows #DIV/0!. Which is the most likely cause?</p>","options":["B2 is empty","C2 contains a formula","The column is narrow","C2 is 0 or empty"],"correct":3,"why":"Dividing by zero or an empty cell causes #DIV/0!.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Nested IF","q":"<p>F2 = 64. What does =IF(F2>=80,\"A\",IF(F2>=65,\"B\",IF(F2>=50,\"C\",\"D\"))) display?</p>","options":["D","B","A","C"],"correct":3,"why":"64 fails ≥80 and ≥65 but passes ≥50: C.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Power","q":"<p>What does =2^3+1 display?</p>","options":["8","7","16","9"],"correct":3,"why":"2³ = 8, then + 1 = 9.","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"Valid formulas","q":"<p>Select EVERY formula that correctly adds the values in A1 to A4.</p>","options":["=SUM(A1-A4)","=A1+A2+A3+A4","=SUM(A1:A4)","SUM(A1:A4)","=SUM(A1,A2,A3,A4)"],"correct":[1, 2, 4],"why":"A dash means minus, and without = the last one is text.","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"Error causes","q":"<p>Select ALL the situations that produce #VALUE!.</p>","options":["=A2/0","=A2+B2 where B2 contains 'n/a' typed as text","=A2*B2 where A2 contains 'twelve'","=SUMM(A2:A9)"],"correct":[1, 2],"why":"Calculating with text gives #VALUE!. Division by zero gives #DIV/0!; a misspelt function gives #NAME?.","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"Counting functions","q":"<p>The range A2:A6 contains: 15, 'Absent', (empty), 0, 22. Select EVERY correct statement.</p>","options":["=COUNT(A2:A6) gives 3","=COUNTA(A2:A6) gives 4","=AVERAGE(A2:A6) gives 9.25","=COUNTBLANK(A2:A6) gives 1"],"correct":[0, 1, 3],"why":"COUNT counts 15, 0 and 22 (3). COUNTA counts 4 non-empty cells. AVERAGE = 37 ÷ 3 ≈ 12.33.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Function purposes","q":"<p>Match each function to what it returns.</p>","left":["MAX","COUNTA","RANK","VLOOKUP"],"right":["The largest value in a range","The number of non-empty cells","A value's position in a list","Information from a table row matching a lookup value"],"why":"Each function has one clear job.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Errors and causes","q":"<p>Match each error to its cause.</p>","left":["#REF!","#NAME?","#N/A","#####"],"right":["A referenced cell was deleted","Excel does not recognise a name or unquoted text","A lookup value was not found","The column is too narrow"],"why":"Each error value names its problem.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Operators","q":"<p>Match each operator to its meaning.</p>","left":[">=","<>","^","/"],"right":["Greater than or equal to","Not equal to","Raise to a power","Divide"],"why":"Know every operator symbol.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"AND function","q":"<p>A youth cricket camp in Antigua accepts players aged 13 to 16 inclusive. Age is in C2. Which formula returns TRUE only for eligible players?</p>","options":["=AND(C2>=13,C2<=16)","=C2>=13 AND C2<=16","=AND(C2>13,C2<16)","=OR(C2>=13,C2<=16)"],"correct":0,"why":"Both conditions must hold, so AND; >= and <= include 13 and 16. OR would be true for every age, and the last option is not valid Excel syntax.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Logical test","q":"<p>Which of these is a LOGICAL TEST that could be used inside an IF function?</p>","options":["B2<>\"Paid\"","B2*15%","SUM(B2:B9)","\"Overdue\""],"correct":0,"why":"A logical test has a TRUE or FALSE answer. B2<>\"Paid\" asks whether B2 is not equal to Paid.","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"Elements of a formula","q":"<p>Select ALL the elements that can make up a formula.</p>","options":["Functions","Constants","Operators","Sheet tabs","References"],"correct":[0, 1, 2, 4],"why":"A formula is built from references, operators, constants and functions.","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"Function Library","q":"<p>Select EVERY category found in the Function Library on the Formulas tab.</p>","options":["Logical","Math & Trig","Merge & Center","Date & Time","Financial"],"correct":[0, 1, 3, 4],"why":"Merge & Center is an alignment command on the Home tab.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Voting age","q":"<p>D2 holds a citizen's age, 17. What does =IF(D2<=17,\"Non-voting age\",\"Voting age\") display?</p>","options":["Voting age","Non-voting age","TRUE","17"],"correct":1,"why":"17 <= 17 is TRUE, so the value-if-true is shown.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Over 60","q":"<p>A student is given PASS if the mark is OVER 60, else FAIL. Mark is in B4 and equals 60. Which formula is correct, and what does it show?</p>","options":["=IF(B4<60,\"PASS\",\"FAIL\") shows FAIL","=IF(B4>60,\"PASS\",\"FAIL\") shows FAIL","=IF(B4>60,PASS,FAIL) shows FAIL","=IF(B4>=60,\"PASS\",\"FAIL\") shows PASS"],"correct":1,"why":"'Over 60' means > 60, so exactly 60 fails.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"NOW vs TODAY","q":"<p>Which function displays the current date AND time?</p>","options":["=NOW()","=TIME","=DATE()","=TODAY()"],"correct":0,"why":"NOW returns date and time; TODAY returns only the date.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Function with range","q":"<p>In a Guyana rice farm sheet, yields are in C2:C25. Which formula finds the smallest yield?</p>","options":["=MIN(C2:C25)","=LOW(C2:C25)","=SMALL(C2:C25)","=MIN(C2-C25)"],"correct":0,"why":"MIN returns the smallest number in a range.","fb":[]}]},{"id":"q_s5u3","title":"Section 5 · Replicating formulae, absolute addressing and named ranges quiz","desc":"<p>Topic quiz for Section 5: Replicating formulae, absolute addressing and named ranges. 22 items.</p>","minutes":0,"items":[{"t":"mc","title":"Relative copy","q":"<p>Cell D3 contains =B3+C3. It is copied to D9. What does D9 contain?</p>","options":["=D9+C9","=B3+C3","=B9+C9","=B6+C6"],"correct":2,"why":"Six rows down: 3 → 9 in both references.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Absolute meaning","q":"<p>In the formula =C4*$F$1, what does $F$1 mean?</p>","options":["F1 is a named range","F1 will change when the formula is copied","F1 will not change when the formula is copied","F1 contains a dollar amount"],"correct":2,"why":"$ locks both column and row; it has nothing to do with currency.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"F4 key","q":"<p>Tariq has typed =B4*D1 and his cursor is just after D1. He presses F4 once. What does the formula become?</p>","options":["=B4*$D$1","=B4*$D1","=B4*D$1","=$B$4*D1"],"correct":0,"why":"The first press of F4 makes the reference fully absolute.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Exchange rate","q":"<p>A Curaçao travel agent stores the exchange rate in H2 and prices in B5:B40. Which formula in C5 converts the price and can be copied down?</p>","options":["=B5*H2","=B5*$H$2","=$B$5*H2","=$B5*H$3"],"correct":1,"why":"The price moves; the rate is locked.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Mixed reference","q":"<p>In which reference is only the ROW locked?</p>","options":["$C7","C$7","C7","$C$7"],"correct":1,"why":"The $ is in front of 7, the row number.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Copy across","q":"<p>G2 contains =SUM(B2:F2). It is copied to G3. What is in G3?</p>","options":["=SUM(B2:F2)","=SUM(B2:F3)","=SUM(B3:F3)","=SUM(C2:G2)"],"correct":2,"why":"One row down: 2 → 3.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Named range","q":"<p>Cell B2 is named VAT. Which formula is equivalent to =C5*$B$2?</p>","options":["=C5*$VAT$","=VAT(C5)","=C5*VAT","=C5*B2VAT"],"correct":2,"why":"A name can replace the absolute reference directly.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Invalid name","q":"<p>Which of these is NOT a valid range name?</p>","options":["TaxRate2","TaxRate","Tax_Rate","Tax Rate"],"correct":3,"why":"Names cannot contain spaces.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Freeze panes","q":"<p>To keep row 1 and column A visible while scrolling, which cell should be selected before Freeze Panes?</p>","options":["A2","A1","B2","B1"],"correct":2,"why":"Freeze Panes freezes rows above and columns left of the active cell.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Insert row","q":"<p>A total in B10 is =SUM(B2:B9). A row is inserted above row 5. What and where is the total now?</p>","options":["B10: =SUM(B2:B10)","B10: =SUM(B2:B9)","B11: =SUM(B2:B10)","B11: =SUM(B2:B9)"],"correct":2,"why":"The total moves down one row and the range expands.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Delete referenced cell","q":"<p>A Jamaican shop's formulas all use the rate in $C$1. Someone deletes column C. What do the formulas show?</p>","options":["0","#NAME?","#REF!","#VALUE!"],"correct":2,"why":"The referenced cell no longer exists.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Paste Values","q":"<p>Rhea wants to send a supplier her price list without the formulas. What should she use?</p>","options":["Cut and paste","Paste Special ▸ Values","Format Painter","Paste Special ▸ Formulas"],"correct":1,"why":"Paste Values keeps only the results.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Cut vs copy","q":"<p>Cell E2 contains =C2*D2. It is CUT and pasted into E8. What is in E8?</p>","options":["=C2*D2","=C8*D8","=C6*D6","#REF!"],"correct":0,"why":"Moving a formula does not change its references.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Percentage of total","q":"<p>Sales are in B2:B11 and the total in B12. Which formula in C2 gives each item's share and can be copied down?</p>","options":["=B2/$B$12","=B2/B12","=$B$2/B12","=B12/B2"],"correct":0,"why":"The part moves; the total is locked.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Hide","q":"<p>Columns D and E are hidden. Which selection lets you unhide them?</p>","options":["Columns C and F","Row 1","Column A","Column D only"],"correct":0,"why":"Select the columns on both sides of the hidden ones, then Unhide.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Running total","q":"<p>C2 contains =SUM($B$2:B2). What does C6 contain after copying down?</p>","options":["=SUM($B$6:B6)","=SUM(B2:B6)","=SUM($B$2:B6)","=SUM($B$2:$B$2)"],"correct":2,"why":"The locked start stays; the relative end moves.","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"Should be absolute","q":"<p>A grade book will have each formula copied down a column. Select EVERY reference that should be absolute.</p>","options":["The range of averages used by RANK","Each student's own average in column F","The grade table used by VLOOKUP","The pass mark stored in J1, used in an IF"],"correct":[0, 2, 3],"why":"Shared single cells and shared ranges must be locked; each student's own value must move.","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"Freeze vs others","q":"<p>Select ALL the true statements.</p>","options":["Hiding a column protects it with a password","Hidden columns are still used in calculations","Freeze Panes affects only what you see on screen","AutoFit is done by double-clicking a column boundary"],"correct":[1, 2, 3],"why":"Hiding is not protection.","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"Copying methods","q":"<p>Select EVERY method that replicates a formula from D2 into D3:D20.</p>","options":["Cut D2 and paste into D3","Copy D2, select D3:D20, paste","Select D2:D20 and press Ctrl + D","Drag the fill handle from D2 to D20"],"correct":[1, 2, 3],"why":"Cutting moves the single formula; it does not replicate it.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Reference forms","q":"<p>Match each reference to its description.</p>","left":["A1","$A$1","A$1","$A1"],"right":["Relative","Absolute","Mixed: row locked","Mixed: column locked"],"why":"The $ locks whatever follows it.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Commands and places","q":"<p>Match each task to where you find it.</p>","left":["Freeze Panes","Define Name","Column Width","Paste Values"],"right":["View tab","Formulas tab","Home ▸ Format","Home ▸ Paste arrow"],"why":"Know where the commands live on the ribbon.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Copy predictions","q":"<p>B2 contains =A2*$E$1. Match each destination to the formula it gets when B2 is copied there.</p>","left":["B3","B10","C2","C5"],"right":["=A3*$E$1","=A10*$E$1","=B2*$E$1","=B5*$E$1"],"why":"Only the relative part moves.","fb":[]}]},{"id":"q_s5u4","title":"Section 5 · Formatting, sorting, filtering and pivot tables quiz","desc":"<p>Topic quiz for Section 5: Formatting, sorting, filtering and pivot tables. 27 items.</p>","minutes":0,"items":[{"t":"mc","title":"Percentage display","q":"<p>Cell B4 in a Barbados credit union's sheet holds 0.065. It is formatted as Percentage with 1 decimal place. What does it show?</p>","options":["65.0%","0.1%","0.065%","6.5%"],"correct":3,"why":"0.065 × 100 = 6.5%.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Currency vs accounting","q":"<p>Which number format lines up the $ signs at the left edge of the cell and shows zeros as a dash?</p>","options":["Accounting","Comma","General","Currency"],"correct":0,"why":"That is the Accounting format.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Stored value","q":"<p>A cell shows 4.57 because it is formatted to 2 decimal places, but the formula bar shows 4.5678. What value does =A1*100 use?</p>","options":["456.78","4.57","457.00","457"],"correct":0,"why":"Formulas use the stored 4.5678, giving 456.78.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Wrap text","q":"<p>A heading 'Number of passengers (adults)' is too long for its column. Which feature keeps it inside the cell on several lines?</p>","options":["Orientation","AutoFit","Wrap Text","Merge & Center"],"correct":2,"why":"Wrap Text breaks the text onto multiple lines within the cell.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Conditional formatting","q":"<p>A teacher wants every mark below 50 to turn red automatically. Which feature should she use?</p>","options":["Font colour","Format Painter","Cell Styles","Conditional formatting"],"correct":3,"why":"Conditional formatting applies formatting when a condition is met.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Record","q":"<p>In a spreadsheet list of Piarco Airport flight arrivals, each row holds one flight's number, airline, time and gate. What is one row called?</p>","options":["A record","A field","A range name","A key"],"correct":0,"why":"A row of data about one item is a record.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Descending","q":"<p>A list of Guyana rice-farm yields is sorted in descending order of Yield. Which farm appears first?</p>","options":["The largest yield","The smallest yield","The first farm typed","The farm name starting with A"],"correct":0,"why":"Descending means largest to smallest.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Secondary key","q":"<p>A list is sorted by Parish, then by Surname. What is Surname?</p>","options":["The secondary key","The primary key","The filter","The criteria"],"correct":0,"why":"The second sort field is the secondary key; it orders records within each parish.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Sorting one column","q":"<p>Leon selects only column C (Scores) and sorts it, ignoring Excel's warning. What is the result?</p>","options":["The whole table sorts correctly","Nothing happens","Scores no longer match the students' names","Excel deletes the names"],"correct":2,"why":"Only the scores move, so records are split apart.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"AutoFilter result","q":"<p>An AutoFilter is set on a list of 200 Carnival troupe members to show only 'Section = Blue Waves'. What happens to the other records?</p>","options":["They are deleted","They are sorted to the bottom","They move to a new sheet","They are hidden"],"correct":3,"why":"Filtered-out records are hidden, not deleted.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Criteria range","q":"<p>In an Advanced Filter criteria range, two conditions are typed on the SAME row. How are they combined?</p>","options":["AND","OR","NOT","They are ignored"],"correct":0,"why":"Same row = AND; different rows = OR.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Pivot table purpose","q":"<p>A Jamaican supermarket chain has 50,000 sales records and wants total sales by store and month. Which tool is MOST efficient?</p>","options":["Freeze Panes","A pivot table","Merge & Center","Find and Replace"],"correct":1,"why":"A pivot table summarises large lists by categories quickly.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Pivot refresh","q":"<p>Source data for a pivot table is corrected. What must be done for the pivot to show the new figures?</p>","options":["Format the pivot","Sort the data","Nothing: it updates automatically","Refresh the pivot table"],"correct":3,"why":"Pivot tables update only when refreshed.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Print titles","q":"<p>A 6-page printout of a hotel's guest list has headings only on page 1. Which feature puts them on every page?</p>","options":["Freeze Panes","Wrap Text","Print Titles","Page Break Preview"],"correct":2,"why":"Print Titles repeats rows at the top of each printed page.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Show formulas","q":"<p>Which keyboard shortcut switches the sheet between showing values and showing formulas?</p>","options":["Ctrl + H","Ctrl + F","Ctrl + `","Ctrl + 1"],"correct":2,"why":"Ctrl + ` (the grave accent) toggles Show Formulas.","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"Number formats","q":"<p>Select EVERY true statement about number formatting.</p>","options":["It changes the value stored in the cell","It changes how a value is displayed","0.5 formatted as Percentage shows 50%","Dates can be shown in several formats"],"correct":[1, 2, 3],"why":"Formatting never changes the stored value.","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"Filters","q":"<p>A stock list has an AutoFilter on. Select ALL the true statements.</p>","options":["=SUM of the column adds only the visible rows","Hidden rows still exist","Criteria on different columns are combined with AND","A Custom AutoFilter can join two conditions on one column with Or"],"correct":[1, 2, 3],"why":"SUM includes hidden rows; SUBTOTAL(9,…) adds only visible rows.","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"Pivot areas","q":"<p>Select EVERY area that appears in the PivotTable Fields pane.</p>","options":["Values","Criteria","Columns","Filters","Rows"],"correct":[0, 2, 3, 4],"why":"There is no Criteria area: that belongs to Advanced Filter.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Formatting tools","q":"<p>Match each task to the formatting tool.</p>","left":["Show a $ sign","Join cells for a title","Copy the look of one cell to another","Colour cells that meet a rule"],"right":["Currency format","Merge & Center","Format Painter","Conditional formatting"],"why":"Each tool has its own job.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Sort and filter terms","q":"<p>Match each term to its meaning.</p>","left":["Ascending","Primary key","Criterion","Filter"],"right":["Smallest to largest, A to Z","The first field used to sort","A test used to select records","Shows only records that meet criteria"],"why":"Precise vocabulary earns marks.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Pivot terms","q":"<p>Match each pivot table action to its result.</p>","left":["Value Field Settings ▸ Count","Show Values As ▸ % of Grand Total","Group dates","Add a field to Filters"],"right":["Counts records instead of adding them","Shows each value as a share of the total","Combines days into months","Shows the table for selected items only"],"why":"Each setting changes the summary.","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"Format Cells tabs","q":"<p>Select EVERY tab found in the Format Cells dialog box (Ctrl + 1).</p>","options":["Border","Fill","Formulas","Alignment","Protection","Number"],"correct":[0, 1, 3, 4, 5],"why":"Format Cells has Number, Alignment, Font, Border, Fill and Protection; Formulas is a ribbon tab.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Merge options","q":"<p>Match each merge option to what it does.</p>","left":["Merge & Center","Merge Across","Merge Cells","Unmerge Cells"],"right":["Combines cells and centres the contents","Merges each selected row separately","Combines cells without centring","Splits merged cells apart again"],"why":"Four options on the Merge & Center drop-down.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"What to highlight before sorting","q":"<p>The VICTORY COLLEGE results sheet has two title rows at the top and a totals row at the bottom. Before a Custom Sort on Average Marks, what should be highlighted?</p>","options":["Only the title rows","The whole sheet including titles and totals","The headings and data only, excluding the title rows and the totals row","Only the Average Marks column"],"correct":2,"why":"Titles and totals must stay where they are; only the records (with their headings) are sorted.","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"Text filters","q":"<p>Select ALL the options offered under Text Filters.</p>","options":["Does Not Contain","Greater Than","Ends With","Contains","Begins With"],"correct":[0, 2, 3, 4],"why":"Greater Than is a Number Filter.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Extract over 5000","q":"<p>A sales sheet lists products and their Quarter 3 sales. To extract the products whose Quarter 3 sales were more than 5000, which filter option should be used on the Quarter 3 column?</p>","options":["Sort Largest to Smallest","Text Filters ▸ Contains","Number Filters ▸ Greater Than","Number Filters ▸ Less Than"],"correct":2,"why":"Number Filters ▸ Greater Than, then type 5000.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Date format","q":"<p>A Montserrat school records an exam date stored as 46142. Which format shows it as 30-Apr-26?</p>","options":["Percentage","dd/mm/yyyy","General","dd-mmm-yy"],"correct":3,"why":"dd-mmm-yy gives day, short month name and 2-digit year.","fb":[]}]},{"id":"q_s5u5","title":"Section 5 · Charts and multiple worksheets quiz","desc":"<p>Topic quiz for Section 5: Charts and multiple worksheets. 22 items.</p>","minutes":0,"items":[{"t":"mc","title":"Trend chart","q":"<p>The Meteorological Department of Trinidad and Tobago wants to show how rainfall changed each month from June to November. Which chart is MOST appropriate?</p>","options":["Column chart of one month","Pie chart","Bubble chart","Line chart"],"correct":3,"why":"Change over time is best shown with a line chart.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Parts of a whole","q":"<p>A family wants to show what share of their monthly income goes to rent, food, transport and savings. Which chart?</p>","options":["Pie chart","Line chart","Area chart","Scatter chart"],"correct":0,"why":"Shares of one total: pie chart.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Comparing categories","q":"<p>A teacher wants to display the total score of each of 25 students. Which chart is MOST appropriate?</p>","options":["Line chart","Pie chart","Bar or column chart","Doughnut chart"],"correct":2,"why":"Individual scores are categories to compare, not parts of a whole or a time series.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Legend","q":"<p>A chart shows two data series: 2025 sales and 2026 sales. Which element tells the reader which colour is which year?</p>","options":["Legend","Axis title","Gridlines","Chart title"],"correct":0,"why":"The legend is the key to the series colours.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Axis title","q":"<p>On a column chart of money raised per class, which axis title is BEST for the vertical axis?</p>","options":["Series1","Chart 1","Classes","Amount raised ($)"],"correct":3,"why":"The value axis title names the measure and units.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Non-adjacent selection","q":"<p>To chart country names in column A and figures in column C (skipping B), what should you do while selecting?</p>","options":["Hold Shift","Hold Ctrl","Hold Alt","Press F4"],"correct":1,"why":"Ctrl lets you select non-adjacent ranges.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Chart sheet","q":"<p>Ms. Daniel wants the chart to fill its own sheet named PIE. Which command?</p>","options":["Move Chart ▸ New sheet","Switch Row/Column","Insert ▸ Table","Freeze Panes"],"correct":0,"why":"Move Chart places the chart on a new chart sheet.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Sheet reference","q":"<p>Which formula refers to cell D4 on a sheet named Feb?</p>","options":["=Feb(D4)","=D4!Feb","=Feb:D4","=Feb!D4"],"correct":3,"why":"SheetName!Cell.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Spaces in sheet names","q":"<p>Which formula correctly uses B3 on a sheet named Exchange Rates?</p>","options":["=(Exchange Rates)!B3","=\"Exchange Rates\"!B3","=Exchange Rates!B3","='Exchange Rates'!B3"],"correct":3,"why":"Names with spaces go in single quotes.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"3-D reference","q":"<p>A workbook has sheets Week1 to Week4 in order, each with total sales in E10. Which formula adds all four?</p>","options":["=SUM(Week1:Week4!E10)","=SUM(E10:E10)","=Week1:Week4+E10","=SUM(Week1!E10:Week4!E10)"],"correct":0,"why":"First sheet:last sheet!cell.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Linked cell update","q":"<p>Summary!C5 contains =Jan!C5. Jan!C5 changes from 80 to 95. What does Summary!C5 show?</p>","options":["175","#REF!","95","80"],"correct":2,"why":"Linked cells update automatically.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Renaming","q":"<p>What is the quickest way to rename a sheet tab?</p>","options":["Press Delete","File ▸ Rename","Double-click the tab and type","Insert ▸ Name"],"correct":2,"why":"Double-click (or right-click ▸ Rename).","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Importing","q":"<p>Shanelle copies a table of prices from a Word document and pastes it into Excel. What is this called?</p>","options":["Importing","Freezing","Filtering","Exporting"],"correct":0,"why":"Bringing data in from outside the spreadsheet program is importing.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Template","q":"<p>Which BEST describes a spreadsheet template?</p>","options":["A chart style","A blank workbook","A pre-prepared spreadsheet with formulas, formatting and layout already set up","A backup copy"],"correct":2,"why":"Templates are ready-made designs: you add data.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Macro","q":"<p>A clerk at a Trinidad shipping agency formats a new sheet the same way every week. Which feature can do all the steps in one action?</p>","options":["A 3-D reference","A macro","A pivot table","A legend"],"correct":1,"why":"Macros record and replay a sequence of steps.","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"Chart elements","q":"<p>Select EVERY item that is a chart element.</p>","options":["Legend","Data labels","Axis titles","Chart title","Fill handle"],"correct":[0, 1, 2, 3],"why":"The fill handle belongs to cells, not charts.","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"Sheet operations","q":"<p>Select ALL the commands available when you right-click a sheet tab.</p>","options":["Insert","Rename","Sort A to Z","Move or Copy","Tab Color"],"correct":[0, 1, 3, 4],"why":"Sorting is a Data command, not a tab command.","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"Pie chart rules","q":"<p>Select EVERY statement that is true of pie charts.</p>","options":["They need two or more data series","They have no axes","They work best with a few slices","They show parts of one whole"],"correct":[1, 2, 3],"why":"A pie chart uses one data series.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Chart to purpose","q":"<p>Match each chart type to its best use.</p>","left":["Column","Line","Pie","Bar"],"right":["Comparing values across categories","Showing a trend over time","Showing shares of one total","Comparing many categories with long labels"],"why":"Choose by the question the chart answers.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Options","q":"<p>Match each Excel Options category to what it controls.</p>","left":["Proofing","Formulas","Save","General"],"right":["AutoCorrect and spelling","Automatic or manual calculation","AutoRecover and default file location","User name and Live Preview"],"why":"Each Options page has its own area.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Worksheet tasks","q":"<p>Match each task to its method.</p>","left":["Insert a worksheet","Reposition a worksheet","Copy a worksheet","Delete a worksheet"],"right":["Click ⊕ or press Shift + F11","Drag the sheet tab","Move or Copy ▸ Create a copy","Right-click the tab ▸ Delete"],"why":"All from the sheet tabs.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Series1","q":"<p>A chart legend shows 'Series1' and the axis shows 1, 2, 3 instead of quarter names. What is the BEST fix?</p>","options":["Delete the chart","Change the chart to a pie","Use Select Data to edit the series name and axis labels","Increase the font size"],"correct":2,"why":"Select Data ▸ Edit lets you choose the label cells.","fb":[]}]}]},{"pkg":"Topic Quizzes · Section 6","quizzes":[{"id":"q_s6u1","title":"Section 6 · Database concepts and terminology quiz","desc":"<p>Topic quiz for Section 6: Database concepts and terminology. 27 items.</p>","minutes":0,"items":[{"t":"mc","title":"Defining a database","q":"<p>A Form 4 student at Bayleaf Hill Secondary writes four definitions of a database in the notes. Which one would earn full marks in Paper 02?</p>","options":["A large spreadsheet with many rows","Any file saved on a computer","An organised collection of related data that can be easily accessed, managed and updated","A program used to store data"],"correct":2,"why":"A database is an organised collection of related data. A program is a DBMS, and a spreadsheet or any file is not necessarily organised related data.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Software, not data","q":"<p>The Grand Anse Market co-operative installs a program that lets staff create tables, run queries and print reports about vendors. What is this program?</p>","options":["A flat file","A primary key","A database management system (DBMS)","A database"],"correct":2,"why":"Software that creates and manages databases is a DBMS. The vendor data it holds is the database.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Counting records","q":"<p>Gouyave Guesthouse's tblGuest has a heading row and 18 rows of guest data, each with 7 columns. How many records and fields does it have?</p>","options":["126 records","7 records and 18 fields","18 records and 7 fields","19 records and 7 fields"],"correct":2,"why":"Each guest row is a record (18; the heading row is not a record) and each column is a field (7).","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Formal words","q":"<p>In a textbook from Trinidad, a table is described as a relation, a row as a tuple and a column as an attribute. Which everyday database word means the same as attribute?</p>","options":["Table","Record","Field","Key"],"correct":2,"why":"Attribute = field (column). Tuple = record (row).","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Phone field type","q":"<p>The Grenville Clinic stores patient phone numbers such as 543-0912. A volunteer suggests the Number data type. Why is Text better?</p>","options":["Number fields cannot hold seven digits","Text fields are faster to sort","Phone numbers contain symbols like dashes and are never used in calculations","Text fields use less storage than numbers"],"correct":2,"why":"Phone numbers contain non-numeric symbols and are never added or averaged, so Text is correct.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Money","q":"<p>Island Power, an electricity company, records the amount due on each bill. Which data type is MOST suitable for AmountDue?</p>","options":["Currency","Date/Time","Yes/No","Text"],"correct":0,"why":"Money values should use Currency, which shows $ and two decimal places and avoids rounding errors.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Two answers only","q":"<p>The La Sagesse Community Centre records whether each child has a signed photo consent form. Which data type should the field PhotoConsent use?</p>","options":["AutoNumber","Logical (Yes/No)","Currency","Long Text"],"correct":1,"why":"Only two possible values: logical (Boolean) Yes/No.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Choosing a primary key","q":"<p>The Carriacou Ferry Line's PASSENGER table has the fields PassengerName, DateOfBirth, Nationality and PassportNo. Which field is the BEST primary key?</p>","options":["Nationality","DateOfBirth","PassportNo","PassengerName"],"correct":2,"why":"A passport number is unique to each traveller. Names, birthdays and nationalities repeat.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Key that links","q":"<p>In the Grenadian Carnival Committee database, SECTION (SectionID, SectionName, Leader) and MASQUERADER (MasqID, Name, Phone, SectionID). What is SectionID in MASQUERADER?</p>","options":["Candidate key","Composite key","Primary key","Foreign key"],"correct":3,"why":"SectionID is SECTION's primary key copied into MASQUERADER to link the tables: a foreign key.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Two-field key","q":"<p>A CXC results table stores StudentNo, SubjectCode and Grade. A student sits many subjects and each subject is sat by many students. What should the primary key be?</p>","options":["StudentNo alone","Grade","SubjectCode alone","StudentNo and SubjectCode together (a composite key)"],"correct":3,"why":"Neither field is unique alone, but each student–subject pair occurs once, so they form a composite key.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Searching field","q":"<p>The librarian at Island Reads often searches for members by LastName, even though several members share the same surname. LastName is an example of a:</p>","options":["composite key","secondary key","foreign key","primary key"],"correct":1,"why":"A non-unique field used for searching or sorting is a secondary key.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Flat-file problem","q":"<p>A Jamaican youth club stores member addresses on every attendance row of one spreadsheet. The club's file is large and slow because the same addresses appear hundreds of times. This is called:</p>","options":["data verification","data validation","data encryption","data redundancy"],"correct":3,"why":"Storing the same data more than once is data redundancy.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Relationship type","q":"<p>At Young Street Bakery, one customer can place many orders, but each order is placed by one customer. What is the relationship between CUSTOMER and ORDER?</p>","options":["Many-to-many","One-to-one","No relationship","One-to-many"],"correct":3,"why":"One customer, many orders; each order has one customer: one-to-many.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Many-to-many","q":"<p>Students at Seagrape Bay Secondary School can join many clubs and each club has many students. How should a database designer store this relationship?</p>","options":["Create a linking table holding StudentID and ClubID","Put a ClubID field in the STUDENT table","Merge both tables into one flat file","Put a StudentID field in the CLUB table"],"correct":0,"why":"A many-to-many relationship is resolved with a linking (junction) table, giving two one-to-many relationships.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"DBMS disadvantage","q":"<p>A small Barbados craft shop is told a database will cost money for software and training. Which of these is ALSO a genuine limitation of using a database?</p>","options":["Many users can share data","A single failure or security breach can affect all the data at once","Data redundancy is reduced","Data can be searched quickly"],"correct":1,"why":"The other three are advantages. Centralised data means one failure or breach affects everything, so backups and security are essential.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Manual databases","q":"<p>Your teacher asks her Form 4 class at Flamboyant Heights College for an example of a MANUAL database. Which answer is correct?</p>","options":["A MySQL server at a hotel","A spreadsheet on a laptop","An online banking app","A telephone directory printed on paper"],"correct":3,"why":"A manual database is kept on paper: telephone directories, library card catalogues, patient record cards and printed voter lists.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Collection of fields","q":"<p>In the Hummingbird Ridge High School database, all the fields describing one student (ID, name, gender, date of birth) together form a:</p>","options":["database","entity","record","table"],"correct":2,"why":"A collection of related fields about one entity is a record (tuple).","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Not an Access data type","q":"<p>Jaden is choosing data types in Microsoft Access for his SBA. Which of these is NOT a data type available in Access?</p>","options":["Number","Short Text","Yes/No","Character"],"correct":3,"why":"Access offers Short Text, Number, Yes/No, Currency, Date/Time and others, but no data type called Character.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Column word","q":"<p>In the Storms database from class, the column Category holds a value for every hurricane. Which formal term describes a column of data in a table?</p>","options":["Attribute","Key","Row","Tuple"],"correct":0,"why":"A column is a field, formally an attribute. A tuple is a row (record).","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Could be the key","q":"<p>In tblStudent, both StudentID and CXCCandidateNo are unique. What is any field that COULD be used as the primary key called?</p>","options":["A candidate key","A field size","A foreign key","A composite key"],"correct":0,"why":"Any field (or combination) able to act as the primary key is a candidate key.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Name the term","q":"<p>Give the database term for each description from the class notes.</p>","left":["The details about a particular entity","Linking of tables to share records","The type of data a field can store","The amount of information a field can store","A plan showing field names, data types and field sizes"],"right":["Tuple (record)","Relationship","Data type (field type)","Field size","Table structure"],"why":"These five terms appear together in many end-of-section questions.","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"Advantages","q":"<p>The St. George’s Public Health Office moves from paper cards to a database. Select EVERY genuine advantage it will gain.</p>","options":["Less redundant data","No need for any backups","Faster searching for a patient's record","No staff training needed","Different staff can be given different access levels"],"correct":[0, 2, 4],"why":"Speed, less redundancy and controlled access are advantages. Backups are still essential and staff do need training.","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"Candidate keys","q":"<p>A Calabash Point table STUDENT (StudentID, CXCNo, Email, FirstName, LastName, Form) has StudentID, CXCNo and Email all unique for every student. Select EVERY candidate key.</p>","options":["CXCNo","Email","Form","FirstName","StudentID"],"correct":[0, 1, 4],"why":"Any field that is unique for every record could be the primary key: StudentID, CXCNo and Email.","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"Spot the DBMS","q":"<p>The IT teacher at a Guyana school lists items on the board for a Paper 01 revision game. Select EVERY item that is database management system software.</p>","options":["LibreOffice Base","MySQL","Microsoft Excel","A list of every GRENLEC customer","Microsoft Access"],"correct":[0, 1, 4],"why":"Access, MySQL and LibreOffice Base are DBMS. A customer list is a database; Excel is a spreadsheet program.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Terms and meanings","q":"<p>Mrs Duzon explains the Island Reads Library tables to a new volunteer. Match each database term to its meaning.</p>","left":["Record","Field","Table","Primary key"],"right":["All the data about one item (a row)","One category of data (a column)","A collection of records about one entity","A field that uniquely identifies each record"],"why":"Rows are records, columns are fields, a table holds records about one entity, and the primary key makes each record unique.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Keys","q":"<p>A CXC results database uses several kinds of key. Match each key to its description.</p>","left":["Foreign key","Composite key","Secondary key","Candidate key"],"right":["Matches the primary key of another table","Two or more fields that together are unique","A non-unique field used to search or sort","Any field that could be chosen as the primary key"],"why":"Each key type has one job: link, combine, search, or be eligible as the PK.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Relationships","q":"<p>Match each pair from an Antigua school database to its relationship.</p>","left":["A student and their own locker","A form teacher's class and its students","Students and the sports they play","A hotel and its rooms"],"right":["One-to-one","One-to-many (class to students)","Many-to-many","One-to-many (hotel to rooms)"],"why":"Ask the how-many question both ways for each pair.","fb":[]}]},{"id":"q_s6u2","title":"Section 6 · Creating tables, keys and relationships quiz","desc":"<p>Topic quiz for Section 6: Creating tables, keys and relationships. 21 items.</p>","minutes":0,"items":[{"t":"mc","title":"Structure view","q":"<p>Kezia is building her SBA database for an Antigua guesthouse. She needs to add a field and change another field's data type. Which view must she use?</p>","options":["Design View","Form View","Datasheet View","Print Preview"],"correct":0,"why":"Field names, data types and properties are all part of the structure, edited in Design View.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Default value","q":"<p>The Island Reads Library wants today's date to appear automatically in DateOut for each new loan. Which property and setting achieves this?</p>","options":["Input Mask: 00/00/0000","Format: Short Date","Default Value: Date()","Validation Rule: =Date()"],"correct":2,"why":"Default Value fills in a value for new records; Date() returns today's date. A rule of =Date() would reject any other date.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Range rule","q":"<p>A Barbados swim club records each swimmer's age. Members must be from 8 to 17 years old inclusive. Which validation rule is correct?</p>","options":["Between 8 And 17","8 Or 17",">=8 Or <=17",">8 And <17"],"correct":0,"why":"Between includes both ends. >8 And <17 rejects 8 and 17; the Or version accepts every number.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"List rule","q":"<p>A Trinidad school's tblStudent has a House field. Valid houses are Red, Blue and Gold. Which validation rule is correct?</p>","options":["Between \"Red\" And \"Gold\"","In (\"Red\",\"Blue\",\"Gold\")","\"Red\" And \"Blue\" And \"Gold\"","Like \"R*\""],"correct":1,"why":"In checks the value against a list. No value can equal all three at once, and Like \"R*\" only allows words starting with R.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Mask meaning","q":"<p>The BZE Taxi Association uses the input mask >LL\\-000 for permit codes. What would be stored if a dispatcher types tx482?</p>","options":["TX-482","TX482","The entry is rejected","tx-482"],"correct":0,"why":"> converts letters to uppercase, LL requires two letters, \\- shows a dash, 000 requires three digits.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Validation text","q":"<p>A student sets the validation rule >=0 on a Price field but leaves Validation Text blank. A user types -5. What happens?</p>","options":["Access rejects it and shows its own general error message","Access deletes the record","Access changes it to 5","The value is accepted"],"correct":0,"why":"The rule still works; without validation text the user sees a system message instead of a friendly one.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Presence check","q":"<p>At Willemstad Dental Clinic, every patient record must include a LastName. Which field property setting enforces this?</p>","options":["Field Size: 255","Indexed: No","Required: Yes","Format: >"],"correct":2,"why":"Required = Yes means the field cannot be left blank: a presence check.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Matching key types","q":"<p>In tblCustomer, CustomerID is an AutoNumber primary key. In tblOrder, CustomerID will be the foreign key. Which data type should it be in tblOrder?</p>","options":["AutoNumber","Short Text","Number (Long Integer)","Currency"],"correct":2,"why":"AutoNumber is stored as a Long Integer, so the foreign key must be Number (Long Integer).","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Composite key steps","q":"<p>In a Jamaican school's tblExamEntry, StudentID and SubjectCode together must form the primary key. How is this set in Design View?</p>","options":["Type both field names into one row","Set StudentID as the key, then set SubjectCode as the key","Set Indexed to Yes on both fields","Hold Ctrl, select both row selectors, then click Primary Key"],"correct":3,"why":"Selecting both rows together and clicking Primary Key creates one composite key; doing them separately replaces the first key.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Opening relationships","q":"<p>Rohan wants to link tblTeam and tblPlayer in his SBA about a St. Kitts football league. Where does he create a permanent relationship?</p>","options":["Create ▸ Table Design","Database Tools ▸ Relationships","External Data ▸ Text File","Home ▸ Sort & Filter"],"correct":1,"why":"Relationships are created in the Relationships window on the Database Tools tab.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Orphan record","q":"<p>Referential integrity is enforced between tblPatient and tblVisit at an Old Road clinic. A receptionist tries to add a visit for PatientID 450, but there is no patient 450. What happens?</p>","options":["The visit is saved with a blank patient","Access refuses the record","Access creates patient 450 automatically","The visit is saved and a warning is printed later"],"correct":1,"why":"A foreign key value must match an existing primary key, so the orphan record is refused.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Cascade delete","q":"<p>Cascade Delete Related Records is ticked between tblMember and tblLoan at a library. A librarian deletes a member who has five loans. What happens?</p>","options":["The member and all five related loans are deleted","Access refuses the deletion","The loans are moved to another member","Only the member is deleted; the loans remain"],"correct":0,"why":"Cascade Delete automatically deletes the related records along with the parent record.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"1 and infinity","q":"<p>In the Relationships window of a hotel database, the line between tblGuest and tblBooking shows 1 at tblGuest and ∞ at tblBooking. What does this tell you?</p>","options":["The tables are not related","One guest can have many bookings, and referential integrity is enforced","One booking has many guests","It is a one-to-one relationship"],"correct":1,"why":"The ∞ (many) is on the booking side, and the symbols only appear when integrity is enforced.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Shrink warning","q":"<p>A volunteer changes the Field Size of a Short Text field called Village from 30 to 4. The field contains 'Bartica'. After saving and accepting the warning, what is stored?</p>","options":["The record is deleted","Bartica","tica","Bart"],"correct":3,"why":"Values longer than the new size are truncated to the first four characters.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Saving records","q":"<p>Shanice types a new member into the datasheet of tblMember, then clicks on the next row. She has not pressed Ctrl+S. Is the new record saved?</p>","options":["Only after the table is closed and reopened","Only if AutoSave is ticked in Excel","No, records are saved only with Ctrl+S","Yes, Access saves a record automatically when you move to another record"],"correct":3,"why":"Access saves each record as soon as the focus leaves it.","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"Format checks","q":"<p>The Liberta Marina wants to make sure boat codes follow the pattern SB followed by three digits. Select EVERY setting that would enforce this pattern.</p>","options":["Validation Rule: Like \"SB*\"","Validation Rule: Like \"SB###\"","Field Size: 5","Input Mask: \"SB\"000","Default Value: \"SB\""],"correct":[1, 3],"why":"Both the mask and the Like rule with # force SB plus three digits. Like \"SB*\" accepts SB followed by anything; Field Size and Default Value do not check the pattern.","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"Referential integrity refusals","q":"<p>Integrity is enforced between tblClass (one) and tblStudent (many) with no cascades. Select EVERY action Access will refuse.</p>","options":["Changing ClassID 4B to 4X while students use 4B","Adding a new class 4F with no students","Changing a student's phone number","Deleting class 5A, which still has 24 students","Adding a student with ClassID 4Z when there is no class 4Z"],"correct":[0, 3, 4],"why":"Orphan students, deleting a parent with children, and changing a referenced key are refused. Non-key changes and childless parents are fine.","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"Data loss risks","q":"<p>Select EVERY structure change that could cause existing data to be lost.</p>","options":["Adding a Caption","Adding a new field","Reducing a Short Text field size","Changing a Short Text field to Number","Deleting a field"],"correct":[2, 3, 4],"why":"Shrinking, deleting or converting can lose values. Adding a field or caption does not affect stored data.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Properties","q":"<p>A Guyana sports club is setting field properties. Match each property to its purpose.</p>","left":["Field Size","Format","Default Value","Validation Text"],"right":["Limits how many characters can be stored","Changes how data is displayed","Fills in a value automatically for new records","Message shown when a rule is broken"],"why":"Size limits storage; Format changes appearance; Default fills in; Validation Text explains the error.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Mask characters","q":"<p>Match each input mask character to its meaning.</p>","left":["0","L","9","<"],"right":["Digit, required","Letter, required","Digit or space, optional","Converts following letters to lowercase"],"why":"0 and L are required; 9 is optional; < makes lowercase.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Validation rules","q":"<p>Match each requirement for an ORIA parking database to its validation rule.</p>","left":["Hours parked must be at least 1","Fee cannot be negative","Zone must be A, B or C","Entry date cannot be in the future"],"right":[">=1",">=0","In (\"A\",\"B\",\"C\")","<=Date()"],"why":"Read each requirement carefully: at least = >=, not negative = >=0, a list = In, not in the future = <=Date().","fb":[]}]},{"id":"q_s6u3","title":"Section 6 · Queries quiz","desc":"<p>Topic quiz for Section 6: Queries. 24 items.</p>","minutes":0,"items":[{"t":"mc","title":"Text criteria","q":"<p>A St. Kitts hotel database stores RoomType values such as Single, Double and Suite. Which criterion under RoomType finds only suites?</p>","options":["\"Suite\"","=Suite?","Suite*","#Suite#"],"correct":0,"why":"Text criteria go in double quotes. # is for dates; * and ? are wildcards used with Like.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Date criteria","q":"<p>A Barbados clinic wants appointments on or after 1 March 2026. Which criterion under AppointmentDate is correct?</p>","options":[">=2026-03-01","After 01/03/2026",">=#2026-03-01#",">\"01/03/2026\""],"correct":2,"why":"Dates go between # signs; on or after means >=.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Between","q":"<p>The Main Street Bakery wants cakes priced from $10 to $25 inclusive. Which criterion under Price is correct?</p>","options":["Between 10 And 25","10 To 25",">=10 Or <=25",">10 And <25"],"correct":0,"why":"Between includes both end values. The Or version accepts every price.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Wildcard","q":"<p>A Guyana music store wants every song title containing the word Love anywhere. Which criterion under Title is correct?</p>","options":["Like \"?Love?\"","\"Love\"","Like \"Love*\"","Like \"*Love*\""],"correct":3,"why":"Stars on both sides mean the text can appear anywhere. Love* only finds titles that start with Love.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Single character","q":"<p>BookIDs are B001 to B012. Which BookIDs does Like \"B00?\" return?</p>","options":["B001 to B009","B010 to B012","B001 to B012","None"],"correct":0,"why":"? matches exactly one character after B00, so B001 to B009.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"AND in the grid","q":"<p>In Query Design, a student types \"Gros Islet\" under District and \"Student\" under MemberType on the same Criteria row. Which members are returned?</p>","options":["All members","No members","Members who live in Gros Islet and are Students","Members who live in Gros Islet or are Students"],"correct":2,"why":"Criteria on the same row are joined with AND.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"OR in the grid","q":"<p>A Trinidad library query has \"Poetry\" on the Criteria row under Genre and \"Drama\" on the or row under Genre. Which books are returned?</p>","options":["No books","Only Poetry books","Books that are both Poetry and Drama","Poetry books and Drama books"],"correct":3,"why":"Different rows are joined with OR, so either genre is returned.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"The Show box","q":"<p>A query uses Returned: No to find loans still out, but the librarian does not want the Returned column displayed. What should be done?</p>","options":["Untick the Show box under Returned","Delete the criterion","Move Returned to the Sort row","Change Returned to Text"],"correct":0,"why":"Unticking Show hides the column while keeping its criterion.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Sort order","q":"<p>A query must list members by District, and alphabetically by LastName within each district. How must the grid be set up?</p>","options":["Only LastName sorted","District to the left of LastName, both Ascending","District Descending only","LastName to the left of District, both Ascending"],"correct":1,"why":"The leftmost sorted field is the primary sort key.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Calculated field syntax","q":"<p>A Jamaican tuck shop query has Quantity and UnitPrice. Which Field row entry calculates the value of each item line?</p>","options":["LineTotal: [Quantity]*[UnitPrice]","SUM(Quantity, UnitPrice)","[LineTotal]: Quantity x UnitPrice","LineTotal = Quantity*UnitPrice"],"correct":0,"why":"Name, colon, then the expression with field names in square brackets.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Parameter prompt","q":"<p>Mrs Duzon wants a query that asks \"Which district?\" every time it runs and lists members from that district. What goes in the Criteria row under District?</p>","options":["[Which district?]","\"Which district?\"","Like \"Which district?\"","#Which district?#"],"correct":0,"why":"A prompt in square brackets makes a parameter query.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Pop-up surprise","q":"<p>Kayla runs her calculated field Total: [Qty]*[Prise] and Access displays a box saying Enter Parameter Value: Prise. What is the MOST likely cause?</p>","options":["The table is empty","She created a parameter query on purpose","Calculated fields are not allowed","The field name Price is misspelt, so Access treats it as a parameter"],"correct":3,"why":"Any bracketed name that is not a field is treated as a parameter.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"SQL order","q":"<p>Which SQL statement is written in the correct order?</p>","options":["SELECT Title FROM tblBook WHERE Price > 20;","FROM tblBook SELECT Title WHERE Price > 20;","SELECT Title WHERE Price > 20 FROM tblBook;","WHERE Price > 20 SELECT Title FROM tblBook;"],"correct":0,"why":"The order is SELECT, FROM, WHERE, then ORDER BY.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Reading SQL","q":"<p>Using the Island Reads tblBook, what does SELECT Title FROM tblBook WHERE Author = \"Maya Brooks\" ORDER BY Title; return?</p>","options":["Front Street Stories, Hurricane Season","Every title","Only Hurricane Season","Hurricane Season, Front Street Stories"],"correct":0,"why":"Maya Brooks wrote both; ORDER BY Title sorts A→Z, so F comes before H.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Multi-table","q":"<p>A query must show each member's LastName and the Title of every book they have borrowed. Which tables are needed?</p>","options":["tblBook only","tblMember and tblBook only","tblMember, tblLoan and tblBook","tblLoan only"],"correct":2,"why":"tblLoan links members to books; without it, the two tables cannot be joined.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Counting results","q":"<p>Using the Island Reads tblBook, how many records does a query with Price: <20 return?</p>","options":["2","3","5","4"],"correct":3,"why":"Guavaberry Summer $18.75, The Sulphur Springs Mystery $15.00, Mysteries of the Pitons $19.50 and Silk Cotton Folk Tales $12.00.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Counting by group","q":"<p>The South Hill Community Library wants to know how many books it has in each genre without counting by hand. Which Access feature gives this most directly?</p>","options":["Find (Ctrl+F)","An input mask on Genre","A Totals query with Group By on Genre and Count on BookID","Filter by Selection on Genre"],"correct":2,"why":"A Totals query groups records and counts each group. Find and filters locate records but do not count groups.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Filter versus query","q":"<p>A Grenada school secretary wants a list of Form 5 students with unpaid fees that she can save, run every Monday and use as the source of a printed report. Which should she create?</p>","options":["A validation rule","A saved select query","A filter on the students table","A new table typed by hand"],"correct":1,"why":"A query is saved, reusable and can feed a report; a filter is temporary.","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"Same result","q":"<p>Select EVERY criterion under Genre that returns exactly the Fiction and Mystery books.</p>","options":["\"Fiction\" on the Criteria row and \"Mystery\" on the or row","\"Fiction\" Or \"Mystery\"","Like \"F*\"","In (\"Fiction\",\"Mystery\")","\"Fiction\" And \"Mystery\""],"correct":[0, 1, 3],"why":"Or in one cell, In, and separate rows all give OR. And in one cell returns nothing; Like \"F*\" returns only genres starting with F.","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"Boundary check","q":"<p>Select EVERY Island Reads book returned by Price: Between 21 And 26.</p>","options":["Front Street Stories ($21.00)","Cricket Legends of the West Indies ($28.00)","Guavaberry Summer ($18.75)","Hurricane Season ($24.50)","Steelpan Rhythms ($26.00)"],"correct":[0, 3, 4],"why":"Between includes both ends, so $21.00 and $26.00 are in; $28.00 and $18.75 are outside.","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"Valid calculated fields","q":"<p>Select EVERY correctly written calculated field.</p>","options":["FullName: [FirstName] & \" \" & [LastName]","Discount: [Price]*0.1","DaysLate: Date()-[DateDue]","Tax: Price*5%","Total = [Qty]*[Price]"],"correct":[0, 1, 2],"why":"Each correct one has Name: and fields in square brackets. = is not the syntax, and Price needs brackets.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Grid rows","q":"<p>Match each Query Design grid row to its job.</p>","left":["Field","Sort","Show","Criteria"],"right":["Chooses which field is used","Orders the results","Displays or hides the column","Sets the condition records must meet"],"why":"These four rows are the heart of every select query.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"SQL clauses","q":"<p>Match each SQL clause to its meaning.</p>","left":["SELECT","FROM","WHERE","ORDER BY"],"right":["The fields to show","The table(s) to use","The conditions","The sort order"],"why":"SELECT what, FROM where, WHERE which, ORDER BY how.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Wildcards","q":"<p>Match each Access wildcard to what it matches.</p>","left":["*","?","#","[A-C]"],"right":["Any number of characters","Exactly one character","Exactly one digit","One character from A to C"],"why":"Access uses *, ? and #; standard SQL uses % and _.","fb":[]}]},{"id":"q_s6u4","title":"Section 6 · Forms and reports quiz","desc":"<p>Topic quiz for Section 6: Forms and reports. 22 items.</p>","minutes":0,"items":[{"t":"mc","title":"Purpose of a form","q":"<p>A receptionist at a Portsmouth dive shop enters new customers every day. Which Access object is designed for this?</p>","options":["A macro","A form","A report","A query"],"correct":1,"why":"Forms are designed for entering and viewing data, usually one record at a time.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Where data is saved","q":"<p>Shanice types a new member into frmMember and closes the form. Where is the new record stored?</p>","options":["Nowhere until she prints it","In the table the form is based on","In the report","In the form"],"correct":1,"why":"Forms do not store data; the record is saved in the underlying table.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Columnar layout","q":"<p>In the Form Wizard, which layout shows one record at a time with each field on its own line and its label on the left?</p>","options":["Columnar","Tabular","Justified","Datasheet"],"correct":0,"why":"Columnar shows one record with fields in a column.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Drop-down","q":"<p>The Antigua Sailing Club wants members to choose their boat class from a fixed list on the entry form. Which control should be used?</p>","options":["Command button","Label","Combo box","Image"],"correct":2,"why":"A combo box (drop-down list) restricts choices and reduces typing errors.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Subform requirement","q":"<p>Rohan's Form Wizard does not offer the option \"Form with subform(s)\" for his tblTeam and tblPlayer. What is the MOST likely reason?</p>","options":["He chose the Columnar layout","The tables have primary keys","The tables have too many records","There is no relationship between the tables"],"correct":3,"why":"The Wizard needs a one-to-many relationship to create a subform.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Main and sub","q":"<p>In a school database, a main form shows one form class. What should its subform show?</p>","options":["All the form classes","The students in that form class","The report title","The school's logo"],"correct":1,"why":"The subform shows the related many-side records for the main record.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Transaction input","q":"<p>Canefield Book Exchange wants to record loans quickly with almost no typing. Which method is BEST?</p>","options":["Write loans on paper and type them weekly","Scan barcodes on the member card and the book","Use OCR on handwritten slips","Type each loan into the table's datasheet"],"correct":1,"why":"Barcode scanning captures the IDs instantly and accurately; the date can come from the system clock.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Report purpose","q":"<p>Which statement about Access reports is TRUE?</p>","options":["Reports can only use one table","Reports present data from tables or queries in a formatted way for screen, printer or file","Reports store data permanently","Reports are used to enter new records"],"correct":1,"why":"Reports are output objects; they can be based on multi-table queries.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Wizard order","q":"<p>In the Report Wizard, on which step do you find the Summary Options button?</p>","options":["Choosing fields","Choosing the title","Sort order for detail records","Grouping levels"],"correct":2,"why":"Summary Options is on the sorting step.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Grand total","q":"<p>In the Mahaut Hotel's booking report, where should a text box showing the total income for ALL bookings be placed?</p>","options":["Report Footer","Group Footer","Page Header","Detail"],"correct":0,"why":"A grand total prints once at the end: the Report Footer.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Group subtotal","q":"<p>A Carnival troupe report is grouped by Section. Where should =Sum([Fee]) go to show the total fees for each section?</p>","options":["Detail","Page Footer","Section (group) Footer","Report Header"],"correct":2,"why":"Per-group totals go in the group footer.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Counting","q":"<p>Which expression counts the number of records in a group in an Access report?</p>","options":["=Count(*)","=Total()","=Sum(*)","=Number([ID])"],"correct":0,"why":"Count(*) counts records.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Specific title","q":"<p>Which report title would earn the most marks in a CSEC SBA?</p>","options":["Report1","rptLoans","Island Reads Library: Overdue Loans, October 2026","Database report"],"correct":2,"why":"A specific title states who, what and when.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Print preview","q":"<p>Before printing her SBA report, Kayla wants to see exactly how each page will look. Which view should she use?</p>","options":["Print Preview","SQL View","Design View","Datasheet View"],"correct":0,"why":"Print Preview shows the pages exactly as they will print.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Error in report","q":"<p>A report shows #Error where =Sum([Price]) was placed in the Page Footer. What should the student do?</p>","options":["Delete the report","Move the text box to the Report Footer","Change Sum to Add","Change the field to Text"],"correct":1,"why":"Aggregate functions do not work in page sections.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Name the feature","q":"<p>The Island Reads librarian describes three jobs. Name the database feature that does each one.</p>","left":["Display records from a table that meet a particular criterion","Present information retrieved from tables in a presentable manner","Type data into the database using a specially designed template rather than straight into a table"],"right":["Query","Report","Form"],"why":"Query finds, report presents, form enters.","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"Form advantages","q":"<p>The King George V Street Pharmacy switches from typing into a datasheet to a data entry form. Select EVERY genuine advantage.</p>","options":["The form stores a second copy of the data","Validation rules no longer apply","Drop-down lists reduce spelling errors","Only the needed fields are shown","Clear labels and instructions for each field"],"correct":[2, 3, 4],"why":"Forms do not store data, and table validation still applies.","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"Summary features","q":"<p>Select EVERY summary option offered by the Report Wizard's Summary Options for a numeric field.</p>","options":["Min","Max","Avg","Sum","Median"],"correct":[0, 1, 2, 3],"why":"The Wizard offers Sum, Avg, Min and Max. Median is not an option.","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"Report outputs","q":"<p>Select EVERY way an Access report can be output.</p>","options":["Typed into by the user to change data","Printed on paper","Saved as a PDF file","On screen in Print Preview"],"correct":[1, 2, 3],"why":"Reports go to screen, printer or file; they cannot be used to edit data.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Objects and jobs","q":"<p>Match each Access object to its main job.</p>","left":["Table","Query","Form","Report"],"right":["Stores data","Selects and calculates data","Enters and views data on screen","Presents formatted output"],"why":"Store, ask, enter, present.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Report sections","q":"<p>Match each report section to when it prints.</p>","left":["Report Header","Page Header","Detail","Report Footer"],"right":["Once at the start","Top of every page","Once for every record","Once at the end"],"why":"Header and footer of the report print once; page sections every page; detail per record.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Controls","q":"<p>Match each form control to its use.</p>","left":["Label","Text box","Check box","Command button"],"right":["Fixed text such as a title","Shows or accepts a field's value","Yes/No values","Runs an action like Save"],"why":"Each control has one main job.","fb":[]}]}]},{"pkg":"Topic Quizzes · Section 7","quizzes":[{"id":"q_s7u1","title":"Section 7 · Problem-solving steps and divide and conquer quiz","desc":"<p>Topic quiz for Section 7: Problem-solving steps and divide and conquer. 25 items.</p>","minutes":0,"items":[{"t":"mc","title":"First step","q":"<p>Before writing any code for a new hurricane shelter registration system in Gros Islet, the programmer interviews the shelter manager to find out what data must be captured and what reports are needed. Which problem-solving step is this?</p>","options":["Develop the algorithm","Select the optimum solution","Test and validate the solution","Define the problem"],"correct":3,"why":"Finding out what is needed is defining the problem: you must first understand the problem.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"After choosing","q":"<p>A Form 5 team has just selected a Microsoft Form as the optimum way to collect lunch orders. According to the problem-solving steps, what should they do NEXT?</p>","options":["Develop and represent the algorithm","Validate the solution","Propose other solutions","Define the problem"],"correct":0,"why":"After selecting the optimum solution, you develop and represent the algorithm.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which step?","q":"<p>Your teacher tells her class: 'The problem must be interpreted and understood clearly.' To which problem-solving step does this statement refer?</p>","options":["Analyse the problem","Select the optimum solution","Test and validate the solution","Define the problem"],"correct":0,"why":"Interpreting and understanding the problem clearly (often with an IPO chart) is analysing it.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"What is a problem?","q":"<p>A Form 4 student is asked to define the word 'problem' for a Paper 02 question. Which of the following is the best definition?</p>","options":["A program that will not compile","A list of instructions for a computer","A diagram with Input, Processing and Output","A question or situation that motivates a person to search for a solution"],"correct":3,"why":"A problem is a question that motivates a person to search for a solution.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"IPO chart purpose","q":"<p>Kayla draws a three-column table for her tuck shop project to summarise the problem for Ms. Daniels. What is the main purpose of this IPO chart?</p>","options":["To test the program for errors","To store the data permanently","To show the modules as a hierarchy","To break the problem into its three main parts: input, processing and output"],"correct":3,"why":"An IPO chart breaks a problem into Input, Processing and Output.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Most efficient","q":"<p>A small bakery in Rodney Bay wants to total daily sales. Options: (A) a calculator and notebook, (B) a free spreadsheet on the laptop it already owns, (C) a custom point-of-sale system costing $4,000. Sales are about 60 per day. Which is MOST efficient?</p>","options":["A, because it needs no electricity","B, because it is free, fast and accurate for 60 sales a day","C, because it is the most advanced","All are equally efficient"],"correct":1,"why":"B fits the volume and costs nothing extra; C is overkill and A is slower and error-prone.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Validation","q":"<p>A program that calculates LUCELEC bills gives the correct answer for every test case, but customers complain that it does not show how many kWh they used, which they need to check their bills. Which problem-solving activity was done poorly?</p>","options":["Validating the solution against the users' needs","Testing","Choosing a programming language","Drawing a flowchart"],"correct":0,"why":"Results were correct (testing passed), but the solution did not meet the users' real needs (validation).","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Top-down meaning","q":"<p>Ms. Hodge tells her Form 4 class to use top-down design for their tuck shop project. What does she want them to do?</p>","options":["Write the program from the last line to the first","Start coding the hardest part first","Draw the flowchart before defining the problem","Break the whole problem into smaller sub-problems and refine each one"],"correct":3,"why":"Top-down design starts with the whole and breaks it into smaller parts.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Structure chart top","q":"<p>In a structure chart for a Soufrière marina's booking system, what does the single box at the very top represent?</p>","options":["The most difficult sub-task","The final output","The whole problem (main module)","The first line of code"],"correct":2,"why":"The top box is the whole problem.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Not in a structure chart","q":"<p>Devon is drawing a structure chart for a cricket scoring app for the St. Lucian Cricket League. Which of the following should NOT appear on it?</p>","options":["A diamond asking 'Is the batter out?'","A box called 'Update scoreboard'","A box called 'Record runs'","A line joining 'Score match' to 'Record runs'"],"correct":0,"why":"Structure charts show modules only; decisions belong in flowcharts.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Benefit of decomposition","q":"<p>A team at a Castries software company splits a hotel booking app into modules. Which is a benefit of this approach?</p>","options":["No algorithm is needed","The program will always run faster","Different programmers can work on different modules at the same time","The program no longer needs testing"],"correct":2,"why":"Parallel teamwork is a key benefit; testing is still needed.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Stepwise refinement","q":"<p>A student refines 'Calculate a student's average' into: 'Add the three test scores' and 'Divide the total by 3'. What is this process called?</p>","options":["Verification","Validation","Stepwise refinement","Compilation"],"correct":2,"why":"Breaking a step into more detailed steps is stepwise refinement.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Representation","q":"<p>A teacher asks for the algorithm 'as a diagram using standard symbols such as diamonds and parallelograms'. Which representation is required?</p>","options":["Pseudocode","Flowchart","Narrative","Structure chart"],"correct":1,"why":"Diamonds and parallelograms are flowchart symbols.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Constraint","q":"<p>An Antigua charity wants a donation tracker that 'must run on the two old laptops we already have, with no Internet'. In problem definition, what is this?</p>","options":["An output","A module","A constraint","A test case"],"correct":2,"why":"Limits on equipment or connectivity are constraints.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Order check","q":"<p>Which sequence shows the problem-solving steps in the correct order?</p>","options":["Define → Analyse → Propose and evaluate → Select the optimum → Develop algorithm → Test and validate","Define → Develop algorithm → Analyse → Propose → Select → Test","Develop algorithm → Define → Test → Propose → Analyse → Select","Propose → Define → Analyse → Select → Test → Develop algorithm"],"correct":0,"why":"Define, analyse, propose and evaluate, select the optimum, develop the algorithm, test and validate.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Reuse","q":"<p>In a school canteen system, both 'Print receipt' and 'Print daily report' need to send text to the same printer. What does good modular design suggest?</p>","options":["Remove the daily report","Put all code in the main module","Create one 'Send to printer' module used by both","Write the printer code twice"],"correct":2,"why":"A shared module avoids duplicated code.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Testing tool","q":"<p>During the testing step, a student works through her algorithm by hand with sample data, recording each variable's value in a table. What is this table called?</p>","options":["Structure chart","IPO chart","Truth table","Trace table"],"correct":3,"why":"A trace table records variable values during a dry run.","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"Benefits of top-down design","q":"<p>A Barbados software start-up is deciding whether to use top-down design for a cruise passenger app. Select EVERY genuine benefit.</p>","options":["Modules can be tested separately","It removes the need for an algorithm","Errors are easier to locate","Work can be shared among programmers","The finished program cannot contain errors"],"correct":[0, 2, 3],"why":"Separate testing, teamwork and easier error-finding are real benefits; it does not remove the need for algorithms or guarantee zero errors.","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"Defining the problem","q":"<p>For a LUCELEC bill calculator, select EVERY item that belongs in the problem definition.</p>","options":["Input: kWh used this month","The colour of the program's buttons","Output: amount due","Constraint: must produce bills for 20,000 customers overnight","Processing: kWh × rate + service charge"],"correct":[0, 2, 3, 4],"why":"Inputs, outputs, processing and constraints define the problem; button colour is a design detail.","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"Evaluation criteria","q":"<p>Select EVERY criterion that is sensible for evaluating proposed solutions to a school attendance problem.</p>","options":["Which option the programmer's best friend prefers","Ease of use for teachers","Speed","Cost","Reliability during power cuts"],"correct":[1, 2, 3, 4],"why":"Personal preference is not an objective evaluation criterion.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Step to activity","q":"<p>Match each problem-solving step to an activity from a Trinidad taxi-booking project.</p>","left":["Analyse the problem","Propose and evaluate solutions","Select the optimum solution","Develop the algorithm","Test and validate"],"right":["List the inputs: pickup point, destination, number of passengers","Compare an SMS service with a WhatsApp bot and a web app","Choose the WhatsApp bot because drivers already use WhatsApp","Write pseudocode to calculate the fare","Run sample bookings and ask drivers if it works for them"],"why":"Each activity matches the purpose of its step.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Vocabulary","q":"<p>Match each term to its meaning.</p>","left":["Module","Decomposition","Stepwise refinement","Structure chart"],"right":["A self-contained part of a solution","Breaking a problem into smaller parts","Adding more detail to each step, level by level","A diagram showing modules as a hierarchy"],"why":"These are the core top-down design terms.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Module to language","q":"<p>Match each way of writing a reusable module to its language or tool.</p>","left":["Procedure ShowMenu;","def calculate_total(price, qty):","[define deliver supplies]","PROCEDURE PrintReceipt … ENDPROCEDURE"],"right":["Pascal","Python","Scratch-style blocks","Pseudocode"],"why":"Each representation has its own way to name a module.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Symptom vs problem","q":"<p>Students complain that the Wi-Fi at a St. Lucian school is 'slow at lunch'. An IT student investigates and finds 400 phones share one access point. Which statement is the better problem definition?</p>","options":["'The Wi-Fi is slow'","'Ban phones at lunch'","'Buy faster Internet'","'One access point cannot serve 400 devices at lunchtime; coverage must support all users with acceptable speed'"],"correct":3,"why":"A good definition states the root cause and a measurable requirement; the others are a symptom or jump to solutions.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Refinement level","q":"<p>In the refinement 1 Take order → 1.2 Get items → 1.2.1 Scan item barcode, what level is 'Scan item barcode' if 'Tuck Shop System' is level 0?</p>","options":["Level 1","Level 4","Level 2","Level 3"],"correct":3,"why":"Tuck Shop System (0) → Take order (1) → Get items (2) → Scan item barcode (3).","fb":[]}]},{"id":"q_s7u2","title":"Section 7 · Defining problems: IPO, variables, constants and data types quiz","desc":"<p>Topic quiz for Section 7: Defining problems: IPO, variables, constants and data types. 26 items.</p>","minutes":0,"items":[{"t":"mc","title":"IPO input","q":"<p>A program for a Five Cays bakery reads the number of croissants sold and the price of one croissant, then displays the day's croissant income. Which is an INPUT?</p>","options":["Price of one croissant","Multiply number by price","Display the income","Day's croissant income"],"correct":0,"why":"The price is supplied to the program; the income is output.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Processing column","q":"<p>In the IPO chart for a Bon Bini Power & Water bill (bill = units × rate + service charge), which entry belongs in the PROCESSING column?</p>","options":["units","bill","Customer name","Calculate bill = units × rate + service charge"],"correct":3,"why":"Calculations are processing.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Variable definition","q":"<p>A teacher describes 'a named storage location in memory whose value can change while the program runs'. What is she describing?</p>","options":["An algorithm","A data type","A variable","A constant"],"correct":2,"why":"That is the definition of a variable.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Constant example","q":"<p>A program calculates the area of circular water tanks for a St. Kitts hardware store. Which value should be a constant?</p>","options":["Customer name","Radius of each tank","PI (3.14159)","Area of each tank"],"correct":2,"why":"PI never changes; the others vary.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Assignment result","q":"<p>What is the value of points after these steps? points ← 10, points ← points + 5, points ← points × 2</p>","options":["20","15","30","10"],"correct":2,"why":"10 → 15 → 30.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Invalid identifier","q":"<p>A student in Antigua writes these Python variable names for a cricket app. Which is INVALID?</p>","options":["6overs","_temp","Over6","runs_total"],"correct":0,"why":"Identifiers cannot start with a digit.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Data type: real","q":"<p>A Barbados fish market records the weight of each catch, for example 12.75 kg. Which data type should it use?</p>","options":["Real","Integer","Character","Boolean"],"correct":0,"why":"Weights have decimal parts.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Data type: string","q":"<p>The tourism office (as an illustrative example) stores visitors' phone numbers such as +1 284 555 0144. The BEST data type is:</p>","options":["String","Integer, because it contains digits","Real","Boolean"],"correct":0,"why":"Phone numbers contain symbols and are not used in arithmetic.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Data type: character","q":"<p>A school stores each student's house as one letter: R, B, G or Y. The most suitable data type is:</p>","options":["String","Integer","Character","Real"],"correct":2,"why":"One symbol: Character.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Boolean values","q":"<p>Which pair lists the ONLY possible values of a Boolean variable?</p>","options":["0 and 100","Yes and Maybe","TRUE and FALSE","A and B"],"correct":2,"why":"Booleans hold TRUE or FALSE.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Python input type","q":"<p>Shanice writes in Python: a = input() and b = input(), then print(a + b). She types 4 and 6. What is printed?</p>","options":["An error","4 6","46","10"],"correct":2,"why":"input() returns strings, so + joins them: \"46\".","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Pascal declaration","q":"<p>In a Pascal program for a Jamaican bus company, which line correctly declares a variable to hold the fare, such as 2.75?</p>","options":["fare : real;","fare = 2.75;","fare : integer;","fare : boolean;"],"correct":0,"why":"Fares have decimals: real.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Hidden input","q":"<p>Problem: 'Calculate the average of the marks entered for a class.' The number of students differs each time. What must the algorithm also obtain?</p>","options":["The highest mark","The teacher's name","Nothing else","The number of marks (or a stop value to end input)"],"correct":3,"why":"To divide and to know when input ends, you need a count or a sentinel.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Why constants","q":"<p>Why should a programmer write VAT_RATE ← 0.15 once at the top instead of typing 0.15 in every formula? (Illustrative rate.)</p>","options":["If the rate changes, only one line needs editing, and formulas are easier to read","Constants use no memory","It is required by every language","It makes the program run faster"],"correct":0,"why":"Maintenance and readability are the reasons.","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"Valid identifiers","q":"<p>Select EVERY valid Pascal identifier for a Carnival app.</p>","options":["begin","total2","costume-size","bandName","deposit_paid"],"correct":[1, 3, 4],"why":"Hyphens are not allowed and 'begin' is a reserved word.","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"Outputs","q":"<p>Problem: 'Read a student's name and three test scores; display the name, the average and PASS if the average is 50 or more.' Select EVERY output.</p>","options":["name","calculate the average","PASS (when earned)","the three test scores","average"],"correct":[0, 2, 4],"why":"Scores are inputs; calculating is processing.","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"Should be integers","q":"<p>Select EVERY value that is best stored as an integer.</p>","options":["Average rainfall in mm","Number of hurricanes this season","Number of goals scored","Number of students in Form 4B","Price of a bake-and-saltfish"],"correct":[1, 2, 3],"why":"Counts are whole numbers; prices and averages need decimals.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Types in Python and Pascal","q":"<p>Match each CSEC data type to its Python name.</p>","left":["Integer","Real","String","Boolean"],"right":["int","float","str","bool"],"why":"Python's names for the standard types.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"IPO columns","q":"<p>Match each item from a cinema ticket problem (a cinema in Little Bay) to its IPO column.</p>","left":["Number of tickets","Total = tickets × price","Total cost shown on screen","Ticket price fixed at $12"],"right":["Input","Processing","Output","Constant (not an input)"],"why":"Typed data is input, calculation is processing, displayed result is output, fixed price is a constant.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Assignment in each form","q":"<p>Match each representation to how it writes 'add 1 to count'.</p>","left":["Pseudocode","Python","Pascal","Blocks"],"right":["count ← count + 1","count = count + 1","count := count + 1;","[change count by 1]"],"why":"The same assignment in four forms.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Storage","q":"<p>A shelter registration program must still have the list of registered families after the laptop is restarted. What must the IPO chart's storage column include?</p>","options":["A Boolean","Only variables in RAM","Saving the data to a file or database on secondary storage","A constant"],"correct":2,"why":"RAM is volatile; persistent data needs a file or database.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Leading zeros","q":"<p>A Trinidad school stores student numbers like 000845 as integers. What problem will this cause?</p>","options":["The program cannot run","The numbers become decimals","The numbers will be stored as 845, losing the leading zeros","Nothing"],"correct":2,"why":"Integers do not keep leading zeros; use a string.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Literal type","q":"<p>In the revision sheet, the value '335' appears in single quotation marks in a pseudocode algorithm for an Albina shop. What data type is it?</p>","options":["String","Character","Real","Integer"],"correct":0,"why":"Several characters inside quotes form a string, even if they are digits.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Literal or variable","q":"<p>Which of the following items from a pseudocode algorithm is a VARIABLE rather than a literal constant?</p>","options":["salary","'tom'","94.4","'x'"],"correct":0,"why":"salary is a name that holds a value; the others are literal values.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Identifier definition","q":"<p>A student writes: 'Num1, Sum, Total_Cost and XYZ10 are all ________.' Which word completes the sentence correctly?</p>","options":["Keywords","Identifiers","Operators","Literals"],"correct":1,"why":"Identifiers are the names given to program elements such as variables and constants.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"IPO processing pattern","q":"<p>Match each line of the Processing column for 'calculate and display a 17% VAT on the price of an item' to its role.</p>","left":["Accept Price","Calculate VAT (Price * 0.17)","Store results in VAT","Display VAT"],"right":["Input step","Calculation with its formula","Storage in a variable","Output step"],"why":"The class pattern: Accept, Calculate, Store results in, Display.","fb":[]}]},{"id":"q_s7u3","title":"Section 7 · Algorithms and pseudocode quiz","desc":"<p>Topic quiz for Section 7: Algorithms and pseudocode. 31 items.</p>","minutes":0,"items":[{"t":"mc","title":"Algorithm property","q":"<p>A Form 4 student's directions to the Basseterre ferry dock say 'Walk until you feel tired, then turn'. Which property of an algorithm does this break?</p>","options":["It has too many steps","It has no output","It uses a loop","It is not precise/unambiguous"],"correct":3,"why":"'Until you feel tired' means different things to different people.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Terminates","q":"<p>Which instruction would stop an algorithm from terminating?</p>","options":["IF x > 0 THEN Write x ENDIF","WHILE x > 0 DO x ← x + 1 ENDWHILE (x starts at 5)","REPEAT … UNTIL count = 10 (count increases by 1 each pass from 0)","FOR i = 1 TO 100 DO"],"correct":1,"why":"x starts positive and only grows, so the WHILE condition is always TRUE.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Assignment symbol","q":"<p>In CXC pseudocode, what does the line total ← total + price do?</p>","options":["Compares total with total + price","Declares a constant","Prints the total","Adds price to the current total and stores the result in total"],"correct":3,"why":"← is assignment: evaluate the right, store on the left.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"MOD","q":"<p>A Barbados school puts students into groups of 4. With 23 students, which expression gives the number of students left over?</p>","options":["23 MOD 4","4 MOD 23","23 DIV 4","23 / 4"],"correct":0,"why":"23 MOD 4 = 3 (23 = 5 × 4 + 3).","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"DIV","q":"<p>Using the same 23 students in groups of 4, what is 23 DIV 4?</p>","options":["5","3","6","5.75"],"correct":0,"why":"DIV gives the whole-number part: 5 full groups.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"IF-THEN-ELSE output","q":"<p>Algorithm: Read temp; IF temp > 30 THEN Write \"Hot\" ELSE Write \"Pleasant\" ENDIF. At Bradshaw Airport the reading is exactly 30. What is output?</p>","options":["Hot","Nothing","Both","Pleasant"],"correct":3,"why":"30 > 30 is FALSE, so the ELSE branch runs.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Boundary wording","q":"<p>A Carnival competition is open to people aged 'at least 16'. Which condition is correct?</p>","options":["age >= 16","age > 16","age < 16","age = 16"],"correct":0,"why":"'At least 16' includes 16.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"AND","q":"<p>A scholarship at a Jamaican college needs an average of at least 80 AND attendance of at least 90%. Which student qualifies?</p>","options":["Average 95, attendance 70%","Average 85, attendance 88%","Average 79, attendance 99%","Average 80, attendance 90%"],"correct":3,"why":"Both conditions must be TRUE; 80 and 90 meet the 'at least' boundaries.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"OR","q":"<p>A museum in Basseterre gives free entry IF age < 12 OR age >= 65. Who pays?</p>","options":["A 40-year-old","A 65-year-old","A 7-year-old","An 80-year-old"],"correct":0,"why":"Only the 40-year-old makes both parts FALSE.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"ELSE IF order","q":"<p>A programmer writes: IF score >= 50 THEN grade ← \"C\" ELSE IF score >= 65 THEN grade ← \"B\" ELSE IF score >= 80 THEN grade ← \"A\" ENDIF. What grade does a score of 90 get?</p>","options":["None","C","B","A"],"correct":1,"why":"90 >= 50 is TRUE first, so it gets C. The tests are in the wrong order.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"FOR count","q":"<p>How many times does the body of FOR k = 3 TO 8 DO run?</p>","options":["8","3","5","6"],"correct":3,"why":"3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8: six times.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Accumulator init","q":"<p>In an algorithm that adds up the sales of a Fort Street T-shirt stall, where should total ← 0 be placed?</p>","options":["Before the loop","Inside the loop, first line","It is not needed","After the loop"],"correct":0,"why":"Initialise once, before the loop.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Sentinel","q":"<p>An algorithm reads the weights of fish landed at Frigate Bay until the value -1 is entered. What is -1 called?</p>","options":["A counter","A constant","An accumulator","A sentinel value"],"correct":3,"why":"A special value that signals the end of the data.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"REPEAT minimum","q":"<p>What is the minimum number of times the body of a REPEAT … UNTIL loop runs?</p>","options":["0","2","1","It depends on the FOR counter"],"correct":2,"why":"The condition is tested after the body.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Trace a loop","q":"<p>What is output? x ← 2; FOR i = 1 TO 3 DO x ← x * 2 ENDFOR; Write x</p>","options":["6","16","4","8"],"correct":1,"why":"2 → 4 → 8 → 16.","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"Control structures","q":"<p>Select EVERY one of the three basic control structures.</p>","options":["Validation","Iteration","Selection","Compilation","Sequence"],"correct":[1, 2, 4],"why":"Sequence, selection and iteration.","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"Loops that suit unknown counts","q":"<p>Select EVERY loop structure that is suitable when the number of repetitions is NOT known in advance.</p>","options":["FOR … TO … ENDFOR","IF … THEN … ENDIF","WHILE … ENDWHILE","REPEAT … UNTIL"],"correct":[2, 3],"why":"WHILE and REPEAT are condition-controlled; FOR is count-controlled; IF is not a loop.","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"Even number tests","q":"<p>Select EVERY condition that is TRUE only when n is even (n is a positive integer).</p>","options":["n MOD 2 = 0","n MOD 2 = 1","NOT (n MOD 2 = 1)","n DIV 2 = 0"],"correct":[0, 2],"why":"Even numbers have remainder 0 when divided by 2.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Keyword pairs","q":"<p>Match each opening keyword to its closing keyword.</p>","left":["IF … THEN","FOR … DO","WHILE … DO","REPEAT"],"right":["ENDIF","ENDFOR","ENDWHILE","UNTIL"],"why":"Every structure must be closed.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Patterns","q":"<p>Match each loop pattern to its typical line of pseudocode.</p>","left":["Counter","Accumulator","Highest so far","Sentinel test"],"right":["count ← count + 1","total ← total + value","IF value > highest THEN highest ← value","WHILE value <> -1 DO"],"why":"The four standard loop patterns.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Operators","q":"<p>Match each pseudocode operator to its meaning.</p>","left":["<>","MOD","DIV",">="],"right":["not equal to","remainder after division","whole-number division","greater than or equal to"],"why":"CXC pseudocode operators.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Priming read","q":"<p>In a WHILE loop that reads scores until -1, why is there a Read before the loop as well as inside it?</p>","options":["So the condition has a value to test the first time","To make the loop run at least twice","To read every score twice","Because WHILE always needs two inputs"],"correct":0,"why":"The first (priming) read gives the condition something to test.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Bounded loop count","q":"<p>The quiz asks: how many times will the statement FOR NUM = 1 TO 20 DO repeat, in a program printing raffle tickets for the Frangipani Hill fun day?</p>","options":["21","19","1","20"],"correct":3,"why":"1 to 20 inclusive is 20 repetitions.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"STEP count","q":"<p>A Carnival Village light show program uses FOR NUM = 10 TO 100 STEP 10 DO. How many times does the loop body run?</p>","options":["100","11","10","9"],"correct":2,"why":"10, 20, 30 … 100: ten values.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Starting at zero","q":"<p>How many times will this loop write ### on a Sandy Point restaurant receipt? For Count = 0 to 3 do Write (\"###\")</p>","options":["4","Infinitely","3","0"],"correct":0,"why":"0, 1, 2, 3: four times.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Bounded vs unbounded","q":"<p>A program reads cruise passenger counts until the operator types 0. What kind of iteration is this?</p>","options":["Sequence","Selection","Unbounded iteration","Bounded iteration"],"correct":2,"why":"The number of repetitions depends on a condition, not a fixed count.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"BODMAS","q":"<p>In pseudocode for a Jamaican hardware store, Total ← 10 + 4 * 3 is evaluated. What is stored in Total?</p>","options":["22","17","34","42"],"correct":0,"why":"Multiplication before addition: 4 * 3 = 12, then 10 + 12 = 22.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Truth table","q":"<p>Using Boolean logic, what is the value of TRUE AND FALSE?</p>","options":["TRUE","FALSE","1","It cannot be worked out"],"correct":1,"why":"AND is TRUE only when both parts are TRUE.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Prompting statement","q":"<p>In an algorithm for a Barbados library, which statement is a PROMPTING statement?</p>","options":["IF Days > 14 THEN","Fine ← Days * 0.50","Read Name","Write \"Enter the borrower's name\""],"correct":3,"why":"A prompt uses Write to ask the user to enter data.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Statement types","q":"<p>Match each pseudocode keyword or structure to its statement type, as in the unit test.</p>","left":["Read","Write (showing a result)","IF … ELSE … ENDIF","FOR … ENDFOR","WHILE … ENDWHILE"],"right":["Input statement","Output statement","Selection statement","Bounded iteration statement","Unbounded iteration statement"],"why":"Each keyword belongs to one kind of statement.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Assignment symbols","q":"<p>Match each assignment symbol to where it is used.</p>","left":["←",":=","= (on a line by itself)"],"right":["Pseudocode","Pascal","Python (and some pseudocode and flowcharts)"],"why":"← in pseudocode, := in Pascal, = in Python.","fb":[]}]},{"id":"q_s7u4","title":"Section 7 · Flowcharts quiz","desc":"<p>Topic quiz for Section 7: Flowcharts. 22 items.</p>","minutes":0,"items":[{"t":"mc","title":"Input symbol","q":"<p>A flowchart for a Barbados car-park machine must show the step 'read the number of hours parked'. Which symbol should be used?</p>","options":["Diamond","Terminator","Parallelogram","Rectangle"],"correct":2,"why":"Input uses the parallelogram.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Process symbol","q":"<p>In a flowchart for a LUCELEC bill, which symbol should contain 'bill ← units * rate'?</p>","options":["Parallelogram","Rectangle","Diamond","Connector"],"correct":1,"why":"Assignments and calculations go in rectangles.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Decision exits","q":"<p>How many exits should a decision symbol have in a CSEC flowchart?</p>","options":["One","Three","As many as needed","Two, labelled Yes and No"],"correct":3,"why":"Two labelled exits.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Terminator","q":"<p>Which symbol marks the beginning and end of a flowchart for the St. Lucian Carnival ticket app?</p>","options":["Circle","Rectangle","Rounded rectangle (terminator)","Diamond"],"correct":2,"why":"Terminators mark Start and Stop.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Connector use","q":"<p>Devon's flowchart for a Trinidad bus schedule reaches the bottom of the page halfway through. What should he use to continue on the next page?</p>","options":["A second Start","A longer arrow off the page","A diamond","A connector with a matching label"],"correct":3,"why":"Matching connectors join parts of a long flowchart.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Loop recognition","q":"<p>In a flowchart, an arrow from the bottom of a rectangle returns to a diamond higher up. What does this show?</p>","options":["An error","Selection","Sequence","Iteration (loop)"],"correct":3,"why":"Arrows going back up show repetition.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Post-test loop","q":"<p>In a flowchart, the diamond comes AFTER the body, and its No exit goes back to the top of the body. Which pseudocode structure is this?</p>","options":["FOR … ENDFOR","REPEAT … UNTIL","WHILE … ENDWHILE","IF … ENDIF"],"correct":1,"why":"A test after the body is post-test: REPEAT-UNTIL.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Trace selection","q":"<p>A flowchart for a Jamaican cinema: Read age; diamond 'age < 12?'; Yes → price ← 8; No → price ← 15; Write price. What is output for age 12?</p>","options":["15","8","Nothing","12"],"correct":0,"why":"12 < 12 is No, so price = 15.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Trace loop","q":"<p>A flowchart sets x ← 1, then: diamond 'x < 20?' Yes → x ← x * 2 → back to diamond; No → Write x. What is output?</p>","options":["32","64","20","16"],"correct":0,"why":"1, 2, 4, 8, 16, 32; 32 < 20 is No.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"FOR in a flowchart","q":"<p>Flowcharts have no FOR symbol. How is FOR i = 1 TO 10 usually drawn?</p>","options":["One rectangle saying FOR","i ← 1; diamond 'i <= 10?'; body; i ← i + 1; arrow back to the diamond","A diamond only","A connector labelled FOR"],"correct":1,"why":"Initialise, test, body, increment, loop back.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Wrong symbol","q":"<p>Which of the following is drawn INCORRECTLY?</p>","options":["Write total in a parallelogram","Stop in a terminator","total ← 0 in a rectangle","Read name in a rectangle"],"correct":3,"why":"Read belongs in a parallelogram.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Selection vs loop","q":"<p>A diamond's Yes and No paths both continue downwards and meet before Stop. This represents:</p>","options":["A REPEAT loop","An IF-THEN-ELSE","A connector","A WHILE loop"],"correct":1,"why":"Branches that rejoin below are selection.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Decision count","q":"<p>A flowchart assigns one of five shirt sizes (S, M, L, XL, XXL) by chest measurement using chained decisions. What is the minimum number of decision symbols needed?</p>","options":["6","3","4","5"],"correct":2,"why":"n outcomes need n − 1 chained decisions.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Flowchart advantage","q":"<p>Why might a systems analyst show a flowchart, rather than pseudocode, to the manager of an Antigua hotel?</p>","options":["The visual flow of decisions and loops is easier for non-programmers to follow","Flowcharts cannot contain errors","Flowcharts run faster","Pseudocode cannot show loops"],"correct":0,"why":"Flowcharts are visual and easy to follow.","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"Symbols in a WHILE loop","q":"<p>Select EVERY symbol you would expect in a flowchart of a WHILE loop that reads and totals scores until -1.</p>","options":["Parallelogram","Diamond","Terminator","Rectangle","Predefined process (required)"],"correct":[0, 1, 2, 3],"why":"The predefined process is optional; the other four are needed.","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"Flowchart rules","q":"<p>Select EVERY rule of good flowchart drawing.</p>","options":["Draw input in a rectangle","Let lines cross wherever convenient","Make every path reach Stop","Have exactly one Start","Label both exits of every decision"],"correct":[2, 3, 4],"why":"Avoid crossing lines; input uses a parallelogram.","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"Valid diamond text","q":"<p>Select EVERY piece of text that could correctly appear inside a decision symbol.</p>","options":["age >= 18 AND age <= 25?","total > 100?","pin = 4821?","count ← count + 1","Read age"],"correct":[0, 1, 2],"why":"Diamonds contain conditions, not inputs or assignments.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Pseudocode to symbol","q":"<p>Match each pseudocode line to its flowchart symbol.</p>","left":["Start","Read score","total ← total + score","IF score >= 50 THEN"],"right":["Terminator","Parallelogram","Rectangle","Diamond"],"why":"One symbol per kind of statement.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Picture to structure","q":"<p>Match each flowchart pattern to its pseudocode structure.</p>","left":["Diamond above the body, arrow returns to it","Body above a diamond, No returns to the top","Diamond with branches that rejoin below","Counter set, tested, body, counter + 1, back to test"],"right":["WHILE … ENDWHILE","REPEAT … UNTIL","IF … THEN … ELSE … ENDIF","FOR … ENDFOR"],"why":"Position of the diamond and direction of arrows identify the structure.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Symbol shapes","q":"<p>Match each symbol to its shape.</p>","left":["Decision","Input/Output","Process","Connector"],"right":["Diamond","Parallelogram","Rectangle","Small circle"],"why":"Standard flowchart shapes.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Hidden infinite loop","q":"<p>A flowchart: n ← 10; diamond 'n > 0?'; Yes → Write n → back to the diamond; No → Stop. What happens?</p>","options":["Outputs 10 forever (infinite loop)","Outputs 10 to 1","Outputs 10 once","Outputs nothing"],"correct":0,"why":"Nothing in the loop changes n, so the condition stays TRUE.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Scratch repeat until","q":"<p>A Scratch-style program uses [repeat until &lt;score &gt; 100&gt;]. Which CXC pseudocode loop behaves the same way?</p>","options":["FOR score = 1 TO 100","REPEAT … UNTIL score > 100","WHILE NOT (score > 100) DO … ENDWHILE","IF score > 100 THEN"],"correct":2,"why":"Scratch's repeat-until tests first, so it matches WHILE with the condition negated.","fb":[]}]},{"id":"q_s7u5","title":"Section 7 · Testing algorithms: dry runs and trace tables quiz","desc":"<p>Topic quiz for Section 7: Testing algorithms: dry runs and trace tables. 21 items.</p>","minutes":0,"items":[{"t":"mc","title":"Desk checking","q":"<p>Before typing her SBA program, a Breadfruit Valley Secondary student works through her algorithm on paper with sample marks to see if it gives the right averages. What is this called?</p>","options":["Debugging the program","Validation check","Desk checking","Compiling"],"correct":2,"why":"Manually going through an algorithm with test values is desk checking.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Trace table purpose","q":"<p>What is a trace table mainly used for when testing an algorithm for a Trinidad payroll system?</p>","options":["Storing the payroll data","Translating the algorithm into Pascal","Recording the value of each variable step by step to find logic errors","Drawing the structure chart"],"correct":2,"why":"A trace table simulates execution to find logic errors.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Sequence trace","q":"<p>What is output? a ← 5; b ← a * 2; a ← b - a; Write a, b</p>","options":["0, 10","5, 5","5, 10","10, 5"],"correct":2,"why":"b = 10; a = 10 − 5 = 5.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Swap","q":"<p>To swap the values of x and y in an algorithm for an Antigua cricket app, which sequence is correct?</p>","options":["temp ← x; x ← y; y ← temp","x ← y; y ← x","y ← x; x ← y","temp ← x; y ← temp; x ← y"],"correct":0,"why":"A temporary variable keeps the first value safe.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Selection trace","q":"<p>What is output? x ← 7; IF x MOD 2 = 0 THEN Write \"Even\" ELSE Write \"Odd\" ENDIF</p>","options":["Even","Odd","7","1"],"correct":1,"why":"7 MOD 2 = 1, so the condition is FALSE.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"FOR trace","q":"<p>What is the final value of s? s ← 0; FOR i = 1 TO 4 DO s ← s + i ENDFOR</p>","options":["10","4","6","15"],"correct":0,"why":"1 + 2 + 3 + 4 = 10.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Rows for FOR","q":"<p>A student traces FOR i = 3 TO 8 DO using one row per iteration. How many rows should the table have?</p>","options":["5","3","8","6"],"correct":3,"why":"3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8: six rows.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"WHILE boundary","q":"<p>How many times does the body run? p ← 15; WHILE p >= 10 DO p ← p − 1 ENDWHILE</p>","options":["6","10","5","15"],"correct":0,"why":"p = 15, 14, 13, 12, 11, 10 pass the test.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"WHILE output","q":"<p>What is output? n ← 1; WHILE n < 9 DO Write n; n ← n + 2 ENDWHILE</p>","options":["9","1 3 5 7","3 5 7 9","1 3 5 7 9"],"correct":1,"why":"When n = 9, 9 < 9 is FALSE.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Boundary data","q":"<p>A Dominican youth football league accepts players aged 12 to 15. Which is a BOUNDARY test value?</p>","options":["13","40","15","-1"],"correct":2,"why":"15 is at the upper edge.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Erroneous data","q":"<p>A program reads the number of goals scored in a match. Which is ERRONEOUS test data?</p>","options":["0","-2","7","3"],"correct":1,"why":"A negative number of goals is impossible.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Logic error","q":"<p>An algorithm for a Guyana shop should give a discount on orders OVER $100, but uses >= 100. What kind of error is this?</p>","options":["No error","Syntax error","Run-time error","Logic error"],"correct":3,"why":"It runs but gives a wrong result for exactly $100.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Expected results","q":"<p>When writing a test plan, where should the EXPECTED result come from?</p>","options":["The output of the algorithm being tested","The previous tester","Working out the correct answer from the problem statement","A random guess"],"correct":2,"why":"Expected results must be independent of the algorithm.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"MOD DIV trace","q":"<p>What is output? n ← 352; d ← n MOD 10; n ← n DIV 10; Write d, n</p>","options":["35, 2","2, 352","3, 52","2, 35"],"correct":3,"why":"352 MOD 10 = 2; 352 DIV 10 = 35.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Infinite loop","q":"<p>Which loop never ends? (All variables are integers.)</p>","options":["i ← 1; WHILE i <= 5 DO i ← i + 1 ENDWHILE","i ← 10; WHILE i > 0 DO i ← i − 2 ENDWHILE","i ← 1; WHILE i > 0 DO i ← i + 1 ENDWHILE","i ← 1; REPEAT i ← i * 2 UNTIL i > 50"],"correct":2,"why":"i starts positive and only grows, so i > 0 is always TRUE.","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"Test data types","q":"<p>The Mero Beach Swim Club accepts ages 8 to 16 inclusive. Select EVERY boundary test value.</p>","options":["8","-1","12","17","7","16"],"correct":[0, 3, 4, 5],"why":"Boundaries are the edges (8, 16) and values just outside (7, 17).","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"Desk check facts","q":"<p>Select EVERY statement that is true about desk checking.</p>","options":["It needs a computer","It uses test data","It finds syntax errors in Pascal code","It is done on paper","It is done before the algorithm is coded"],"correct":[1, 3, 4],"why":"Desk checks are manual, before coding, with test data; there is no code yet to have syntax errors.","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"Trace mistakes","q":"<p>Select EVERY common mistake when completing a trace table.</p>","options":["Ignoring BODMAS","Using a variable's old value instead of its latest value","Writing one row per iteration","Recording each condition as TRUE or FALSE","Stopping a loop one pass early"],"correct":[0, 1, 4],"why":"Recording conditions and one row per iteration are good practice.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Error types","q":"<p>Match each error to its description.</p>","left":["Syntax error","Logic error","Run-time error"],"right":["Breaks the grammar rules of the language","Runs but gives a wrong result","Crashes while running, e.g. dividing by zero"],"why":"The three kinds of error.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Test data","q":"<p>For a Carnival competition open to ages 16 to 25, match each value to its type.</p>","left":["20","25","-5","26"],"right":["Normal","Boundary (highest valid)","Erroneous (impossible)","Boundary (just outside)"],"why":"Normal, boundary and erroneous data.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Trace outputs","q":"<p>Match each algorithm to its output.</p>","left":["m ← 10; n ← 20; p ← m; m ← n; n ← p; Write m, n","m ← 10; n ← 20; m ← n; n ← m; Write m, n","x ← 3; x ← x * x + 1; Write x"],"right":["20, 10","20, 20","10"],"why":"Swap with temp; broken swap; BODMAS 9 + 1.","fb":[]}]},{"id":"q_s7u6","title":"Section 7 · Thinking in blocks: Scratch-style programming and robots quiz","desc":"<p>Topic quiz for Section 7: Thinking in blocks: Scratch-style programming and robots. 20 items.</p>","minutes":0,"items":[{"t":"mc","title":"Hat block","q":"<p>In a Scratch-style program for a Port of Spain museum guide robot, which block must be at the top of a script for it to run when the green flag is clicked?</p>","options":["[when ▶ clicked]","[forever]","[say [Hello]]","[repeat (10)]"],"correct":0,"why":"Event (hat) blocks start scripts.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"No syntax errors","q":"<p>Why do beginners at a St. Kitts coding club rarely make syntax errors in block-based programming?</p>","options":["Blocks only snap together in valid ways","Blocks cannot contain loops","Blocks run faster","Blocks have no variables"],"correct":0,"why":"The shapes prevent invalid combinations.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Repeat = FOR","q":"<p>Which CXC pseudocode structure is equivalent to the block [repeat (5)]?</p>","options":["IF … THEN","WHILE TRUE DO","FOR i = 1 TO 5 DO … ENDFOR","REPEAT … UNTIL"],"correct":2,"why":"A fixed number of repeats is a bounded FOR loop.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Turning","q":"<p>Kaya faces North. She runs [turn right (90)] twice. Which way does she face?</p>","options":["West","East","North","South"],"correct":3,"why":"North → East → South.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Position","q":"<p>Kaya starts at (1, 1) facing East and runs [drive forward (2)] [turn left (90)] [drive forward (3)]. Where is she?</p>","options":["(3, 4)","(3, 1)","(1, 4)","(4, 3)"],"correct":0,"why":"East 2 → (3,1); turn to North; up 3 → (3,4).","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Sensor as input","q":"<p>In a beach robot program, the block (distance) reports how far away the nearest object is. In an IPO chart, this is:</p>","options":["Output","Processing","Storage","Input"],"correct":3,"why":"Sensors provide input data.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Forever loop","q":"<p>Which pseudocode best represents the block [forever]?</p>","options":["FOR i = 1 TO 100 DO","IF TRUE THEN","WHILE TRUE DO … ENDWHILE","Stop"],"correct":2,"why":"A loop whose condition never becomes FALSE.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Scratch repeat until","q":"<p>A block program uses [repeat until <(weight) = 0>]. Which pseudocode loop matches it exactly?</p>","options":["WHILE weight <> 0 DO … ENDWHILE","REPEAT … UNTIL weight = 0","IF weight = 0 THEN","FOR weight = 1 TO 0"],"correct":0,"why":"Scratch tests first and continues while the condition is FALSE.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Counter block","q":"<p>Which block increases a counter called hits by 1 in a cricket practice robot program?</p>","options":["[set [hits] to (1)]","[say (hits)]","[change [hits] by (1)]","(hits) + (1)"],"correct":2,"why":"change by 1 adds 1 to the current value.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Custom block","q":"<p>A team makes a custom block [define deliver supplies (packs)]. In Pascal this most closely corresponds to:</p>","options":["A procedure with a parameter","A variable","A comment","A constant"],"correct":0,"why":"Custom blocks are modules: procedures.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Line follower","q":"<p>A line-following robot at the Piarco Airport cargo shed uses: if &lt;sees black&gt; then turn left else turn right, inside forever. The sensor reads white three times in a row. What is happening?</p>","options":["It has stopped","It is reversing","It has drifted off the line and is turning right","It is exactly on the line"],"correct":2,"why":"White means the ELSE branch: turn right each time.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Right-hand rule","q":"<p>Using the right-hand rule in a maze, what should Kaya do first if there is NO wall on her right?</p>","options":["Stop","Turn left","Reverse","Turn right and move forward"],"correct":3,"why":"Rule 1: if the right is open, turn right and step.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Debug","q":"<p>A robot should count bottles over 10 squares, but [set bottles to 0] is inside the [repeat (10)] block. What is the effect?</p>","options":["The count is reset every square","It counts twice","The robot stops","It counts correctly"],"correct":0,"why":"Initialisation inside the loop resets the counter.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Event message","q":"<p>Mission control broadcasts 'storm warning'. Which block lets a robot react to it?</p>","options":["[when ▶ clicked]","[forever]","[when I receive (storm warning)]","[wait (1) seconds]"],"correct":2,"why":"Message events start scripts.","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"Concepts in blocks","q":"<p>Select EVERY block that represents iteration.</p>","options":["[forever]","[repeat until < >]","[if < > then]","[say [Hi]]","[repeat (4)]"],"correct":[0, 1, 4],"why":"Loops are iteration; if is selection; say is output.","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"Sensing blocks","q":"<p>Select EVERY block that provides input to a robot program.</p>","options":["[ask [Name?] and wait]","[turn right (90)]","(distance)","&lt;bumper pressed?&gt;","[say [Done]]"],"correct":[0, 2, 3],"why":"Sensors and ask are inputs; turning is processing/motion; say is output.","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"Square shapes","q":"<p>Kaya should drive a closed square and finish facing the way she started. Select EVERY program that does this.</p>","options":["[repeat (3)] [drive forward (2)] [turn right (90)]","[repeat (2)] [drive forward (2)] [turn right (180)]","[repeat (4)] [drive forward (2)] [turn left (90)]","[repeat (4)] [drive forward (2)] [turn right (90)]"],"correct":[2, 3],"why":"Four 90° turns in the same direction close a square; option 4 drives back and forth on a line.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Block to concept","q":"<p>Match each block to its programming concept.</p>","left":["[set [score] to (0)]","[if < > then … else]","[repeat (10)]","[say (score)]"],"right":["Assignment","Selection","Bounded iteration","Output"],"why":"Every block maps onto a core concept.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Block colours","q":"<p>Match each category to its colour in Scratch-style tools.</p>","left":["Events","Motion","Control","Operators"],"right":["Yellow","Blue","Orange","Green"],"why":"The standard palette colours.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Flowchart to block","q":"<p>Match each flowchart pattern to its block.</p>","left":["Diamond whose branches rejoin","Diamond with an arrow back up, exit on Yes","Start terminator","Output parallelogram"],"right":["[if < > then … else]","[repeat until < >]","[when ▶ clicked]","[say …]"],"why":"Each flowchart pattern has a matching block.","fb":[]}]}]},{"pkg":"Topic Quizzes · Section 8","quizzes":[{"id":"q_s8u1","title":"Section 8 · Programming languages and translators quiz","desc":"<p>Topic quiz for Section 8: Programming languages and translators. 25 items.</p>","minutes":0,"items":[{"t":"mc","title":"Only language the CPU runs","q":"<p>Tamara is explaining to her younger brother how his phone runs games. Which programming language can a CPU execute directly, without any translation?</p>","options":["Python","Assembly language","SQL","Machine code"],"correct":3,"why":"Machine code is binary and is the only language the CPU understands directly. Assembly needs an assembler; Python and SQL are high-level and need translation.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Mnemonics","q":"<p>A technician at a Jacmel marina repairs a fuel-pump controller. In its program he sees the instructions LDA, ADD and STA. These are examples of:</p>","options":["Python built-in functions","keywords used in a 4GL","mnemonics used in assembly language","machine-code opcodes written in binary"],"correct":2,"why":"LDA, ADD and STA are short memory aids (mnemonics) used in assembly language, a 2GL.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Machine-dependent","q":"<p>Bay Volt's assembly-language routine works perfectly on its old meter chip but will not run on the new chip from a different manufacturer. This is because assembly language is:</p>","options":["a fourth-generation language","interpreted","machine-dependent","portable"],"correct":2,"why":"Assembly is written for one processor's instruction set, so it is machine-dependent and must be rewritten for a different processor.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"4GL example","q":"<p>The Sauteurs Credit Union wants a list of members whose loans are overdue, written as a single statement that describes the result wanted. Which language is the BEST fit?</p>","options":["Pascal","Machine code","Assembly","SQL"],"correct":3,"why":"SQL is a fourth-generation, non-procedural language: you state what data you want and the database works out how to find it.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Assembler","q":"<p>Kezia writes a program using mnemonics for a small robot kit at Sea-Almond Secondary School. Which translator converts her program into machine code?</p>","options":["Compiler","Assembler","Linker","Interpreter"],"correct":1,"why":"An assembler translates assembly-language mnemonics into machine code.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Compiler output","q":"<p>Andre compiles his Pascal program for a Carnival costume shop with Free Pascal. What does the compiler produce if there are no errors?</p>","options":["A flowchart of the program","A new version of the source code with comments","A list of mnemonics","An executable (object code) file"],"correct":3,"why":"A compiler translates the whole source program into object code, which is saved as an executable file.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Interpreter behaviour","q":"<p>Keisha runs her Python quiz program. The first four questions appear and work, then the program crashes on the fifth question with an error message. This happens because an interpreter:</p>","options":["translates and executes one statement at a time","only translates assembly language","produces an executable before running","translates the whole program before running any of it"],"correct":0,"why":"An interpreter translates and runs one statement at a time, so earlier statements ran before it reached the faulty one.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Why compile for sale","q":"<p>A Kingston software firm sells an inventory program to supermarkets. Why would it distribute a compiled executable rather than the source code?</p>","options":["Compiled programs never contain errors","Customers then need an interpreter","The source code stays private and the program runs faster","Executables are easier for customers to edit"],"correct":2,"why":"Distributing only the executable protects the source code, and compiled code runs quickly without a translator.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"3GL example","q":"<p>Which of these is a third-generation (procedural) language that a student might use for the SBA?</p>","options":["Prolog","Machine code","Pascal","SQL"],"correct":2,"why":"Pascal is a procedural 3GL. Prolog is a 5GL, SQL is a 4GL and machine code is a 1GL.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"5GL feature","q":"<p>A university research team in Trinidad builds an expert system by entering facts such as \"hurricane season runs from June to November\" and rules, then asks the system questions. Which generation of language are they MOST likely using?</p>","options":["4GL","3GL","2GL","5GL"],"correct":3,"why":"Stating facts, rules and constraints and letting the system reason out answers is the hallmark of a 5GL such as Prolog.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Portability","q":"<p>Which statement BEST explains why high-level languages are described as portable?</p>","options":["They need no translator","They run faster than machine code","The same source code can be translated to run on different types of computer","They can be carried on a USB flash drive"],"correct":2,"why":"Portable means machine-independent: the same source can be re-translated for different processors.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Interpreter disadvantage","q":"<p>The St. Kitts Ferry Company runs its booking program through an interpreter. Which is a DISADVANTAGE of this approach?</p>","options":["The program runs more slowly, because each statement is translated every time it runs","The program cannot be changed","It produces too many executable files","Errors cannot be found"],"correct":0,"why":"Interpreted programs are translated during every run, which makes them slower than compiled programs.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"High-level statement","q":"<p>A Form 5 student writes the single Python statement <code>average = total / count</code>. Which statement about it is TRUE?</p>","options":["It will usually be translated into several machine-code instructions","It is machine code","It can be run directly by the CPU","It must be translated by an assembler"],"correct":0,"why":"One high-level statement typically becomes several machine instructions (load, divide, store).","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"HTML trap","q":"<p>Rohan says that HTML is a high-level programming language like Python. Why is he WRONG?</p>","options":["HTML is a 5GL","HTML is a markup language that describes how content is displayed; it has no selection or loops","HTML must be compiled","HTML is a low-level language"],"correct":1,"why":"HTML is a markup language. It structures and formats content but cannot make decisions or repeat steps.","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"Low-level features","q":"<p>Select EVERY statement that is true of low-level languages.</p>","options":["They are the best choice for beginners","They are easier to debug than Python","They can produce very fast, compact code","They are machine-dependent","They give direct control of the hardware"],"correct":[2, 3, 4],"why":"Low-level languages are machine-dependent, fast and compact, and give direct hardware control. They are harder to debug and a poor choice for beginners.","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"Compiler facts","q":"<p>The ICT club at a school in Barbados is comparing translators. Select EVERY statement that is true of a COMPILER.</p>","options":["Reports syntax errors before the program runs","Produces object code that can be saved","Translates the whole program at once","Must be present every time the program runs","Translates assembly language"],"correct":[0, 1, 2],"why":"A compiler translates the whole program, produces saveable object code and reports syntax errors before execution. It is not needed at run time and does not translate assembly.","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"High-level languages","q":"<p>Select EVERY high-level language in this list.</p>","options":["Java","JavaScript","Visual Basic","Assembly","Machine code"],"correct":[0, 1, 2],"why":"Java, Visual Basic and JavaScript are high-level. Assembly and machine code are the two low-level languages.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Generations and examples","q":"<p>Match each generation to an example language.</p>","left":["1GL","2GL","3GL","4GL"],"right":["Machine code","Assembly language","Python","SQL"],"why":"1GL machine code, 2GL assembly, 3GL procedural languages such as Python, 4GL query languages such as SQL.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Translator jobs","q":"<p>Match each translator to its job.</p>","left":["Assembler","Compiler","Interpreter"],"right":["Converts assembly mnemonics into machine code","Converts a whole high-level program into an executable","Converts and runs a high-level program one statement at a time"],"why":"Each translator is defined by what goes in and how it translates.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Acronyms","q":"<p>The revision sheet lists four abbreviations. Match each to its full name.</p>","left":["BASIC","FORTRAN","COBOL","BIT"],"right":["Beginner's All-purpose Symbolic Instruction Code","FORmula TRANslator","COmmon Business-Oriented Language","BInary digiT"],"why":"These expansions are classic short-answer items.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Translators are…","q":"<p>When the Coralstone College technician lists the software on the lab computers, under which heading should she place the Free Pascal compiler?</p>","options":["Application software","System software","Utility hardware","Firmware only"],"correct":1,"why":"Translators (assemblers, compilers and interpreters) are system software, alongside the operating system and utilities.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"C++ type","q":"<p>Jaden reads that a game engine is written in C++. C++ is BEST described as:</p>","options":["a low-level, machine-dependent language","a markup language","a high-level, object-oriented language","a fourth-generation query language"],"correct":2,"why":"C++ is a high-level (3GL) language that adds object-oriented features to C.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Before and after","q":"<p>A program fragment reads <code>ReadLn(num1); ReadLn(num2); Sum := num1 + num2;</code> before translation and appears as rows of 1s and 0s afterwards. What are the two versions called?</p>","options":["Machine code, then assembly","Pseudocode, then flowchart","Source code, then object code","Object code, then source code"],"correct":2,"why":"The program as written is source code; the translated machine-code version is object code.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Procedural exception","q":"<p>Procedural languages belong to every generation of high-level language EXCEPT the generation in which you only state what you want. Which pair of generations is non-procedural?</p>","options":["3GL only","1GL and 2GL","4GL and 5GL","2GL and 3GL"],"correct":2,"why":"3GLs are procedural; 4GLs and 5GLs state what is wanted, not how.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Terms","q":"<p>Match each term to its meaning.</p>","left":["Source code","Object code","Syntax","Opcode"],"right":["The program as written by the programmer","The machine-code output of a compiler","The grammar rules of a language","The part of a machine instruction that says what operation to perform"],"why":"Source code goes in, object code comes out; syntax is the language's grammar; an opcode names the operation.","fb":[]}]},{"id":"q_s8u2","title":"Section 8 · Steps in implementation, errors and testing quiz","desc":"<p>Topic quiz for Section 8: Steps in implementation, errors and testing. 24 items.</p>","minutes":0,"items":[{"t":"mc","title":"First step","q":"<p>Before Kenya can test her program for Sauteurs library, what must she do FIRST?</p>","options":["Link the object code","Execute the program","Create the source code","Maintain the program"],"correct":2,"why":"Implementation starts with creating (typing and saving) the source code.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Linker job","q":"<p>Jaden's Pascal program uses writeln and sqrt. Which program joins his object code with the library routines for these to produce an executable?</p>","options":["Linker","Assembler","Interpreter","Editor"],"correct":0,"why":"The linker combines object code with library routines into a single executable.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Syntax error example","q":"<p>Which line of Python contains a SYNTAX error?</p>","options":["count = count + 1","total = price * 2","print(\"Total:\", total","if total >= 100:"],"correct":2,"why":"The print statement is missing its closing bracket, which breaks Python's grammar rules.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Logic error definition","q":"<p>The New Amsterdam Scooter Rentals program runs without crashing but charges every customer for one day fewer than they rented. This is BEST described as a:</p>","options":["logic error","linking error","syntax error","run-time error"],"correct":0,"why":"The program runs to completion but produces a wrong result: a logic error.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Run-time error example","q":"<p>A program for the Cap-Haïtien Hardware store asks for the number of boxes and divides the total weight by it. Which input would MOST likely cause a run-time error?</p>","options":["12","0","100","1"],"correct":1,"why":"Dividing by 0 is impossible, so the program crashes while running.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Boundary data","q":"<p>A program accepts quiz scores from 0 to 20. Which value is BOUNDARY test data?</p>","options":["10","\"twenty\"","25","20"],"correct":3,"why":"20 is at the upper edge of the valid range. 10 is normal; 25 and \"twenty\" are erroneous.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Erroneous data","q":"<p>A program for the Carnival Village car park accepts parking times from 1 to 12 hours. Which is ERRONEOUS test data?</p>","options":["15","6","12","1"],"correct":0,"why":"15 is outside the valid range and should be rejected.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Dry run","q":"<p>Before typing her program, Tamara works through her algorithm on paper, writing the value of each variable after every step. This is called:</p>","options":["a dry run","maintenance","linking","compiling"],"correct":0,"why":"Working through an algorithm by hand with a trace table is a dry run (desk check).","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Pascal semicolon","q":"<p>Free Pascal reports: <i>Fatal: Syntax error, \";\" expected but \"identifier TOTAL\" found</i> on line 9. Where is the mistake MOST likely to be?</p>","options":["On line 9, where TOTAL is misspelt","In the var section","In the program heading","At the end of line 8, where a semicolon is missing"],"correct":3,"why":"The compiler noticed the problem when it reached line 9, but the missing semicolon belongs at the end of the previous statement.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Adaptive maintenance","q":"<p>A shop's checkout program is changed because the government introduces a new tax rate. Which type of maintenance is this?</p>","options":["Adaptive","Preventive testing","Perfective","Corrective"],"correct":0,"why":"Changing a program to suit a new environment, law or rule is adaptive maintenance.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Why logic errors are hard","q":"<p>Why are logic errors usually harder to find than syntax errors?</p>","options":["The translator gives no error message; the program runs but gives wrong results","They only happen in Pascal","They can only be found by the linker","They always crash the computer"],"correct":0,"why":"Logic errors produce no message; you must compare actual results with expected results.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Interpreter and run-time","q":"<p>Devon's Python program prints three lines of output, then shows <i>IndexError: list index out of range</i>. Which statement is TRUE?</p>","options":["It is a run-time error that occurred while the program was executing","The linker failed","It is a logic error, because no message appeared","It is a syntax error found before running"],"correct":0,"why":"An IndexError is raised during execution: a run-time error.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Expected result","q":"<p>When preparing a test plan for a program that calculates the cost of 4 guavaberry tarts at $3.50 each, what should the tester do BEFORE running the test?</p>","options":["Compile the program twice","Nothing; just run it and see","Delete any comments","Work out the expected result ($14.00) by hand"],"correct":3,"why":"Expected results must be worked out beforehand so that actual results can be compared with them.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Best debugging step","q":"<p>Shanice's loop total is always the last number entered. Which is the MOST efficient way to find the cause?</p>","options":["Run it on a faster computer","Retype the whole program","Change every + to *","Trace the total through each pass of the loop, with a trace table or temporary print statements"],"correct":3,"why":"Tracing values shows that total is being reset inside the loop.","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"Run-time causes","q":"<p>Select EVERY situation that would cause a run-time error.</p>","options":["Dividing by a variable that holds 0","Using + instead of * in a formula","Reading position 10 of a 5-item list","Typing \"abc\" when int(input()) expects a number","Forgetting a colon after if"],"correct":[0, 2, 3],"why":"Division by zero, failed conversion and index out of range happen while running. A missing colon is a syntax error; the wrong operator is a logic error.","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"Test plan columns","q":"<p>Select EVERY item a good test plan should include for each test.</p>","options":["The actual result","The programmer's favourite colour","The test data used","The type of test data (normal, boundary or erroneous)","The expected result"],"correct":[0, 2, 3, 4],"why":"A test plan records the data, its type, the expected result and the actual result, so results can be compared.","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"Implementation steps","q":"<p>Select EVERY step that is part of implementing a program.</p>","options":["Maintaining","Executing","Linking","Translating","Buying a faster CPU"],"correct":[0, 1, 2, 3],"why":"The steps are create source code, translate, link, execute and maintain.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Endless loop","q":"<p>Andre's VBA macro for the Rincon Pharmacy never finishes and Excel stops responding. Inside his Do While loop, the counter is never changed. This is BEST described as:</p>","options":["a syntax error","a run-time error caused by an endless loop","a linking error","a hardware fault"],"correct":1,"why":"A loop whose exit condition is never met runs forever while the program executes: a run-time error caused by a logic slip. Ctrl+Break stops it.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Saving VBA","q":"<p>Tamara writes a VBA macro in Excel, saves the workbook as an ordinary .xlsx file and reopens it. Her code has vanished. What should she have done?</p>","options":["Pressed F5 before saving","Saved as a PDF","Turned off the Developer tab","Saved as Excel Macro-Enabled Workbook (.xlsm)"],"correct":3,"why":"Only macro-enabled workbooks (.xlsm) keep VBA modules.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Manual test","q":"<p>Before typing her program, Shanice fills in a table showing the value of every variable after each step of her algorithm. This manual test is a:</p>","options":["trace table","user manual","linker","compiler listing"],"correct":0,"why":"A trace table is a manual test that tracks each variable's value step by step to reveal logic errors.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Programming terms","q":"<p>Match each description to the correct programming term.</p>","left":["Errors reported by translators when the rules of the language are not obeyed","A program in high-level or assembly-level language","An error in a program","Input values used to see if a program produces the correct results"],"right":["Syntax error","Source code","Bug","Test data"],"why":"These four definitions come straight from the programming unit test list.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Error types","q":"<p>Match each error type to its symptom.</p>","left":["Syntax error","Run-time error","Logic error"],"right":["The program will not translate","The program crashes part-way through","The program finishes but the output is wrong"],"why":"Classify errors by when and how they show themselves.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Test data types","q":"<p>A program accepts ages 16 to 60. Match each test value to its type.</p>","left":["35","16","75"],"right":["Normal","Boundary","Erroneous"],"why":"35 is typical; 16 is the lower edge; 75 is outside the range.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Maintenance types","q":"<p>Match each change to the type of maintenance.</p>","left":["Fixing a crash reported by users","Moving the program to new tablets","Adding a print-receipt feature"],"right":["Corrective","Adaptive","Perfective"],"why":"Corrective fixes faults, adaptive suits a new environment, perfective improves or adds features.","fb":[]}]},{"id":"q_s8u3","title":"Section 8 · Variables, data types, input/output and selection in code quiz","desc":"<p>Topic quiz for Section 8: Variables, data types, input/output and selection in code. 26 items.</p>","minutes":0,"items":[{"t":"mc","title":"Variable definition","q":"<p>In Tamara's program for the Corozal Market stall, the value of <i>total</i> increases each time an item is sold. <i>total</i> is BEST described as a:</p>","options":["constant","variable","reserved word","data type"],"correct":1,"why":"A variable is a named memory location whose value can change during the program.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Constant use","q":"<p>A program uses the ferry fare $30 in ten places. Why should the programmer declare it as a constant?</p>","options":["Constants run faster than any variable","Constants can be changed by the user while the program runs","If the fare changes, only one line needs editing","Constants do not need a data type"],"correct":2,"why":"A constant keeps a fixed value in one place, which makes the program easier to read and maintain.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Structural parts","q":"<p>Which list gives the structural parts of a Pascal program in the correct order?</p>","options":["Program, Const, Var, Begin … End.","Var, Program, Begin, Const, End.","Program, Begin, Var, Const, End.","Begin, Var, Const, Program, End."],"correct":0,"why":"Header first, then constant and variable declarations, then the main block from Begin to End.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Program header","q":"<p>Which line is a valid Pascal program header?</p>","options":["Program Sum_four_numbers (input, output);","Var Program;","Begin Sum_four_numbers;","Program Sum four numbers;"],"correct":0,"why":"A header starts with Program and a valid identifier (no spaces); (input, output) is optional in Free Pascal.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Valid identifier","q":"<p>Which is a VALID identifier in Pascal, Python and VBA?</p>","options":["total score","total_score","2ndScore","total-score"],"correct":1,"why":"Identifiers start with a letter or underscore and may contain letters, digits and underscores only.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Snake case","q":"<p>Rohan names his variable <i>discount_cost</i>. Which naming style is this?</p>","options":["Pascal notation","Snake case","Reserved word","Camel notation"],"correct":1,"why":"Words joined with underscores are snake case.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Data type: real","q":"<p>Which data type should store the rainfall at Piarco Airport in millimetres, such as 23.4?</p>","options":["Boolean","Integer","Char","Real"],"correct":3,"why":"A value with a decimal part needs the real (floating point) data type.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Char","q":"<p>A program stores 'M' or 'F' for each student. The MOST suitable Pascal data type is:</p>","options":["Integer","Real","Char","Boolean"],"correct":2,"why":"A single character is stored as Char. (VBA has no Char type, so it would use a String.)","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Write vs WriteLn","q":"<p>Which Pascal statement displays its output and then moves the cursor to the start of the next line?</p>","options":["Write","ClrScr","Read","WriteLn"],"correct":3,"why":"WriteLn writes, then moves to the next line; Write stays on the same line.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Semicolon","q":"<p>In Pascal, what is the purpose of the semicolon?</p>","options":["To end the whole program","To start a comment","To enclose text in prompts","To separate statements so the compiler moves on to the next one"],"correct":3,"why":"The semicolon ends a statement; the full stop ends the program; inverted commas enclose text.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Pascal comments","q":"<p>Which of these is a valid Pascal comment?</p>","options":["# calculate VAT","{ calculate VAT }","// calculate VAT","' calculate VAT"],"correct":1,"why":"Pascal comments use { } or (* *). # is Python and ' is VBA.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"MOD","q":"<p>A Carnival rota repeats every 4 days. Which expression gives the remainder when day 18 is divided by 4?</p>","options":["18 / 4","18 * 4","18 DIV 4","18 MOD 4"],"correct":3,"why":"MOD gives the remainder: 18 MOD 4 = 2.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"TRUNC","q":"<p>What does <code>WriteLn(Trunc(12.7));</code> display?</p>","options":["1","12.7","12","13"],"correct":2,"why":"Trunc chops off the fraction part; Round(12.7) would give 13.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"DIV result","q":"<p>Andre's program calculates <code>hours := 200 Div 60;</code>. What is stored in hours?</p>","options":["3","4","20","3.33"],"correct":0,"why":"Div is whole-number division: 200 Div 60 = 3.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Pascal else","q":"<p>Kenya's Pascal program will not compile. The lines are <code>If mark >= 50 Then WriteLn('Pass'); Else WriteLn('Fail');</code>. What is wrong?</p>","options":["The condition needs ==","Else must be in capitals","There must be no semicolon before Else","WriteLn should be print"],"correct":2,"why":"In Pascal a semicolon ends the If statement, so the Else that follows causes a syntax error.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"VBA End If","q":"<p>Which line correctly closes an IF block in VBA?</p>","options":["ENDIF","End;","End If","fi"],"correct":2,"why":"VBA uses End If (two words); ENDIF is pseudocode.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Equality in Python","q":"<p>Which Python condition correctly checks whether the answer typed is \"Y\"?</p>","options":["if answer &lt;> \"Y\":","if answer = \"Y\":","if answer == \"Y\":","if answer := \"Y\":"],"correct":2,"why":"Python compares with ==; = is assignment.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"AND result","q":"<p>For a student with age = 16 and paid = False, what is the value of <code>(age >= 14) And paid</code>?</p>","options":["An error","True","False","16"],"correct":2,"why":"AND needs both parts true; paid is False, so the result is False.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Selection names","q":"<p>A selection statement is also called a:</p>","options":["comment or remark","loop statement or counter","conditional statement or logic statement","declaration or header"],"correct":2,"why":"Selection, conditional and logic statement all describe a decision between alternatives.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Elif order","q":"<p>The hurricane program tests <code>wind >= 74</code> FIRST and <code>wind >= 157</code> LAST. A 160 mph storm will be reported as:</p>","options":["An error","Category 1","Not a hurricane","Category 5"],"correct":1,"why":"The first true test wins, and 160 >= 74, so it reports Category 1: a logic error caused by the order.","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"Pascal syntax","q":"<p>Select EVERY line that is valid Pascal.</p>","options":["Dim x As Integer","print(\"Hi\")","total := 0;","ReadLn(x);","If x > 5 Then"],"correct":[2, 3, 4],"why":"print() is Python and Dim is VBA.","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"Pascal types","q":"<p>Select EVERY valid Pascal data type name.</p>","options":["Integer","float","Boolean","Char","Real"],"correct":[0, 2, 3, 4],"why":"float is Python's name; Pascal uses Real.","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"True conditions","q":"<p>With mark = 75, select EVERY condition that is TRUE.</p>","options":["Not (mark > 70)","(mark > 50) And (mark &lt; 80)","mark >= 75","mark > 75","mark &lt;> 50"],"correct":[1, 2, 4],"why":"75 >= 75 is true; 75 > 75 is false; 75 &lt;> 50 is true; 50 &lt; 75 &lt; 80 is true; NOT(true) is false.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Operators","q":"<p>Match each pseudocode or Pascal operator to its Python equivalent.</p>","left":["← or :=","&lt;>","DIV","MOD"],"right":["=","!=","//","%"],"why":"Assignment, not-equal, whole-number division and remainder.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Types and values","q":"<p>Match each value to its most suitable data type.</p>","left":["'M'","'Broad Street'","42","98.6"],"right":["Char","String","Integer","Real"],"why":"One character, text, whole number, decimal.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Boolean operators","q":"<p>Match each Boolean operator to its effect.</p>","left":["AND","OR","NOT"],"right":["Narrows: both conditions must be true","Broadens: either condition may be true","Excludes: reverses the condition"],"why":"AND narrows, OR broadens, NOT excludes.","fb":[]}]},{"id":"q_s8u4","title":"Section 8 · Loops and arrays in code quiz","desc":"<p>Topic quiz for Section 8: Loops and arrays in code. 23 items.</p>","minutes":0,"items":[{"t":"mc","title":"Iteration","q":"<p>Kenya writes a program that asks for 30 students' marks using one Read statement inside a loop. Repeating a sequence of instructions like this is called:</p>","options":["declaration","iteration","compilation","selection"],"correct":1,"why":"Iteration (repetition) means repeating a sequence of instructions.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Bounded","q":"<p>Which construct is used for bounded iteration?</p>","options":["FOR … ENDFOR","IF … THEN … ELSE","REPEAT … UNTIL","WHILE … ENDWHILE"],"correct":0,"why":"FOR repeats a fixed number of times; WHILE and REPEAT are unbounded.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Bottom test","q":"<p>The conditions of this control structure are tested at the bottom of the loop, so the statements in the loop body are executed at least once. Name the structure.</p>","options":["FOR","REPEAT … UNTIL","WHILE","CASE"],"correct":1,"why":"REPEAT … UNTIL tests at the bottom.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"No Begin/End","q":"<p>In Pascal, there is no need to use Begin and End to group more than one statement in which loop?</p>","options":["Repeat … Until","For … Do","If … Then","While … Do"],"correct":0,"why":"All statements between Repeat and Until form the loop body.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Loop count","q":"<p>How many times will FOR NUM = 1 TO 20 DO execute?</p>","options":["19","1","21","20"],"correct":3,"why":"1 to 20 inclusive is 20 times.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Sentinel","q":"<p>A program for the Grand Anse food bank adds donation weights until the volunteer enters -1. The value -1 is called the:</p>","options":["counter","accumulator","subscript","sentinel (rogue) value"],"correct":3,"why":"A sentinel is a special value that signals the end of input.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Accumulator line","q":"<p>Which line is an accumulator (totalling) statement?</p>","options":["count := count + 1;","For i := 1 To 10 Do","ReadLn(price);","total := total + price;"],"correct":3,"why":"Adding each value to a running total is accumulating; adding 1 is counting.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Initialise","q":"<p>Why must total be set to 0 BEFORE the loop starts?</p>","options":["So the loop runs faster","So the sentinel is counted","So the total starts from nothing and is not reset on every pass","Because Pascal needs every variable to be 0"],"correct":2,"why":"Initialise once, before the loop; inside, it would reset each time.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Endless loop","q":"<p>A Do While loop in Shanice's VBA macro never stops. The MOST likely cause is:</p>","options":["too many MsgBox lines","the variable in the condition is never changed inside the loop","a missing Dim statement","the workbook is .xlsx"],"correct":1,"why":"If the loop never updates the tested variable, the condition never changes.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Python range","q":"<p>How many times does <code>for i in range(1, 13):</code> run?</p>","options":["13","1","11","12"],"correct":3,"why":"range(1, 13) produces 1 to 12.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"VBA loop","q":"<p>Which VBA loop keeps running until the user types 999?</p>","options":["If price = 999 Then … End If","Select Case price","Do … Loop Until price = 999","For i = 1 To 999 … Next i"],"correct":2,"why":"Do … Loop Until is VBA's REPEAT … UNTIL.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Array definition","q":"<p>An array is BEST described as:</p>","options":["a list of reserved words","a collection of data of the same type sharing one name","a type of flowchart symbol","a loop that counts"],"correct":1,"why":"An array stores many values of one data type under one name.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Array size","q":"<p>What is the size of the array declared as <code>Num : Array[1..5] Of Integer;</code>?</p>","options":["1","6","4","5"],"correct":3,"why":"The index range 1..5 gives five elements.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Subscript","q":"<p>In <code>scores[7] := 82;</code>, what is the 7?</p>","options":["The data type","The array size","The value stored","The subscript (index) of the element"],"correct":3,"why":"The number in brackets is the index or subscript.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Vector","q":"<p>A one-dimensional array is also called a:</p>","options":["table","record","matrix","vector"],"correct":3,"why":"One-dimensional: vector. Two-dimensional: matrix.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Binary search","q":"<p>Why can binary search NOT be used on the unsorted list 54, 12, 88, 3, 41?</p>","options":["It needs exactly 10 items","It needs the data in order so it can discard half of the list each time","It only works on strings","It only works in Python"],"correct":1,"why":"Binary search relies on sorted data to decide which half to discard.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Cells","q":"<p>In Excel VBA, <code>Cells(4, 3).Value</code> refers to cell:</p>","options":["C4","4C","D3","B4"],"correct":0,"why":"Row 4, column 3 (C): C4.","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"Unbounded","q":"<p>Select EVERY unbounded loop construct.</p>","options":["REPEAT … UNTIL","FOR","For … Next (VBA)","WHILE … DO","Do While … Loop (VBA)"],"correct":[0, 3, 4],"why":"WHILE, REPEAT and VBA's Do loops are condition-controlled; FOR loops are bounded.","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"Array operations","q":"<p>Select EVERY operation that can be performed on an array, according to the unit test topics.</p>","options":["Compile","Read","Write","Traverse","Search"],"correct":[1, 2, 3, 4],"why":"Read, write, traverse and search; compiling is something done to a program.","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"Linear search","q":"<p>Select EVERY statement that is true of linear search.</p>","options":["It can stop early when the item is found","It works on unsorted data","It checks elements one at a time","It always needs fewer comparisons than binary search"],"correct":[0, 1, 2],"why":"Linear search is simple and works on any order, but is usually slower than binary search on large sorted lists.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Loop types","q":"<p>Match each Pascal statement to its type of control structure.</p>","left":["for","while","repeat","if-then-else"],"right":["Bounded iteration","Unbounded iteration, tested at the top","Unbounded iteration, tested at the bottom","Selection"],"why":"From the unit test list of Pascal statements and control structures.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Loop syntax","q":"<p>Match each pseudocode loop to its VBA form.</p>","left":["FOR i ← 1 TO 5 DO … ENDFOR","WHILE x &lt;> 0 DO … ENDWHILE","REPEAT … UNTIL x = 0"],"right":["For i = 1 To 5 … Next i","Do While x &lt;> 0 … Loop","Do … Loop Until x = 0"],"why":"VBA maps each pseudocode loop to a For or Do form.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Array terms","q":"<p>Match each term to its meaning.</p>","left":["Element","Index range","Traversing"],"right":["One item in an array","The size of the array, e.g. [1..10]","Visiting each element one by one"],"why":"Core array vocabulary.","fb":[]}]},{"id":"q_s8u5","title":"Section 8 · Documenting programs quiz","desc":"<p>Topic quiz for Section 8: Documenting programs. 21 items.</p>","minutes":0,"items":[{"t":"mc","title":"Documentation definition","q":"<p>Documentation is BEST described as:</p>","options":["the machine code produced by a compiler","the test data used by a programmer","a list of reserved words","a written, detailed description of a program and how it was developed"],"correct":3,"why":"Documentation describes the program, its purpose and how it works.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Reason","q":"<p>Which is a reason for documenting a program?</p>","options":["To make it easy for another programmer to maintain the program","To remove syntax errors","To reduce the size of the executable","To make the program run faster"],"correct":0,"why":"Documentation helps future programmers understand and maintain the code, and helps users use it.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Internal audience","q":"<p>Internal documentation is designed mainly for the:</p>","options":["hardware technician","user","programmer","examiner"],"correct":2,"why":"Internal documentation (in the code) is for programmers.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"External example","q":"<p>Which is an example of EXTERNAL documentation?</p>","options":["Meaningful variable names","A user manual","Indentation","A comment in the code"],"correct":1,"why":"A user manual is a separate document for the user.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Pascal comment","q":"<p>Which of these is a correctly written Pascal comment?</p>","options":["// total marks","{ total marks }","# total marks","' total marks"],"correct":1,"why":"Pascal uses { } or (* *).","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"VBA comment","q":"<p>In the Excel Visual Basic Editor, comments start with which symbol and appear in which colour?</p>","options":["// and grey","# and blue","' (apostrophe) and green","{ and red"],"correct":2,"why":"VBA comments start with an apostrophe and show in green.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Meaningful identifier","q":"<p>Which identifier is the MOST meaningful for the total rainfall in a month?</p>","options":["var","t","x1","monthlyRainfall"],"correct":3,"why":"monthlyRainfall says exactly what it stores.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Procedure head","q":"<p>A procedure-head comment should include:</p>","options":["the author's name, date created and date modified, and what the procedure does","the test results only","the machine code","only the procedure name"],"correct":0,"why":"Heads record effects, assumptions, author and dates.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Categories","q":"<p>Internal documentation comments fall into three categories: heads of procedures, declarations and:</p>","options":["error messages","user manuals","complex segments of code","flowcharts"],"correct":2,"why":"The third category is comments on complex segments of the body.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Indentation purpose","q":"<p>Why do programmers indent the statements inside a loop?</p>","options":["To show clearly which statements belong to the loop","To hide the statements from users","The compiler requires it in Pascal","To make the program run faster"],"correct":0,"why":"Indentation shows structure; Pascal ignores it (Python, however, needs it).","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Option Explicit","q":"<p>What does Option Explicit do in a VBA module?</p>","options":["Forces every variable to be declared before use","Explains the code to users","Hides the code","Runs the macro automatically"],"correct":0,"why":"It catches misspelt variable names as compile errors.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"User manual content","q":"<p>Which item belongs in a USER manual?</p>","options":["The procedure-head comments","How to start the program and what to type at each prompt","The data type of each variable","The source code listing"],"correct":1,"why":"User manuals give non-technical, step-by-step instructions.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Technical manual","q":"<p>Kenya's technical documentation lists every variable with its data type and purpose. This list is often called a:</p>","options":["flowchart","trace table","data dictionary (variable list)","user guide"],"correct":2,"why":"A data dictionary/variable list documents the variables.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"SBA program","q":"<p>Which feature MUST the program in the SBA programming component normally demonstrate?</p>","options":["Machine code","Use of a printer","Use of a database","Selection and iteration"],"correct":3,"why":"SBA programs are expected to use selection (IF/CASE) and iteration (loops).","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Comments and running","q":"<p>Which statement about comments is TRUE?</p>","options":["Comments must end with a semicolon","Comments are shown to the user","Comments slow the program down","Comments are ignored by the translator and do not affect how the program runs"],"correct":3,"why":"Translators ignore comments.","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"Readability","q":"<p>Select EVERY feature that improves program readability.</p>","options":["Indentation","Comments","Several statements on one line","Meaningful identifiers","White space between sections"],"correct":[0, 1, 3, 4],"why":"Cramming statements together harms readability.","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"User manual items","q":"<p>Select EVERY item that belongs in a user manual.</p>","options":["Variable data types","Troubleshooting common error messages","Installation instructions","The algorithm in pseudocode","Screenshots of each screen"],"correct":[1, 2, 4],"why":"Algorithms and data types belong in technical documentation.","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"Procedure head info","q":"<p>Select EVERY item that a procedure-head comment should convey.</p>","options":["Author's name","Date modified","Date created","The user's password","The effect of the procedure"],"correct":[0, 1, 2, 4],"why":"Heads record author, dates, effects and assumptions.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Documentation types","q":"<p>Match each documentation item to its type.</p>","left":["Comment explaining a loop","User guide","Program-head comment"],"right":["Internal: complex segment","External","Internal: head"],"why":"Location and audience decide the type.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Comment syntax","q":"<p>Match each language to its comment style.</p>","left":["Pascal","Python","VBA"],"right":["{ comment }","# comment","' comment"],"why":"Each language has its own comment marker.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Readers","q":"<p>Match each document to its main reader.</p>","left":["Comments and identifiers","User manual","Test plan with results"],"right":["Maintenance programmer","Person using the program","Moderator or tester checking the program"],"why":"Documentation is written for a particular audience.","fb":[]}]},{"id":"q_s8u6","title":"Section 8 · One algorithm, many languages quiz","desc":"<p>Topic quiz for Section 8: One algorithm, many languages. 21 items.</p>","minutes":0,"items":[{"t":"mc","title":"Logic vs syntax","q":"<p>When the beach clean-up program is rewritten from Pascal into Python, which of these changes?</p>","options":["The conditions tested","The keywords and punctuation","The order of the steps","The purpose of the program"],"correct":1,"why":"Keywords and punctuation are syntax; the logic stays the same.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Block to code","q":"<p>Which Pascal statement matches the block <b>[change (score) by (1)]</b>?</p>","options":["score := 1;","score := score + 1;","score = score + 1","WriteLn(score + 1);"],"correct":1,"why":"change by adds to the current value.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Repeat until block","q":"<p>The Scratch-style block <b>[repeat until &lt;(weight) = (0)&gt;]</b> is equivalent to which pseudocode?</p>","options":["FOR weight ← 1 TO 0 DO","IF weight = 0 THEN","WHILE weight = 0 DO","WHILE weight &lt;> 0 DO"],"correct":3,"why":"Repeat until X is the same as while NOT X; Scratch also tests before each pass.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Custom block","q":"<p>A custom block such as [drive forward (distance)] is MOST like which feature of Pascal?</p>","options":["A procedure","A constant","A comment","An array"],"correct":0,"why":"A named, reusable set of instructions with a parameter: a procedure.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Flowchart symbol","q":"<p>When converting the statement <b>Read weight</b> into a flowchart, which symbol is used?</p>","options":["Terminator","Diamond","Rectangle","Parallelogram"],"correct":3,"why":"Input and output use parallelograms.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"ELSE IF in a flowchart","q":"<p>In a flowchart, an ELSE IF is drawn as:</p>","options":["a second diamond on the first diamond's No path","a loop-back arrow","a terminator","a rectangle"],"correct":0,"why":"Each extra condition is another decision on the No branch.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"JavaScript output","q":"<p>Which statement displays a message in JavaScript's console?</p>","options":["MsgBox \"Done\"","WriteLn('Done');","console.log(\"Done\");","print(\"Done\")"],"correct":2,"why":"console.log is JavaScript's output to the console.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"C comparison","q":"<p>Which C condition correctly tests whether weight is not equal to 0?</p>","options":["weight NOT 0","weight != 0","weight &lt;> 0","weight =! 0"],"correct":1,"why":"C, C++, Java, Python and JavaScript use != for not equal.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Java constant","q":"<p>Which Java line declares a constant TARGET of 50?</p>","options":["final double TARGET = 50;","Const TARGET = 50;","TARGET := 50;","Dim TARGET As Double = 50"],"correct":0,"why":"Java uses final for constants.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"VB.NET loop end","q":"<p>In Visual Basic (.NET), a While loop is closed with:</p>","options":["End While","ENDWHILE","Loop","}"],"correct":0,"why":"VB.NET uses While … End While; VBA uses Do While … Loop.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Language choice","q":"<p>The Portsmouth clean-up organisers want a live total on their website. The BEST language is:</p>","options":["Pascal","Assembly","COBOL","JavaScript"],"correct":3,"why":"JavaScript runs in every web browser.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Language choice 2","q":"<p>The bag weights are already in an Excel workbook. Which language best adds a 'Total' button?</p>","options":["Prolog","Java","C","VBA"],"correct":3,"why":"VBA is built into Excel and can read cells directly.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"C++ extraction","q":"<p>In the C++ statement <code>cin >> weight;</code>, the >> is called the:</p>","options":["right shift of weight","comparison operator","insertion operator","extraction operator"],"correct":3,"why":">> extracts data from the input stream into a variable.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"C++ const","q":"<p>What happens when this C++ is compiled: <code>const int a = 5; a++;</code>?</p>","options":["It prints 5","A run-time error","A compile error","It prints 6"],"correct":2,"why":"A const variable is read-only, so the compiler refuses to increment it.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Integer division","q":"<p>In C, what is the value of <code>7 / 2</code> when both are int?</p>","options":["4","3","3.5","An error"],"correct":1,"why":"int / int gives an int; the fraction is discarded.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"a.out","q":"<p>On a Linux computer, a student compiles a C++ program without naming the output file. The executable is called:</p>","options":["program.exe","a.exe","main.out","a.out"],"correct":3,"why":"a.out is the default executable name on UNIX and Linux.","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"Logic items","q":"<p>Select EVERY item that is part of the LOGIC (algorithm) and therefore stays the same when translating.</p>","options":["Stopping the loop at 0","Ending statements with semicolons","Initialising total to 0 before the loop","Testing total >= 50 after the loop","Using := for assignment"],"correct":[0, 2, 3],"why":":= and semicolons are syntax.","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"Curly-brace family","q":"<p>Select EVERY language that uses { } to mark blocks.</p>","options":["Pascal","C","Java","VBA","JavaScript"],"correct":[1, 2, 4],"why":"Pascal uses Begin … End and VBA uses End If, Loop and Next.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Output statements","q":"<p>Match each language to its output statement.</p>","left":["Pascal","Python","VBA","C++"],"right":["WriteLn","print","MsgBox","cout &lt;&lt;"],"why":"Same job, different spelling.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Loop syntax","q":"<p>Match each language to its WHILE loop opening.</p>","left":["Pascal","Python","VBA","Java"],"right":["While x &lt;> 0 Do","while x != 0:","Do While x &lt;> 0","while (x != 0) {"],"why":"The WHILE idea in four syntaxes.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Representations","q":"<p>Match each representation to its description.</p>","left":["Pseudocode","Flowchart","Blocks"],"right":["English-like structured statements","Diagram of symbols joined by flow lines","Snap-together visual instructions"],"why":"Three ways to express the same algorithm before (or instead of) a programming language.","fb":[]}]}]},{"pkg":"Section Structured Questions (Paper 02 style)","quizzes":[{"id":"p2_s1","title":"Section 1: Section 1 structured question: Bay Breeze Guest House","desc":"<p>Structured question: Paper 02 style, Section 1 (15 marks)</p>","minutes":0,"items":[{"t":"essay","title":"Section 1 structured question: Bay Breeze Guest House","q":"<p class=\"passage\"><b>Bay Breeze Guest House</b> in Gros Islet has 12 rooms. The owner, Mrs. Baron, keeps bookings in a paper diary and writes receipts by hand. Last season two guests were booked into the same room, and a payment of EC$450 was recorded as EC$540. She plans to buy one computer for the reception desk, booking software, a printer for receipts and invoices, and to keep guest records safe during hurricane season.</p><p>Answer all parts.</p><ol type=\"a\"><li><b>Distinguish</b> between data and information, using an example from the guest house. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li><b>Recommend</b> a type of computer for the reception desk and <b>justify</b> it with TWO specifications. <i>(3 marks)</i></li><li><b>State</b> whether the booking software should be off-the-shelf or custom-written, giving ONE reason. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li><b>Suggest</b> a suitable printer for receipts and give ONE reason. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li><b>Identify</b> the processing mode needed to prevent double bookings and <b>explain</b> why. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li><b>Name</b> ONE validation check and ONE verification method that would help with payment amounts, explaining which would catch the EC$540 error. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li><b>Describe</b> how Mrs. Baron should back up her guest records during hurricane season. <i>(2 marks)</i></li></ol>","points":15,"fb":"<h4>Marking guide</h4><p>Mark scheme: (a) 1 + 1. (b) 1 type, 1 per linked spec (max 2). (c) 1 choice, 1 reason. (d) 1 + 1. (e) 1 mode, 1 explanation. (f) 1 for both checks named, 1 for explaining that verification catches 540. (g) 1 regular backup, 1 off-site/cloud.</p><h4>Model answer</h4><p><b>(a)</b> Data is raw, unprocessed facts, such as each guest's name, room number and dates of stay. Information is data that has been processed to be meaningful, such as a report showing that occupancy was 85% in Carnival week.</p><p><b>(b)</b> A desktop computer, because it stays at the reception desk. It should have at least 8 GB RAM so the booking software, e-mail and browser run smoothly together, and an SSD so programs load quickly while guests wait. A UPS is also advisable.</p><p><b>(c)</b> Off-the-shelf booking software, because a 12-room guest house has common needs; a package is cheaper, available immediately and already tested by many users.</p><p><b>(d)</b> A thermal printer for receipts, because it is quick, quiet and needs no ink.</p><p><b>(e)</b> Real-time processing, because the booking record is updated instantly when a room is booked, so the room immediately shows as taken and cannot be booked a second time.</p><p><b>(f)</b> A range check (amount between 0 and 2,000) and double entry of the amount. EC$540 is within the range, so the range check would accept it; double entry would catch it because the second entry (450) would not match.</p><p><b>(g)</b> Back up the booking data daily to an external hard drive kept at the guest house, and also to cloud storage, so an off-site copy survives if the building is flooded or damaged by a hurricane.</p>"}]},{"id":"p2_s2","title":"Section 2: Paper 02 practice: Seaside Medical Centre","desc":"<p>Structured question: networks, mobile and web technologies (15 marks)</p>","minutes":0,"items":[{"t":"essay","title":"Paper 02 practice: Seaside Medical Centre","q":"<p><b>Seaside Medical Centre</b> in Santiago de los Caballeros has 20 computers, a server holding patient records and a shared printer. It is opening a second clinic in San Pedro de Macorís and wants both sites to share the same records. Doctors visit elderly patients at home using tablets on 4G/5G. The centre's new patient portal at <code>https://portal.seasidemedical.do/appointments/book.html</code> lets patients book appointments; only staff can see the internal staff-policy pages.</p><p>Answer all parts.</p><ol type=\"a\"><li><b>Distinguish</b> between a LAN and a WAN, and <b>state</b> which network is needed to link the two clinics. <i>(3 marks)</i></li><li><b>State</b> the function of a router and of a switch in the Santiago de los Caballeros clinic. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li><b>Explain</b> why the centre should use a client–server network rather than peer-to-peer. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li><b>Identify</b> TWO mobile technologies that help doctors on home visits, and <b>explain</b> how each helps. <i>(4 marks)</i></li><li><b>Identify</b> the protocol and the top-level domain in the portal's URL, and <b>explain</b> why the protocol is important for patients. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li>What term describes the staff-only policy pages? <b>Justify</b> your answer. <i>(2 marks)</i></li></ol>","points":15,"fb":"<h4>Marking guide</h4><p>(a) 1 LAN description; 1 WAN description in contrast; 1 WAN/MAN chosen for the link. (b) 1 router function; 1 switch function. (c) 1 central server; 1 security/backup reason linked to records. (d) For each of two technologies: 1 named, 1 explained. (e) 1 https and .do both correct; 1 encryption/privacy explanation. (f) 1 intranet; 1 justification (staff/members only).</p><h4>Model answer</h4><p><b>(a)</b> A LAN covers a small area such as one building and is usually owned by one organisation, whereas a WAN covers a large geographical area, linking networks that are far apart. The two clinics are at different sites, so their LANs must be joined into a wider network: a WAN (or, since both are on one island, a MAN), for example through a secure VPN link over the Internet.</p><p><b>(b)</b> The router connects the clinic's LAN to other networks, such as the Internet and the San Pedro de Macorís clinic, forwarding data packets between them. The switch connects the 20 computers, server and printer within the LAN, sending data only to the device it is addressed to.</p><p><b>(c)</b> Patient records are confidential, so a central server lets the centre control exactly who can access them, keep audit logs and back them up every night in one place. Peer-to-peer would leave records spread across PCs with weak security and no central backup.</p><p><b>(d)</b> First, 4G/5G mobile data lets the doctor's tablet reach the patient-records server securely from the patient's home, so the doctor can check history and update notes immediately. Second, GPS lets the doctor navigate quickly to patients' homes, even in unfamiliar areas, so less time is wasted travelling.</p><p><b>(e)</b> The protocol is HTTPS and the top-level domain is .do. HTTPS encrypts the data travelling between the patient's browser and the server, so personal and medical details cannot be read if intercepted.</p><p><b>(f)</b> An intranet, because the pages are private to the organisation and can only be accessed by its own staff after logging in, not by patients or the public.</p>"}]},{"id":"p2_s3","title":"Section 3: Section 3 Paper 02: The Carnival ticket hack","desc":"<p>Structured question: misuse, impact, countermeasures and professionals (15 marks)</p>","minutes":0,"items":[{"t":"essay","title":"Section 3 Paper 02: The Carnival ticket hack","q":"<p class=\"passage\">The SKB Carnival Tickets Ltd sells tickets for Carnival Village shows through its website and a mobile app. Customer names, e-mail addresses and card details are stored on a server in its ground-floor office in Basseterre. Two weeks before Carnival: (1) staff received e-mails appearing to come from the company's bank asking them to 'verify their login'; one staff member did so; (2) customer card details were then copied and used for fraudulent purchases; (3) the ticket website was flooded with traffic and was offline for the final weekend of sales. The company has four staff and no IT employees. The only backup is on a USB drive kept in the office.</p><p>Answer all parts.</p><ol type=\"a\"><li><b>Identify</b> the form of misuse in EACH of incidents (1), (2) and (3). <i>(3 marks)</i></li><li><b>Explain</b> ONE impact of these incidents on the customers and ONE impact on the company. <i>(4 marks)</i></li><li><b>Recommend</b> TWO software countermeasures and ONE physical countermeasure the company should introduce, explaining how EACH reduces the risk. <i>(6 marks)</i></li><li><b>State</b> TWO duties of a computer professional the company could hire to help prevent future attacks, naming the role. <i>(2 marks)</i></li></ol>","points":15,"fb":"<h4>Marking guide</h4><p>(a) 1 each. (b) 1 impact + 1 explanation for customers; 1 + 1 for the company. (c) For each of three countermeasures: 1 suitable measure + 1 explanation (two must be software, one physical). (d) 1 per relevant duty with a named role (max 2).</p><h4>Model answer</h4><p><b>(a)</b> (1) Phishing. (2) Data theft leading to credit-card fraud (identity theft). (3) A (distributed) denial-of-service attack.</p><p><b>(b)</b> Customers suffered fraudulent charges on their cards, so they had the stress of disputing payments and cancelling cards, and their personal data is now exposed. The company lost ticket sales on the busiest weekend because the site was offline, and its reputation suffered, so customers may not trust it next year; it may also face legal action for failing to protect card data.</p><p><b>(c)</b> Software: turn on multi-factor authentication for all staff accounts, so a password stolen by phishing is not enough to log in. Use a payment provider so card numbers are never stored on the company's server (or encrypt stored data), so a breach does not expose card details. Physical: keep an automatic backup in the cloud or at another location and move the server off the ground floor into a locked room, so a flood, fire or theft cannot destroy both the data and the backup.</p><p><b>(d)</b> A cybersecurity analyst would monitor the systems for suspicious logins and attacks, and train staff to recognise phishing e-mails.</p>"}]},{"id":"p2_s4","title":"Section 4: Section 4 Paper 02: The Port of Spain Youth Arts Festival","desc":"<p>Structured question: word processing and web page design (15 marks)</p>","minutes":0,"items":[{"t":"essay","title":"Section 4 Paper 02: The Port of Spain Youth Arts Festival","q":"<p><b>Port of Spain Youth Arts Festival</b> committee is preparing for its first festival. The secretary must send a personalised invitation letter to 300 sponsors whose details are in an Excel file (Title, FirstName, LastName, Company, Address1, District, Donation). She is also writing a 25-page festival programme with sections and sub-sections, and the committee wants a small website for teenagers and their parents, with pages for Home, Events, Gallery and Contact, and a downloadable PDF schedule.</p><p>Answer all parts.</p><ol type='a'><li><b>Identify</b> the primary document and the data source for the invitation letters, and <b>describe</b> how the secretary could send letters ONLY to sponsors in the District of San Fernando. <i>(4 marks)</i></li><li><b>Outline</b> TWO steps needed to produce an automatic table of contents for the programme. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li><b>State</b> TWO things the committee should consider when planning the website. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li><b>Write</b> the HTML for a link to the PDF schedule stored as files/schedule.pdf, and for a link that opens an e-mail to arts@posyaf.example.tt. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li><b>Evaluate</b> the following decision: “Put twelve full-size 5 MB photos on the home page so visitors see the festival's quality straight away.” <i>(3 marks)</i></li><li><b>Explain</b> why the website should be tested after it is uploaded, even though it worked on the secretary's laptop. <i>(2 marks)</i></li></ol>","points":15,"fb":"<h4>Marking guide</h4><p>(a) 1 primary document, 1 data source, 1 filter/Edit Recipient List, 1 District = San Fernando. (b) 1 heading styles, 1 References ▸ Table of Contents. (c) 1 each valid consideration. (d) 1 each correct link. (e) 1 judgement with reason (load time), 1 effect on audience, 1 improvement. (f) 1 reason, 1 example.</p><h4>Model answer</h4><p><b>(a)</b> The primary document is the invitation letter containing the standard text and merge fields such as «Title» «LastName» and «Company». The data source is the Excel file of the 300 sponsors. To send letters only to San Fernando sponsors, she clicks Mailings ▸ Edit Recipient List ▸ Filter… and sets Field: District, Comparison: Equal to, Compare to: San Fernando, then previews and chooses Finish &amp; Merge.</p><p><b>(b)</b> Apply the Heading 1 style to section headings and Heading 2 to sub-sections; then place the cursor on a blank page after the cover and choose References ▸ Table of Contents ▸ Automatic Table 1 (updating it after final edits).</p><p><b>(c)</b> The purpose of the site (to inform teenagers and parents about the festival and promote it) and the intended audience (teenagers and their parents, mostly on phones), which affect the language, layout and design. (Also: the number of pages needed, the layout and the content of each page.)</p><p><b>(d)</b></p><pre><code>&lt;a href=\"files/schedule.pdf\"&gt;Download the festival schedule (PDF)&lt;/a&gt;\n&lt;a href=\"mailto:arts@posyaf.example.tt\"&gt;E-mail the committee&lt;/a&gt;</code></pre><p><b>(e)</b> This is a poor decision. Twelve 5 MB photos total about 60 MB, so the home page would load very slowly on teenagers' phones and use up their mobile data; many visitors would leave before seeing anything. The committee should resize and compress the photos (to roughly 200 KB each), show one or two on the home page and place the rest in the Gallery as thumbnails linking to larger versions.</p><p><b>(f)</b> Some problems only appear once the site is on the web server: for example, the PDF may not have been uploaded, a link may point to a file on the secretary's C: drive, or the server may treat Schedule.PDF and schedule.pdf as different files. Testing in different browsers and on phones after upload confirms the site works for real visitors.</p>"}]},{"id":"p2_s5","title":"Section 5: Paper 02 practice: Sandbox Tree College results","desc":"<p>Structured question: spreadsheets (15 marks)</p>","minutes":0,"items":[{"t":"essay","title":"Paper 02 practice: Sandbox Tree College results","q":"<p>The IT teacher at Sandbox Tree College records Form 5 test results in a spreadsheet. Part of it is shown below.</p><div class=\"xlgrid\" style=\"overflow-x:auto;max-width:100%;margin:.7em 0\"><table class=\"xl\" style=\"border-collapse:collapse;font:13px/1.35 'Segoe UI',system-ui,Arial,sans-serif\"><tr><th style=\"background:rgba(127,127,127,.16);border:1px solid rgba(127,127,127,.42);padding:2px 7px;font-weight:600;font-size:12px;min-width:26px\"></th><th style=\"background:rgba(127,127,127,.16);border:1px solid rgba(127,127,127,.42);padding:2px 7px;font-weight:600;font-size:12px;min-width:26px\">A</th><th style=\"background:rgba(127,127,127,.16);border:1px solid rgba(127,127,127,.42);padding:2px 7px;font-weight:600;font-size:12px;min-width:26px\">B</th><th style=\"background:rgba(127,127,127,.16);border:1px solid rgba(127,127,127,.42);padding:2px 7px;font-weight:600;font-size:12px;min-width:26px\">C</th><th style=\"background:rgba(127,127,127,.16);border:1px solid rgba(127,127,127,.42);padding:2px 7px;font-weight:600;font-size:12px;min-width:26px\">D</th><th style=\"background:rgba(127,127,127,.16);border:1px solid rgba(127,127,127,.42);padding:2px 7px;font-weight:600;font-size:12px;min-width:26px\">E</th><th style=\"background:rgba(127,127,127,.16);border:1px solid rgba(127,127,127,.42);padding:2px 7px;font-weight:600;font-size:12px;min-width:26px\">F</th><th style=\"background:rgba(127,127,127,.16);border:1px solid rgba(127,127,127,.42);padding:2px 7px;font-weight:600;font-size:12px;min-width:26px\">G</th></tr><tr><th style=\"background:rgba(127,127,127,.16);border:1px solid rgba(127,127,127,.42);padding:2px 7px;font-weight:600;font-size:12px;min-width:26px\">1</th><td style=\"border:1px solid rgba(127,127,127,.42);padding:3px 8px;white-space:nowrap\">Sandbox Tree College – Form 5 IT Results</td><td style=\"border:1px solid rgba(127,127,127,.42);padding:3px 8px;white-space:nowrap\"></td><td style=\"border:1px solid rgba(127,127,127,.42);padding:3px 8px;white-space:nowrap\"></td><td style=\"border:1px solid rgba(127,127,127,.42);padding:3px 8px;white-space:nowrap\"></td><td style=\"border:1px solid rgba(127,127,127,.42);padding:3px 8px;white-space:nowrap\"></td><td style=\"border:1px solid rgba(127,127,127,.42);padding:3px 8px;white-space:nowrap\"></td><td style=\"border:1px solid rgba(127,127,127,.42);padding:3px 8px;white-space:nowrap\"></td></tr><tr><th style=\"background:rgba(127,127,127,.16);border:1px solid rgba(127,127,127,.42);padding:2px 7px;font-weight:600;font-size:12px;min-width:26px\">2</th><td style=\"border:1px solid rgba(127,127,127,.42);padding:3px 8px;white-space:nowrap;font-weight:700;background:rgba(127,178,255,.13)\">Student</td><td style=\"border:1px solid rgba(127,127,127,.42);padding:3px 8px;white-space:nowrap;font-weight:700;background:rgba(127,178,255,.13)\">Test 1</td><td style=\"border:1px solid rgba(127,127,127,.42);padding:3px 8px;white-space:nowrap;font-weight:700;background:rgba(127,178,255,.13)\">Test 2</td><td style=\"border:1px solid rgba(127,127,127,.42);padding:3px 8px;white-space:nowrap;font-weight:700;background:rgba(127,178,255,.13)\">Test 3</td><td style=\"border:1px solid rgba(127,127,127,.42);padding:3px 8px;white-space:nowrap;font-weight:700;background:rgba(127,178,255,.13)\">Total</td><td style=\"border:1px solid rgba(127,127,127,.42);padding:3px 8px;white-space:nowrap;font-weight:700;background:rgba(127,178,255,.13)\">Average</td><td style=\"border:1px solid rgba(127,127,127,.42);padding:3px 8px;white-space:nowrap;font-weight:700;background:rgba(127,178,255,.13)\">Status</td></tr><tr><th style=\"background:rgba(127,127,127,.16);border:1px solid rgba(127,127,127,.42);padding:2px 7px;font-weight:600;font-size:12px;min-width:26px\">3</th><td style=\"border:1px solid rgba(127,127,127,.42);padding:3px 8px;white-space:nowrap\">Kaylee</td><td style=\"border:1px solid rgba(127,127,127,.42);padding:3px 8px;white-space:nowrap;text-align:right\">72</td><td style=\"border:1px solid rgba(127,127,127,.42);padding:3px 8px;white-space:nowrap;text-align:right\">65</td><td style=\"border:1px solid rgba(127,127,127,.42);padding:3px 8px;white-space:nowrap;text-align:right\">80</td><td style=\"border:1px solid rgba(127,127,127,.42);padding:3px 8px;white-space:nowrap;text-align:right\">217</td><td style=\"border:1px solid rgba(127,127,127,.42);padding:3px 8px;white-space:nowrap;text-align:right\">72.33</td><td style=\"border:1px solid rgba(127,127,127,.42);padding:3px 8px;white-space:nowrap\">Pass</td></tr><tr><th style=\"background:rgba(127,127,127,.16);border:1px solid rgba(127,127,127,.42);padding:2px 7px;font-weight:600;font-size:12px;min-width:26px\">4</th><td style=\"border:1px solid rgba(127,127,127,.42);padding:3px 8px;white-space:nowrap\">Marcus</td><td style=\"border:1px solid rgba(127,127,127,.42);padding:3px 8px;white-space:nowrap;text-align:right\">48</td><td style=\"border:1px solid rgba(127,127,127,.42);padding:3px 8px;white-space:nowrap;text-align:right\">55</td><td style=\"border:1px solid rgba(127,127,127,.42);padding:3px 8px;white-space:nowrap;text-align:right\">41</td><td style=\"border:1px solid rgba(127,127,127,.42);padding:3px 8px;white-space:nowrap;text-align:right\">144</td><td style=\"border:1px solid rgba(127,127,127,.42);padding:3px 8px;white-space:nowrap;text-align:right\">48.00</td><td style=\"border:1px solid rgba(127,127,127,.42);padding:3px 8px;white-space:nowrap\">Fail</td></tr><tr><th style=\"background:rgba(127,127,127,.16);border:1px solid rgba(127,127,127,.42);padding:2px 7px;font-weight:600;font-size:12px;min-width:26px\">5</th><td style=\"border:1px solid rgba(127,127,127,.42);padding:3px 8px;white-space:nowrap\">Tamara</td><td style=\"border:1px solid rgba(127,127,127,.42);padding:3px 8px;white-space:nowrap;text-align:right\">88</td><td style=\"border:1px solid rgba(127,127,127,.42);padding:3px 8px;white-space:nowrap;text-align:right\">92</td><td style=\"border:1px solid rgba(127,127,127,.42);padding:3px 8px;white-space:nowrap;text-align:right\">85</td><td style=\"border:1px solid rgba(127,127,127,.42);padding:3px 8px;white-space:nowrap;text-align:right\">265</td><td style=\"border:1px solid rgba(127,127,127,.42);padding:3px 8px;white-space:nowrap;text-align:right\">88.33</td><td style=\"border:1px solid rgba(127,127,127,.42);padding:3px 8px;white-space:nowrap\">Pass</td></tr><tr><th style=\"background:rgba(127,127,127,.16);border:1px solid rgba(127,127,127,.42);padding:2px 7px;font-weight:600;font-size:12px;min-width:26px\">6</th><td style=\"border:1px solid rgba(127,127,127,.42);padding:3px 8px;white-space:nowrap\">Devon</td><td style=\"border:1px solid rgba(127,127,127,.42);padding:3px 8px;white-space:nowrap;text-align:right\">60</td><td style=\"border:1px solid rgba(127,127,127,.42);padding:3px 8px;white-space:nowrap;text-align:right\">58</td><td style=\"border:1px solid rgba(127,127,127,.42);padding:3px 8px;white-space:nowrap;text-align:right\">66</td><td style=\"border:1px solid rgba(127,127,127,.42);padding:3px 8px;white-space:nowrap;text-align:right\">184</td><td style=\"border:1px solid rgba(127,127,127,.42);padding:3px 8px;white-space:nowrap;text-align:right\">61.33</td><td style=\"border:1px solid rgba(127,127,127,.42);padding:3px 8px;white-space:nowrap\">Pass</td></tr><tr><th style=\"background:rgba(127,127,127,.16);border:1px solid rgba(127,127,127,.42);padding:2px 7px;font-weight:600;font-size:12px;min-width:26px\">7</th><td style=\"border:1px solid rgba(127,127,127,.42);padding:3px 8px;white-space:nowrap\">Shanice</td><td style=\"border:1px solid rgba(127,127,127,.42);padding:3px 8px;white-space:nowrap;text-align:right\">35</td><td style=\"border:1px solid rgba(127,127,127,.42);padding:3px 8px;white-space:nowrap;text-align:right\">47</td><td style=\"border:1px solid rgba(127,127,127,.42);padding:3px 8px;white-space:nowrap;text-align:right\">52</td><td style=\"border:1px solid rgba(127,127,127,.42);padding:3px 8px;white-space:nowrap;text-align:right\">134</td><td style=\"border:1px solid rgba(127,127,127,.42);padding:3px 8px;white-space:nowrap;text-align:right\">44.67</td><td style=\"border:1px solid rgba(127,127,127,.42);padding:3px 8px;white-space:nowrap\">Fail</td></tr><tr><th style=\"background:rgba(127,127,127,.16);border:1px solid rgba(127,127,127,.42);padding:2px 7px;font-weight:600;font-size:12px;min-width:26px\">8</th><td style=\"border:1px solid rgba(127,127,127,.42);padding:3px 8px;white-space:nowrap;font-weight:700;background:rgba(127,178,255,.13)\">Class total</td><td style=\"border:1px solid rgba(127,127,127,.42);padding:3px 8px;white-space:nowrap;font-weight:700;background:rgba(127,178,255,.13)\"></td><td style=\"border:1px solid rgba(127,127,127,.42);padding:3px 8px;white-space:nowrap;font-weight:700;background:rgba(127,178,255,.13)\"></td><td style=\"border:1px solid rgba(127,127,127,.42);padding:3px 8px;white-space:nowrap;font-weight:700;background:rgba(127,178,255,.13)\"></td><td style=\"border:1px solid rgba(127,127,127,.42);padding:3px 8px;white-space:nowrap;text-align:right;font-weight:700;background:rgba(127,178,255,.13)\">944</td><td style=\"border:1px solid rgba(127,127,127,.42);padding:3px 8px;white-space:nowrap;font-weight:700;background:rgba(127,178,255,.13)\"></td><td style=\"border:1px solid rgba(127,127,127,.42);padding:3px 8px;white-space:nowrap;font-weight:700;background:rgba(127,178,255,.13)\"></td></tr></table><div style=\"font-size:.86em;opacity:.85;margin-top:3px\">Columns E, F and G and cell E8 contain formulas.</div></div><p>Answer all parts.</p><ol type=\"a\"><li>(i) <b>State</b> the range that contains all the test marks. (ii) <b>State</b> the cell address of Tamara's Test 2 mark. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li><b>Write</b> the formula, using a function, that was entered in (i) E3 and (ii) F3. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li>The formulas in E3 and F3 were copied down to row 7. <b>State</b> the type of cell referencing that makes this work and the formula that appears in E7. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li><b>Write</b> the formula in G3 that displays \"Pass\" if the average is 50 or more and \"Fail\" otherwise. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li>Column H must show each student's share of the class total in E8. <b>Write</b> a formula for H3 that can be copied down to H7 and <b>state</b> how H3:H7 should be formatted. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li>After a new formula is entered, a cell shows <b>######</b>. <b>Explain</b> the problem and how to solve it. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li><b>Outline</b> the steps to sort the list in descending order of Average. <i>(3 marks)</i></li></ol>","points":15,"fb":"<h4>Marking guide</h4><p>(a) 1 + 1. (b) 1 + 1 (equals sign and correct range required). (c) 1 relative; 1 =SUM(B7:D7). (d) 1 test F3&gt;=50; 1 correct quoted outputs. (e) 1 =E3/$E$8 (accept E$8); 1 Percentage. (f) 1 column too narrow; 1 widen/AutoFit. (g) 1 selection excluding title and totals; 1 Data ▸ Sort / Custom Sort; 1 Average + descending.</p><h4>Model answer</h4><p><b>(a)</b> (i) B3:D7 (ii) C5.</p><p><b>(b)</b> (i) <code>=SUM(B3:D3)</code> (ii) <code>=AVERAGE(B3:D3)</code></p><p><b>(c)</b> <b>Relative</b> cell referencing: the references change by the number of rows the formula is copied. E7 contains <code>=SUM(B7:D7)</code>.</p><p><b>(d)</b> <code>=IF(F3&gt;=50,\"Pass\",\"Fail\")</code></p><p><b>(e)</b> <code>=E3/$E$8</code>, with E8 made absolute so every row divides by the class total; format H3:H7 as <b>Percentage</b> (e.g. one decimal place). Kaylee's share is about 23.0%.</p><p><b>(f)</b> ###### means the column is not wide enough to display the result. Increase the column width (drag the boundary or double-click it to AutoFit) so the value can be displayed.</p><p><b>(g)</b> Step 1: Select A2:G7 (the headings and data, but not the title row or the Class total row). Step 2: Choose Data ▸ Sort (or Home ▸ Sort &amp; Filter ▸ Custom Sort) and tick My data has headers. Step 3: In Sort by choose Average, Order Largest to Smallest (descending), and click OK.</p>"}]},{"id":"p2_s6","title":"Section 6: Section 6 Paper 02: the Carnival Committee database","desc":"<p>Structured question: database management (15 marks)</p>","minutes":0,"items":[{"t":"essay","title":"Section 6 Paper 02: the Carnival Committee database","q":"<p>Jewel Carnival Committee in Belize City keeps its troupe records in an Access database with two tables:</p><div class=\"passage\"><p><b>SECTION</b> (<u>SectionID</u>, SectionName, Leader)<br><b>MASQUERADER</b> (<u>MasqID</u>, FirstName, LastName, Age, SectionID, CostumeFee, Paid)</p></div><div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>SectionID</th><th>SectionName</th><th>Leader</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>S1</td><td>Blue Wave</td><td>Ms T. Brooks</td></tr><tr><td>S2</td><td>Guavaberry Groove</td><td>Mr D. Lake</td></tr><tr><td>S3</td><td>Fire Fete</td><td>Ms K. Usher</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>MasqID</th><th>FirstName</th><th>LastName</th><th>Age</th><th>SectionID</th><th>CostumeFee</th><th>Paid</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>M01</td><td>Keisha</td><td>Lake</td><td>17</td><td>S1</td><td>$350.00</td><td>No</td></tr><tr><td>M02</td><td>Marcus</td><td>Hodge</td><td>22</td><td>S2</td><td>$275.00</td><td>Yes</td></tr><tr><td>M03</td><td>Aaliyah</td><td>Castillo</td><td>16</td><td>S1</td><td>$350.00</td><td>Yes</td></tr><tr><td>M04</td><td>Tevin</td><td>Brooks</td><td>19</td><td>S3</td><td>$420.00</td><td>No</td></tr><tr><td>M05</td><td>Shanice</td><td>Carty</td><td>25</td><td>S2</td><td>$275.00</td><td>No</td></tr><tr><td>M06</td><td>Jaden</td><td>Peterson</td><td>15</td><td>S3</td><td>$420.00</td><td>Yes</td></tr><tr><td>M07</td><td>Gloria</td><td>Fleming</td><td>34</td><td>S1</td><td>$350.00</td><td>No</td></tr><tr><td>M08</td><td>Rohan</td><td>Baptiste</td><td>28</td><td>S3</td><td>$420.00</td><td>No</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Answer all parts.</p><ol type=\"a\"><li>(i) <b>Identify</b> the primary key of MASQUERADER. (ii) <b>Identify</b> the foreign key in MASQUERADER. (iii) <b>State</b> the relationship between SECTION and MASQUERADER. <i>(3 marks)</i></li><li><b>State</b> the most suitable data type for CostumeFee and for Paid. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li><b>Write</b> a validation rule that allows only masqueraders aged 12 to 70 inclusive. <i>(1 mark)</i></li><li>A query has CostumeFee: <code>&gt;300</code> and Paid: <code>No</code> on the same Criteria row. <b>List</b> the MasqIDs it returns. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li><b>Write</b> a calculated field named FeeWithLevy that adds a 10% levy to CostumeFee. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li>The committee wants a printed report listing masqueraders grouped by SectionName, with the total costume fees for each section. (i) <b>Explain</b> why the report must be based on a query. (ii) <b>Write</b> the expression for each section's total and <b>name</b> the section of the report where it is placed. (iii) <b>State</b> the total shown for Fire Fete. <i>(5 marks)</i></li></ol>","points":15,"fb":"<h4>Marking guide</h4><p>Mark scheme: (a) 1 each. (b) 1 each. (c) 1. (d) all four IDs 2; three correct with no wrong 1. (e) name and colon 1, expression 1. (f)(i) needs both tables/join 1; (ii) Sum expression 1, group footer 1; (iii) $1,260.00 2 (1 for a correct method). Total 15.</p><h4>Model answer</h4><p><b>(a)</b> (i) MasqID. (ii) SectionID. (iii) One-to-many from SECTION to MASQUERADER: one section has many masqueraders, but each masquerader is in only one section.</p><p><b>(b)</b> CostumeFee: Currency. Paid: Yes/No.</p><p><b>(c)</b> <code>Between 12 And 70</code></p><p><b>(d)</b> M01, M04, M07, M08 (M05's fee of $275 is not over $300; M03 and M06 have paid).</p><p><b>(e)</b> <code>FeeWithLevy: [CostumeFee]*1.1</code></p><p><b>(f)</b> (i) SectionName is stored in SECTION while the fees are in MASQUERADER, so a query joining the two tables on SectionID must supply both. (ii) <code>=Sum([CostumeFee])</code> in the SectionName Footer (group footer). (iii) Fire Fete: 3 × $420.00 = <b>$1,260.00</b>.</p>"}]},{"id":"p2_s7","title":"Section 7: The water company bill","desc":"<p>Paper 02 structured question: IPO, pseudocode, trace and testing (15 marks)</p>","minutes":0,"items":[{"t":"essay","title":"The water company bill","q":"<p>A water company in The Bahamas charges customers as follows:</p><table><thead><tr><th>Gallons used</th><th>Charge</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1000 gallons or more</td><td>$100 fixed charge plus $0.05 per gallon</td></tr><tr><td>500 to 999 gallons</td><td>$50 fixed charge plus $0.03 per gallon</td></tr><tr><td>Below 500 gallons</td><td>$50 fixed charge plus $0.02 per gallon</td></tr></tbody></table><p>A program must accept a customer's name and the gallons used, then display the name and the charge.</p><p>Answer all parts.</p><ol type=\"a\"><li>Draw an <b>IPO table</b> for the problem. <i>(3 marks)</i></li><li><b>Write</b> a pseudocode algorithm for the problem, using prompts. <i>(6 marks)</i></li><li><b>State</b> the charge for (i) 1200 gallons, (ii) 500 gallons, (iii) 499 gallons. <i>(3 marks)</i></li><li><b>Explain</b> why 500 and 499 are good test values, and <b>name</b> the type of test data they are. <i>(3 marks)</i></li></ol>","points":15,"fb":"<h4>Marking guide</h4><p>(a) 1 inputs, 1 processing (accept/calculate/display), 1 output. (b) prompt (1), read (1), first band (1), second band (1), third band (1), output name and charge (1). (c) 1 each. (d) 1 'boundary', 2 for a clear reason (edge of band / >= check).</p><h4>Model answer</h4><p><b>(a)</b></p><table><thead><tr><th>Input</th><th>Processing</th><th>Output</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Name<br>Gallons</td><td>Accept Name, Gallons<br>Calculate Charge (by band: fixed charge + Gallons * rate)<br>Store results in Charge<br>Display Name and Charge</td><td>Name<br>Charge</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p><b>(b)</b></p><pre>Start\n  Write \"Enter customer name and gallons used: \"\n  Read Name, Gallons\n  IF Gallons &gt;= 1000 THEN\n    Charge ← 100 + Gallons * 0.05\n  ELSE IF Gallons &gt;= 500 THEN\n    Charge ← 50 + Gallons * 0.03\n  ELSE\n    Charge ← 50 + Gallons * 0.02\n  ENDIF\n  Write Name, \"owes $\", Charge\nStop</pre>\n<p><b>(c)</b> (i) 100 + 1200 × 0.05 = <b>$160</b> (ii) 50 + 500 × 0.03 = <b>$65</b> (iii) 50 + 499 × 0.02 = <b>$59.98</b></p>\n<p><b>(d)</b> They are <b>boundary</b> (extreme) test data: 500 is the smallest value in the middle band and 499 the largest in the lowest band. Testing both sides of the edge checks that the condition uses &gt;= correctly; a slip such as &gt; 500 would charge 500 gallons at the wrong rate.</p>\n<p>The same algorithm in Pascal:</p><pre>Program Water_Bill; {Calculates a customer's water bill}\nUses Crt;\nVar name: string;\nVar gallons, charge: real;\nBegin\n  Clrscr;\n  charge := 0;\n  Write('Enter customer name: ');\n  ReadLn(name);\n  Write('Enter gallons used: ');\n  ReadLn(gallons);\n  If gallons &gt;= 1000 Then\n    charge := 100 + gallons * 0.05\n  Else If gallons &gt;= 500 Then\n    charge := 50 + gallons * 0.03\n  Else\n    charge := 50 + gallons * 0.02;\n  WriteLn(name, ' owes $', charge:8:2);\n  ReadLn;\nEnd.</pre>"}]},{"id":"p2_s8","title":"Section 8: Paper 02 practice: the Dominica Schools' Rainfall Project","desc":"<p>Paper 02 structured question: program implementation (15 marks)</p>","minutes":0,"items":[{"t":"essay","title":"Paper 02 practice: the Dominica Schools' Rainfall Project","q":"<p><b>Read the scenario and the program, then answer the questions that follow.</b></p><p class=\"passage\">Students at Mahoe Grove High School record the rainfall each day for a week at the school's weather station. Their Pascal program reads the seven readings, totals them and reports the average and the number of rainy days (days with more than 0 mm).</p><pre><code class=\"language-pascal\">1  Program Rain_Week;\n2  Var\n3    day, rainyDays : Integer;\n4    rain, total : Real;\n5  Begin\n6    total := 0;\n7    rainyDays := 0;\n8    For day := 1 To 7 Do\n9      Begin\n10       ReadLn(rain);\n11       total := total + rain;\n12       If rain &gt;= 0 Then\n13         rainyDays := rainyDays + 1\n14     End;\n15   WriteLn('Average: ', total / 7:0:1);\n16   WriteLn('Rainy days: ', rainyDays)\n17 End.</code></pre><p>Answer ALL parts.</p><ol type=\"a\"><li><b>Name</b> the type of loop used and <b>state</b> whether it is bounded or unbounded iteration. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li><b>Identify</b> ONE variable used as an accumulator and ONE used as a counter. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li>The program reports 7 rainy days even for a dry week. <b>Identify</b> the line with the error, <b>state</b> the type of error and <b>write</b> the corrected line. <i>(3 marks)</i></li><li><b>Construct</b> a trace table for the first THREE days if the readings are 0, 4.5 and 12, showing day, rain, total and rainyDays. <i>(4 marks)</i></li><li><b>Explain</b> TWO ways in which the program's internal documentation could be improved. <i>(4 marks)</i></li></ol>","points":15,"fb":"<h4>Marking guide</h4><p><b>Mark scheme.</b> (a) 1 FOR; 1 bounded with reason. (b) 1 each. (c) 1 line 12; 1 logic error; 1 correction (&gt; 0). (d) 1 per correct row (3) + 1 correct column layout; accept the uncorrected trace (rainyDays 1, 2, 3) only if stated. (e) 2 marks per improvement (1 improvement, 1 reason).</p><h4>Model answer</h4><p><b>(a)</b> A FOR loop. It is bounded iteration because it repeats a fixed, known number of times (7).</p><p><b>(b)</b> Accumulator: total. Counter: rainyDays.</p><p><b>(c)</b> Line 12 contains a logic error: rain &gt;= 0 is true for dry days (0 mm), so every day is counted. It should read: If rain &gt; 0 Then</p><p><b>(d)</b> (corrected program) Day 1: rain 0, total 0, rainyDays 0. Day 2: rain 4.5, total 4.5, rainyDays 1. Day 3: rain 12, total 16.5, rainyDays 2.</p><p><b>(e)</b> 1. Add a program-head comment giving the purpose, author and dates created and modified, so a maintenance programmer can quickly understand what the program does and when it last changed. 2. Add a prompt before ReadLn, e.g. Write('Rainfall for day ', day, ' (mm): '), and comments beside the declarations explaining total and rainyDays, so both users and programmers know what each value means.</p>"}]}]},{"pkg":"Paper 01 Mock Papers","quizzes":[{"id":"pp_csecmock1","title":"Mock Paper 01 · Set 1","desc":"<p><b>CSEC Information Technology · Grade One · Mock Paper 01 · Set 1</b> · 1 hour 15 minutes · 60 items</p><ol><li>Answer <b>ALL</b> items. Each item has four options (A), (B), (C), (D). Choose the BEST answer.</li><li>Items 1–35 cover Sections 1–3 (Computer Fundamentals; Networks and Web Technologies; Social and Economic Impact of ICT), items 36–50 Sections 4–6 (Word Processing and Web Page Design; Spreadsheets; Database Management) and items 51–60 Sections 7–8 (Problem-Solving and Program Design; Program Implementation), as in the CXC paper.</li><li>You have about 75 seconds per item. Start the optional 75-minute timer to practise under exam conditions.</li><li>Submit to see your score by section, the key and an explanation of why each option is right or wrong.</li></ol><p class=\"tip\">An original mock paper written in the format of the CXC CSEC Information Technology Paper 01 (syllabus CXC 30/G/SYLL 17).</p>","minutes":75,"items":[{"t":"mc","title":"Item 1","q":"<p>Shanice uses her phone to pay her bmobile bill, a laptop to type her SBA and the school Wi-Fi to upload it to Google Classroom. Which term BEST describes the combined use of computers, software and telecommunications to capture, process, store and send information?</p>","options":["Information Technology","Data processing","Computer science","Telecommunications"],"correct":0,"why":"Key: A. Information Technology is the use of computers, software AND telecommunications to handle information, which is everything Shanice does. C is the study of computation and programming, not the everyday use described. D is only one part (turning data into information). C covers sending data over distance but not the computers and software.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 2","q":"<p>A weather office in Barbados must run hurricane-track models that need trillions of calculations per second. Which type of computer is MOST suitable?</p>","options":["Desktop computer","Mainframe","Supercomputer","Embedded computer"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. Supercomputers are built for raw calculation speed, which complex weather models need. C is designed to handle huge numbers of transactions and users at once (banks, airlines), not maximum calculation speed. B is far too slow. D runs one dedicated job inside a device.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 3","q":"<p>Zion is playing a football game on his laptop. Each time he scores, the game checks \"IF score > 50\" to unlock a new stadium. Which part of the CPU carries out this comparison?</p>","options":["Cache memory","Control unit (CU)","Read-only memory (ROM)","Arithmetic logic unit (ALU)"],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. The ALU performs arithmetic and logical comparisons such as greater than. D fetches and decodes instructions and directs the other parts, but it does not do the comparison itself. A is fast memory holding frequently used data. C stores start-up instructions.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 4","q":"<p>At the CJIA check-in desk an agent scans a passport, the system looks up the booking, prints a boarding pass and saves the check-in record. Which stage of the IPOS cycle is the saving of the record?</p>","options":["Processing","Input","Output","Storage"],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. Keeping the check-in record for later use is storage. Scanning the passport is A; looking up the booking is B; printing the boarding pass is D.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 5","q":"<p>Island Bloom Florists in Holetown wants staff in two shops to open the same price list from anywhere. They have a reliable Internet connection and a small budget for hardware. Which storage option BEST suits them?</p>","options":["An external hard drive kept in the main shop","Cloud storage","A USB flash drive carried by the manager","DVD-R discs posted between the shops"],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. Cloud storage lets both shops reach one up-to-date file from anywhere with the Internet, and needs no new hardware. A can only be used in one place. B is slow and cannot be updated once burned. D is easily lost and only one person can use it at a time.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 6","q":"<p>A supermarket on Fort Street wants cashiers to enter product details quickly and accurately at the till. Which input device is MOST appropriate?</p>","options":["Light pen","Optical mark reader","Graphics tablet","Barcode scanner"],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. A barcode scanner reads the product code directly, which is fast and accurate. B is for drawing. C reads pencil marks on forms such as multiple-choice answer sheets. A selects points on a screen and is rarely used today.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 7","q":"<p>The IT technician at Palmetto Point Secondary is listing the software on the lab computers for an audit. Which of the following should she list as SYSTEM software?</p>","options":["A disk defragmenter","A school timetable app written for Sandbox Tree College","A spreadsheet program","A photo-editing program"],"correct":0,"why":"Key: A. A defragmenter is a utility, and utilities are system software because they look after the computer itself. B and D are general-purpose application software. A is custom (bespoke) application software.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 8","q":"<p>Mr. Castillo, a network technician, types commands such as ipconfig and ping to test the school network quickly. Which type of user interface is he using?</p>","options":["Graphical user interface","Touch interface","Menu-driven interface","Command line interface"],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. Typing text commands at a prompt is a command line interface, which experts like for speed and control. A uses windows, icons and pointers. B offers a list of choices. C relies on taps and swipes.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 9","q":"<p>Kayla needs a laptop for video editing her Carnival TikToks. Which specification would make the BIGGEST difference to how smoothly the editing software runs?</p>","options":["A larger battery","16 GB RAM instead of 4 GB","Two USB ports instead of one","A 1 TB hard disk instead of a 512 GB hard disk"],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. Video editing holds large amounts of data in memory; more RAM stops the system swapping to storage and slowing down. A bigger disk in A stores more but does not make editing faster. Extra ports in D and a bigger battery in B do not affect processing speed.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 10","q":"<p>Jaden's printer shows \"Paper jam\". He has removed the jammed sheet but the message is still on. What should he do NEXT?</p>","options":["Buy a new USB cable","Replace the ink cartridges","Reinstall the operating system","Turn the printer off and on again, then send the document again"],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. After clearing a jam, restarting the printer resets the sensor and clears the queue, the simplest fix first. Reinstalling the operating system in D is drastic and unrelated. Ink in C has nothing to do with a jam. A cable in B would cause a connection error, not a jam message.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 11","q":"<p>Ms. Brooks enters her Form 4 IT test results into a spreadsheet. Which of the following is DATA rather than information?</p>","options":["The highest mark in the test was 91, scored by Tariq","Three students scored below the pass mark","The list 34, 67, 91, 45, 78","The class average for the IT test is 63%"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. Raw numbers with no context or meaning are data. A, C and D have all been processed and given context, so they are information.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 12","q":"<p>Daniel finds a website claiming a new hurricane will hit Belize next week. The page was updated today, but it names no author and its owner sells hurricane shutters. Which TWO criteria for reliable information does the page FAIL?</p>","options":["Authority and objectivity","Coverage and currency","Accuracy and coverage","Currency and usability"],"correct":0,"why":"Key: A. No named author means no authority, and a seller of shutters gains from spreading fear, so objectivity fails. Currency, in A and C, is fine because the page was updated today. Coverage, in A and D, is about how complete the information is, which is not the problem described. Usability, in C, is a website design criterion, not a test of reliability.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 13","q":"<p>A clerk types a student's date of birth into the school database, then a second clerk types it again and the computer compares the two entries. This is an example of</p>","options":["validation by a presence check","validation by a range check","verification by proofreading","verification by double entry"],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. Entering the data twice and comparing the copies is double entry, a verification method that checks the data was copied correctly. D and B are validation checks performed by the computer against rules, not second entries. A means visually reading the data against the source.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 14","q":"<p>The Oranjestad Courier's subscription form must reject a phone number unless it has exactly seven digits. Which validation check is this?</p>","options":["Consistency check","Presence check","Range check","Length check"],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. Checking the number of characters is a length check. A checks a value lies between limits (e.g. age 13 to 19). B checks a field is not empty. C compares two fields against each other.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 15","q":"<p>BEL prints meter readings for all 40,000 customers in one run every month, processing every record in customer-number order. Which file access method is MOST suitable?</p>","options":["Direct (random)","Index-sequential","Serial","Sequential"],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. When every record is processed in key order in one batch run, sequential access is efficient. D access suits finding single records instantly. C supports both and adds an index overhead not needed here. B files are stored in the order records arrived, not in customer-number order.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 16","q":"<p>The computers in one Sandbox Tree College lab are connected to each other and to a printer in the same room. This network is a</p>","options":["WAN","LAN","PAN","MAN"],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. A local area network covers a small area such as a room or building. C spans countries. B spans a town or city. D links one person's devices (phone, earbuds, watch) within a few metres.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 17","q":"<p>Seaside Credit Union has a website for its staff only, reached with a staff login, where staff read policies and book leave. This is</p>","options":["the Internet","an extranet","an intranet","a peer-to-peer network"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. An intranet is a private network using web technology for an organisation's own staff. A also lets selected outsiders (suppliers, partners) in. A is the public global network. D describes how computers share resources, not a private staff website.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 18","q":"<p>Breadfruit Valley Secondary is replacing its peer-to-peer lab with a CLIENT–SERVER network. Which statement about the new network is TRUE?</p>","options":["It does not need any network cables or wireless signals.","A central server manages files, security and backups for the client computers.","Every computer has equal status and shares its own files.","It cannot be used in a school."],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. In client-server networks a powerful server provides services centrally. A describes peer-to-peer. B is false: most schools use client-server. C is false: every network needs a transmission medium.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 19","q":"<p>Sea-Almond Secondary School's lab LAN must be connected to AlliouaganaTel's network so students can reach the Internet. Which device connects the LAN to the Internet and decides the best path for data packets?</p>","options":["Router","Switch","Network interface card","Hub"],"correct":0,"why":"Key: A. A router joins different networks (the LAN and the ISP's network) and routes packets between them. A forwards data only to the right device within a LAN. D broadcasts to every device in a LAN. C connects one computer to a network.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 20","q":"<p>A new data link between Las Américas Airport and the cruise pier must carry very high bandwidth and must not be affected by electrical interference. Which medium is BEST?</p>","options":["Coaxial cable","Twisted pair cable","Fibre-optic cable","Infrared"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. Fibre carries light, giving very high bandwidth and immunity to electrical interference. A and C carry electrical signals that suffer interference and lower speeds over distance. B works only over a few metres in line of sight.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 21","q":"<p>A walkie-talkie lets only ONE person speak at a time, but each person can both talk and listen. This mode of transmission is</p>","options":["half-duplex","full-duplex","simplex","broadband"],"correct":0,"why":"Key: A. Data flows in both directions but only one direction at a time. A is one direction only (like a radio broadcast). C is both directions at the same time (like a phone call). D is about capacity, not direction.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 22","q":"<p>Which mobile technology lets Aaliyah tap her phone on a card reader at a King Street shop to pay?</p>","options":["Satellite","Infrared","Near-field communication (NFC)","GPS"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. NFC works over a few centimetres and is used for contactless payment. A finds location. D needs line of sight and is used in TV remotes. C is used for long-distance links.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 23","q":"<p>The school posts the new timetable at https://www.mahoegrove.sr/students/timetable.html (an address). In this URL, what does .sr represent?</p>","options":["The protocol used to transfer the page","The file type of the page","The country-code top-level domain for Suriname","The name of the folder holding the page"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. .sr is Suriname’s country-code top-level domain. The protocol is https, which C describes. /students/ is the folder in D. .html is the file type in B.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 24","q":"<p>Dante types www.carnivalsxm.org into his browser and the Carnival page appears a moment later. What role did DNS play?</p>","options":["It stores a copy of every website.","It translates the domain name into the server's IP address.","It encrypts the page so it cannot be read.","It speeds up your Wi-Fi."],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. The Domain Name System looks up the IP address for a name, like a phone book. Encryption in B is done by HTTPS. C and A are not DNS functions.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 25","q":"<p>Two Form 4 students argue about whether \"the Internet\" and \"the Web\" are the same. Which statement settles the argument CORRECTLY?</p>","options":["The Internet and the Web are exactly the same thing.","The Internet is a service that runs on the Web.","E-mail is part of the World Wide Web.","The Web is a service that runs on the Internet."],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. The Internet is the global network; the Web is the collection of linked pages accessed over it using HTTP. B and A confuse the two. E-mail, in D, is a separate Internet service, not part of the Web.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 26","q":"<p>An attacker floods the Roseau cruise port booking website with so many fake requests that real cruise passengers cannot reach it. This attack is</p>","options":["software piracy","a denial-of-service attack","phishing","pharming"],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. DoS attacks overload a server to make it unavailable, which attacks availability. C tricks users into giving details. B redirects users to a fake site. D is illegal copying of software.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 27","q":"<p>Keisha receives a text: \"Your bank card is blocked. Click bit.ly/unblock to fix it.\" This is an example of</p>","options":["a worm","vishing","smishing","pharming"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. Smishing is phishing by SMS text message. A uses voice calls. C secretly redirects a real web address to a fake site. B is self-replicating malware.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 28","q":"<p>Malware that encrypts the files on The Port-au-Prince Courier's computers and demands payment to unlock them is</p>","options":["a Trojan horse","spyware","adware","ransomware"],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. Ransomware holds data hostage until a ransom is paid. A shows unwanted adverts. D secretly collects information. C disguises itself as useful software; it may deliver ransomware but is not defined by demanding payment.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 29","q":"<p>Hackers steal the customer records of a Caribbean hotel chain and publish them online. Which is the MOST serious impact on the ORGANISATION?</p>","options":["Customers must change their e-mail passwords","The hackers become famous","Loss of reputation and customer trust, plus possible legal penalties","The hotel's printers stop working"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. Organisations lose trust, bookings and may face fines under data protection laws. D is an impact on individuals. C is not an impact on the organisation. B is unrelated to a data leak.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 30","q":"<p>Which countermeasure BEST protects data on a laptop that might be stolen from a car?</p>","options":["Installing a firewall on the router at home","Encrypting the hard drive","Using a surge protector","Installing a larger screen"],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. Encryption makes the data unreadable without the key, even if the laptop is stolen. A is irrelevant. D protects against power spikes. A firewall at home in B does not protect a stolen device.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 31","q":"<p>A school wants to protect its server room against power cuts during storms so the server can shut down safely. What should it install?</p>","options":["A firewall","CCTV cameras","An uninterruptible power supply (UPS)","Antivirus software"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. A UPS gives battery power for long enough to shut down safely. D and C protect against software threats. A deters intruders.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 32","q":"<p>Which is the STRONGEST password for Kevin's school e-mail?</p>","options":["Gu@vaberry-Sunset-27!","password123","kevin2009","12345678"],"correct":0,"why":"Key: A. A long passphrase with upper and lower case, numbers and symbols is hard to guess or crack. D uses his name and birth year. A and C are among the most common passwords.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 33","q":"<p>Since the bank introduced online banking and ATMs, it needs fewer tellers but has hired cybersecurity staff. This shows that automation</p>","options":["only destroys jobs","has no effect on employment","causes some job loss but also creates new jobs requiring new skills","only creates jobs"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. Automation replaces routine jobs but creates skilled roles, so retraining matters. B, D and C are one-sided or false.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 34","q":"<p>Which IT professional is responsible for designing the structure of a database, managing access rights and making sure the data is backed up?</p>","options":["Systems analyst","Computer technician","Database administrator","Web designer"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. The DBA designs, secures and maintains databases. C designs websites. B studies the current system and designs new systems. A repairs and maintains hardware.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 35","q":"<p>Doctors at a hospital in St. Kitts use a video link to examine a patient on Nevis. This use of ICT is called</p>","options":["computer-aided design","telemedicine","e-commerce","telecommuting"],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. Telemedicine delivers health care at a distance. B is working from home. D is buying and selling online. A is designing using computers.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 36","q":"<p>Tamara copies a paragraph from a web page into her Word report but wants it to match the font of her report, not the website. Which paste option should she use?</p>","options":["Paste as Picture","Paste Link","Keep Text Only","Keep Source Formatting"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. Keep Text Only strips the web formatting so the text takes on the document's style. C keeps the website's fonts. A makes uneditable text. B links to the source instead of copying it.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 37","q":"<p>To write the chemical formula H<sub>2</sub>O correctly in a word processor, the 2 should be formatted as</p>","options":["superscript","small caps","subscript","bold"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. Subscript lowers small text below the line, as in H₂O. D raises it (as in 10²). A and C do not change the position.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 38","q":"<p>Jerome inserted a table of contents in his SBA report, then added a new heading. The new heading does NOT appear in the TOC. What should he do?</p>","options":["Insert a page break before the heading","Apply a Heading style to it and then update the table of contents","Make the heading bold and larger","Type the heading into the TOC by hand"],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. A TOC is built from heading styles and must be updated after changes. Typing by hand (C) breaks the automatic links and page numbers. Bold and size (D) are not a heading style. A page break (B) does not add an entry.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 39","q":"<p>Before building a website for the Simpson Bay Lagoon clean-up, a team draws boxes showing every page and how the pages link together. This diagram is a</p>","options":["spreadsheet chart","storyboard of one page","site map","mail merge"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. A site map shows all the pages of a site and the links between them. C (wireframe) shows the layout of a single page. D and B are not planning tools for a site.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 40","q":"<p>On a long FAQ page, the link \"Back to top\" jumps to the start of the SAME page. This hyperlink goes to</p>","options":["another web page","a bookmark (anchor) on the same page","an e-mail address","a user-created file"],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. Links to a place on the same page use a bookmark or anchor such as href=\"#top\". B, A and D are the other hyperlink types.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 41","q":"<p>In a spreadsheet, the value 450 is in the cell where column D meets row 7. The cell address is</p>","options":["D:7","D7","R7D","7D"],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. A cell address is the column letter followed by the row number. B reverses them. A is a range format. D is not valid.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 42","q":"<p>Cells B2:B31 hold the marks of 30 students. Which formula counts how many students scored 50 or more?</p>","options":["=COUNT(B2:B31,\">=50\")","=SUM(B2:B31>=50)","=COUNTIF(B2:B31,\">=50\")","=IF(B2:B31>=50,1,0)"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. COUNTIF counts cells in a range that meet a condition. COUNT in B counts numbers and ignores the criterion. C does not count: SUM ignores the TRUE/FALSE results (older versions show #VALUE!). IF in D tests one value, not a count.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 43","q":"<p>Cell D2 contains =B2*$F$1. If D2 is copied to D5, what formula is in D5?</p>","options":["=B5*$F$1","=B2*$F$1","=B5*$F$4","=E5*$F$1"],"correct":0,"why":"Key: A. The relative reference B2 moves down 3 rows to B5; the absolute reference $F$1 does not change. A changes the absolute reference. B changes nothing. C moves the column, but the copy was straight down.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 44","q":"<p>A teacher wants marks below 50 to turn red automatically. Which feature should she use?</p>","options":["Merge and centre","Freeze panes","Wrap text","Conditional formatting"],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. Conditional formatting changes a cell's appearance when a condition is met. D joins cells. A keeps headings visible. B shows long text on several lines.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 45","q":"<p>Mrs. Duzant wants to show what PERCENTAGE of the year's Carnival budget went to each of five categories. Which chart is MOST appropriate?</p>","options":["Line chart","Column chart showing months","Pie chart","Scatter chart"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. A pie chart shows parts of one whole. B shows trends over time. D shows the relationship between two numeric variables. C compares values over time, not shares of a total.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 46","q":"<p>Which is an advantage of a database over a set of separate spreadsheets for a school's records?</p>","options":["Data is stored once and linked, reducing duplication and inconsistency","It cannot be accessed by more than one user","It never needs backing up","It does not need a computer"],"correct":0,"why":"Key: A. Related tables avoid repeating data, so it stays consistent. C is false. A is false and would be a disadvantage. D is false.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 47","q":"<p>In a table of students, which field is the MOST suitable primary key?</p>","options":["StudentID","Surname","Form","DateOfBirth"],"correct":0,"why":"Key: A. A primary key must be unique for every record; IDs are assigned to be unique. Surnames (A), birth dates (B) and forms (D) can repeat.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 48","q":"<p>A field PhoneNumber holds numbers such as 5421234 that are never used in calculations. The BEST data type is</p>","options":["Yes/No","Text (short text)","Currency","Number"],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. Phone numbers are identifiers, not quantities; Text keeps leading zeros and allows symbols like + or -. C could drop a leading zero and suggests arithmetic. D is for money. B stores only two values.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 49","q":"<p>Which query criterion in the Surname field finds all surnames beginning with \"Ri\" (Richardson, Rijk, Richards)?</p>","options":["Like \"Ri*\"","= \"Ri\"","Between \"R\" And \"I\"","Like \"*Ri\""],"correct":0,"why":"Key: A. The * wildcard matches any characters after Ri. A matches only the exact word Ri. C finds names ENDING in ri. B is an invalid range.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 50","q":"<p>A report lists all sponsors of the Carnival parade grouped by Category, with the total donation for each category. Which report features are used?</p>","options":["Grouping and a summary (Sum) calculation","An input mask","A validation rule","A subform and a parameter"],"correct":0,"why":"Key: A. Grouping collects records by Category; Sum gives the total per group. B relates to forms and queries. D and A are table-design features.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 51","q":"<p>The St. Kitts and Nevis Youth Cricket League wants a program to calculate batting averages. Which should be the FIRST step?</p>","options":["Define the problem","Test the solution","Choose a programming language","Write the program"],"correct":0,"why":"Key: A. You must understand exactly what is required before designing a solution. B, C and A come later.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 52","q":"<p>A program reads the prices of items bought and outputs the total. In an IPO chart, \"total\" belongs under</p>","options":["Processing only","Output","Storage only","Input"],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. The total is the result displayed. Prices are B. Adding them is processing (C), and nothing here needs to be kept for later (D).","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 53","q":"<p>Kemar writes the steps for a robot that sorts bags at CJIA. Which of the following is NOT a property his algorithm should have?</p>","options":["Each step is precise and unambiguous.","It may run forever if the data is unusual.","The steps are in a logical order.","It has a finite number of steps."],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. An algorithm must terminate. A, B and C are required properties.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 54","q":"<p>Your teacher writes this pseudocode on the board for a warm-up. What is printed?<br><code style=\"white-space:pre\">count ← 0<br>FOR i ← 1 TO 4 DO<br>    count ← count + i<br>ENDFOR<br>PRINT count</code><br></p>","options":["5","0","10","4"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. count accumulates 1 + 2 + 3 + 4 = 10. B counts iterations instead of summing. A is the final value plus one. A ignores the loop.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 55","q":"<p>A program accepts exam marks from 0 to 100. Which value is BOUNDARY test data?</p>","options":["\"abc\"","57","100","-20"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. Boundary data sits at the edge of the valid range (0 or 100). B is normal data. D and C are erroneous data.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 56","q":"<p>A programmer at a Barbados engineering firm writes code for a tiny sensor chip using instructions like MOV A, 5 and ADD A, B. Which statement BEST describes the language she is using?</p>","options":["A low-level language that uses mnemonics such as ADD and MOV","A high-level language that uses English-like words such as PRINT","Binary code made only of 0s and 1s","A fourth-generation query language"],"correct":0,"why":"Key: A. Assembly (2GL) uses short mnemonics that map to machine instructions. A describes a 3GL such as Python. D is machine code (1GL). C describes 4GLs such as SQL.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 57","q":"<p>Akeem's program stops with an error when a user types \"ten\" instead of 10 at an int(input()) prompt. This is a</p>","options":["run-time error","logic error","syntax error","compilation error"],"correct":0,"why":"Key: A. The code is valid but crashes while running because of bad input. C or A would be found before running. D gives wrong answers without crashing.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 58","q":"<p>A Form 5 program for the school nurse asks each student for their age, which must be stored as a whole number. Which Python statement does this correctly?</p>","options":["age = input(int(\"Enter age: \"))","age = int(input(\"Enter age: \"))","input(age) = int","int age = input(\"Enter age: \")"],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. input() returns text, and int() converts it to an integer. B applies int() to the prompt text. D is Java-style. A is not valid syntax.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 59","q":"<p>Jaylen tests this Python code in the Code Lab. What is the output?<br><code style=\"white-space:pre\">total = 0<br>for i in range(1, 5):<br>    total = total + i<br>print(total)</code><br></p>","options":["5","4","15","10"],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. range(1, 5) gives 1, 2, 3, 4 (it stops BEFORE 5), so the total is 10. D wrongly includes 5. A and B count instead of adding.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 60","q":"<p>Your teacher asks her class to document the bus-fare program they wrote for their SBA. Which of these is INTERNAL documentation?</p>","options":["A printed user manual","A comment such as # calculate the discount","The technical specification of the server","A video tutorial for users"],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. Internal documentation is inside the program code: comments, meaningful names, indentation. A, B and C are external documentation.","fb":[]}]},{"id":"pp_csecmock2","title":"Mock Paper 01 · Set 2","desc":"<p><b>CSEC Information Technology · Grade One · Mock Paper 01 · Set 2</b> · 1 hour 15 minutes · 60 items</p><ol><li>Answer <b>ALL</b> items. Each item has four options (A), (B), (C), (D). Choose the BEST answer.</li><li>Items 1–35 cover Sections 1–3 (Computer Fundamentals; Networks and Web Technologies; Social and Economic Impact of ICT), items 36–50 Sections 4–6 (Word Processing and Web Page Design; Spreadsheets; Database Management) and items 51–60 Sections 7–8 (Problem-Solving and Program Design; Program Implementation), as in the CXC paper.</li><li>You have about 75 seconds per item. Start the optional 75-minute timer to practise under exam conditions.</li><li>Submit to see your score by section, the key and an explanation of why each option is right or wrong.</li></ol><p class=\"tip\">An original mock paper written in the format of the CXC CSEC Information Technology Paper 01 (syllabus CXC 30/G/SYLL 17).</p>","minutes":75,"items":[{"t":"mc","title":"Item 1","q":"<p>Sunny Isle Tours, a small tour company in Montego Bay, has moved all its bookings onto computers. Which of the following is a LIMITATION of relying on IT?</p>","options":["It allows customers to pay online.","It stores records in a small physical space.","It depends on a steady electricity supply and working networks.","It processes large amounts of data quickly."],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. When power or networks fail (for example after a hurricane) IT systems stop, a real limitation. D, C and B are all advantages of IT.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 2","q":"<p>A smart fridge in a Layou hotel kitchen sends an alert when its temperature rises. The computer inside the fridge is BEST described as</p>","options":["an embedded computer","a mainframe computer","a desktop computer","a supercomputer"],"correct":0,"why":"Key: A. An embedded computer is built into another device to perform one dedicated task. B and A are large, powerful systems. C is a general-purpose personal computer.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 3","q":"<p>Shaquille's Carnival parade video is 3 GB. His school limits uploads in megabytes. About how many megabytes is the video?</p>","options":["3,072 MB","24 MB","3,000,000 MB","300 MB"],"correct":0,"why":"Key: A. 1 GB = 1,024 MB, so 3 x 1,024 = 3,072 MB. A confuses MB with KB. D divides instead of multiplying. B multiplies by 8, confusing bytes with bits.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 4","q":"<p>Which of the following storage media is the MOST durable to carry daily in a school bag because it has no moving parts?</p>","options":["DVD","Magnetic tape","Solid-state drive (SSD)","Hard disk drive (HDD)"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. An SSD uses flash memory with no moving parts, so it survives knocks. B has spinning platters and a moving head that can be damaged by a drop. C scratches easily. D is used for backups in data centres, not carried daily.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 5","q":"<p>In an ATM transaction at a bank on Bridge Street, which step is OUTPUT?</p>","options":["The computer checks the balance.","The customer types the PIN.","The new balance is saved in the bank's database.","The machine dispenses cash and prints a receipt."],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. Cash and a receipt are produced for the user, so that is output. Typing the PIN in C is input; checking the balance in B is processing; saving the balance in D is storage.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 6","q":"<p>Coral Reef Dive Shop in Frigate Bay plans to keep ALL its files ONLY in the cloud. Which is a DISADVANTAGE of this plan?</p>","options":["The provider usually keeps backup copies.","Files cannot be reached when the Internet connection is down.","The business does not have to buy and maintain servers.","Files can be shared with staff in other locations."],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. Cloud storage depends on Internet access, a real problem during outages after storms. A, C and D are advantages.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 7","q":"<p>Mrs. Richardson, a teacher with limited use of her hands, wants to type lesson notes. Which input method is MOST suitable?</p>","options":["A light pen","Voice recognition using a microphone","A standard mouse","A graphics tablet"],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. Speech recognition lets her dictate without using her hands. A, C and B all need fine hand movement.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 8","q":"<p>A pharmacy in Brades prints small labels for medicine bottles and receipts at the counter. The printer must be quiet, fast and need no ink. Which printer is BEST?</p>","options":["Dot-matrix printer","Thermal printer","Inkjet printer","Plotter"],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. Thermal printers use heat-sensitive paper, need no ink and are quick and quiet, ideal for labels and receipts. B is noisy. A needs cartridges. C draws large technical drawings.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 9","q":"<p>LibreOffice can be downloaded free, and anyone may view and change its program code. It is BEST described as</p>","options":["open-source software","shareware","custom-written software","proprietary software"],"correct":0,"why":"Key: A. Open-source software makes the source code available for anyone to study and modify. D is a free trial of commercial software. C keeps its code secret and is licensed by the owner. A is written for one particular organisation.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 10","q":"<p>Grandpa Arrindell, who has never used a computer, wants to buy tickets at a ferry kiosk in Marigot. Which interface would be EASIEST for him?</p>","options":["A touch interface with large labelled buttons","A graphical interface needing right-click menus","A command line interface","A programming console"],"correct":0,"why":"Key: A. Large touch buttons are intuitive and need no prior skills. A and B need memorised commands. Right-click menus in C assume mouse experience.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 11","q":"<p>A Wallblake Road electronics store advertises a laptop with four specifications. Which one tells you about the PROCESSOR?</p>","options":["8 GB DDR4","Intel Core i5, 3.2 GHz, 6 cores","15.6-inch Full HD","512 GB SSD"],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. The processor is described by its make/model, clock speed (GHz) and number of cores. D is RAM. A is storage. C is the screen.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 12","q":"<p>A computer in the Bayleaf Hill lab shuts itself down after about 20 minutes and the case feels very hot. What is the MOST likely cause?</p>","options":["The printer is out of paper.","The monitor cable is loose.","The fan or air vents are blocked with dust, so the computer is overheating.","The keyboard is faulty."],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. Automatic shutdown with a hot case points to overheating, usually from dust or a failed fan. A loose monitor cable in C gives no picture but does not shut down the PC. B and D would not cause shutdowns.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 13","q":"<p>A customer fills in a paper form to open an account at Seabreeze Credit Union; the form is then keyed into the computer. The paper form is a</p>","options":["turnaround document","machine-readable document","source document","report"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. The original document from which data is first captured is a source document. B is output by a computer, filled in by a person and fed back in (like a utility bill stub). C can be read directly by a machine. A is output, not input.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 14","q":"<p>A clerk types Rhea's age as 15 although she is 16. The system accepts it because it lies between 11 and 19. Why can a VALID entry like this still be WRONG?</p>","options":["Validation checks that data was copied exactly from the source document.","Valid data is always checked by a second person.","Validation is only done after data has been printed.","Validation only checks that data is sensible and follows rules, not that it is the true value."],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. An age of 15 passes a range check of 11 to 19 even if the student is really 16. C describes verification. D describes double checking, not validation. B is false: validation happens on entry.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 15","q":"<p>An online form for the Carnival Development Committee checks that the Date Returned is not earlier than the Date Borrowed for costume rentals. This is a</p>","options":["type check","range check","consistency check","check digit"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. Comparing two related fields to make sure they agree is a consistency (inconsistency) check. C compares one value to fixed limits. B checks the kind of data (number, text). D validates a code using a calculated extra digit.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 16","q":"<p>Jamal links his phone to his wireless earbuds and smartwatch using Bluetooth. This forms a</p>","options":["MAN","WAN","LAN","PAN"],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. A personal area network connects one person's devices within a few metres. D covers a building. C covers a city. A covers countries.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 17","q":"<p>A government wants to connect offices in Port of Spain, San Fernando and Chaguanas so they share one system across the island. The MOST appropriate network type is a</p>","options":["WLAN in one office","MAN","PAN","LAN"],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. A metropolitan area network spans a town or island-sized area. D is a few metres. B or C covers one building, not several towns.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 18","q":"<p>A distributor lets its supermarket customers log in to its private network to check stock and place orders. This private network shared with outside partners is an</p>","options":["PAN","extranet","Internet","intranet"],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. An extranet extends a private network to selected outside users. B is for staff only. D is public. A links one person's devices.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 19","q":"<p>The school buys ten new desktop computers for its lab network. Which device is REQUIRED in EACH computer so that it can connect to the network?</p>","options":["Modem","Network interface card (NIC)","Repeater","Router"],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. Every networked device needs a NIC (wired or wireless) to send and receive data. D joins networks; A converts signals for the ISP line; C boosts a weak signal. None of these goes inside each computer.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 20","q":"<p>A school's Wi-Fi signal is weak in classrooms far from the office. What should the technician add?</p>","options":["A hub in the office","A longer keyboard cable","A second modem","A wireless access point (or extender) nearer the classrooms"],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. An extra access point broadcasts Wi-Fi where coverage is poor. A does not improve Wi-Fi coverage. B is unrelated. D connects wired devices in one place.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 21","q":"<p>GTT advertises a home Internet package with \"100 Mbps bandwidth\". Bandwidth is BEST defined as</p>","options":["the strength of a password","the amount of data that can be transmitted in a given time","the length of a network cable","the number of computers on a network"],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. Bandwidth is the capacity of a channel, measured in bits per second (e.g. Mbps). D, B and A are unrelated.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 22","q":"<p>After a hurricane, GSM masts are down but a relief team still needs to send data from a remote beach. Which technology is MOST likely to work?</p>","options":["Satellite communication","4G mobile data","Bluetooth","Infrared"],"correct":0,"why":"Key: A. Satellite links do not depend on local masts or cables. B and C have very short range. 4G in D needs working cell masts.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 23","q":"<p>A Belize City mobile provider is upgrading its masts from 4G to 5G. Which is the MAIN advantage customers will notice?</p>","options":["It uses no radio waves","It is only for voice calls","Much faster data speeds and lower latency","It works without any cell towers"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. 5G offers higher speeds and very low delay. D is false: it still needs masts (more of them). C is false: it uses radio waves. A is false: it is mainly for data.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 24","q":"<p>Why should Imani check for HTTPS before typing her card number on a website?</p>","options":["HTTPS proves that the shop's products are good quality.","HTTPS encrypts data between her browser and the server.","HTTPS makes the page load faster.","HTTPS blocks all advertisements."],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. HTTPS uses encryption so intercepted data cannot be read. B, D and A are not what HTTPS does; a padlock does not even prove a business is honest.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 25","q":"<p>Which Internet service lets a family in Barbados make a cheap video call to relatives in Guyana over the Internet?</p>","options":["VoIP","DNS","A wiki","FTP"],"correct":0,"why":"Key: A. Voice over IP carries voice and video calls over the Internet. A transfers files. D translates domain names. B is a collaboratively edited website.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 26","q":"<p>Which of the CIA principles is broken when a student changes his grades in the school database without permission?</p>","options":["Confidentiality","Integrity","Authentication","Availability"],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. Integrity means data is accurate and only changed by authorised people. A is about who can SEE the data. B is about data being reachable when needed. C is a security process, not one of the CIA three.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 27","q":"<p>An IT audit at a San Pedro school finds that the office computers have not had operating system updates for two years. This weakness is called a</p>","options":["vulnerability","threat","attack","countermeasure"],"correct":0,"why":"Key: A. A vulnerability is a weakness that could be exploited. A is something that might exploit it. B reduces the risk. C is the actual attempt.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 28","q":"<p>Tyrone downloads a \"free\" game which secretly lets a hacker control his computer. The program is a</p>","options":["cookie","worm","Trojan horse","firewall"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. A Trojan pretends to be useful software while hiding a malicious function. A spreads by itself across networks without needing a disguise. D protects a network. C is a small file a website stores in the browser.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 29","q":"<p>A student copies paragraphs from a website into her SBA report without acknowledging the source. This is</p>","options":["industrial espionage","software piracy","hacking","plagiarism"],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. Presenting someone else's work as your own is plagiarism. B is copying software illegally. D is unauthorised access. C is stealing a company's secrets.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 30","q":"<p>Mean comments about a Form 4 student spread in a WhatsApp group and on Instagram. Which is an impact of this cyberbullying on the INDIVIDUAL student?</p>","options":["Anxiety, low self-esteem and avoiding school","A fall in the company's share price","Loss of national security","Election results being changed"],"correct":0,"why":"Key: A. Cyberbullying harms victims' mental health and attendance. C and A are impacts on governments. D is an impact on organisations.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 31","q":"<p>Two-factor authentication (2FA) on Nadia's Instagram means she must</p>","options":["enter her password AND a code sent to her phone","use two different browsers","type her password twice","change her password every two days"],"correct":0,"why":"Key: A. 2FA combines something you know (password) with something you have (phone code). B is still one factor. C and A are not authentication factors.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 32","q":"<p>The St. John’s Courier installs a firewall between its office network and the Internet. A firewall protects the network by</p>","options":["filtering incoming and outgoing traffic according to rules","providing power during outages","removing viruses from infected files","making copies of files"],"correct":0,"why":"Key: A. Firewalls allow or block traffic based on rules. Removing viruses in B is antivirus software. A is a UPS. D is a backup.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 33","q":"<p>Which backup plan BEST protects a Cockburn Town accounting firm's data from a hurricane that floods its office?</p>","options":["Saving files twice in the same folder","Printing every document","Daily backups stored off-site or in the cloud","Backups on a second hard drive in the same office"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. Off-site or cloud copies survive damage to the building. D and C keep copies in the same place. Paper in B would also be flooded and is hard to restore.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 34","q":"<p>Ms. Hodge now works from home three days a week, connecting to her office over the Internet. This is</p>","options":["telecommuting","outsourcing","the gig economy","e-learning"],"correct":0,"why":"Key: A. Telecommuting is working away from the office using ICT. C involves short-term freelance jobs. B is contracting work to another firm. A is learning online.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 35","q":"<p>A bank in Antigua is closing two branches and moving customers to online and mobile banking. Which is a DRAWBACK of this for its customers?</p>","options":["Older customers without Internet skills may be left out.","Fewer queues in branches.","Transfers between accounts are faster.","Customers can bank 24 hours a day."],"correct":0,"why":"Key: A. The digital divide can exclude people without skills or access. A, C and D are benefits.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 36","q":"<p>Mr. Lake's newsletter spells \"Phillipsburg\" wrongly 23 times. The FASTEST way to correct every one is</p>","options":["undo","the format painter","the thesaurus","Find and Replace with Replace All"],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. Replace All changes every occurrence at once. C suggests synonyms. A copies formatting. D reverses the last action.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 37","q":"<p>Three teachers are editing the same sports-day programme, and the head of department wants to see every change and accept or reject each one. Which feature should they turn on?</p>","options":["Track Changes","Restrict Editing","Mail merge","Word count"],"correct":0,"why":"Key: A. Track Changes records each insertion and deletion so it can be accepted or rejected. B stops people editing. A creates personalised copies. C counts words.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 38","q":"<p>In a mail merge to send invitations to 120 parents, which item is the DATA SOURCE?</p>","options":["The printed letters","The letter containing merge fields","The list of parents' names and addresses","The envelope labels"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. The data source holds the records (names, addresses) that fill the fields. B is the primary (main) document. B and D are the merged output.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 39","q":"<p>Jada is coding the home page for her school's Earth Day website and must include the logo with alternative text for screen readers. Which HTML code is correct?</p>","options":["&lt;a href=\"logo.png\"&gt;Logo&lt;/a&gt;","&lt;img src=\"logo.png\" alt=\"Flamboyant Heights College logo\"&gt;","&lt;logo src=\"logo.png\"&gt;","&lt;img alt=\"logo.png\"&gt;"],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. The img tag needs src for the file and alt for the description. B has no src, so no picture shows. C creates a hyperlink to the image file. A is not a real HTML tag.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 40","q":"<p>A business website looks fine on a desktop but on a phone the menu runs off the edge of the screen. Which evaluation criterion does it FAIL?</p>","options":["Authority of the author","Currency of information","Display on different devices (responsiveness)","Accuracy of content"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. A site should adapt to different screen sizes. C, D and B are about the content, not how it displays.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 41","q":"<p>Mama's Johnny Cakes, a food stall in San Fernando, wants to plan its monthly budget. Which is the BEST reason to use a spreadsheet?</p>","options":["It replaces the need for an accountant entirely.","It can store photographs of every product.","Totals recalculate automatically when a value changes, so 'what-if' questions can be tested.","It prevents all errors in data entry."],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. Automatic recalculation makes what-if analysis fast. A is not the purpose. C is false: wrong inputs give wrong outputs. D overstates what a spreadsheet does.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 42","q":"<p>Cell A1 = 10, A2 = 4, A3 = 2. What is the result of =A1-A2*A3?</p>","options":["2","-18","12","6"],"correct":0,"why":"Key: A. Multiplication comes before subtraction (BODMAS): 4*2 = 8, then 10-8 = 2. D works left to right: (10-4)*2 = 12. C and A are miscalculations.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 43","q":"<p>A scorekeeper's sheet has 300 rows of cricket scores. She wants the column headings in row 1 to stay visible as she scrolls down. Which feature should she use?</p>","options":["Merge cells","Sort ascending","Hide row 1","Freeze panes"],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. Freezing panes locks the top row in place. D would hide the headings. B reorders data. A joins cells.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 44","q":"<p>The tourism office has 2,000 rows of visitor data with the columns Month, Country and Visitors. The manager wants a quick summary table showing total visitors for each country, which can be rearranged by month without writing formulas. Which feature should be used?</p>","options":["A line chart","Freeze panes","A pivot table","Conditional formatting"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. A pivot table groups and summarises large data sets (e.g. Sum of Visitors by Country) and can be rearranged by dragging fields. C only keeps headings visible. A displays data but does not summarise it into a table. B changes the look of cells that meet a condition.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 45","q":"<p>In a workbook, cell B4 on the sheet named Summary must show the value in cell C10 of the sheet named January. Which formula is correct?</p>","options":["=January!C10","=SUM(January)","=C10!January","=January:C10"],"correct":0,"why":"Key: A. A reference to another sheet uses SheetName!Cell. D reverses the parts. B uses a range colon wrongly. A is not valid without a range.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 46","q":"<p>The Customers table of Island Water Sports holds the ID, name, phone and e-mail of each customer. In database terminology, all the details stored about ONE customer form a</p>","options":["query","field","table","record"],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. A record (row) holds all the fields for one entity. A is one item such as Surname. C holds many records. B asks a question of the data.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 47","q":"<p>Table Orders contains a field CustomerID which is the primary key of table Customers. In table Orders, CustomerID is a</p>","options":["primary key","candidate key","foreign key","composite key"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. A foreign key is a field in one table that is the primary key of another, creating the link. It is not the primary key (A) of Orders. C is made of two or more fields. B is any field that could be the primary key of its own table.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 48","q":"<p>In a table of library books, each book has ONE publisher, but a publisher can publish MANY books. What is the relationship between Publishers and Books?</p>","options":["No relationship","One-to-one","Many-to-many","One-to-many"],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. One publisher relates to many books. A would mean each publisher has one book. B would need a linking table. A is incorrect.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 49","q":"<p>A query must show members aged 13 to 17 inclusive. Which criterion in the Age field is correct?</p>","options":["Between 13 And 17",">13 And <17","<13 Or >17","13 Or 17"],"correct":0,"why":"Key: A. Between includes both end values. D excludes 13 and 17. C finds only those exact ages. A finds everyone outside the range.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 50","q":"<p>The guidance counsellor needs an alphabetical list of the names of all students in Form 4B from the Students table. Which SQL statement produces it?</p>","options":["SELECT Students FROM Name WHERE Form = \"4B\";","SELECT Name FROM Students ORDER BY Form;","SELECT Name FROM Students WHERE Form = \"4B\" ORDER BY Name;","SELECT * FROM Students WHERE Name = \"4B\";"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. SELECT field FROM table WHERE condition ORDER BY field is the right structure. B swaps table and field. D has no WHERE. C tests the wrong field.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 51","q":"<p>Breaking the problem \"organise the sports day\" into smaller tasks such as \"register athletes\", \"record results\" and \"award points\" is called</p>","options":["top-down design (stepwise refinement)","debugging","a dry run","compiling"],"correct":0,"why":"Key: A. Top-down design divides a big problem into smaller, manageable sub-problems. C removes errors. D translates code. A tests an algorithm by hand.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 52","q":"<p>A program stores the value of GCT as 0.15, and this value never changes while the program runs. GCT should be declared as a</p>","options":["Boolean","string","constant","variable"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. A constant holds a value that does not change during execution. C can change. D and A are data types, and 0.15 is real, not string or Boolean.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 53","q":"<p>Kiara is drawing a flowchart for a voter-registration check. Which symbol should she use for the DECISION \"Is age >= 18?\"</p>","options":["Oval","Parallelogram","Rectangle","Diamond"],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. Decisions are diamonds with Yes/No exits. B is a process. C is input/output. D is start/stop.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 54","q":"<p>A savings game doubles a player's coins each round. How many times does the body of this loop execute?<br><code style=\"white-space:pre\">n ← 1<br>WHILE n &lt; 20 DO<br>    n ← n * 2<br>ENDWHILE</code><br></p>","options":["10","4","20","5"],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. n takes the values 1, 2, 4, 8, 16 at the start of each pass (5 passes), becoming 32, which ends the loop. A forgets the pass that starts with n = 16. C and D assume n goes up by one or two.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 55","q":"<p>Before typing her loop into the Code Lab, Precious completes a trace table for it. A trace table is used to</p>","options":["store the program on disk","translate pseudocode into machine code","draw the symbols of a flowchart","record the values of variables step by step to check an algorithm's logic"],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. A trace table records variable values during a dry run. D, A and B are not its purpose.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 56","q":"<p>Which translator converts the WHOLE high-level program into machine code BEFORE it runs, producing an executable file?</p>","options":["Compiler","Interpreter","Linker","Assembler"],"correct":0,"why":"Key: A. A compiler translates the whole program at once into an executable. B translates and runs line by line. D translates assembly language. A joins compiled modules.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 57","q":"<p>Put these steps in program implementation in the correct order: (1) execute, (2) create source code, (3) maintain, (4) translate/compile, (5) link.</p>","options":["2, 4, 5, 1, 3","2, 5, 4, 1, 3","1, 2, 4, 5, 3","4, 2, 5, 1, 3"],"correct":0,"why":"Key: A. Write the source code, translate it, link it, run it, then maintain it. A compiles before writing. B links before compiling. C executes first.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 58","q":"<p>Andre's Python program for a cricket scoreboard will not run. Which line contains a SYNTAX error?</p>","options":["total = total + 1","print(\"Total is\" total)","if total > 50:","print(\"Total is\", total)"],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. A comma is missing between the arguments, breaking the language rules. B, A and D are valid Python.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 59","q":"<p>A program stores the scores from four rounds of a dominoes tournament. What is printed?<br><code style=\"white-space:pre\">scores = [12, 45, 7, 30]<br>big = scores[0]<br>for s in scores:<br>    if s &gt; big:<br>        big = s<br>print(big)</code><br></p>","options":["30","12","45","7"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. The loop keeps the largest value seen: 12, then 45; 7 and 30 are smaller. B is only the starting value. A is the last element. D is the smallest.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 60","q":"<p>Which Python loop is MOST suitable for reading prices until the cashier types 0?</p>","options":["print(price)","if price == 0:","for i in range(10):","while price != 0:"],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. A while loop repeats an unknown number of times until the sentinel 0. A for loop with range (D) repeats a fixed number of times. C chooses once. B only outputs.","fb":[]}]},{"id":"pp_csecmock3","title":"Mock Paper 01 · Set 3","desc":"<p><b>CSEC Information Technology · Grade One · Mock Paper 01 · Set 3</b> · 1 hour 15 minutes · 60 items</p><ol><li>Answer <b>ALL</b> items. Each item has four options (A), (B), (C), (D). Choose the BEST answer.</li><li>Items 1–35 cover Sections 1–3 (Computer Fundamentals; Networks and Web Technologies; Social and Economic Impact of ICT), items 36–50 Sections 4–6 (Word Processing and Web Page Design; Spreadsheets; Database Management) and items 51–60 Sections 7–8 (Problem-Solving and Program Design; Program Implementation), as in the CXC paper.</li><li>You have about 75 seconds per item. Start the optional 75-minute timer to practise under exam conditions.</li><li>Submit to see your score by section, the key and an explanation of why each option is right or wrong.</li></ol><p class=\"tip\">An original mock paper written in the format of the CXC CSEC Information Technology Paper 01 (syllabus CXC 30/G/SYLL 17).</p>","minutes":75,"items":[{"t":"mc","title":"Item 1","q":"<p>The Trinidadian tourism office uses computers and the Internet to collect visitor numbers and publish monthly reports. Which statement BEST describes the relationship between IT and information shown here?</p>","options":["IT is used only to store information, not to process it.","IT and information mean the same thing.","Information can only exist on a computer.","IT provides the tools that turn data into useful information and communicate it."],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. IT is the set of tools (hardware, software, networks) used to capture, process, store and share information. B confuses tool and product. C is false: information exists on paper too. D ignores processing and communication.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 2","q":"<p>A delivery rider for a Port of Spain pharmacy compares devices he could carry on his scooter. Which list puts them in order from MOST portable to LEAST portable?</p>","options":["Laptop, smartphone, smartwatch, desktop","Smartphone, desktop, smartwatch, laptop","Desktop, laptop, smartphone, smartwatch","Smartwatch, smartphone, laptop, desktop"],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. A smartwatch is worn on the wrist, then a phone in the pocket, then a laptop in a bag, and a desktop stays on a desk. A and D mix the order; C is the reverse.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 3","q":"<p>Leah was typing her SBA when the power went off and her laptop shut down; the unsaved work was lost. Which type of memory lost its contents?</p>","options":["Flash memory in an SSD","RAM","ROM","A memory card"],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. RAM is volatile; it needs power to keep its contents. D is permanent. Flash memory in B and A is non-volatile, so data survives without power.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 4","q":"<p>A Barbados film club stores finished films on Blu-ray discs for its archive. A Blu-ray disc is an example of which type of secondary storage?</p>","options":["Primary","Solid-state","Magnetic","Optical"],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. Blu-ray, DVD and CD are read and written with a laser, so they are optical. C storage uses magnetised surfaces (hard disks, tape). D storage uses flash chips. B storage is RAM/ROM, not a disc.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 5","q":"<p>A temperature sensor in a school's server room sends readings to a computer, which switches on an air conditioner if the room is above 24 °C. Switching on the air conditioner is</p>","options":["input","processing","storage","output"],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. The signal sent to the actuator that turns on the air conditioner is the result produced by the system, so it is output. The sensor reading is B; comparing with 24 °C is D; logging readings would be A.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 6","q":"<p>Which factor is the MOST important reason a school might keep its exam results on a LOCAL server rather than in the cloud?</p>","options":["It wants full physical control over who can reach the sensitive data.","It wants the provider to handle all backups automatically.","It wants staff to reach the results from any country.","It wants to avoid buying any hardware."],"correct":0,"why":"Key: A. Local storage keeps sensitive data on premises under the school's own control. D, A and C are reasons to choose cloud storage instead.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 7","q":"<p>The Government of Haiti wants to read the marks on 3,000 multiple-choice census forms where people shaded boxes in pencil. Which device should it use?</p>","options":["Optical mark reader (OMR)","Magnetic ink character reader (MICR)","Optical character reader (OCR)","Barcode reader"],"correct":0,"why":"Key: A. OMR detects the position of shaded marks on a pre-printed form. C converts printed or written letters into editable text. D reads the magnetic numbers on cheques. A reads printed barcodes.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 8","q":"<p>A blind student at Seagrape Bay Secondary School needs to read the text of a website. Which output device would help MOST?</p>","options":["A plotter","A projector","A refreshable braille display","A dot-matrix printer"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. A braille display raises pins to show the text as braille cells that can be read by touch. C, D and B all produce visual output.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 9","q":"<p>A guesthouse in San Pedro pays a programmer to write a booking system just for its six rooms. This software is</p>","options":["open-source","custom-written (bespoke)","off-the-shelf","a utility program"],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. Software written for the specific needs of one organisation is custom (bespoke). B software is bought ready-made by many users. D describes how the code is licensed, not who it was written for. A maintains the computer.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 10","q":"<p>A hotel in Montego Bay is thinking of letting guests control room lights with voice commands such as \"Turn off the lamp\". Which is the MAIN disadvantage of a voice interface?</p>","options":["It needs the user to memorise many commands.","It may misunderstand accents and does not work well in noisy places.","It needs a mouse and keyboard.","It cannot be used by people who cannot see."],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. Voice interfaces struggle with background noise (a fete, a classroom) and some Caribbean accents. C describes a command line. D is wrong: voice suits visually impaired users. C is wrong: voice needs neither.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 11","q":"<p>The school office must choose between two PCs for typing letters and keeping student records. PC1: Core i3, 8 GB RAM, 256 GB SSD, $650. PC2: Core i9, 64 GB RAM, 4 TB SSD, $3,200. Which choice is BEST justified?</p>","options":["PC1, because a Core i3 is faster than a Core i9","PC2, because records need 4 TB of storage","PC1, because it meets the needs of office software at a much lower cost","PC2, because the most powerful computer is always the best choice"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. Evaluating a system means matching the specification to the purpose and budget; letters and records need little power. D ignores cost and purpose. C grossly overestimates the storage needed. B is false: a Core i9 is faster.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 12","q":"<p>After Hurricane season repairs, a classroom PC shows \"No Internet\" but other PCs are online. What should the technician check FIRST?</p>","options":["Whether the network cable is plugged in or the Wi-Fi is connected on that PC","Whether the PC needs more RAM","Whether the ISP has shut down the island's Internet","Whether the printer driver is installed"],"correct":0,"why":"Key: A. Troubleshooting starts with the simplest, local cause; if only one PC is offline, its own connection is most likely at fault. A is unlikely because other PCs work. D and B do not cause a lost connection.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 13","q":"<p>Which source is MOST likely to give ACCURATE, CURRENT information on an approaching storm?</p>","options":["A video posted by an account that sells generators","The official meteorological service website, updated today","A blog post written three years ago","A forwarded WhatsApp voice note with no sender details"],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. An official, named authority with a current date scores best on authority and currency. A has no authority. C is out of date. B is not objective because the seller benefits from fear.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 14","q":"<p>A Caribbean bank account number ends in an extra digit calculated from the other digits, so that a single mistyped digit is detected. This extra digit is a</p>","options":["check digit","presence check","range check","batch total"],"correct":0,"why":"Key: A. A check digit is calculated from the other digits and recalculated on entry; a mismatch shows an error. B checks limits. D checks the field is not empty. A adds up values in a batch of forms.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 15","q":"<p>The Bayleaf Hill Secondary library system must find any one student's borrowing record instantly, but also print a full list in student ID order at the end of term. Which file organisation is BEST?</p>","options":["Sequential only","Serial","Index-sequential","Magnetic tape storage"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. Index-sequential stores records in key order (for the full list) and keeps an index for fast direct access to one record. A serial file (C) is unordered. A sequential-only file (B) needs every earlier record read first to find one. Tape in D supports only sequential access.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 16","q":"<p>A small family-run guesthouse links its three computers so each can share files and the one printer, without buying a server. In this PEER-TO-PEER network,</p>","options":["only one computer can print","computers must be in different countries","each computer can share its own resources and there is no dedicated server","one central server controls every computer"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. Peer-to-peer computers have equal status and share directly. A describes client-server. B and C are false.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 17","q":"<p>A regional bank is reviewing its networks. Which of the following is the BEST example of a WAN?</p>","options":["The Wi-Fi in a café","A phone linked to earbuds","The network that connects a bank's branches in St. Kitts, Antigua and Barbados","Five computers in a Lelydorp office"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. A WAN spans large distances, such as several countries. C and A are LANs/WLANs. B is a PAN.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 18","q":"<p>The Hummingbird Ridge computer lab uses a STAR topology with all PCs cabled to one central switch. What happens if one workstation's cable is cut?</p>","options":["Data travels the other way round the ring.","The whole network stops working.","Every computer loses Internet but not files.","Only that workstation loses its connection."],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. Each device has its own link to the central switch, so one cable failure affects only that device. D describes a bus backbone failure. C is false. A describes a ring topology.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 19","q":"<p>Which device converts digital signals from a computer into a form that can travel over a telephone or cable line, and back again?</p>","options":["Modem","Switch","Network interface card","Hub"],"correct":0,"why":"Key: A. A modem modulates and demodulates signals. A and B connect devices inside a LAN. C connects a device to the LAN.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 20","q":"<p>A TV remote that only sends signals to the TV uses which mode of transmission?</p>","options":["Half-duplex","Multiplex","Full-duplex","Simplex"],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. Data travels in one direction only. A allows both ways, one at a time. D allows both ways at once. B combines several signals on one channel.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 21","q":"<p>A technician is wiring a new Form 5 classroom so each PC connects to a switch on the wall. Which transmission medium is MOST likely to be used?</p>","options":["Twisted pair (Ethernet) cable","Satellite","Microwave dish","Fibre-optic undersea cable"],"correct":0,"why":"Key: A. Twisted pair is cheap and standard for short LAN links. Undersea fibre (C), satellite (A) and microwave (D) are for long-distance links.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 22","q":"<p>During a power cut, Malik turns on the hotspot on his phone so his sister can finish her homework on her laptop. A hotspot allows the phone to</p>","options":["receive TV signals","share its mobile data connection with other devices over Wi-Fi","make the battery last longer","print documents without a printer"],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. A hotspot turns the phone into a wireless access point. C, B and A are not hotspot functions.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 23","q":"<p>Which is a DISADVANTAGE of relying on mobile data for an online exam at home in a hilly area of St. Kitts?</p>","options":["Mobile phones cannot use the Internet.","Mobile data cannot carry web pages.","Coverage and signal strength may be poor or unreliable.","Mobile data has no cost."],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. Hills and distance from masts weaken signals, a real problem on small islands. B and A are false. C is false and would not be a disadvantage.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 24","q":"<p>After the Carnival J'ouvert jump-up, Tia saves her phone video to her cloud drive to free space. Which term describes sending the video FROM her phone TO the cloud server?</p>","options":["Browsing","Streaming","Downloading","Uploading"],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. Sending data from your device to a remote server is uploading. C is the reverse. D is viewing pages. C is playing media while it downloads.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 25","q":"<p>A group of Form 5 students all edit the same online page of revision notes, and the page keeps a history of changes. This is a</p>","options":["web browser","search engine","blog","wiki"],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. A wiki is a site many users can edit collaboratively, with version history. C is mainly written by one author in dated posts. A finds pages. D displays pages.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 26","q":"<p>A fake website looks exactly like the bank's real site, and users are sent there even when they type the correct address because the DNS has been tampered with. This is</p>","options":["phishing","vishing","pharming","spam"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. Pharming redirects a correct address to a fake site by poisoning DNS or a hosts file. B relies on a link in a message. C uses phone calls. D is unsolicited bulk e-mail.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 27","q":"<p>Someone secretly records data passing over an unsecured café Wi-Fi network. This misuse is</p>","options":["propaganda","electronic eavesdropping","a denial-of-service attack","software piracy"],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. Intercepting communications without permission is electronic eavesdropping. D overloads a service. B is illegal copying. C spreads biased information.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 28","q":"<p>A stall in a Caribbean market sells burned copies of a popular football video game for $5 each. Selling these copies is</p>","options":["software piracy and copyright infringement","phishing","plagiarism","identity theft"],"correct":0,"why":"Key: A. Unauthorised copying and selling of software breaks copyright. D is passing off work as your own in writing. B tricks people into giving information. A uses someone else's personal details.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 29","q":"<p>False stories spread on social media before an election claim that a candidate has left the island. Which impact on GOVERNMENT is MOST likely?</p>","options":["The candidate's phone battery runs down","Slower printers in government offices","Loss of public trust and interference with fair elections","Higher electricity bills"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. Fake news can mislead voters and damage trust in democratic processes. C, A and B are unrelated.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 30","q":"<p>SKELEC is reviewing the security of its server room. Which of these is a PHYSICAL security measure?</p>","options":["Encryption","Antivirus software","A biometric fingerprint lock on the door","A strong password policy"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. A lock controls physical entry. A, C and D are software or logical measures.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 31","q":"<p>The school gives teachers permission to EDIT grades but students may only VIEW their own. This countermeasure is</p>","options":["access privileges (user rights)","a backup","a firewall","an audit trail"],"correct":0,"why":"Key: A. Access privileges set what each user may read or change. B records who did what. D filters network traffic. C copies data.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 32","q":"<p>A viral post claims the St. Vincent ferry has sunk, and Josh's friends are sharing it. Which practice is part of good digital citizenship?</p>","options":["Posting classmates' photos without asking","Thinking before sharing and checking a story is true","Sharing your password with your best friend","Clicking every link to see where it goes"],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. Responsible users check facts before sharing. B, D and A put people at risk.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 33","q":"<p>Which group is MOST at risk of job loss when a supermarket installs self-checkout machines?</p>","options":["Software developers","Cybersecurity analysts","Cashiers","Network technicians"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. Routine, repetitive tasks such as scanning items are the easiest to automate. C, A and D are roles that automation creates or needs more of.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 34","q":"<p>The person who studies a business's current manual system, gathers requirements and designs a new computer system is a</p>","options":["graphic artist","programmer","network administrator","systems analyst"],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. The systems analyst investigates problems and designs solutions. B writes the code from the design. C manages the network. D creates images and visuals.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 35","q":"<p>A fisherman in Grenada uses an app to check wave heights and fish prices at different markets before he sets out. This BEST shows ICT in</p>","options":["entertainment","agriculture and fisheries","law enforcement","journalism"],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. Weather and market-price apps support fishing decisions. B, A and C are different fields.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 36","q":"<p>Shania wants page 1 of her report in portrait and page 2, a wide table, in landscape. What must she insert between the pages?</p>","options":["A footnote","A manual line break","A column break","A section break (next page)"],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. Orientation is set per section, so a section break lets each part have its own layout. D affects columns only. B starts a new line. C adds a note at the bottom of the page.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 37","q":"<p>The guidance office sends out a form letter and wants to stop anyone changing it, but still allow it to be opened and read. Which option is BEST?</p>","options":["Insert a comment","Restrict Editing so the document is read-only","Spell check","Track Changes"],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. Restricting editing (or marking as read-only with a password) prevents changes while allowing reading. B records changes but allows them. C and A do not protect the document.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 38","q":"<p>On a fillable registration form for the Carnival Village food tent, applicants must choose ONE of: Johnny cakes, Mango drinks, Johnny cakes and drinks. Which form control is MOST suitable?</p>","options":["A date picker","A plain text box","A drop-down list","A picture content control"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. A drop-down list limits the entry to set choices, preventing typing errors. C allows anything. D is for dates. A is for images.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 39","q":"<p>A Form 5 group is designing a six-page website for a hurricane-preparedness campaign. Which design feature BEST helps users move between its pages?</p>","options":["Long paragraphs of text","A consistent navigation bar on every page","Very large images on the home page","A different background colour on each page"],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. Consistent navigation in the same place lets users find any page. B confuses users. D slows loading. A makes reading harder.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 40","q":"<p>A web page contains: &lt;a href=\"mailto:info@carnivalsxm.org\"&gt;Contact us&lt;/a&gt;. What happens when a visitor clicks \"Contact us\"?</p>","options":["The browser opens the Carnival website home page","A new e-mail message opens addressed to info@carnivalsxm.org","The visitor jumps to the top of the page","A file downloads to the computer"],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. The mailto: link type opens the user's e-mail program. D needs a #bookmark. B needs a link to a file. A needs a web address.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 41","q":"<p>In a sports-day sheet, C2 holds a student's house points. Which formula displays \"Qualify\" if the points are 20 or more, otherwise \"Try again\"?</p>","options":["=COUNTIF(C2,\">=20\")","=IF(C2>20,\"Qualify\",\"Try again\")","=IF(C2>=20,\"Qualify\",\"Try again\")","=IF(C2>=20,\"Try again\",\"Qualify\")"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. IF(test, value if true, value if false) with >= includes exactly 20. C excludes 20. D swaps the results. A returns a count (0 or 1), not the words.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 42","q":"<p>A price list is in A2:B50 (code in column A, price in column B). Which formula finds the price of the item code typed in E2?</p>","options":["=VLOOKUP(E2,$A$2:$B$50,2,FALSE)","=VLOOKUP(E2,$A$2:$B$50,1,FALSE)","=VLOOKUP($A$2:$B$50,E2,2,FALSE)","=LOOKUP(2,E2)"],"correct":0,"why":"Key: A. VLOOKUP(lookup value, table, column number, FALSE for exact match) returns column 2, the price. C has the arguments in the wrong order. B returns column 1, the code itself. D is incorrect syntax.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 43","q":"<p>A cell shows #DIV/0!. What is the MOST likely cause?</p>","options":["A function name is misspelled","The column is too narrow","The formula divides by an empty cell or a cell containing zero","A referenced cell was deleted"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. #DIV/0! means division by zero (empty cells count as 0). A misspelled function in B gives #NAME?. A narrow column in C shows ####. A deleted cell in D gives #REF!.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 44","q":"<p>A sheet of costume orders must be sorted by Band (A to Z) and, within each band, by Cost (highest first). Which is correct?</p>","options":["Primary key Cost descending; secondary key Band ascending","Sort Band only, then type the costs in order","Primary key Band descending; secondary key Cost ascending","Primary key Band ascending; secondary key Cost descending"],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. The main grouping is the primary key; the order within each group is the secondary key. D swaps the keys. A reverses both directions. B is manual and error-prone.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 45","q":"<p>A school has one worksheet per term and a Year sheet with =SUM(Term1:Term3!D5). What does this formula do?</p>","options":["Counts the sheets in the workbook","Adds cells D1 to D5 on the Term1 sheet only","Adds cell D5 on every sheet from Term1 to Term3","Links to an external file named Term3"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. This is a 3-D reference: the same cell across a range of sheets. D, B and C misread the reference.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 46","q":"<p>A small clinic in Puerto Plata plans to replace its paper patient cards with a computerised database. Which is a DISADVANTAGE?</p>","options":["Staff need training, and setup costs money","Reports can be produced automatically","Records can be searched quickly","Several staff can see the same up-to-date record"],"correct":0,"why":"Key: A. Costs and training are genuine drawbacks. B, A and D are advantages.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 47","q":"<p>A linking table StudentClub records which students belong to which clubs. Its primary key is made from BOTH StudentID and ClubID. This is a</p>","options":["secondary key","single-field primary key","composite key","foreign key only"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. A primary key made of two or more fields is composite. Each part is ALSO a foreign key, but together they form the key, so D is incomplete. C is used for sorting or searching. A is a single field.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 48","q":"<p>In the school sports federation's Athletes table, the Gender field must only accept M or F. Which table property enforces this?</p>","options":["Caption","Default value","Validation rule","Field size of 50"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. A validation rule such as \"M\" Or \"F\" rejects anything else. A fills a value in automatically. C changes the label. B allows 50 characters.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 49","q":"<p>In a query for a Basseterre hardware store, a new column is typed as LineTotal: [Quantity]*[UnitPrice]. This is a</p>","options":["primary key","calculated field","foreign key","parameter query"],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. A calculated field creates a new value from other fields. A prompts for a value. D and B are keys in tables.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 50","q":"<p>A data-entry screen shows one customer at the top and a list of that customer's orders underneath. This design is a</p>","options":["report with grouping","parameter query","table in Datasheet View","main form with a subform"],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. A subform shows the related 'many' records for the record in the main form. A is for printing. C asks for a value. D shows rows of one table.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 51","q":"<p>After designing an algorithm to calculate bus fares, Ravi tests it with several fares to make sure it gives the right answers. Which problem-solving step is this?</p>","options":["Test and validate the solution","Define the problem","Determine the most efficient solution","Propose and evaluate solutions"],"correct":0,"why":"Key: A. Checking that the algorithm gives correct results is testing and validation. A, D and C are earlier steps.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 52","q":"<p>Which data type is MOST suitable for a variable that records whether a student has paid the Carnival trip fee?</p>","options":["Real","Boolean","Character","Integer"],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. Paid or not paid has only two values: TRUE/FALSE. D and B are numbers. C holds one symbol.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 53","q":"<p>A cashier at a Carnival food booth enters the price of each item sold and types -1 when the day is over. Which control structure should the algorithm use?</p>","options":["A sequence of assignment statements","A constant declaration","A single IF-THEN statement","A loop controlled by a sentinel value"],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. -1 is a sentinel (rogue) value that ends a loop such as WHILE or REPEAT-UNTIL. A runs once in order. C chooses once. B declares data.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 54","q":"<p>What is printed when this pseudocode runs with score = 75?<br><code style=\"white-space:pre\">IF score &gt;= 80 THEN<br>    PRINT \"Distinction\"<br>ELSE IF score &gt;= 60 THEN<br>    PRINT \"Credit\"<br>ELSE<br>    PRINT \"Pass\"<br>ENDIF</code><br></p>","options":["Pass","Credit Pass","Distinction","Credit"],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. 75 is not >= 80, but it is >= 60, so Credit is printed and the rest is skipped. D needs 80 or more. A is only for scores below 60. C wrongly runs two branches.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 55","q":"<p>A trace of an algorithm shows the variable total ends at 30, but the correct total is 40. The program ran without crashing. What type of error does the algorithm contain?</p>","options":["Logic error","Hardware error","Syntax error","Run-time error"],"correct":0,"why":"Key: A. The algorithm runs but gives a wrong result, a logic error. A stops translation. D crashes the program while it runs. C is unrelated.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 56","q":"<p>Testing a program on the computer with prepared test data (normal, boundary and erroneous) is DIFFERENT from a dry run because</p>","options":["test data is never used in computer testing","a dry run happens after the program is sold","a dry run uses a compiler","the program is actually executed by the computer rather than traced by hand"],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. A dry run is a manual trace; computer testing runs the code. B, A and C are false.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 57","q":"<p>A Trinidad doubles vendor's Pascal program stores the price of a meal, such as 12.50. Which declaration creates this variable?</p>","options":["var price : integer;","var price : boolean;","var price : real;","var price : char;"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. A price with decimals needs the real data type. C holds whole numbers only. B holds true/false. A holds one character.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 58","q":"<p>What is the output of this Python code when temp = 31?<br><code style=\"white-space:pre\">if temp &gt; 32:<br>    print(\"Hot\")<br>elif temp &gt; 28:<br>    print(\"Warm\")<br>else:<br>    print(\"Cool\")</code><br></p>","options":["Hot","Warm","Hot Warm","Cool"],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. 31 is not greater than 32, but it is greater than 28, so Warm prints. A needs more than 32. D needs 28 or below. B wrongly runs two branches.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 59","q":"<p>Which Python code performs a LINEAR SEARCH for the name \"Zion\" in the list names?</p>","options":["names = \"Zion\"","found = False<br>for n in names:<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;if n == \"Zion\":<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;found = True","names.sort()","print(names[0])"],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. A linear search checks each element in turn and sets a flag if found. A sorts. C prints the first element. B overwrites the list.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 60","q":"<p>Kyle's bus-fare program uses the variable name x for the total fare. His teacher asks him to rename it total_fare. Why?</p>","options":["Short names make the program run faster.","Meaningful identifiers make the code easier to read and maintain.","It makes the program use less memory.","Python does not allow single-letter names."],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. Meaningful names are part of good internal documentation. D and C are false: names do not affect speed or memory noticeably. B is false: single-letter names are allowed.","fb":[]}]}]},{"pkg":"Paper 02 Mock Papers","quizzes":[{"id":"pp_csecp2m1","title":"Paper 02 · Mock 1","desc":"<p><b>CSEC Information Technology · Paper 02 · Mock 1</b> · 2 hours · 90 marks</p><ol><li>This paper consists of <b>FOUR</b> compulsory structured questions. Answer <b>ALL</b> questions.</li><li>Marks: Question 1 (20), Question 2 (25), Question 3 (20), Question 4 (25). Plan about 1⅓ minutes per mark: 25 minutes for a 20-mark question and 33 minutes for a 25-mark question, with 10 minutes to check.</li><li>Each question is built around one Caribbean scenario and mixes theory (Sections 1–3), productivity tools (Sections 4–6) and problem-solving and programming (Sections 7–8).</li><li>Label every part (a), (b), (i), (ii). Use the command word: <i>state</i>, <i>list</i> and <i>identify</i> need short answers; <i>describe</i>, <i>explain</i>, <i>distinguish</i>, <i>justify</i> and <i>evaluate</i> need developed points linked to the scenario.</li><li>Type formulas exactly as you would enter them (e.g. <code>=SUM(B2:B9)</code>), query criteria as they would appear in the design grid, and code with indentation. Programs may be written in Python, Pascal or Visual Basic (VBA), the languages CXC accepts.</li><li>Stuck? Use <b>Dial it down</b> for a scaffolded version of a question, or <b>Grade One challenge</b> to stretch yourself after you finish.</li></ol><p class=\"tip\">This is an original mock paper written in the format of the CXC CSEC Information Technology Paper 02 (syllabus CXC 30/G/SYLL 17).</p>","minutes":120,"items":[{"t":"essay","title":"Question 1 · Island Pulse Radio goes digital","q":"<p>Answer ALL parts.</p><ol type=\"a\"><li>A listener types a song request on the station's web form; the request appears on Jahmal's screen in a queue, the queue is saved, and the song plays on air. Using this example, <b>describe</b> each stage of the input–process–output–storage (IPOS) cycle. <i>(4 marks)</i></li><li>Two computers are offered for the studio:<br><b>Computer P:</b> desktop, Intel Core i7 3.4 GHz (8 cores), 16 GB RAM, 1 TB SSD, 6 USB ports, $1,350.<br><b>Computer Q:</b> tablet, 2.0 GHz quad-core, 4 GB RAM, 64 GB flash storage, 1 USB-C port, $600.<br><b>(i)</b> State which computer you would recommend. <i>(1 mark)</i><br><b>(ii)</b> Justify your choice by referring to THREE of the specifications. <i>(3 marks)</i></li><li>The music library is 45 GB. <b>(i)</b> Convert 45 GB to megabytes, showing your working. <i>(1 mark)</i> <b>(ii)</b> <b>Discuss</b> ONE advantage and ONE disadvantage of storing the library in the cloud rather than on a local drive at the station. <i>(3 marks)</i></li><li>The request form has the fields <b>ListenerName</b>, <b>Phone</b> (7 digits), <b>Age</b> and <b>SongTitle</b>. <b>(i)</b> Name and describe a suitable validation check for Phone and a different one for Age. <i>(2 marks)</i> <b>(ii)</b> <b>Distinguish</b> between validation and verification. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li>A post on social media says Island Pulse Radio is \"closing down for good\". It has no author, no date and comes from an account created yesterday. <b>Explain</b> how a listener could use TWO criteria to decide whether the post is reliable. <i>(4 marks)</i></li></ol><p style=\"text-align:right\"><b>[20 marks]</b></p>","points":20,"fb":"<h4>Marking guide</h4><p><b>Mark scheme</b></p><ul><li>(a) 1 mark for each IPOS stage correctly applied to the scenario (4). A stage named without the example: max 2 overall.</li><li>(b)(i) Computer P (1). (ii) 1 mark each for THREE specifications linked to a studio need: processor/cores, RAM, SSD capacity/speed, ports, price within budget (3). Choosing Q earns 0 for (i) but a justification that correctly compares specs may earn 1.</li><li>(c)(i) 46,080 MB with working (1); accept 45,000 MB only if 1 GB = 1,000 MB is stated. (ii) Advantage (1), disadvantage (1), development linked to the station (1).</li><li>(d)(i) Suitable check for Phone (length/type/format) (1); different suitable check for Age (range/type) (1). (ii) Validation definition (1); verification definition (1).</li><li>(e) 1 mark for each criterion named, 1 mark for each applied to the post (4).</li></ul><h4>Model answer</h4><p><b>(a)</b> <b>Input:</b> the listener types the song title and name into the web form and presses Send. <b>Processing:</b> the system checks the request and places it in order in the request queue. <b>Output:</b> the queue is displayed on Jahmal's screen and the song is played on air through the speakers/transmitter. <b>Storage:</b> the request queue (and a log of songs played) is saved on the computer's drive for later use.</p><p><b>(b)(i)</b> Computer P. <b>(ii)</b> Its Core i7 3.4 GHz 8-core processor can run audio-mixing and streaming software at the same time without lag. Its 16 GB of RAM lets large audio files and several programs stay in memory, so playback does not stutter on air. Its 1 TB SSD can hold the 45 GB library more than 20 times over and is fast and shock-resistant, and its six USB ports connect the mixing desk, microphones and backup drive. At $1,350 it is within the $1,500 budget. Computer Q's 64 GB storage could not hold the library and backups, and its single port is too few.</p><p><b>(c)(i)</b> 45 × 1,024 = <b>46,080 MB</b>. <b>(ii)</b> Advantage: a cloud copy is stored off-site, so if the studio drive fails again (or the building is damaged in a hurricane) the library can be restored and even played from another location. Disadvantage: the station depends on its Internet connection; if WaitukubuliTel's service is down, files cannot be reached, and large uploads/downloads are slow and there is a monthly subscription cost.</p><p><b>(d)(i)</b> Phone: a <b>length check</b> rejects any entry that is not exactly 7 characters (a type check could also allow digits only). Age: a <b>range check</b> accepts only ages between, for example, 10 and 100. <b>(ii)</b> <b>Validation</b> is an automatic check by the computer that data is sensible and follows set rules, but it cannot tell if the data is true. <b>Verification</b> checks that data has been entered or copied accurately from its source, for example by double entry or by proofreading against the original.</p><p><b>(e)</b> <b>Authority:</b> the post has no named author and comes from an account created yesterday, so the listener cannot tell who wrote it or whether they have any link to the station. They should check the station's official website or verified page. <b>Currency:</b> the post has no date, so even if a closure was once discussed, the information may be out of date. <b>Accuracy</b> could also be checked by comparing it with The Roseau Courier or the station's own announcements. The post fails these tests, so it is not reliable.</p>"},{"t":"essay","title":"Question 2 · Caribbean Youth Games village network and website","q":"<p>The Bahamas is hosting the <b>Caribbean Youth Games 2026</b>. The organising committee has an office in Nassau with 20 computers, a media centre at the Thomas A. Robinson National Stadium 3 km away, and a results desk at each of four venues across the island. Athletes from 14 countries will register on the Games website before they travel.</p><p>The committee's IT officer, Ms. Sheranne Rolle, must link the sites, launch the website and keep athlete data safe.</p><p>Answer ALL parts.</p><ol type=\"a\"><li><b>(i)</b> Identify the type of network that would link the 20 computers in the Nassau office, and the type that would link the office, the media centre and the four venues. <i>(2 marks)</i> <b>(ii)</b> State the function of a <b>switch</b> and of a <b>router</b> in the committee's network. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li>The link between the office and the media centre must carry live HD video of events. <b>Recommend</b> a transmission medium and justify your choice by comparing it with ONE other medium. <i>(4 marks)</i></li><li>The home page of the Games website must have: the Games logo at the top, a navigation bar linking to <i>Schedule</i>, <i>Results</i> and <i>Register</i> pages, a link to e-mail <code>media@cyg2026.bs</code>, and a link that jumps to the <i>Venues</i> section lower on the same page. <b>(i)</b> Write the HTML for the logo (with alternative text), ONE navigation link, the e-mail link and the same-page link with its target. <i>(5 marks)</i> <b>(ii)</b> State TWO design features (other than those listed) that would make the site easy to use on a phone. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li>Athletes enter their name, date of birth, passport number and medical details on the Register page. <b>(i)</b> Explain how a <b>phishing</b> attack could target the athletes, and the effect on them. <i>(3 marks)</i> <b>(ii)</b> Describe THREE countermeasures the committee should use to protect the registration data, including ONE physical and ONE software measure. <i>(6 marks)</i></li><li>Explain why the committee should use <b>HTTPS</b> rather than HTTP for the Register page. <i>(1 mark)</i></li></ol><p style=\"text-align:right\"><b>[25 marks]</b></p>","points":25,"fb":"<h4>Marking guide</h4><p><b>Mark scheme</b></p><ul><li>(a)(i) LAN (1); MAN or WAN (1). (ii) Switch function (1); router function (1).</li><li>(b) Fibre-optic recommended (1); two justified properties (2); comparison with another medium (1). Wireless/microwave accepted if well justified (max 3).</li><li>(c)(i) img with src (1) and alt (1); navigation hyperlink to a page (1); mailto link (1); #bookmark link with matching id/name target (1). Minor syntax slips: penalise once. (ii) Any two mobile-friendly features (2).</li><li>(d)(i) How phishing works (1), the deception/fake link (1), effect on athletes (1). (ii) Three countermeasures, 1 for naming and 1 for describing how it protects the data (6); must include one physical and one software measure, else max 4.</li><li>(e) Encryption (1).</li></ul><h4>Model answer</h4><p><b>(a)(i)</b> The 20 office computers form a <b>LAN</b> (local area network). The network linking the office, media centre and four venues across the island is a <b>MAN</b> (metropolitan area network); WAN is also acceptable. <b>(ii)</b> The <b>switch</b> connects the computers within the LAN and forwards each message only to the computer it is meant for. The <b>router</b> connects the committee's LAN to other networks, such as the ISP and the Internet, and chooses the best path for data packets.</p><p><b>(b)</b> I recommend <b>fibre-optic cable</b>. It carries data as light, giving very high bandwidth for live HD video, and it can run the 3 km to the media centre without the signal weakening. It is also immune to electrical interference and hard to tap. <b>Twisted-pair</b> cable is cheaper but loses signal after about 100 m, has much lower bandwidth and suffers interference, so the video would stutter.</p><p><b>(c)(i)</b></p><pre>&lt;img src=\"cyg-logo.png\" alt=\"Caribbean Youth Games 2026 logo\"&gt;\n&lt;nav&gt;\n  &lt;a href=\"schedule.html\"&gt;Schedule&lt;/a&gt;\n  &lt;a href=\"results.html\"&gt;Results&lt;/a&gt;\n  &lt;a href=\"register.html\"&gt;Register&lt;/a&gt;\n&lt;/nav&gt;\n&lt;a href=\"mailto:media@cyg2026.bs\"&gt;E-mail the media team&lt;/a&gt;\n&lt;a href=\"#venues\"&gt;Jump to Venues&lt;/a&gt;\n...\n&lt;h2 id=\"venues\"&gt;Venues&lt;/h2&gt;</pre><p><b>(ii)</b> A responsive layout that changes to a single column on narrow screens; large, well-spaced buttons that are easy to tap (also: compressed images for fast loading on mobile data, readable font sizes, a collapsible menu).</p><p><b>(d)(i)</b> An attacker sends athletes an e-mail or WhatsApp message that looks as if it comes from the Games committee, saying their registration is incomplete, with a link to a fake copy of the Register page. Athletes who type in their passport number and medical details hand them to the criminal, who can use them for identity theft, travel fraud or blackmail, and the athletes may lose money or their place at the Games. <b>(ii)</b> <b>Physical:</b> keep the server in a locked room with keycard or fingerprint entry and CCTV, so no one can steal or tamper with it. <b>Software:</b> encrypt the athlete database so stolen files cannot be read, and protect the network with a firewall that blocks unauthorised traffic. <b>Access privileges:</b> give each staff member a username and strong password (with 2FA) and allow only the medical team to view medical fields, so fewer people can leak or change them.</p><p><b>(e)</b> HTTPS encrypts all data sent between the athlete's browser and the web server, so passport and medical details cannot be read if intercepted (and the padlock shows the site's certificate).</p>"},{"t":"essay","title":"Question 3 · Bayside Hardware sales sheet and stock database","q":"<p><b>Bayside Hardware</b> in Charlestown sells building supplies. The manager, Mr. Romney, keeps weekly sales in a spreadsheet:</p><div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>A</th><th>B</th><th>C</th><th>D</th><th>E</th><th>F</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th>1</th><td colspan=\"4\"><b>Bayside Hardware · weekly sales</b></td><td>Discount rate</td><td>5%</td></tr><tr><th>2</th><td><b>Code</b></td><td><b>Item</b></td><td><b>Unit price ($)</b></td><td><b>Qty sold</b></td><td><b>Sales ($)</b></td><td><b>Restock?</b></td></tr><tr><th>3</th><td>P101</td><td>Plywood sheet</td><td>38.50</td><td>42</td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><th>4</th><td>C220</td><td>Cement bag</td><td>14.75</td><td>130</td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><th>5</th><td>N015</td><td>Box of nails</td><td>6.20</td><td>18</td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><th>6</th><td>H310</td><td>Hurricane strap</td><td>3.95</td><td>260</td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><th>7</th><td>T088</td><td>Tarpaulin</td><td>22.00</td><td>9</td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><th>8</th><td></td><td><b>Total</b></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>He also wants a database with two tables: <b>Suppliers</b> (SupplierID, SupplierName, Island, Phone) and <b>Products</b> (Code, Item, UnitPrice, InStock, SupplierID).</p><p>Answer ALL parts.</p><ol type=\"a\"><li>Write the formula for cell <b>E3</b> to calculate Sales as Unit price × Qty sold, and the formula for <b>E8</b> to total the sales. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li>Mr. Romney wants a column G showing the discounted sales, using the discount rate in F1. <b>(i)</b> Write the formula for <b>G3</b> so that it can be copied down to G7. <i>(2 marks)</i> <b>(ii)</b> Explain why your formula needs an absolute reference. <i>(1 mark)</i></li><li>Write the formula for <b>F3</b> that displays \"Yes\" if fewer than 20 were sold and \"No\" otherwise. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li>State the function that would find the item with the highest quantity sold value, writing the formula for D9. <i>(1 mark)</i></li><li>Mr. Romney wants a chart comparing the Sales of the five items. Name the most suitable chart type and state TWO labels the chart should have. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li><b>(i)</b> Identify the primary key of each database table and the foreign key. <i>(3 marks)</i> <b>(ii)</b> State the type of relationship between Suppliers and Products. <i>(1 mark)</i> <b>(iii)</b> Suggest a suitable data type for UnitPrice and for InStock. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li>Describe a query to list the Item, UnitPrice and SupplierName of every product supplied from <b>Trinidad</b> with a UnitPrice above $10, sorted by Item. State the tables, fields and criteria. <i>(4 marks)</i></li></ol><p style=\"text-align:right\"><b>[20 marks]</b></p>","points":20,"fb":"<h4>Marking guide</h4><p><b>Mark scheme</b></p><ul><li>(a) =C3*D3 (1); =SUM(E3:E7) (1). Missing = sign: penalise once in the question.</li><li>(b)(i) Correct calculation (1) with $F$1 (1). (ii) Reason (1).</li><li>(c) Condition D3&lt;20 (1); correct outputs in quotes in the right order (1).</li><li>(d) =MAX(D3:D7) (1).</li><li>(e) Column/bar (1); two of: chart title, axis titles, data labels (1).</li><li>(f)(i) SupplierID (1), Code (1), foreign key SupplierID in Products (1). (ii) One-to-many (1). (iii) Currency (1); Number (1).</li><li>(g) Both tables/join (1); correct fields (1); both criteria (1); sort by Item (1).</li></ul><h4>Model answer</h4><p><b>(a)</b> E3: <code>=C3*D3</code> &nbsp; E8: <code>=SUM(E3:E7)</code></p><p><b>(b)(i)</b> G3: <code>=E3*(1-$F$1)</code> (or <code>=E3-E3*$F$1</code>). <b>(ii)</b> The discount rate is in one cell only. $F$1 is an absolute reference, so when the formula is copied down to G7 it still points to F1. A relative F1 would change to F2, F3 … and give wrong results.</p><p><b>(c)</b> F3: <code>=IF(D3&lt;20,\"Yes\",\"No\")</code> (copied down: Box of nails and Tarpaulin show Yes).</p><p><b>(d)</b> D9: <code>=MAX(D3:D7)</code>, which returns 260 (Hurricane strap).</p><p><b>(e)</b> A <b>column (or bar) chart</b>, because it compares separate items. It should have a chart title such as \"Bayside Hardware weekly sales by item\" and axis titles: \"Item\" (x-axis) and \"Sales ($)\" (y-axis). Data labels are also useful.</p><p><b>(f)(i)</b> Suppliers: primary key <b>SupplierID</b>. Products: primary key <b>Code</b>. <b>SupplierID</b> in Products is the foreign key. <b>(ii)</b> One-to-many: one supplier supplies many products. <b>(iii)</b> UnitPrice: Currency. InStock: Number (Integer), the quantity in stock.</p><p><b>(g)</b> Create a select query in Design View using BOTH tables, joined on SupplierID.</p><div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><tr><th>Field</th><td>Item</td><td>UnitPrice</td><td>SupplierName</td><td>Island</td></tr><tr><th>Table</th><td>Products</td><td>Products</td><td>Suppliers</td><td>Suppliers</td></tr><tr><th>Sort</th><td>Ascending</td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><th>Show</th><td>✔</td><td>✔</td><td>✔</td><td>☐</td></tr><tr><th>Criteria</th><td></td><td>&gt;10</td><td></td><td>\"Trinidad\"</td></tr></table></div><p>Both criteria are on the same row, so they are joined with AND. SQL: <code>SELECT Item, UnitPrice, SupplierName FROM Products INNER JOIN Suppliers ON Products.SupplierID = Suppliers.SupplierID WHERE Island = \"Trinidad\" AND UnitPrice &gt; 10 ORDER BY Item;</code></p>"},{"t":"essay","title":"Question 4 · Water-taxi fares across Chaguaramas Bay","q":"<p><b>Lagoon Hop</b> runs water taxis across Chaguaramas Bay. The fare is <b>$6.00</b> for an adult and <b>$3.50</b> for a child (under 12). At the end of each trip the captain enters the age of each passenger, one at a time, and types <b>-1</b> when there are no more passengers. The program must output the number of passengers, the total fare collected and, if the total is more than <b>$60</b>, the message <b>\"Full boat bonus\"</b>.</p><p>Answer ALL parts.</p><ol type=\"a\"><li>Complete an IPO (defining) chart for the problem. <i>(3 marks)</i></li><li>Identify ONE constant and ONE variable in the problem and state a suitable data type for each. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li>Write pseudocode for the algorithm using a WHILE loop and the sentinel value -1. <i>(8 marks)</i></li><li>Complete a trace table for your algorithm with the input data <b>34, 8, 10, -1</b>. Show columns for age, count, total and the output. <i>(4 marks)</i></li><li>Write the algorithm in Python. <i>(5 marks)</i></li><li>Give ONE example of each: normal, boundary and erroneous test data for the age, with a reason. <i>(3 marks)</i></li></ol><p style=\"text-align:right\"><b>[25 marks]</b></p>","points":25,"fb":"<h4>Marking guide</h4><p><b>Mark scheme</b></p><ul><li>(a) Input (1), processing (1), output (1).</li><li>(b) Constant with type (1); variable with type (1).</li><li>(c) Initialise count and total (1); first READ before the loop (1); WHILE with sentinel condition (1); IF-THEN-ELSE with &lt;12 (1); correct fare added in each branch (1); counter incremented (1); READ inside the loop (1); output of count/total and bonus test after the loop (1).</li><li>(d) Initial row (1); each of three passes correct (2, -1 per error); final output correct (1). Marks follow the candidate's own algorithm (error carried forward).</li><li>(e) Input with int() (1); while loop with sentinel (1); if/else (1); counter and accumulator (1); output and bonus test (1).</li><li>(f) 1 mark each, value + reason.</li></ul><h4>Model answer</h4><p><b>(a)</b></p><div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><tr><th>Input</th><th>Processing</th><th>Output</th></tr><tr><td>Age of each passenger (until -1 is entered)</td><td>For each age: if under 12 add 3.50 to total, otherwise add 6.00; add 1 to count. After the last passenger, compare total with 60.</td><td>Number of passengers; total fare; \"Full boat bonus\" if total &gt; 60</td></tr></table></div><p><b>(b)</b> Constant: CHILD_FARE = 3.50 (real/float). Variable: count, an integer that increases by 1 for each passenger (also: age integer, total real).</p><p><b>(c)</b></p><pre>START\n   ADULT_FARE ← 6.00\n   CHILD_FARE ← 3.50\n   count ← 0\n   total ← 0\n   PRINT \"Enter age (-1 to finish)\"\n   READ age\n   WHILE age &lt;&gt; -1 DO\n      IF age &lt; 12 THEN\n         total ← total + CHILD_FARE\n      ELSE\n         total ← total + ADULT_FARE\n      ENDIF\n      count ← count + 1\n      READ age\n   ENDWHILE\n   PRINT \"Passengers: \", count\n   PRINT \"Total fare: $\", total\n   IF total &gt; 60 THEN\n      PRINT \"Full boat bonus\"\n   ENDIF\nSTOP</pre><p><b>(d)</b></p><div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><tr><th>age</th><th>age &lt; 12?</th><th>count</th><th>total</th><th>Output</th></tr><tr><td>–</td><td>–</td><td>0</td><td>0.00</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>34</td><td>No</td><td>1</td><td>6.00</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>Yes</td><td>2</td><td>9.50</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>Yes</td><td>3</td><td>13.00</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>-1</td><td>–</td><td>3</td><td>13.00</td><td>Passengers: 3<br>Total fare: $13.00<br>(no bonus: 13.00 is not &gt; 60)</td></tr></table></div><p><b>(e)</b></p><pre># Lagoon Hop water-taxi fares\nADULT_FARE = 6.00\nCHILD_FARE = 3.50\ncount = 0\ntotal = 0.0\nage = int(input(\"Enter age (-1 to finish): \"))\nwhile age != -1:\n    if age &lt; 12:\n        total = total + CHILD_FARE\n    else:\n        total = total + ADULT_FARE\n    count = count + 1\n    age = int(input(\"Enter age (-1 to finish): \"))\nprint(\"Passengers:\", count)\nprint(\"Total fare: $\", format(total, \".2f\"))\nif total &gt; 60:\n    print(\"Full boat bonus\")</pre><p><b>(f)</b> <b>Normal:</b> 30, a typical adult age that should add $6.00. <b>Boundary:</b> 11 and 12, either side of the point where the fare changes (11 → $3.50, 12 → $6.00). <b>Erroneous:</b> -5 or \"ten\", which are not valid ages and should be rejected (\"ten\" would crash int()).</p>"}]},{"id":"pp_csecp2m2","title":"Paper 02 · Mock 2","desc":"<p><b>CSEC Information Technology · Paper 02 · Mock 2</b> · 2 hours · 90 marks</p><ol><li>This paper consists of <b>FOUR</b> compulsory structured questions. Answer <b>ALL</b> questions.</li><li>Marks: Question 1 (20), Question 2 (25), Question 3 (20), Question 4 (25). Plan about 1⅓ minutes per mark: 25 minutes for a 20-mark question and 33 minutes for a 25-mark question, with 10 minutes to check.</li><li>Each question is built around one Caribbean scenario and mixes theory (Sections 1–3), productivity tools (Sections 4–6) and problem-solving and programming (Sections 7–8).</li><li>Label every part (a), (b), (i), (ii). Use the command word: <i>state</i>, <i>list</i> and <i>identify</i> need short answers; <i>describe</i>, <i>explain</i>, <i>distinguish</i>, <i>justify</i> and <i>evaluate</i> need developed points linked to the scenario.</li><li>Type formulas exactly as you would enter them (e.g. <code>=SUM(B2:B9)</code>), query criteria as they would appear in the design grid, and code with indentation. Programs may be written in Python, Pascal or Visual Basic (VBA), the languages CXC accepts.</li><li>Stuck? Use <b>Dial it down</b> for a scaffolded version of a question, or <b>Grade One challenge</b> to stretch yourself after you finish.</li></ol><p class=\"tip\">This is an original mock paper written in the format of the CXC CSEC Information Technology Paper 02 (syllabus CXC 30/G/SYLL 17).</p>","minutes":120,"items":[{"t":"essay","title":"Question 1 · Relief CAY: data after the storm","q":"<p>Answer ALL parts.</p><ol type=\"a\"><li><b>Distinguish</b> between data and information, using an example from Relief CAY. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li><b>(i)</b> Identify the source document in the scenario. <i>(1 mark)</i> <b>(ii)</b> Explain why the voucher is a <b>turnaround document</b>. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li>Relief CAY wants to stop typing the tick-box needs by hand. <b>Name</b> a suitable input device and <b>explain</b> how it would work with the forms. <i>(3 marks)</i></li><li>For each field below, name a suitable validation check and give ONE example of data it would reject: <b>(i)</b> Number of people (1 to 20); <b>(ii)</b> District (must be one of the island's districts); <b>(iii)</b> Household name. <i>(6 marks)</i></li><li>The household file is stored on a server. Most of the time a clerk must find ONE household quickly when a voucher is scanned; at the end of each week a full report is printed in household-number order. <b>Recommend</b> a file organisation method and justify it. <i>(3 marks)</i></li><li>One laptop that was in the flood now switches on, but the screen stays black and the fan is very loud. <b>Outline</b> THREE troubleshooting steps a volunteer should take, in a sensible order. <i>(3 marks)</i></li></ol><p style=\"text-align:right\"><b>[20 marks]</b></p>","points":20,"fb":"<h4>Marking guide</h4><p><b>Mark scheme</b></p><ul><li>(a) Data with example (1); information with example (1).</li><li>(b)(i) Registration form (1). (ii) Output by computer (1), filled in and re-input (1).</li><li>(c) OMR (1); how it works (1); benefit (1). OCR earns 1 only if justified for the name/address boxes.</li><li>(d) For each field: suitable check (1) and valid rejected example (1).</li><li>(e) Index-sequential (1); direct access reason (1); sequential reason (1). Direct (random) alone earns max 2.</li><li>(f) 1 mark per sensible step; max 2 if the order is illogical or unsafe (e.g. opening the case while it is plugged in).</li></ul><h4>Model answer</h4><p><b>(a)</b> <b>Data</b> is raw, unprocessed facts with no context, e.g. the number \"4\" written in the Number of people box. <b>Information</b> is data that has been processed so it has meaning and is useful, e.g. \"312 households in East End still need roofing\" in the weekly report.</p><p><b>(b)(i)</b> The paper registration form filled in door to door. <b>(ii)</b> The voucher is printed (output) by the computer with the household's barcode; the clerk then writes the items collected on it, and it is scanned back into the computer as input. A document that leaves the computer and returns as input is a turnaround document.</p><p><b>(c)</b> An <b>optical mark reader (OMR)</b>. The forms are printed with tick boxes in fixed positions; volunteers shade the boxes in dark ink, and the OMR shines light on each form to detect which positions are marked. It reads hundreds of forms quickly and avoids keying errors.</p><p><b>(d)(i)</b> Range check: only 1 to 20 accepted; rejects 0 or 25. <b>(ii)</b> Lookup (table) check against a list of valid districts (George Town, West Bay, East End, …); rejects \"Miami\". <b>(iii)</b> Presence check: the name must not be left blank; rejects an empty field. (A type check rejecting digits in a name is also acceptable.)</p><p><b>(e)</b> <b>Index-sequential</b>. Records are stored in household-number order, so the weekly report can read the file sequentially from start to end. An index lets the system jump straight to the one household when a voucher is scanned, giving fast direct access.</p><p><b>(f)</b> 1. Switch the laptop off, unplug it, remove the battery if possible and let it dry out completely before trying again, since flood water can short-circuit it. 2. Check the power and charger, then connect an external monitor through HDMI; if the picture appears there, the laptop's own screen or its cable is damaged. 3. Check that the vents and fan are not clogged with mud; if the fan stays loud or there is still no picture, back up the drive (remove it if needed) and send the laptop to a technician.</p>"},{"t":"essay","title":"Question 2 · Caribbean Health Connect: mobile care and safe communication","q":"<p><b>Caribbean Health Connect</b> is a telemedicine service linking clinics in Anguilla, St. Kitts and Montserrat with specialist doctors in Barbados. Patients book video consultations on a smartphone app, and nurses send readings from wearable devices. The service also e-mails monthly health newsletters and appointment letters to 2,400 patients.</p><p>Last month, staff received e-mails that looked as if they came from the IT department asking them to \"confirm your password\". Two staff replied, and patient records were later posted online.</p><p>Answer ALL parts.</p><ol type=\"a\"><li><b>Explain</b> TWO ways in which mobile communication technologies support the telemedicine service. <i>(4 marks)</i></li><li>State TWO problems patients in rural areas of the islands may face when using the app. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li><b>(i)</b> Name the type of attack described in the scenario. <i>(1 mark)</i> <b>(ii)</b> Discuss ONE impact of the data leak on the PATIENTS and ONE impact on the ORGANISATION. <i>(4 marks)</i> <b>(iii)</b> Recommend TWO measures to stop a repeat, other than antivirus software. <i>(4 marks)</i></li><li>Name the IT professional who would be responsible for <b>(i)</b> deciding which staff may view which patient records and maintaining the database, and <b>(ii)</b> monitoring the network for attacks. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li>The 2,400 appointment letters are produced by mail merge. <b>(i)</b> Name the TWO files needed and state what each contains. <i>(2 marks)</i> <b>(ii)</b> Explain how the clinic could send letters ONLY to patients in St. Kitts. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li>The 30-page staff handbook needs a table of contents that updates automatically. Describe the TWO steps needed. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li>Telemedicine has reduced the number of patients flown to Barbados. State ONE benefit and ONE drawback of telemedicine for the region. <i>(2 marks)</i></li></ol><p style=\"text-align:right\"><b>[25 marks]</b></p>","points":25,"fb":"<h4>Marking guide</h4><p><b>Mark scheme</b></p><ul><li>(a) 2 marks per way: technology named (1) + how it supports the service (1).</li><li>(b) 1 mark each.</li><li>(c)(i) Phishing (1). (ii) Patient impact (2: identify + develop); organisation impact (2). (iii) 2 marks per measure: named (1) + how it prevents a repeat (1). Antivirus earns 0.</li><li>(d) 1 mark each.</li><li>(e)(i) Each file with content (1 each). (ii) Filter/query (1) on the Island field = St. Kitts (1).</li><li>(f) Heading styles (1); insert/update TOC (1).</li><li>(g) 1 mark each.</li></ul><h4>Model answer</h4><p><b>(a)</b> First, 4G and 5G mobile data give enough bandwidth for live video consultations, so a patient in Anguilla can see a specialist in Barbados from home or the local clinic without travelling. Second, wearable devices such as smartwatches and glucose monitors connect by Bluetooth to the nurse's smartphone, and the readings are uploaded over mobile data, letting doctors monitor chronic patients continuously. (Also: SMS/app reminders, GPS for locating patients or ambulances.)</p><p><b>(b)</b> Weak or no mobile signal in hilly or remote areas, so video calls drop; the cost of a smartphone and a data plan, which some patients cannot afford.</p><p><b>(c)(i)</b> Phishing (spear phishing aimed at staff). <b>(ii)</b> <b>Patients:</b> private medical details such as HIV status or mental-health notes are exposed, which can cause embarrassment, discrimination at work and stress, and personal details can be used for identity fraud. <b>Organisation:</b> it loses the trust of patients and partner clinics, patients may leave, and it may face legal action or fines under data-protection law, plus the cost of investigating the breach. <b>(iii)</b> 1. Train all staff to recognise phishing e-mails (check the sender's address, never send passwords by e-mail, report suspicious messages). 2. Turn on multi-factor authentication for all staff accounts so that a stolen password on its own cannot be used to log in. (Also: e-mail filters that flag spoofed senders, access privileges, encryption of records.)</p><p><b>(d)(i)</b> Database administrator. <b>(ii)</b> Network administrator (or cybersecurity analyst).</p><p><b>(e)(i)</b> The <b>primary (main) document</b>: the appointment letter with merge fields such as «FirstName» and «AppointmentDate». The <b>data source</b>: the patient list containing names, addresses, island and appointment details. <b>(ii)</b> In Edit Recipient List, filter (or use a query) on the Island field so that Island equals \"St. Kitts\", then complete the merge; only matching records produce letters.</p><p><b>(f)</b> 1. Apply the built-in Heading 1 / Heading 2 styles to every chapter and section heading. 2. Insert the automatic table of contents (References ▸ Table of Contents) where it should appear, and use Update Table after editing.</p><p><b>(g)</b> Benefit: patients on small islands can see specialists quickly without the cost of flights and hotels. Drawback: the doctor cannot do a hands-on physical examination, and the service fails when Internet connections are poor (e.g. after a storm).</p>"},{"t":"essay","title":"Question 3 · Carnival Queen Show: judging sheet and ticket database","q":"<p>The <b>Belize Carnival Queen Show</b> uses a spreadsheet to total judges' scores. Part of the sheet <b>Scores</b> is shown:</p><div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>A</th><th>B</th><th>C</th><th>D</th><th>E</th><th>F</th><th>G</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th>1</th><td><b>Contestant</b></td><td><b>Costume</b></td><td><b>Talent</b></td><td><b>Interview</b></td><td><b>Total</b></td><td><b>Average</b></td><td><b>Rank</b></td></tr><tr><th>2</th><td>Miss San Ignacio</td><td>27</td><td>25</td><td>18</td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><th>3</th><td>Miss Dangriga</td><td>29</td><td>22</td><td>19</td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><th>4</th><td>Miss Belize City</td><td>24</td><td>28</td><td>20</td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><th>5</th><td>Miss Orange Walk</td><td>26</td><td>27</td><td>16</td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><th>6</th><td>Miss Placencia</td><td>23</td><td>24</td><td>21</td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Tickets are sold from a database with the tables <b>Patrons</b> (PatronID, FirstName, LastName, Phone) and <b>Tickets</b> (TicketNo, PatronID, ShowDate, Section, Price).</p><p>Answer ALL parts.</p><ol type=\"a\"><li>Write formulas for: <b>(i)</b> E2, the total of the three scores; <b>(ii)</b> F2, the average of the three scores, rounded to 1 decimal place; <b>(iii)</b> G2, the rank of the contestant's total among all five (highest = 1), that can be copied down to G6. <i>(5 marks)</i></li><li>Explain why the range in your RANK formula must be absolute. <i>(1 mark)</i></li><li>The organisers want the five rows sorted so the winner is at the top. State the sort key and the order. <i>(1 mark)</i></li><li>A summary sheet named <b>Results</b> must show the winning total from the Scores sheet. Write a formula for cell B2 on Results that finds the highest total on the Scores sheet. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li>State TWO formatting features that would make the sheet easier to read for the judges. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li><b>(i)</b> State the primary key of each table and identify the foreign key. <i>(2 marks)</i> <b>(ii)</b> Suggest a validation rule for Section, which may only be VIP, Grandstand or Bleachers. <i>(1 mark)</i></li><li>Write the criteria (as they would appear in Query Design) for a query that lists the names of patrons who bought <b>VIP</b> tickets for the show on <b>25/04/2026</b>. <i>(3 marks)</i></li><li>State the purpose of a <b>main form with a subform</b> in this database and say which table belongs in each part. <i>(3 marks)</i></li></ol><p style=\"text-align:right\"><b>[20 marks]</b></p>","points":20,"fb":"<h4>Marking guide</h4><p><b>Mark scheme</b></p><ul><li>(a)(i) SUM formula (1). (ii) AVERAGE (1) with ROUND to 1 dp (1). (iii) RANK of E2 (1) with absolute range (1).</li><li>(b) Reason (1).</li><li>(c) Total descending (1).</li><li>(d) Sheet reference Scores! (1); MAX of E2:E6 (1).</li><li>(e) Any two suitable features (1 each).</li><li>(f)(i) Both PKs (1), FK (1). (ii) Rule (1).</li><li>(g) \"VIP\" in Section (1); date in ShowDate with # or correct format (1); on the same row / both tables used (1).</li><li>(h) Purpose (1); Patrons main (1); Tickets sub (1).</li></ul><h4>Model answer</h4><p><b>(a)(i)</b> E2: <code>=SUM(B2:D2)</code> <b>(ii)</b> F2: <code>=ROUND(AVERAGE(B2:D2),1)</code> <b>(iii)</b> G2: <code>=RANK(E2,$E$2:$E$6)</code> (or <code>=RANK.EQ(E2,$E$2:$E$6,0)</code>). Totals: 70, 70, 72, 69, 68, so Miss Belize City ranks 1, Miss San Ignacio and Miss Dangriga tie on 2.</p><p><b>(b)</b> When G2 is copied down, a relative range would shift to E3:E7, E4:E8 … and would leave out contestants at the top; $E$2:$E$6 keeps the comparison range fixed.</p><p><b>(c)</b> Primary sort key: Total, in descending order (largest first).</p><p><b>(d)</b> <code>=MAX(Scores!E2:E6)</code> (returns 72).</p><p><b>(e)</b> Bold, shaded column headings; conditional formatting that highlights the highest total in gold (also: borders, freeze the top row, one decimal place for averages).</p><p><b>(f)(i)</b> Patrons: PatronID. Tickets: TicketNo. PatronID in Tickets is the foreign key linking to Patrons. <b>(ii)</b> Validation rule: <code>\"VIP\" Or \"Grandstand\" Or \"Bleachers\"</code> with validation text \"Choose VIP, Grandstand or Bleachers\".</p><p><b>(g)</b> Use both tables, joined on PatronID:</p><div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><tr><th>Field</th><td>FirstName</td><td>LastName</td><td>Section</td><td>ShowDate</td></tr><tr><th>Table</th><td>Patrons</td><td>Patrons</td><td>Tickets</td><td>Tickets</td></tr><tr><th>Show</th><td>✔</td><td>✔</td><td>☐</td><td>☐</td></tr><tr><th>Criteria</th><td></td><td></td><td>\"VIP\"</td><td>#25/04/2026#</td></tr></table></div><p>Both criteria are on the same row (AND).</p><p><b>(h)</b> A main form with a subform shows one record and its related records together, so staff can look up a patron and see or add all the tickets that patron bought in one screen. Main form: Patrons. Subform: Tickets (linked by PatronID).</p>"},{"t":"essay","title":"Question 4 · Cricket fundraiser: runs for roofs","q":"<p>The <b>Leeward Youth Cricket Club</b> holds a \"Runs for Roofs\" fundraiser. A local business donates <b>$5 for every run</b> scored by each of the <b>8</b> batters, plus a <b>$50 bonus</b> for every batter who scores <b>30 or more</b>. The club needs a program that reads the runs scored by the 8 batters into an array, then outputs the total runs, the highest score, the number of batters who earned the bonus and the total donation.</p><p>Answer ALL parts.</p><ol type=\"a\"><li>Using top-down design, break the problem into THREE sub-tasks. <i>(3 marks)</i></li><li>Write pseudocode for the algorithm. Use an array <code>runs[1..8]</code> and a FOR loop. <i>(8 marks)</i></li><li>Draw (describe in words) the flowchart symbol you would use for: <b>(i)</b> reading runs; <b>(ii)</b> testing whether runs ≥ 30; <b>(iii)</b> adding runs to the total. <i>(3 marks)</i></li><li>Complete a trace table for the FIRST FOUR batters only, with the data <b>12, 45, 30, 7</b>. Show total, highest and bonusCount. <i>(4 marks)</i></li><li>A student types <code>for i in range(1, 8):</code> to read all 8 scores into a Python list. <b>(i)</b> Explain what is wrong and what type of error it is. <b>(ii)</b> Write the corrected line. <i>(3 marks)</i></li><li><b>(i)</b> Python is translated by an interpreter. State ONE difference between an interpreter and a compiler. <b>(ii)</b> Give TWO examples of internal documentation the student should include in the program. <i>(4 marks)</i></li></ol><p style=\"text-align:right\"><b>[25 marks]</b></p>","points":25,"fb":"<h4>Marking guide</h4><p><b>Mark scheme</b></p><ul><li>(a) 1 mark per logical sub-task.</li><li>(b) Initialisation (1); FOR 1 TO 8 (1); READ into runs[i] (1); accumulate total (1); IF for highest (1); IF ≥ 30 with counter (1); donation calculation after the loop (1); output (1).</li><li>(c) 1 mark per symbol.</li><li>(d) 1 mark per correct row (error carried forward from the candidate's algorithm).</li><li>(e)(i) Explanation (1); logic error (1). (ii) Corrected line (1).</li><li>(f)(i) Compiler (1), interpreter (1). (ii) Two examples (1 each).</li></ul><h4>Model answer</h4><p><b>(a)</b> 1. <b>Input:</b> read the runs of the 8 batters into the array. 2. <b>Process:</b> add up the total runs, find the highest score and count batters with 30 or more. 3. <b>Output:</b> calculate the donation and display total, highest, bonus count and donation.</p><p><b>(b)</b></p><pre>START\n   total ← 0\n   highest ← 0\n   bonusCount ← 0\n   FOR i ← 1 TO 8 DO\n      PRINT \"Enter runs for batter \", i\n      READ runs[i]\n      total ← total + runs[i]\n      IF runs[i] &gt; highest THEN\n         highest ← runs[i]\n      ENDIF\n      IF runs[i] &gt;= 30 THEN\n         bonusCount ← bonusCount + 1\n      ENDIF\n   ENDFOR\n   donation ← total * 5 + bonusCount * 50\n   PRINT \"Total runs: \", total\n   PRINT \"Highest score: \", highest\n   PRINT \"Bonus batters: \", bonusCount\n   PRINT \"Donation: $\", donation\nSTOP</pre><p><b>(c)(i)</b> Parallelogram (input/output). <b>(ii)</b> Diamond (decision) with Yes and No exits. <b>(iii)</b> Rectangle (process).</p><p><b>(d)</b></p><div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><tr><th>i</th><th>runs[i]</th><th>total</th><th>highest</th><th>runs[i] ≥ 30?</th><th>bonusCount</th></tr><tr><td>–</td><td>–</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>–</td><td>0</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>12</td><td>12</td><td>12</td><td>No</td><td>0</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>45</td><td>57</td><td>45</td><td>Yes</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>30</td><td>87</td><td>45</td><td>Yes</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>7</td><td>94</td><td>45</td><td>No</td><td>2</td></tr></table></div><p>(If these were the only batters, the donation would be 94 × 5 + 2 × 50 = $570.)</p><p><b>(e)(i)</b> <code>range(1, 8)</code> produces 1 to 7 only, because Python stops BEFORE the end value; the loop runs 7 times and the eighth batter's runs are never read. The program runs without crashing but gives wrong results, so it is a <b>logic error</b>. <b>(ii)</b> <code>for i in range(8):</code> (or <code>for i in range(1, 9):</code>).</p><p><b>(f)(i)</b> A compiler translates the whole program into machine code before it runs and produces an executable file; an interpreter translates and executes the program one statement at a time each time it runs, stopping at the first error. <b>(ii)</b> Comments explaining each section (e.g. <code># count batters who earn the $50 bonus</code>); meaningful identifiers such as <code>bonus_count</code> instead of <code>b</code> (also indentation and a header comment with the author, date and purpose).</p><p>Python program (for reference):</p><pre># Runs for Roofs: Leeward Youth Cricket Club\nruns = []\ntotal = 0\nhighest = 0\nbonus_count = 0\nfor i in range(8):\n    r = int(input(\"Runs for batter \" + str(i + 1) + \": \"))\n    runs.append(r)\n    total = total + r\n    if r &gt; highest:\n        highest = r\n    if r &gt;= 30:\n        bonus_count = bonus_count + 1\ndonation = total * 5 + bonus_count * 50\nprint(\"Total runs:\", total)\nprint(\"Highest score:\", highest)\nprint(\"Bonus batters:\", bonus_count)\nprint(\"Donation: $\", donation)</pre>"}]}]},{"pkg":"Paper 032 Practice Papers","quizzes":[{"id":"pp_csec032p1","title":"Paper 032 practice 1 · Youth Parliament Elections","desc":"<p><b>CSEC Information Technology · Paper 032 · Practice Paper 1</b> · 2 hours · 5 questions · 50 marks</p><ol><li>Paper 032 is the practical alternative to the School-Based Assessment for private candidates. Answer <b>ALL FIVE</b> questions.</li><li>Questions 1–4 test the productivity tools (word processing, web page design, spreadsheets, databases: <b>35 marks</b>). Question 5 tests problem-solving and programming (<b>15 marks</b>).</li><li>Read the case study and download the data files first. Work in real Word, Excel, Access and a code editor (Python, Pascal or VBA), or in the course simulators (the Excel and Access Labs import CSV through <b>File ▸ Open</b>).</li><li>In each answer box, record your evidence: the exact formulas, query designs (field, table, criteria, sort), form and report settings, HTML, and your pseudocode, trace table and program. In the real examination you print or save files as instructed by the invigilator.</li><li>Save your files often, using the file names given in each question. Start the 120-minute timer to practise under exam conditions.</li></ol><p class=\"tip\">This is an original practice paper written in the format of the CXC CSEC Information Technology Paper 032 (syllabus CXC 30/G/SYLL 17).</p>","minutes":120,"items":[{"t":"essay","title":"Question 1 · Word processing: candidate letters and a fillable form","q":"<p><b>Case study · The 2026 National Youth Parliament Elections</b></p><p>The <b>National Youth Electoral Commission (NYEC)</b> runs elections for St. Vincent and the Grenadines’ Youth Parliament. Students from Hummingbird Ridge High School, Flamboyant Heights College and Calabash Point Secondary stand as candidates for four youth parties in four constituencies: Kingstown, Georgetown, Layou and Barrouallie. Students aged 14–19 vote at eight polling stations.</p><p>The Chief Youth Electoral Officer, <b>Ms. Naomi Cato-Carty</b>, has asked you to help prepare the documents, website, turnout spreadsheet, results database and a ballot-counting program. Use the data below. Download each file and open it in your software or in the course labs (Excel Lab and Access Lab: <b>File ▸ Open ▸ Import a CSV file</b>).</p><h4>Data file 1 · Parties.csv</h4><div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>PartyCode</th><th>PartyName</th><th>Leader</th><th>Colour</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>FWD</td><td>Forward Together</td><td>Kayla Richardson</td><td>Orange</td></tr><tr><td>IVO</td><td>Island Voices</td><td>Jaden Ollivierre</td><td>Teal</td></tr><tr><td>NGA</td><td>Next Gen Alliance</td><td>Aaliyah Brooks</td><td>Purple</td></tr><tr><td>GRN</td><td>Green Hills Youth</td><td>Tariq Hodge</td><td>Green</td></tr></tbody></table></div><a class=\"btn ghost\" download=\"Parties.csv\" href=\"data:text/csv;charset=utf-8,PartyCode%2CPartyName%2CLeader%2CColour%0D%0AFWD%2CForward%20Together%2CKayla%20Richardson%2COrange%0D%0AIVO%2CIsland%20Voices%2CJaden%20Ollivierre%2CTeal%0D%0ANGA%2CNext%20Gen%20Alliance%2CAaliyah%20Brooks%2CPurple%0D%0AGRN%2CGreen%20Hills%20Youth%2CTariq%20Hodge%2CGreen%0D%0A\">⬇ Parties.csv</a><h4>Data file 2 · Candidates.csv</h4><p class=\"hint\">Votes = votes received; Budget and Spent = campaign budget and amount spent, in dollars.</p><div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>CandidateID</th><th>FirstName</th><th>LastName</th><th>PartyCode</th><th>School</th><th>Constituency</th><th>Votes</th><th>Budget</th><th>Spent</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>C01</td><td>Shanice</td><td>Gonsalves</td><td>FWD</td><td>Hummingbird Ridge High School</td><td>Kingstown</td><td>412</td><td>500</td><td>455</td></tr><tr><td>C02</td><td>Malik</td><td>Brooks</td><td>IVO</td><td>Flamboyant Heights College</td><td>Kingstown</td><td>388</td><td>500</td><td>310</td></tr><tr><td>C03</td><td>Zion</td><td>Lake</td><td>NGA</td><td>Calabash Point Secondary</td><td>Kingstown</td><td>205</td><td>450</td><td>450</td></tr><tr><td>C04</td><td>Imani</td><td>Cato</td><td>GRN</td><td>Hummingbird Ridge High School</td><td>Georgetown</td><td>297</td><td>400</td><td>120</td></tr><tr><td>C05</td><td>Dante</td><td>Richardson</td><td>FWD</td><td>Flamboyant Heights College</td><td>Georgetown</td><td>341</td><td>500</td><td>498</td></tr><tr><td>C06</td><td>Precious</td><td>Carty</td><td>IVO</td><td>Calabash Point Secondary</td><td>Georgetown</td><td>356</td><td>450</td><td>300</td></tr><tr><td>C07</td><td>Kemar</td><td>Hazel</td><td>NGA</td><td>Hummingbird Ridge High School</td><td>Layou</td><td>433</td><td>500</td><td>275</td></tr><tr><td>C08</td><td>Tia</td><td>Ollivierre</td><td>GRN</td><td>Flamboyant Heights College</td><td>Layou</td><td>189</td><td>300</td><td>95</td></tr><tr><td>C09</td><td>Jaylen</td><td>DaSilva</td><td>FWD</td><td>Calabash Point Secondary</td><td>Layou</td><td>276</td><td>450</td><td>410</td></tr><tr><td>C10</td><td>Rhea</td><td>Peterson</td><td>IVO</td><td>Hummingbird Ridge High School</td><td>Barrouallie</td><td>318</td><td>400</td><td>360</td></tr><tr><td>C11</td><td>Andre</td><td>Duzant</td><td>NGA</td><td>Flamboyant Heights College</td><td>Barrouallie</td><td>302</td><td>450</td><td>220</td></tr><tr><td>C12</td><td>Kiara</td><td>York</td><td>GRN</td><td>Calabash Point Secondary</td><td>Barrouallie</td><td>254</td><td>350</td><td>340</td></tr></tbody></table></div><a class=\"btn ghost\" download=\"Candidates.csv\" href=\"data:text/csv;charset=utf-8,CandidateID%2CFirstName%2CLastName%2CPartyCode%2CSchool%2CConstituency%2CVotes%2CBudget%2CSpent%0D%0AC01%2CShanice%2CGonsalves%2CFWD%2CHummingbird%20Ridge%20High%20School%2CKingstown%2C412%2C500%2C455%0D%0AC02%2CMalik%2CBrooks%2CIVO%2CFlamboyant%20Heights%20College%2CKingstown%2C388%2C500%2C310%0D%0AC03%2CZion%2CLake%2CNGA%2CCalabash%20Point%20Secondary%2CKingstown%2C205%2C450%2C450%0D%0AC04%2CImani%2CCato%2CGRN%2CHummingbird%20Ridge%20High%20School%2CGeorgetown%2C297%2C400%2C120%0D%0AC05%2CDante%2CRichardson%2CFWD%2CFlamboyant%20Heights%20College%2CGeorgetown%2C341%2C500%2C498%0D%0AC06%2CPrecious%2CCarty%2CIVO%2CCalabash%20Point%20Secondary%2CGeorgetown%2C356%2C450%2C300%0D%0AC07%2CKemar%2CHazel%2CNGA%2CHummingbird%20Ridge%20High%20School%2CLayou%2C433%2C500%2C275%0D%0AC08%2CTia%2COllivierre%2CGRN%2CFlamboyant%20Heights%20College%2CLayou%2C189%2C300%2C95%0D%0AC09%2CJaylen%2CDaSilva%2CFWD%2CCalabash%20Point%20Secondary%2CLayou%2C276%2C450%2C410%0D%0AC10%2CRhea%2CPeterson%2CIVO%2CHummingbird%20Ridge%20High%20School%2CBarrouallie%2C318%2C400%2C360%0D%0AC11%2CAndre%2CDuzant%2CNGA%2CFlamboyant%20Heights%20College%2CBarrouallie%2C302%2C450%2C220%0D%0AC12%2CKiara%2CYork%2CGRN%2CCalabash%20Point%20Secondary%2CBarrouallie%2C254%2C350%2C340%0D%0A\">⬇ Candidates.csv</a><h4>Data file 3 · PollingStations.csv</h4><div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Station</th><th>Constituency</th><th>Registered</th><th>BallotsCast</th><th>Rejected</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Kingstown Community Hall</td><td>Kingstown</td><td>620</td><td>488</td><td>12</td></tr><tr><td>Bay Street Youth Room</td><td>Kingstown</td><td>590</td><td>538</td><td>9</td></tr><tr><td>Georgetown Youth Centre</td><td>Georgetown</td><td>710</td><td>512</td><td>14</td></tr><tr><td>Georgetown Church Hall</td><td>Georgetown</td><td>560</td><td>503</td><td>7</td></tr><tr><td>Layou Sports Club</td><td>Layou</td><td>680</td><td>545</td><td>11</td></tr><tr><td>Layou Ballroom</td><td>Layou</td><td>390</td><td>369</td><td>5</td></tr><tr><td>Barrouallie Centre</td><td>Barrouallie</td><td>540</td><td>470</td><td>8</td></tr><tr><td>Barrouallie Library</td><td>Barrouallie</td><td>600</td><td>422</td><td>10</td></tr></tbody></table></div><a class=\"btn ghost\" download=\"PollingStations.csv\" href=\"data:text/csv;charset=utf-8,Station%2CConstituency%2CRegistered%2CBallotsCast%2CRejected%0D%0AKingstown%20Community%20Hall%2CKingstown%2C620%2C488%2C12%0D%0ABay%20Street%20Youth%20Room%2CKingstown%2C590%2C538%2C9%0D%0AGeorgetown%20Youth%20Centre%2CGeorgetown%2C710%2C512%2C14%0D%0AGeorgetown%20Church%20Hall%2CGeorgetown%2C560%2C503%2C7%0D%0ALayou%20Sports%20Club%2CLayou%2C680%2C545%2C11%0D%0ALayou%20Ballroom%2CLayou%2C390%2C369%2C5%0D%0ABarrouallie%20Centre%2CBarrouallie%2C540%2C470%2C8%0D%0ABarrouallie%20Library%2CBarrouallie%2C600%2C422%2C10%0D%0A\">⬇ PollingStations.csv</a><p>Ms. Cato-Carty wants to invite the candidates for <b>Layou</b> to the Results Night.</p><ol type=\"a\"><li>Create a primary (main) document for a letter on NYEC letterhead (organisation name, address <i>12 Bay Street, Kingstown</i>, e-mail <i>info@nyec.vc</i>). The letter must include today's date and the merge fields <b>FirstName</b>, <b>LastName</b>, <b>School</b> and <b>Constituency</b>. The body should invite the candidate to Results Night on <b>Thursday 28 May 2026 at 7:00 p.m.</b> at the St. Vincent and the Grenadines Youth Centre. Use <b>Candidates.csv</b> as the data source. Save as <code>ResultsLetter</code>. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li>Filter the recipients so that letters are produced ONLY for candidates in Layou. Merge to a new document and save it as <code>LayouLetters</code>. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li>Create a fillable electronic form <code>PollingAgentForm</code> headed <b>NYEC Polling Agent Application</b> with: a text field for Full name; a drop-down list for School (Hummingbird Ridge High School, Flamboyant Heights College, Calabash Point Secondary, Other); a drop-down list for Constituency (the four constituencies); a date picker for Date of birth; a check box labelled <i>I agree to follow the NYEC code of conduct</i>. Protect the form so that only the fields can be filled in. <i>(4 marks)</i></li></ol><p>In the answer box, list the steps you took and paste the text of your letter, showing the merge fields in «chevrons», then the names on the merged letters and the controls on your form.</p><p class=\"row\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"btn ghost\" data-gx=\"opengame\" data-page=\"games/wordsim.html?level=3\" data-id=\"wordsim\">📘 Open the Word Lab</button> <span class=\"hint\">Mail merge and form controls are in Level 3 of the Word Lab.</span></p>","points":8,"fb":"<h4>Marking guide</h4><p><b>Mark scheme</b></p><ul><li>(a) Letterhead, date and complete invitation (1); all four merge fields correctly placed from Candidates.csv (1).</li><li>(b) Filter on Constituency = Layou (1); merged document with exactly three letters, saved (1).</li><li>(c) Text field for name (1); BOTH drop-downs with the correct options (1); date picker AND check box (1); form protected for filling in forms (1).</li></ul><h4>Model answer</h4><p><b>(a) Primary document: ResultsLetter</b></p><div class=\"passage\"><p style=\"text-align:center\"><b>NATIONAL YOUTH ELECTORAL COMMISSION</b><br>12 Bay Street, Kingstown, St. Vincent and the Grenadines · info@nyec.vc</p><p>20 May 2026</p><p>«FirstName» «LastName»<br>«School»</p><p>Dear «FirstName»,</p><p><b>Invitation to Results Night</b></p><p>Congratulations on standing as a candidate for the <b>«Constituency»</b> constituency in the 2026 National Youth Parliament Elections. The Commission invites you to Results Night on <b>Thursday 28 May 2026 at 7:00 p.m.</b> at the St. Vincent and the Grenadines Youth Centre, where the results for every constituency will be announced.</p><p>Please arrive by 6:30 p.m. and bring your student ID. One parent or guardian may attend with you. Kindly confirm by e-mail to info@nyec.vc by 25 May.</p><p>Yours sincerely,<br><br>Naomi Cato-Carty<br>Chief Youth Electoral Officer</p></div><p>Steps: Mailings ▸ Start Mail Merge ▸ Letters; Select Recipients ▸ Use an Existing List ▸ <b>Candidates.csv</b>; Insert Merge Field for FirstName, LastName, School, Constituency; date inserted with Insert ▸ Date &amp; Time.</p><p><b>(b)</b> Edit Recipient List ▸ Filter ▸ Field: <b>Constituency</b>, Comparison: <b>Equal to</b>, Compare to: <b>Layou</b>. Preview shows 3 recipients. Finish &amp; Merge ▸ Edit Individual Documents ▸ All, saved as <code>LayouLetters</code>. Letters produced for: <b>Kemar Hazel (Hummingbird Ridge High School), Tia Ollivierre (Flamboyant Heights College), Jaylen DaSilva (Calabash Point Secondary)</b>.</p><p><b>(c) PollingAgentForm</b></p><div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Prompt</th><th>Control</th><th>Settings</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Full name</td><td>Plain-text content control</td><td>Placeholder: Click to type your full name</td></tr><tr><td>School</td><td>Drop-down list</td><td>Hummingbird Ridge High School; Flamboyant Heights College; Calabash Point Secondary; Other</td></tr><tr><td>Constituency</td><td>Drop-down list</td><td>Kingstown; Georgetown; Layou; Barrouallie</td></tr><tr><td>Date of birth</td><td>Date picker</td><td>Format dd/MM/yyyy</td></tr><tr><td>I agree to follow the NYEC code of conduct</td><td>Check box</td><td>Unticked by default</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Protection: Review ▸ Restrict Editing ▸ Editing restrictions: <b>Filling in forms</b> ▸ Yes, Start Enforcing Protection (optional password). The labels can no longer be changed; only the controls can be completed.</p>"},{"t":"essay","title":"Question 2 · Web page design: the Vote Youth SVG home page","q":"<p>Create the home page <code>index.html</code> of the NYEC website <b>Vote Youth SVG</b>. It must contain:</p><ol type=\"a\"><li>A logo image at the top (any image named <code>nyec-logo.png</code>) with suitable alternative text, and the heading <b>Vote Youth SVG 2026</b>. <i>(1 mark)</i></li><li>A navigation bar with links to two other pages, <code>candidates.html</code> and <code>howtovote.html</code>. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li>A hyperlink that opens an e-mail to <code>info@nyec.vc</code>. <i>(1 mark)</i></li><li>A hyperlink to the file <code>PollingAgentForm.docx</code> you created in Question 1. <i>(1 mark)</i></li><li>A section headed <b>Key dates</b> lower on the page, and a link at the top that jumps to it. <i>(1 mark)</i></li><li>A consistent design: page title, readable font, colour scheme and a footer. <i>(1 mark)</i></li></ol><p>Paste your HTML (or describe each element if you used a website builder) in the answer box.</p><p class=\"row\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"btn ghost\" data-gx=\"opengame\" data-page=\"games/websim.html?level=3\" data-id=\"websim\">🌐 Open the Web Page Builder</button> <span class=\"hint\">Build it in the Web Page Builder and switch to Code view to copy your HTML.</span></p>","points":7,"fb":"<h4>Marking guide</h4><p><b>Mark scheme</b></p><ul><li>(a) Logo with alt text and heading (1).</li><li>(b) Link to candidates.html (1); link to howtovote.html in a navigation area (1).</li><li>(c) Working mailto link (1).</li><li>(d) Link to PollingAgentForm.docx (1).</li><li>(e) Bookmark link with matching target (1).</li><li>(f) Title, consistent font/colour and footer (1).</li></ul><h4>Model answer</h4><pre>&lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt;\n&lt;html lang=\"en\"&gt;\n&lt;head&gt;\n  &lt;meta charset=\"utf-8\"&gt;\n  &lt;title&gt;Vote Youth SVG 2026 | Home&lt;/title&gt;\n  &lt;style&gt;\n    body { font-family: Arial, sans-serif; margin: 0; color: #1d2a44; }\n    header, footer { background: #0b5d6b; color: #fff; padding: 12px 20px; }\n    nav a { color: #fff; margin-right: 16px; font-weight: bold; }\n    main { padding: 16px 20px; }\n  &lt;/style&gt;\n&lt;/head&gt;\n&lt;body&gt;\n  &lt;header&gt;\n    &lt;img src=\"nyec-logo.png\" alt=\"National Youth Electoral Commission logo\" width=\"90\"&gt;\n    &lt;h1&gt;Vote Youth SVG 2026&lt;/h1&gt;\n    &lt;nav&gt;\n      &lt;a href=\"index.html\"&gt;Home&lt;/a&gt;\n      &lt;a href=\"candidates.html\"&gt;Candidates&lt;/a&gt;\n      &lt;a href=\"howtovote.html\"&gt;How to vote&lt;/a&gt;\n      &lt;a href=\"#keydates\"&gt;Key dates&lt;/a&gt;\n    &lt;/nav&gt;\n  &lt;/header&gt;\n  &lt;main&gt;\n    &lt;p&gt;Every student aged 14 to 19 can vote for the Youth Parliament. Find your polling station and meet the candidates.&lt;/p&gt;\n    &lt;p&gt;Want to help on polling day? &lt;a href=\"PollingAgentForm.docx\"&gt;Download the Polling Agent application form&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;\n    &lt;p&gt;Questions? &lt;a href=\"mailto:info@nyec.vc\"&gt;E-mail the Commission&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;\n    &lt;h2 id=\"keydates\"&gt;Key dates&lt;/h2&gt;\n    &lt;ul&gt;\n      &lt;li&gt;Polling day: Tuesday 26 May 2026, 8:00 a.m. to 3:00 p.m.&lt;/li&gt;\n      &lt;li&gt;Results Night: Thursday 28 May 2026, 7:00 p.m.&lt;/li&gt;\n    &lt;/ul&gt;\n  &lt;/main&gt;\n  &lt;footer&gt;© 2026 National Youth Electoral Commission · 12 Bay Street, Kingstown&lt;/footer&gt;\n&lt;/body&gt;\n&lt;/html&gt;</pre><p>Design decisions: the logo and navigation sit in the same header on every page (consistency); teal and white give strong contrast; the alt text describes the logo for screen readers.</p>"},{"t":"essay","title":"Question 3 · Spreadsheet: polling-station turnout","q":"<p>Open <b>PollingStations.csv</b> so the headings are in row 1 and the data in rows 2–9. Save the workbook as <code>Turnout</code>. In cell <b>J2</b> type <i>Target turnout</i> and in <b>K2</b> enter <b>75%</b>.</p><ol type=\"a\"><li>In F1 type <i>ValidVotes</i>, G1 <i>Turnout</i>, H1 <i>TargetMet</i>. In F2 enter a formula for the valid votes (ballots cast minus rejected). In G2 enter a formula for turnout (ballots cast ÷ registered), formatted as a percentage with 1 decimal place. Copy both down to row 9. <i>(3 marks)</i></li><li>In H2 enter a formula that displays <b>Yes</b> if the turnout is at least the target in K2 and <b>No</b> otherwise. It must use an absolute reference so it can be copied to H9. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li>In row 10 type <i>Total</i> and use functions to total columns C, D, E and F. In G11 find the highest turnout, and in H11 count how many stations did NOT meet the target. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li>Sort the station rows (2–9) by Turnout, highest first. <i>(1 mark)</i></li><li>Create a column chart of Turnout for each Station, with the title <b>Youth Parliament 2026 Turnout by Polling Station</b> and axis titles. Place it on a new sheet named <b>TurnoutChart</b>. <i>(1 mark)</i></li><li>Copy the data to a sheet named <b>BelowTarget</b> and use a filter to show only the stations where TargetMet is No. <i>(1 mark)</i></li></ol><p>In the answer box, type every formula exactly as entered, the results you got, and how you did the sort, chart and filter.</p><p class=\"row\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"btn ghost\" data-gx=\"opengame\" data-page=\"games/excelsim.html?level=3\" data-id=\"excelsim\">🟩 Open the Excel Lab</button> <span class=\"hint\">Import PollingStations.csv with File ▸ Open.</span></p>","points":10,"fb":"<h4>Marking guide</h4><p><b>Mark scheme</b></p><ul><li>(a) F2 formula (1); G2 formula (1); percentage with 1 dp, copied to row 9 (1).</li><li>(b) Correct IF logic with &gt;= and outputs (1); absolute $K$2 (1).</li><li>(c) SUMs in row 10 (1); MAX and COUNTIF correct (1).</li><li>(d) Sorted by Turnout descending, total row excluded (1).</li><li>(e) Column chart with exact title and axis titles on a sheet named TurnoutChart (1).</li><li>(f) Filter shows only No rows (1).</li></ul><h4>Model answer</h4><p><b>(a)</b> F2: <code>=D2-E2</code> &nbsp; G2: <code>=D2/C2</code> formatted Percentage, 1 decimal place; both filled down to row 9.</p><p><b>(b)</b> H2: <code>=IF(G2&gt;=$K$2,\"Yes\",\"No\")</code>, filled down to H9. $K$2 is absolute so every row compares with the one target cell.</p><p>Results (before sorting):</p><div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Station</th><th>Valid votes (F)</th><th>Turnout (G)</th><th>Target met? (H)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Kingstown Community Hall</td><td>476</td><td>78.7%</td><td>Yes</td></tr><tr><td>Bay Street Youth Room</td><td>529</td><td>91.2%</td><td>Yes</td></tr><tr><td>Georgetown Youth Centre</td><td>498</td><td>72.1%</td><td>No</td></tr><tr><td>Georgetown Church Hall</td><td>496</td><td>89.8%</td><td>Yes</td></tr><tr><td>Layou Sports Club</td><td>534</td><td>80.1%</td><td>Yes</td></tr><tr><td>Layou Ballroom</td><td>364</td><td>94.6%</td><td>Yes</td></tr><tr><td>Barrouallie Centre</td><td>462</td><td>87.0%</td><td>Yes</td></tr><tr><td>Barrouallie Library</td><td>412</td><td>70.3%</td><td>No</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p><b>(c)</b> C10: <code>=SUM(C2:C9)</code> = 4,690; D10: <code>=SUM(D2:D9)</code> = 3,847; E10: <code>=SUM(E2:E9)</code> = 76; F10: <code>=SUM(F2:F9)</code> = 3,771. G11: <code>=MAX(G2:G9)</code> = 94.6% (Layou Ballroom). H11: <code>=COUNTIF(H2:H9,\"No\")</code> = 2.</p><p><b>(d)</b> Select A1:H9 (not the total row) ▸ Data ▸ Sort ▸ Sort by <b>Turnout</b>, Order <b>Largest to Smallest</b>. New order:</p><div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Rank</th><th>Station</th><th>Turnout</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>Layou Ballroom</td><td>94.6%</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Bay Street Youth Room</td><td>91.2%</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Georgetown Church Hall</td><td>89.8%</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Barrouallie Centre</td><td>87.0%</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Layou Sports Club</td><td>80.1%</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>Kingstown Community Hall</td><td>78.7%</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>Georgetown Youth Centre</td><td>72.1%</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>Barrouallie Library</td><td>70.3%</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p><b>(e)</b> Select Station (A1:A9) and Turnout (G1:G9) with Ctrl ▸ Insert ▸ Clustered Column. Chart title: <b>Youth Parliament 2026 Turnout by Polling Station</b>; horizontal axis title <b>Polling station</b>; vertical axis title <b>Turnout (%)</b>; legend removed (one series). Chart Design ▸ Move Chart ▸ New sheet: <b>TurnoutChart</b>.</p><p><b>(f)</b> Copy the sheet, rename it <b>BelowTarget</b>, Data ▸ Filter, then in the TargetMet drop-down tick only <b>No</b>. Rows shown:</p><div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Station</th><th>Constituency</th><th>Turnout</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Georgetown Youth Centre</td><td>Georgetown</td><td>72.1%</td></tr><tr><td>Barrouallie Library</td><td>Barrouallie</td><td>70.3%</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},{"t":"essay","title":"Question 4 · Database: Youth Parliament results","q":"<p>Create a database <code>NYEC2026</code>. Import <b>Parties.csv</b> and <b>Candidates.csv</b> as tables named <b>Parties</b> and <b>Candidates</b>.</p><ol type=\"a\"><li>Set a suitable primary key for each table, make sure Votes, Budget and Spent are numeric fields, and create a one-to-many relationship between the tables, enforcing referential integrity. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li>Create a query <b>IslandVoicesOver350</b> that shows the FirstName, LastName, PartyName, Constituency and Votes of <b>Island Voices</b> candidates who received <b>more than 350</b> votes, sorted by LastName. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li>Create a query <b>LowBudgetRemaining</b> with a calculated field <b>Remaining</b> (Budget minus Spent) that lists candidates with less than $50 remaining. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li>Create a form <b>PartyCandidates</b> that shows each party's details in the main form and its candidates in a subform. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li>Create a report based on both tables showing FirstName, LastName, Constituency and Votes, <b>grouped by PartyName</b>, sorted by LastName, with the total votes for each party. The report title must be on two lines:<br><b>National Youth Electoral Commission</b><br><b>2026 Youth Parliament Results by Party</b> <i>(2 marks)</i></li></ol><p>In the answer box, describe each design: the keys and relationship, each query as a design grid (field, table, sort, criteria), the form and the report (grouping, sorting, totals, title). List the records your queries return.</p><p class=\"row\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"btn ghost\" data-gx=\"opengame\" data-page=\"games/accesssim.html?level=3\" data-id=\"accesssim\">🗃️ Open the Access Lab</button> <span class=\"hint\">Import both CSV files with File ▸ Open.</span></p>","points":10,"fb":"<h4>Marking guide</h4><p><b>Mark scheme</b></p><ul><li>(a) Both primary keys and numeric types (1); one-to-many relationship with referential integrity (1).</li><li>(b) Both tables with correct fields and sort (1); both criteria on one row giving Brooks and Carty (1).</li><li>(c) Correct calculated field named Remaining (1); criterion &lt;50 giving six records (1).</li><li>(d) Main form based on Parties (1); subform showing related Candidates (1).</li><li>(e) Grouped by PartyName, sorted by LastName, with Sum of Votes (1); exact two-line title (1).</li></ul><h4>Model answer</h4><p><b>(a)</b> <b>Parties</b>: primary key <b>PartyCode</b> (Short Text, size 3). <b>Candidates</b>: primary key <b>CandidateID</b> (Short Text); Votes Number (Integer); Budget and Spent Currency. Relationship: Parties.PartyCode (one) → Candidates.PartyCode (many), <b>Enforce Referential Integrity</b> ticked.</p><p><b>Query: IslandVoicesOver350</b></p><div class=\"tablewrap\"><table class=\"qbe\"><tr><th>Field</th><td>FirstName</td><td>LastName</td><td>PartyName</td><td>Constituency</td><td>Votes</td></tr><tr><th>Table</th><td>Candidates</td><td>Candidates</td><td>Parties</td><td>Candidates</td><td>Candidates</td></tr><tr><th>Sort</th><td></td><td>Ascending</td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><th>Show</th><td>✔</td><td>✔</td><td>✔</td><td>✔</td><td>✔</td></tr><tr><th>Criteria</th><td></td><td></td><td>\"Island Voices\"</td><td></td><td>&gt;350</td></tr></table></div><p>Records returned: Malik Brooks (Kingstown, 388 votes); Precious Carty (Georgetown, 356 votes). Both criteria are on the same row (AND).</p><p><b>Query: LowBudgetRemaining</b></p><div class=\"tablewrap\"><table class=\"qbe\"><tr><th>Field</th><td>FirstName</td><td>LastName</td><td>Budget</td><td>Spent</td><td>Remaining: [Budget]-[Spent]</td></tr><tr><th>Table</th><td>Candidates</td><td>Candidates</td><td>Candidates</td><td>Candidates</td><td></td></tr><tr><th>Sort</th><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td>Ascending</td></tr><tr><th>Show</th><td>✔</td><td>✔</td><td>✔</td><td>✔</td><td>✔</td></tr><tr><th>Criteria</th><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td>&lt;50</td></tr></table></div><p>Records returned (6): Zion Lake $0; Dante Richardson $2; Kiara York $10; Jaylen DaSilva $40; Rhea Peterson $40; Shanice Gonsalves $45.</p><p><b>(d)</b> Form Wizard ▸ Table: Parties (PartyCode, PartyName, Leader, Colour) then Table: Candidates (CandidateID, FirstName, LastName, Constituency, Votes) ▸ View data by <b>Parties</b> ▸ <b>Form with subform(s)</b> ▸ Datasheet ▸ titles <b>PartyCandidates</b> and <b>Candidates Subform</b>. Each party record shows its three candidates; new candidates typed into the subform get the party's code automatically.</p><p><b>(e)</b> Report Wizard ▸ fields PartyName (Parties), FirstName, LastName, Constituency, Votes (Candidates) ▸ view by Parties / grouping level <b>PartyName</b> ▸ sort <b>LastName</b> ascending ▸ Summary Options: <b>Sum</b> of Votes ▸ Stepped layout. In Design View, edit the title label to read:</p><div class=\"passage\"><b>National Youth Electoral Commission</b><br><b>2026 Youth Parliament Results by Party</b></div><p>Group totals: Forward Together 1,029; Island Voices 1,062; Next Gen Alliance 940; Green Hills Youth 740; grand total 3,771.</p>"},{"t":"essay","title":"Question 5 · Problem-solving and programming: the ballot counter","q":"<p>At each small polling station, ballots for three candidates are counted by computer. Each ballot is entered as the candidate's number: <b>1</b>, <b>2</b> or <b>3</b>. Any other number (except 0) is a <b>spoilt</b> ballot. Entering <b>0</b> ends the count, and the program must stop after a <b>maximum of 8</b> ballots even if 0 has not been entered. The program must output the votes for each candidate, the number of spoilt ballots and the number of the winning candidate (or \"No clear winner\" if the highest count is shared).</p><ol type=\"a\"><li>Write a problem statement and list the inputs, processing and outputs. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li>Write pseudocode (or draw a flowchart) for the algorithm. Your loop must stop on 0 OR after 8 ballots. <i>(5 marks)</i></li><li>Complete a trace table for the data <b>2, 1, 2, 5, 3, 2, 0</b>. Show vote, count, c1, c2, c3, spoilt and the output. <i>(3 marks)</i></li><li>Write the program in Python (Pascal is acceptable). Save it as <code>BallotCounter</code>. <i>(4 marks)</i></li><li>Run your program with the test data in (c) and record the output. <i>(1 mark)</i></li></ol><p class=\"row\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"btn ghost\" data-gx=\"opengame\" data-page=\"games/codelab.html?level=3\" data-id=\"codelab\">🐍 Open the Code Lab</button> <span class=\"hint\">Use the pseudocode interpreter, trace table tool and Python editor.</span></p>","points":15,"fb":"<h4>Marking guide</h4><p><b>Mark scheme</b></p><ul><li>(a) Problem statement (1); inputs, processing and outputs (1).</li><li>(b) Initialisation (1); loop with BOTH conditions (0 and maximum 8) (1); nested selection for 1, 2, 3 with ELSE spoilt (1); next input inside the loop and counter (1); output and winner/tie logic (1).</li><li>(c) Rows for each ballot correct (2, -1 per error); final output correct (1).</li><li>(d) Correct input and loop (1); if/elif/else counting (1); output (1); program runs without errors and matches the algorithm (1).</li><li>(e) Output recorded and consistent with (c) (1).</li></ul><h4>Model answer</h4><p><b>(a)</b> <b>Problem statement:</b> The NYEC needs a program to count paper ballots at a small polling station. It must accept up to 8 ballot numbers, stopping early when 0 is entered, count the votes for candidates 1, 2 and 3 and the spoilt ballots, and report the winner.</p><div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Input</th><th>Processing</th><th>Output</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Ballot numbers (0 to finish; maximum 8)</td><td>Count ballots; add 1 to c1, c2 or c3, or to spoilt for any other number; stop at 0 or after 8 ballots; compare c1, c2, c3</td><td>Votes for each candidate; spoilt ballots; winning candidate or \"No clear winner\"</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p><b>(b)</b></p><pre>START\n   c1 ← 0\n   c2 ← 0\n   c3 ← 0\n   spoilt ← 0\n   count ← 0\n   PRINT \"Enter ballot (1, 2 or 3; 0 to finish)\"\n   READ vote\n   WHILE (vote &lt;&gt; 0) AND (count &lt; 8) DO\n      count ← count + 1\n      IF vote = 1 THEN\n         c1 ← c1 + 1\n      ELSE IF vote = 2 THEN\n         c2 ← c2 + 1\n      ELSE IF vote = 3 THEN\n         c3 ← c3 + 1\n      ELSE\n         spoilt ← spoilt + 1\n      ENDIF\n      IF count &lt; 8 THEN\n         READ vote\n      ENDIF\n   ENDWHILE\n   PRINT \"Candidate 1: \", c1, \" Candidate 2: \", c2, \" Candidate 3: \", c3\n   PRINT \"Spoilt: \", spoilt\n   IF (c1 &gt; c2) AND (c1 &gt; c3) THEN\n      PRINT \"Winner: Candidate 1\"\n   ELSE IF (c2 &gt; c1) AND (c2 &gt; c3) THEN\n      PRINT \"Winner: Candidate 2\"\n   ELSE IF (c3 &gt; c1) AND (c3 &gt; c2) THEN\n      PRINT \"Winner: Candidate 3\"\n   ELSE\n      PRINT \"No clear winner\"\n   ENDIF\nSTOP</pre><p><b>(c)</b> The loop ran 6 times (fewer than the maximum of 8) because 0 was entered.</p><div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>vote</th><th>Loop runs?</th><th>count</th><th>c1</th><th>c2</th><th>c3</th><th>spoilt</th><th>Output</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>–</td><td>–</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Yes</td><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>Yes</td><td>2</td><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Yes</td><td>3</td><td>1</td><td>2</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Yes</td><td>4</td><td>1</td><td>2</td><td>0</td><td>1</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Yes</td><td>5</td><td>1</td><td>2</td><td>1</td><td>1</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Yes</td><td>6</td><td>1</td><td>3</td><td>1</td><td>1</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>0</td><td>No (vote = 0)</td><td>6</td><td>1</td><td>3</td><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>Candidate 1: 1, Candidate 2: 3, Candidate 3: 1, Spoilt: 1, Winner: Candidate 2</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p><b>(d)</b></p><pre># BallotCounter: NYEC 2026 polling-station count\n# Counts up to 8 ballots for candidates 1-3; 0 ends early.\nc1 = c2 = c3 = 0\nspoilt = 0\ncount = 0\nMAX_BALLOTS = 8\nvote = int(input(\"Enter ballot (1, 2 or 3; 0 to finish): \"))\nwhile vote != 0 and count &lt; MAX_BALLOTS:\n    count = count + 1\n    if vote == 1:\n        c1 = c1 + 1\n    elif vote == 2:\n        c2 = c2 + 1\n    elif vote == 3:\n        c3 = c3 + 1\n    else:\n        spoilt = spoilt + 1\n    if count &lt; MAX_BALLOTS:\n        vote = int(input(\"Enter ballot (1, 2 or 3; 0 to finish): \"))\nprint(\"Candidate 1:\", c1, \"Candidate 2:\", c2, \"Candidate 3:\", c3)\nprint(\"Spoilt:\", spoilt)\nif c1 &gt; c2 and c1 &gt; c3:\n    print(\"Winner: Candidate 1\")\nelif c2 &gt; c1 and c2 &gt; c3:\n    print(\"Winner: Candidate 2\")\nelif c3 &gt; c1 and c3 &gt; c2:\n    print(\"Winner: Candidate 3\")\nelse:\n    print(\"No clear winner\")</pre><p><b>(e)</b> Output with 2, 1, 2, 5, 3, 2, 0:</p><pre>Candidate 1: 1 Candidate 2: 3 Candidate 3: 1\nSpoilt: 1\nWinner: Candidate 2</pre>"}]},{"id":"pp_csec032p2","title":"Paper 032 practice 2 · Carnival Village Committee","desc":"<p><b>CSEC Information Technology · Paper 032 · Practice Paper 2</b> · 2 hours · 5 questions · 50 marks</p><ol><li>Paper 032 is the practical alternative to the School-Based Assessment for private candidates. Answer <b>ALL FIVE</b> questions.</li><li>Questions 1–4 test the productivity tools (word processing, web page design, spreadsheets, databases: <b>35 marks</b>). Question 5 tests problem-solving and programming (<b>15 marks</b>).</li><li>Read the case study and download the data files first. Work in real Word, Excel, Access and a code editor (Python, Pascal or VBA), or in the course simulators (the Excel and Access Labs import CSV through <b>File ▸ Open</b>).</li><li>In each answer box, record your evidence: the exact formulas, query designs (field, table, criteria, sort), form and report settings, HTML, and your pseudocode, trace table and program. In the real examination you print or save files as instructed by the invigilator.</li><li>Save your files often, using the file names given in each question. Start the 120-minute timer to practise under exam conditions.</li></ol><p class=\"tip\">This is an original practice paper written in the format of the CXC CSEC Information Technology Paper 032 (syllabus CXC 30/G/SYLL 17).</p>","minutes":120,"items":[{"t":"essay","title":"Question 1 · Word processing: vendor form and handbook contents","q":"<p><b>Case study · Carnival Village 2026</b></p><p>The <b>Jamaica Carnival Village Committee (CVC)</b> runs the food booths and troupe registrations for Carnival 2026 in Kingston. Each food vendor pays a booth rent of <b>$350 per week</b> plus a <b>10% commission</b> on sales for the three weeks of Carnival Village. Troupes register their revellers, who pay for costumes in instalments before the Grand Parade.</p><p>The CVC secretary, <b>Mr. Elvis Richardson-Lake</b>, has asked you to prepare the vendor documents, the events web page, the booth sales spreadsheet, the costume database and a judging program for the Calypso Monarch competition. Download each file and open it in your software or the course labs.</p><h4>Data file 1 · BoothSales.csv</h4><p class=\"hint\">Sales in dollars for each week of Carnival Village.</p><div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>BoothNo</th><th>Vendor</th><th>FoodType</th><th>Week1</th><th>Week2</th><th>Week3</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>B01</td><td>Mama Rose's Kitchen</td><td>Local food</td><td>1850</td><td>2210</td><td>2640</td></tr><tr><td>B02</td><td>Sorrel Corner</td><td>Drinks</td><td>1320</td><td>1590</td><td>1975</td></tr><tr><td>B03</td><td>Johnny Cake Junction</td><td>Johnny cakes</td><td>980</td><td>1240</td><td>1505</td></tr><tr><td>B04</td><td>Soca Grill</td><td>Barbecue</td><td>2100</td><td>2480</td><td>2900</td></tr><tr><td>B05</td><td>Island Ice</td><td>Drinks</td><td>760</td><td>940</td><td>1210</td></tr><tr><td>B06</td><td>Pate Palace</td><td>Pastries</td><td>640</td><td>820</td><td>990</td></tr><tr><td>B07</td><td>King Street Bakes</td><td>Johnny cakes</td><td>1110</td><td>1305</td><td>1660</td></tr><tr><td>B08</td><td>Harbour Street Fry Fish</td><td>Local food</td><td>1540</td><td>1780</td><td>2150</td></tr><tr><td>B09</td><td>Coconut Dreams</td><td>Drinks</td><td>1025</td><td>1190</td><td>1460</td></tr><tr><td>B10</td><td>Saltfish and Dumplin</td><td>Local food</td><td>1270</td><td>1425</td><td>1690</td></tr></tbody></table></div><a class=\"btn ghost\" download=\"BoothSales.csv\" href=\"data:text/csv;charset=utf-8,BoothNo%2CVendor%2CFoodType%2CWeek1%2CWeek2%2CWeek3%0D%0AB01%2CMama%20Rose%27s%20Kitchen%2CLocal%20food%2C1850%2C2210%2C2640%0D%0AB02%2CSorrel%20Corner%2CDrinks%2C1320%2C1590%2C1975%0D%0AB03%2CJohnny%20Cake%20Junction%2CJohnny%20cakes%2C980%2C1240%2C1505%0D%0AB04%2CSoca%20Grill%2CBarbecue%2C2100%2C2480%2C2900%0D%0AB05%2CIsland%20Ice%2CDrinks%2C760%2C940%2C1210%0D%0AB06%2CPate%20Palace%2CPastries%2C640%2C820%2C990%0D%0AB07%2CHope%20Road%20Bakes%2CJohnny%20cakes%2C1110%2C1305%2C1660%0D%0AB08%2CConstant%20Spring%20Road%20Fry%20Fish%2CLocal%20food%2C1540%2C1780%2C2150%0D%0AB09%2CCoconut%20Dreams%2CDrinks%2C1025%2C1190%2C1460%0D%0AB10%2CSaltfish%20and%20Dumplin%2CLocal%20food%2C1270%2C1425%2C1690%0D%0A\">⬇ BoothSales.csv</a><h4>Data file 2 · Troupes.csv</h4><div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>TroupeID</th><th>TroupeName</th><th>Leader</th><th>Theme</th><th>Phone</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>T1</td><td>Mas Fusion</td><td>Tamara Lake</td><td>Ocean Spirits</td><td>5421180</td></tr><tr><td>T2</td><td>Island Vibes Carnival Group</td><td>Jerome York</td><td>Fire and Gold</td><td>5427734</td></tr><tr><td>T3</td><td>King Street Revellers</td><td>Shania Duzant</td><td>Hurricane Strong</td><td>5203391</td></tr><tr><td>T4</td><td>Molineux Players</td><td>Akeem Hodge</td><td>Flamingo Fiesta</td><td>5806645</td></tr></tbody></table></div><a class=\"btn ghost\" download=\"Troupes.csv\" href=\"data:text/csv;charset=utf-8,TroupeID%2CTroupeName%2CLeader%2CTheme%2CPhone%0D%0AT1%2CMas%20Fusion%2CTamara%20Lake%2COcean%20Spirits%2C5421180%0D%0AT2%2CIsland%20Vibes%20Carnival%20Group%2CJerome%20York%2CFire%20and%20Gold%2C5427734%0D%0AT3%2CHope%20Road%20Revellers%2CShania%20Duzant%2CHurricane%20Strong%2C5203391%0D%0AT4%2CMolineux%20Players%2CAkeem%20Hodge%2CFlamingo%20Fiesta%2C5806645%0D%0A\">⬇ Troupes.csv</a><h4>Data file 3 · Revellers.csv</h4><p class=\"hint\">CostumePrice and AmountPaid in dollars.</p><div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>RevellerID</th><th>FirstName</th><th>LastName</th><th>TroupeID</th><th>Section</th><th>CostumePrice</th><th>AmountPaid</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>R001</td><td>Keisha</td><td>Brooks</td><td>T1</td><td>Frontline</td><td>650</td><td>650</td></tr><tr><td>R002</td><td>Dwayne</td><td>Carty</td><td>T1</td><td>Backline</td><td>280</td><td>150</td></tr><tr><td>R003</td><td>Aaliyah</td><td>Richardson</td><td>T1</td><td>Kids</td><td>120</td><td>120</td></tr><tr><td>R004</td><td>Marcus</td><td>Peterson</td><td>T2</td><td>Frontline</td><td>720</td><td>500</td></tr><tr><td>R005</td><td>Shanice</td><td>Liburd</td><td>T2</td><td>Backline</td><td>300</td><td>300</td></tr><tr><td>R006</td><td>Tyrese</td><td>Brantley</td><td>T2</td><td>Backline</td><td>300</td><td>175</td></tr><tr><td>R007</td><td>Jada</td><td>Nisbett</td><td>T3</td><td>Frontline</td><td>590</td><td>590</td></tr><tr><td>R008</td><td>Kevin</td><td>Hanley</td><td>T3</td><td>Frontline</td><td>590</td><td>300</td></tr><tr><td>R009</td><td>Nadia</td><td>Hazel</td><td>T3</td><td>Backline</td><td>260</td><td>260</td></tr><tr><td>R010</td><td>Zion</td><td>Lake</td><td>T3</td><td>Kids</td><td>110</td><td>60</td></tr><tr><td>R011</td><td>Precious</td><td>York</td><td>T4</td><td>Frontline</td><td>680</td><td>680</td></tr><tr><td>R012</td><td>Andre</td><td>Hodge</td><td>T4</td><td>Backline</td><td>290</td><td>90</td></tr><tr><td>R013</td><td>Imani</td><td>Duzant</td><td>T4</td><td>Kids</td><td>125</td><td>125</td></tr><tr><td>R014</td><td>Ravi</td><td>Singh</td><td>T1</td><td>Frontline</td><td>650</td><td>400</td></tr></tbody></table></div><a class=\"btn ghost\" download=\"Revellers.csv\" href=\"data:text/csv;charset=utf-8,RevellerID%2CFirstName%2CLastName%2CTroupeID%2CSection%2CCostumePrice%2CAmountPaid%0D%0AR001%2CKeisha%2CBrooks%2CT1%2CFrontline%2C650%2C650%0D%0AR002%2CDwayne%2CCarty%2CT1%2CBackline%2C280%2C150%0D%0AR003%2CAaliyah%2CRichardson%2CT1%2CKids%2C120%2C120%0D%0AR004%2CMarcus%2CPeterson%2CT2%2CFrontline%2C720%2C500%0D%0AR005%2CShanice%2CLiburd%2CT2%2CBackline%2C300%2C300%0D%0AR006%2CTyrese%2CBrantley%2CT2%2CBackline%2C300%2C175%0D%0AR007%2CJada%2CNisbett%2CT3%2CFrontline%2C590%2C590%0D%0AR008%2CKevin%2CHanley%2CT3%2CFrontline%2C590%2C300%0D%0AR009%2CNadia%2CHazel%2CT3%2CBackline%2C260%2C260%0D%0AR010%2CZion%2CLake%2CT3%2CKids%2C110%2C60%0D%0AR011%2CPrecious%2CYork%2CT4%2CFrontline%2C680%2C680%0D%0AR012%2CAndre%2CHodge%2CT4%2CBackline%2C290%2C90%0D%0AR013%2CImani%2CDuzant%2CT4%2CKids%2C125%2C125%0D%0AR014%2CRavi%2CSingh%2CT1%2CFrontline%2C650%2C400%0D%0A\">⬇ Revellers.csv</a><h4>Handbook headings (for Question 1)</h4><ol><li>Welcome to Carnival Village (Heading 1)</li><li>Booth Rules (Heading 1) · Opening Hours (Heading 2) · Noise and Music (Heading 2)</li><li>Food Safety (Heading 1)</li><li>Payments and Rent (Heading 1)</li><li>Emergency Procedures (Heading 1)</li></ol><ol type=\"a\"><li>Create a fillable form <code>VendorForm</code> headed <b>Carnival Village 2026 · Food Vendor Application</b> containing: text fields for Business name and Contact person; a drop-down list for Food type (Local food, Drinks, Johnny cakes, Barbecue, Pastries); a date picker for Start date; check boxes for <i>Needs electricity</i> and <i>Needs running water</i>. Protect the form so only the fields can be completed. <i>(4 marks)</i></li><li>Create a document <code>VendorHandbook</code> using the handbook headings in the case study, with at least one sentence under each heading and each Heading 1 starting on a new page. Insert an automatic table of contents on page 1 and page numbers in the footer. <i>(3 marks)</i></li><li>Add a new Heading 1, <b>Waste and Recycling</b>, before Emergency Procedures, and update the table of contents. <i>(1 mark)</i></li></ol><p>In the answer box, list each form control with its settings, the styles you applied, and the final table of contents (headings and page numbers).</p><p class=\"row\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"btn ghost\" data-gx=\"opengame\" data-page=\"games/wordsim.html?level=3\" data-id=\"wordsim\">📘 Open the Word Lab</button> <span class=\"hint\">Form controls and the table of contents are Level 3 missions.</span></p>","points":8,"fb":"<h4>Marking guide</h4><p><b>Mark scheme</b></p><ul><li>(a) Text fields (1); drop-down with the five options (1); date picker and both check boxes (1); protected for filling in forms (1).</li><li>(b) Heading 1 and Heading 2 styles applied correctly (1); automatic TOC on page 1 (1); page numbers in footer and page breaks (1).</li><li>(c) Heading added and TOC updated (1).</li></ul><h4>Model answer</h4><p><b>(a) VendorForm</b></p><div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Prompt</th><th>Control</th><th>Settings</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Business name</td><td>Plain-text content control</td><td>Placeholder: Type the business name</td></tr><tr><td>Contact person</td><td>Plain-text content control</td><td>Placeholder: Type the contact person</td></tr><tr><td>Food type</td><td>Drop-down list</td><td>Local food; Drinks; Johnny cakes; Barbecue; Pastries</td></tr><tr><td>Start date</td><td>Date picker</td><td>Format dd/MM/yyyy</td></tr><tr><td>Needs electricity</td><td>Check box</td><td>Unticked</td></tr><tr><td>Needs running water</td><td>Check box</td><td>Unticked</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Developer ▸ Controls to insert each control; Properties to add the drop-down items. Review ▸ Restrict Editing ▸ <b>Filling in forms</b> ▸ Yes, Start Enforcing Protection.</p><p><b>(b)</b> Heading 1 applied to: Welcome to Carnival Village, Booth Rules, Food Safety, Payments and Rent, Emergency Procedures. Heading 2 applied to: Opening Hours, Noise and Music. A page break (Ctrl+Enter) is inserted before each Heading 1, leaving page 1 for the contents. References ▸ Table of Contents ▸ Automatic Table 1 on page 1; Insert ▸ Page Number ▸ Bottom of Page ▸ Plain Number 2.</p><p><b>(c)</b> New Heading 1 <b>Waste and Recycling</b> inserted on its own page before Emergency Procedures; right-click the TOC ▸ Update Field ▸ <b>Update entire table</b>. Final contents:</p><div class=\"passage\"><pre>Welcome to Carnival Village ............ 2\nBooth Rules ............................ 3\n   Opening Hours ....................... 3\n   Noise and Music ..................... 3\nFood Safety ............................ 4\nPayments and Rent ...................... 5\nWaste and Recycling .................... 6\nEmergency Procedures ................... 7</pre></div>"},{"t":"essay","title":"Question 2 · Web page design: Carnival 2026 events page","q":"<p>Create the page <code>events.html</code> for the CVC website. It must include:</p><ol type=\"a\"><li>The logo <code>cvc-logo.png</code> with alternative text, and the heading <b>Carnival 2026 · What's On</b>. <i>(1 mark)</i></li><li>A navigation bar linking to <code>index.html</code> (Home), <code>troupes.html</code> (Troupes) and <code>vendors.html</code> (Vendors). <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li>A table of at least three events with the column headings Date, Event and Venue (e.g. J'ouvert Jump-up, Grand Parade, Calypso Monarch Finals at Carnival Village). <i>(1 mark)</i></li><li>A link that opens an e-mail to <code>vendors@carnivalvillage.tt</code>. <i>(1 mark)</i></li><li>A link to download <code>VendorForm.docx</code> from Question 1. <i>(1 mark)</i></li><li>A link near the top that jumps to a section headed <b>Safety and Lost Children</b> at the bottom of the page. <i>(1 mark)</i></li></ol><p>Paste your HTML or describe each element.</p><p class=\"row\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"btn ghost\" data-gx=\"opengame\" data-page=\"games/websim.html?level=3\" data-id=\"websim\">🌐 Open the Web Page Builder</button></p>","points":7,"fb":"<h4>Marking guide</h4><p><b>Mark scheme</b></p><ul><li>(a) Logo with alt and heading (1).</li><li>(b) Navigation area with all three page links: 3 links (2), 1–2 links (1).</li><li>(c) Table with Date/Event/Venue headings and three events (1).</li><li>(d) mailto link (1).</li><li>(e) Link to VendorForm.docx (1).</li><li>(f) Bookmark link with matching target (1).</li></ul><h4>Model answer</h4><pre>&lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt;\n&lt;html lang=\"en\"&gt;\n&lt;head&gt;\n  &lt;meta charset=\"utf-8\"&gt;\n  &lt;meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1\"&gt;\n  &lt;title&gt;Carnival 2026 | What's On&lt;/title&gt;\n  &lt;style&gt;\n    body { font-family: Verdana, sans-serif; margin: 0; }\n    header { background: #c8102e; color: #fff; padding: 12px 20px; }\n    nav a { color: #ffd23f; margin-right: 14px; font-weight: bold; }\n    table { border-collapse: collapse; }\n    th, td { border: 1px solid #999; padding: 6px 10px; }\n  &lt;/style&gt;\n&lt;/head&gt;\n&lt;body&gt;\n  &lt;header&gt;\n    &lt;img src=\"cvc-logo.png\" alt=\"Trinidad and Tobago Carnival Village Committee logo\" width=\"100\"&gt;\n    &lt;h1&gt;Carnival 2026 · What's On&lt;/h1&gt;\n    &lt;nav&gt;\n      &lt;a href=\"index.html\"&gt;Home&lt;/a&gt;\n      &lt;a href=\"troupes.html\"&gt;Troupes&lt;/a&gt;\n      &lt;a href=\"vendors.html\"&gt;Vendors&lt;/a&gt;\n    &lt;/nav&gt;\n    &lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=\"#safety\"&gt;Safety and Lost Children&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;\n  &lt;/header&gt;\n  &lt;main&gt;\n    &lt;table&gt;\n      &lt;tr&gt;&lt;th&gt;Date&lt;/th&gt;&lt;th&gt;Event&lt;/th&gt;&lt;th&gt;Venue&lt;/th&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;\n      &lt;tr&gt;&lt;td&gt;Thu 23 April 2026&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td&gt;Calypso Monarch Finals&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td&gt;Carnival Village&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;\n      &lt;tr&gt;&lt;td&gt;Sun 26 April 2026&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td&gt;J'ouvert Jump-up&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td&gt;Port of Spain streets&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;\n      &lt;tr&gt;&lt;td&gt;Thu 30 April 2026&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td&gt;Grand Parade&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td&gt;Frederick Street to Carnival Village&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;\n    &lt;/table&gt;\n    &lt;p&gt;Vendors: &lt;a href=\"VendorForm.docx\"&gt;download the vendor application&lt;/a&gt; or &lt;a href=\"mailto:vendors@carnivalvillage.tt\"&gt;e-mail the vendor desk&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;\n    &lt;h2 id=\"safety\"&gt;Safety and Lost Children&lt;/h2&gt;\n    &lt;p&gt;Lost children are taken to the first-aid tent at the Village entrance. Keep water with you and follow stewards' instructions.&lt;/p&gt;\n  &lt;/main&gt;\n  &lt;footer&gt;© 2026 Trinidad and Tobago Carnival Village Committee&lt;/footer&gt;\n&lt;/body&gt;\n&lt;/html&gt;</pre>"},{"t":"essay","title":"Question 3 · Spreadsheet: food booth sales","q":"<p>Open <b>BoothSales.csv</b> (headings in row 1, data in rows 2–11) and save the workbook as <code>BoothSales</code>. In K2 type <i>Weekly rent</i> and in L2 enter <b>350</b>; in K3 type <i>Commission rate</i> and in L3 enter <b>10%</b>.</p><ol type=\"a\"><li>In G1:J1 type the headings TotalSales, Charges, Net and Status. In G2 use a function to total the three weeks. In H2 calculate the charges: three weeks of rent plus the commission on TotalSales, using absolute references to L2 and L3. In I2 calculate Net (TotalSales minus Charges). Copy the formulas to row 11 and format G:I as currency with 2 decimal places. <i>(4 marks)</i></li><li>In J2 enter a formula that displays <b>Gold vendor</b> if Net is $3,000 or more and <b>Standard</b> otherwise. Copy to J11. <i>(1 mark)</i></li><li>In G12 find the average TotalSales, in G13 the highest TotalSales and in J12 the number of Gold vendors. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li>Sort rows 2–11 by FoodType (A to Z) and then by TotalSales (largest first). <i>(1 mark)</i></li><li>Create a bar or column chart of TotalSales by Vendor titled <b>Carnival Village 2026 Food Booth Sales</b>, with axis titles, on a new sheet named <b>SalesChart</b>. <i>(1 mark)</i></li><li>On a copy of the data named <b>Drinks</b>, filter to show only booths whose FoodType is Drinks. <i>(1 mark)</i></li></ol><p>Record every formula, your results and how you sorted, charted and filtered.</p><p class=\"row\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"btn ghost\" data-gx=\"opengame\" data-page=\"games/excelsim.html?level=3\" data-id=\"excelsim\">🟩 Open the Excel Lab</button> <span class=\"hint\">Import BoothSales.csv with File ▸ Open.</span></p>","points":10,"fb":"<h4>Marking guide</h4><p><b>Mark scheme</b></p><ul><li>(a) SUM (1); Charges formula with BOTH absolute references (1); Net (1); copied and currency 2 dp (1).</li><li>(b) IF correct (1).</li><li>(c) AVERAGE and MAX (1); COUNTIF (1).</li><li>(d) Two-level sort correct (1).</li><li>(e) Chart with exact title, axis titles, on SalesChart (1).</li><li>(f) Filter shows only Drinks booths (1).</li></ul><h4>Model answer</h4><p><b>(a)</b> G2: <code>=SUM(D2:F2)</code> &nbsp; H2: <code>=3*$L$2+G2*$L$3</code> &nbsp; I2: <code>=G2-H2</code>, filled down to row 11 and formatted Currency, 2 decimal places. <b>(b)</b> J2: <code>=IF(I2&gt;=3000,\"Gold vendor\",\"Standard\")</code>.</p><div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Booth</th><th>Vendor</th><th>TotalSales (G)</th><th>Charges (H)</th><th>Net (I)</th><th>Status (J)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>B01</td><td>Mama Rose's Kitchen</td><td>$6,700.00</td><td>$1,720.00</td><td>$4,980.00</td><td>Gold vendor</td></tr><tr><td>B02</td><td>Cocoa Corner</td><td>$4,885.00</td><td>$1,538.50</td><td>$3,346.50</td><td>Gold vendor</td></tr><tr><td>B03</td><td>Johnny Cake Junction</td><td>$3,725.00</td><td>$1,422.50</td><td>$2,302.50</td><td>Standard</td></tr><tr><td>B04</td><td>Soca Grill</td><td>$7,480.00</td><td>$1,798.00</td><td>$5,682.00</td><td>Gold vendor</td></tr><tr><td>B05</td><td>Island Ice</td><td>$2,910.00</td><td>$1,341.00</td><td>$1,569.00</td><td>Standard</td></tr><tr><td>B06</td><td>Pate Palace</td><td>$2,450.00</td><td>$1,295.00</td><td>$1,155.00</td><td>Standard</td></tr><tr><td>B07</td><td>Bridge Street Bakes</td><td>$4,075.00</td><td>$1,457.50</td><td>$2,617.50</td><td>Standard</td></tr><tr><td>B08</td><td>Jeremie Street Fry Fish</td><td>$5,470.00</td><td>$1,597.00</td><td>$3,873.00</td><td>Gold vendor</td></tr><tr><td>B09</td><td>Coconut Dreams</td><td>$3,675.00</td><td>$1,417.50</td><td>$2,257.50</td><td>Standard</td></tr><tr><td>B10</td><td>Saltfish and Dumplin</td><td>$4,385.00</td><td>$1,488.50</td><td>$2,896.50</td><td>Standard</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p><b>(c)</b> G12: <code>=AVERAGE(G2:G11)</code> = $4,575.50; G13: <code>=MAX(G2:G11)</code> = $7,480.00 (Soca Grill); J12: <code>=COUNTIF(J2:J11,\"Gold vendor\")</code> = 4.</p><p><b>(d)</b> Select A1:J11 ▸ Data ▸ Sort ▸ Sort by <b>FoodType</b>, A to Z ▸ Add Level ▸ Then by <b>TotalSales</b>, Largest to Smallest. Result:</p><div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>FoodType</th><th>Vendor</th><th>TotalSales</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Barbecue</td><td>Soca Grill</td><td>$7,480.00</td></tr><tr><td>Drinks</td><td>Cocoa Corner</td><td>$4,885.00</td></tr><tr><td>Drinks</td><td>Coconut Dreams</td><td>$3,675.00</td></tr><tr><td>Drinks</td><td>Island Ice</td><td>$2,910.00</td></tr><tr><td>Johnny cakes</td><td>Bridge Street Bakes</td><td>$4,075.00</td></tr><tr><td>Johnny cakes</td><td>Johnny Cake Junction</td><td>$3,725.00</td></tr><tr><td>Local food</td><td>Mama Rose's Kitchen</td><td>$6,700.00</td></tr><tr><td>Local food</td><td>Jeremie Street Fry Fish</td><td>$5,470.00</td></tr><tr><td>Local food</td><td>Saltfish and Dumplin</td><td>$4,385.00</td></tr><tr><td>Pastries</td><td>Pate Palace</td><td>$2,450.00</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p><b>(e)</b> Select Vendor (B1:B11) and TotalSales (G1:G11) ▸ Insert ▸ Clustered Bar ▸ title <b>Carnival Village 2026 Food Booth Sales</b> ▸ axis titles <b>Vendor</b> and <b>Total sales ($)</b> ▸ Move Chart ▸ New sheet <b>SalesChart</b>.</p><p><b>(f)</b> Copy the sheet, rename it <b>Drinks</b>, Data ▸ Filter ▸ FoodType ▸ tick only Drinks:</p><div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Vendor</th><th>TotalSales</th><th>Net</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Cocoa Corner</td><td>$4,885.00</td><td>$3,346.50</td></tr><tr><td>Island Ice</td><td>$2,910.00</td><td>$1,569.00</td></tr><tr><td>Coconut Dreams</td><td>$3,675.00</td><td>$2,257.50</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},{"t":"essay","title":"Question 4 · Database: troupes and costume payments","q":"<p>Create a database <code>CarnivalTroupes</code> and import <b>Troupes.csv</b> and <b>Revellers.csv</b> as tables named <b>Troupes</b> and <b>Revellers</b>.</p><ol type=\"a\"><li>Set primary keys, make CostumePrice and AmountPaid Currency fields, add a validation rule so Section accepts only Frontline, Backline or Kids, and create a one-to-many relationship with referential integrity. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li>Create a query <b>BayRoadFrontline</b> showing TroupeName, FirstName, LastName and Section for revellers in <b>The Bay Road Revellers</b> who are in the <b>Frontline</b> section. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li>Create a query <b>Balances</b> with a calculated field <b>Balance</b> (CostumePrice minus AmountPaid) showing only revellers who still owe money, largest balance first. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li>Create a form <b>TroupeRevellers</b> with the troupe in the main form and its revellers in a subform. Use the form to add a new reveller: <i>R015, Malik Liburd, T2, Kids, 130, 50</i>. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li>Create a report from the Balances query grouped by <b>TroupeName</b>, sorted by LastName, showing FirstName, LastName, Section and Balance, with the total Balance for each troupe. The title must be on two lines:<br><b>St. Kitts and Nevis Carnival Village Committee</b><br><b>Outstanding Costume Balances by Troupe 2026</b> <i>(2 marks)</i></li></ol><p>Describe each design (keys, rule, relationship, query grids, form, report) and list the records returned. Answer (c) and (e) <i>before</i> adding R015 in (d).</p><p class=\"row\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"btn ghost\" data-gx=\"opengame\" data-page=\"games/accesssim.html?level=3\" data-id=\"accesssim\">🗃️ Open the Access Lab</button> <span class=\"hint\">Import both CSV files with File ▸ Open.</span></p>","points":10,"fb":"<h4>Marking guide</h4><p><b>Mark scheme</b></p><ul><li>(a) Keys and currency types (1); validation rule and enforced relationship (1).</li><li>(b) Both tables and fields (1); both criteria on one row giving Nisbett and Hanley (1).</li><li>(c) Calculated field named Balance (1); &gt;0 and descending sort (1).</li><li>(d) Main form/subform (1); new record entered through the subform (1).</li><li>(e) Grouped by TroupeName with Sum of Balance (1); exact two-line title (1).</li></ul><h4>Model answer</h4><p><b>(a)</b> Troupes: PK <b>TroupeID</b>. Revellers: PK <b>RevellerID</b>; CostumePrice and AmountPaid set to <b>Currency</b>; Section: Validation Rule <code>\"Frontline\" Or \"Backline\" Or \"Kids\"</code>, Validation Text <i>Enter Frontline, Backline or Kids</i>. Relationship: Troupes.TroupeID (one) → Revellers.TroupeID (many), Enforce Referential Integrity.</p><p><b>(b) BayRoadFrontline</b></p><div class=\"tablewrap\"><table class=\"qbe\"><tr><th>Field</th><td>TroupeName</td><td>FirstName</td><td>LastName</td><td>Section</td></tr><tr><th>Table</th><td>Troupes</td><td>Revellers</td><td>Revellers</td><td>Revellers</td></tr><tr><th>Sort</th><td></td><td></td><td>Ascending</td><td></td></tr><tr><th>Show</th><td>✔</td><td>✔</td><td>✔</td><td>✔</td></tr><tr><th>Criteria</th><td>\"The Bay Road Revellers\"</td><td></td><td></td><td>\"Frontline\"</td></tr></table></div><p>Results: Kevin Hanley; Jada Nisbett.</p><p><b>(c) Balances (TroupeName from Troupes is included for the report)</b></p><div class=\"tablewrap\"><table class=\"qbe\"><tr><th>Field</th><td>RevellerID</td><td>FirstName</td><td>LastName</td><td>TroupeName</td><td>Section</td><td>Balance: [CostumePrice]-[AmountPaid]</td></tr><tr><th>Table</th><td>Revellers</td><td>Revellers</td><td>Revellers</td><td>Troupes</td><td>Revellers</td><td></td></tr><tr><th>Sort</th><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td>Descending</td></tr><tr><th>Show</th><td>✔</td><td>✔</td><td>✔</td><td>✔</td><td>✔</td><td>✔</td></tr><tr><th>Criteria</th><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td>&gt;0</td></tr></table></div><p>Results (7): Kevin Hanley $290.00; Ravi Singh $250.00; Marcus Peterson $220.00; Andre Hodge $200.00; Dwayne Carty $130.00; Tyrese Brantley $125.00; Zion Lake $50.00.</p><p><b>(d)</b> Form Wizard ▸ Troupes: TroupeID, TroupeName, Leader, Theme ▸ Revellers: RevellerID, FirstName, LastName, Section, CostumePrice, AmountPaid ▸ view by Troupes ▸ Form with subform(s) ▸ name <b>TroupeRevellers</b>. Navigate to T2 (Island Vibes Carnival Group) and type in the subform's new row: R015 · Malik · Liburd · Kids · 130 · 50. TroupeID T2 is filled in automatically by the link.</p><p><b>(e)</b> Report Wizard ▸ query Balances ▸ fields TroupeName, FirstName, LastName, Section, Balance ▸ grouping level <b>TroupeName</b> ▸ sort <b>LastName</b> ▸ Summary Options: <b>Sum</b> of Balance ▸ title edited in Design View to two lines:</p><div class=\"passage\"><b>St. Kitts and Nevis Carnival Village Committee</b><br><b>Outstanding Costume Balances by Troupe 2026</b></div><p>Group totals (before R015): Mas Fusion $380.00; Island Vibes Carnival Group $345.00; the Bay Road Revellers $340.00; Molineux Players $200.00; grand total $1,265.00.</p>"},{"t":"essay","title":"Question 5 · Problem-solving and programming: Calypso Monarch judging","q":"<p>At the Calypso Monarch Finals, each calypsonian is scored by <b>exactly five</b> judges. Each score must be between <b>0 and 25</b>; if a judge's score is outside this range, the program displays <i>Invalid score, re-enter</i> and reads it again. The program outputs the total, the average (1 decimal place) and the highest score, and displays <b>Crown contender</b> if the average is <b>20 or more</b>, otherwise <b>Keep singing</b>.</p><ol type=\"a\"><li>Write a problem statement and an IPO chart. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li>Write pseudocode or draw a flowchart. Use a FOR loop that runs a maximum of 5 times, with a validation loop inside it. <i>(5 marks)</i></li><li>Complete a trace table using the scores <b>22, 18, 27, 24, 19, 21</b> (27 is invalid and is re-entered as 24). Show i, score, total, highest and output. <i>(3 marks)</i></li><li>Write the program in Python (Pascal accepted) and save it as <code>CalypsoJudge</code>. <i>(4 marks)</i></li><li>Run it with the data in (c) and record the output. <i>(1 mark)</i></li></ol><p class=\"row\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"btn ghost\" data-gx=\"opengame\" data-page=\"games/codelab.html?level=3\" data-id=\"codelab\">🐍 Open the Code Lab</button></p>","points":15,"fb":"<h4>Marking guide</h4><p><b>Mark scheme</b></p><ul><li>(a) Statement (1); IPO (1).</li><li>(b) FOR loop 1 to 5 (1); validation WHILE with OR and message (1); accumulator (1); highest (1); average and IF message after the loop (1).</li><li>(c) Invalid row shown (1); running totals and highest correct (1); final output correct (1).</li><li>(d) Loop and input (1); validation (1); total/highest (1); runs and outputs correctly (1).</li><li>(e) Output recorded (1).</li></ul><h4>Model answer</h4><p><b>(a)</b> <b>Problem statement:</b> The CVC needs a program that accepts five judges' scores for a calypsonian, rejects any score outside 0–25, and reports the total, average and highest score and whether the performer is a crown contender.</p><div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Input</th><th>Processing</th><th>Output</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Five judges' scores (0–25)</td><td>Validate each score; add to total; keep the highest; average = total ÷ 5; compare average with 20</td><td>Total; average (1 dp); highest; \"Crown contender\" or \"Keep singing\"</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p><b>(b)</b></p><pre>START\n   total ← 0\n   highest ← 0\n   FOR i ← 1 TO 5 DO\n      PRINT \"Enter score for judge \", i\n      READ score\n      WHILE (score &lt; 0) OR (score &gt; 25) DO\n         PRINT \"Invalid score, re-enter\"\n         READ score\n      ENDWHILE\n      total ← total + score\n      IF score &gt; highest THEN\n         highest ← score\n      ENDIF\n   ENDFOR\n   average ← total / 5\n   PRINT \"Total: \", total, \" Average: \", average, \" Highest: \", highest\n   IF average &gt;= 20 THEN\n      PRINT \"Crown contender\"\n   ELSE\n      PRINT \"Keep singing\"\n   ENDIF\nSTOP</pre><p><b>(c)</b> The FOR loop runs exactly 5 times (its maximum); the validation loop runs once, for judge 3.</p><div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Judge (i)</th><th>score entered</th><th>Valid (0–25)?</th><th>total</th><th>highest</th><th>Output</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>–</td><td>–</td><td>–</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>22</td><td>Yes</td><td>22</td><td>22</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>18</td><td>Yes</td><td>40</td><td>22</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>27</td><td>No: re-enter</td><td>40</td><td>22</td><td>Invalid score, re-enter</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>24</td><td>Yes</td><td>64</td><td>24</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>19</td><td>Yes</td><td>83</td><td>24</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>21</td><td>Yes</td><td>104</td><td>24</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>after loop</td><td></td><td></td><td>104</td><td>24</td><td>Total: 104, Average: 20.8, Highest: 24, Crown contender</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p><b>(d)</b></p><pre># CalypsoJudge: Calypso Monarch Finals scoring\ntotal = 0\nhighest = 0\nfor i in range(1, 6):\n    score = int(input(\"Enter score for judge \" + str(i) + \": \"))\n    while score &lt; 0 or score &gt; 25:       # validation loop\n        print(\"Invalid score, re-enter\")\n        score = int(input(\"Enter score for judge \" + str(i) + \": \"))\n    total = total + score\n    if score &gt; highest:\n        highest = score\naverage = total / 5\nprint(\"Total:\", total, \"Average:\", format(average, \".1f\"), \"Highest:\", highest)\nif average &gt;= 20:\n    print(\"Crown contender\")\nelse:\n    print(\"Keep singing\")</pre><p><b>(e)</b></p><pre>Invalid score, re-enter\nTotal: 104 Average: 20.8 Highest: 24\nCrown contender</pre>"}]},{"id":"pp_csec032p3","title":"Paper 032 practice 3 · Inter-Schools Sports Federation","desc":"<p><b>CSEC Information Technology · Paper 032 · Practice Paper 3</b> · 2 hours · 5 questions · 50 marks</p><ol><li>Paper 032 is the practical alternative to the School-Based Assessment for private candidates. Answer <b>ALL FIVE</b> questions.</li><li>Questions 1–4 test the productivity tools (word processing, web page design, spreadsheets, databases: <b>35 marks</b>). Question 5 tests problem-solving and programming (<b>15 marks</b>).</li><li>Read the case study and download the data files first. Work in real Word, Excel, Access and a code editor (Python, Pascal or VBA), or in the course simulators (the Excel and Access Labs import CSV through <b>File ▸ Open</b>).</li><li>In each answer box, record your evidence: the exact formulas, query designs (field, table, criteria, sort), form and report settings, HTML, and your pseudocode, trace table and program. In the real examination you print or save files as instructed by the invigilator.</li><li>Save your files often, using the file names given in each question. Start the 120-minute timer to practise under exam conditions.</li></ol><p class=\"tip\">This is an original practice paper written in the format of the CXC CSEC Information Technology Paper 032 (syllabus CXC 30/G/SYLL 17).</p>","minutes":120,"items":[{"t":"essay","title":"Question 1 · Word processing: coach letters and the championship programme","q":"<p><b>Case study · The 2026 Inter-Schools Athletics Championship</b></p><p>The <b>St. Kitts and Nevis Inter-Schools Sports Federation (ISSF)</b> organises the annual athletics championship at Warner Park. Eight secondary schools compete. Points are awarded for medals: <b>5</b> for gold, <b>3</b> for silver and <b>1</b> for bronze. Schools with <b>40 or more points</b> qualify to send a team to the regional championship in Barbados.</p><p>The ISSF secretary, <b>Mrs. Hyacinth Liburd-Romney</b>, has asked you to prepare the coach letters and championship programme, the results web page, the medal spreadsheet, the athletes database and a program to process heat times. Download each file and open it in your software or the course labs.</p><h4>Data file 1 · MedalTable.csv</h4><div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>SchoolCode</th><th>School</th><th>Gold</th><th>Silver</th><th>Bronze</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>SAS</td><td>Sea-Almond Secondary School</td><td>7</td><td>5</td><td>4</td></tr><tr><td>HRH</td><td>Hummingbird Ridge High School</td><td>6</td><td>7</td><td>3</td></tr><tr><td>BVS</td><td>Breadfruit Valley Secondary</td><td>4</td><td>6</td><td>8</td></tr><tr><td>FHC</td><td>Flamboyant Heights College</td><td>3</td><td>2</td><td>5</td></tr><tr><td>STC</td><td>Sandbox Tree College</td><td>5</td><td>3</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>FHA</td><td>Frangipani Hill Academy</td><td>1</td><td>4</td><td>6</td></tr><tr><td>MGH</td><td>Mahoe Grove High School</td><td>2</td><td>1</td><td>3</td></tr><tr><td>SBS</td><td>Seagrape Bay Secondary School</td><td>0</td><td>3</td><td>4</td></tr></tbody></table></div><a class=\"btn ghost\" download=\"MedalTable.csv\" href=\"data:text/csv;charset=utf-8,SchoolCode%2CSchool%2CGold%2CSilver%2CBronze%0D%0ASAS%2CSea-Almond%20Secondary%20School%2C7%2C5%2C4%0D%0AHRH%2CHummingbird%20Ridge%20High%20School%2C6%2C7%2C3%0D%0ABVS%2CBreadfruit%20Valley%20Secondary%2C4%2C6%2C8%0D%0AFHC%2CFlamboyant%20Heights%20College%2C3%2C2%2C5%0D%0ASTC%2CSandbox%20Tree%20College%2C5%2C3%2C2%0D%0AFHA%2CFrangipani%20Hill%20Academy%2C1%2C4%2C6%0D%0AMGH%2CMahoe%20Grove%20High%20School%2C2%2C1%2C3%0D%0ASBS%2CSeagrape%20Bay%20Secondary%20School%2C0%2C3%2C4%0D%0A\">⬇ MedalTable.csv</a><h4>Data file 2 · Schools.csv</h4><div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>SchoolCode</th><th>SchoolName</th><th>District</th><th>Coach</th><th>CoachEmail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>SAS</td><td>Sea-Almond Secondary School</td><td>Basseterre</td><td>Mr. Desmond Lake</td><td>dlake@issf.kn</td></tr><tr><td>HRH</td><td>Hummingbird Ridge High School</td><td>Cotton Ground</td><td>Ms. Carla Brooks</td><td>cbrooks@issf.kn</td></tr><tr><td>BVS</td><td>Breadfruit Valley Secondary</td><td>Basseterre</td><td>Mr. Trevor Hazel</td><td>thazel@issf.kn</td></tr><tr><td>FHC</td><td>Flamboyant Heights College</td><td>Charlestown</td><td>Ms. Joy Peterson</td><td>jpeterson@issf.kn</td></tr><tr><td>STC</td><td>Sandbox Tree College</td><td>Sandy Point</td><td>Mr. Ravi Persaud</td><td>rpersaud@issf.kn</td></tr><tr><td>FHA</td><td>Frangipani Hill Academy</td><td>Cayon</td><td>Ms. Nicole Brantley</td><td>nbrantley@issf.kn</td></tr><tr><td>MGH</td><td>Mahoe Grove High School</td><td>Basseterre</td><td>Mr. Glen Hanley</td><td>ghanley@issf.kn</td></tr><tr><td>SBS</td><td>Seagrape Bay Secondary School</td><td>Gingerland</td><td>Ms. Angela York</td><td>ayork@issf.kn</td></tr></tbody></table></div><a class=\"btn ghost\" download=\"Schools.csv\" href=\"data:text/csv;charset=utf-8,SchoolCode%2CSchoolName%2CDistrict%2CCoach%2CCoachEmail%0D%0ASAS%2CSea-Almond%20Secondary%20School%2CBasseterre%2CMr.%20Desmond%20Lake%2Cdlake%40issf.kn%0D%0AHRH%2CHummingbird%20Ridge%20High%20School%2CCotton%20Ground%2CMs.%20Carla%20Brooks%2Ccbrooks%40issf.kn%0D%0ABVS%2CBreadfruit%20Valley%20Secondary%2CBasseterre%2CMr.%20Trevor%20Hazel%2Cthazel%40issf.kn%0D%0AFHC%2CFlamboyant%20Heights%20College%2CCharlestown%2CMs.%20Joy%20Peterson%2Cjpeterson%40issf.kn%0D%0ASTC%2CSandbox%20Tree%20College%2CSandy%20Point%2CMr.%20Ravi%20Persaud%2Crpersaud%40issf.kn%0D%0AFHA%2CFrangipani%20Hill%20Academy%2CCayon%2CMs.%20Nicole%20Brantley%2Cnbrantley%40issf.kn%0D%0AMGH%2CMahoe%20Grove%20High%20School%2CBasseterre%2CMr.%20Glen%20Hanley%2Cghanley%40issf.kn%0D%0ASBS%2CSeagrape%20Bay%20Secondary%20School%2CGingerland%2CMs.%20Angela%20York%2Cayork%40issf.kn%0D%0A\">⬇ Schools.csv</a><h4>Data file 3 · Athletes.csv</h4><p class=\"hint\">Distance in metres; BestTime in seconds.</p><div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>AthleteID</th><th>FirstName</th><th>LastName</th><th>Gender</th><th>SchoolCode</th><th>Event</th><th>Distance</th><th>BestTime</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>A001</td><td>Kayla</td><td>Liburd</td><td>F</td><td>SAS</td><td>100m</td><td>100</td><td>12.71</td></tr><tr><td>A002</td><td>Jaden</td><td>Richardson</td><td>M</td><td>SAS</td><td>400m</td><td>400</td><td>52.4</td></tr><tr><td>A003</td><td>Tia</td><td>Carty</td><td>F</td><td>HRH</td><td>100m</td><td>100</td><td>13.62</td></tr><tr><td>A004</td><td>Malik</td><td>Hodge</td><td>M</td><td>HRH</td><td>200m</td><td>200</td><td>23.18</td></tr><tr><td>A005</td><td>Shanice</td><td>Duzant</td><td>F</td><td>BVS</td><td>100m</td><td>100</td><td>13.05</td></tr><tr><td>A006</td><td>Tariq</td><td>Nisbett</td><td>M</td><td>BVS</td><td>100m</td><td>100</td><td>11.36</td></tr><tr><td>A007</td><td>Imani</td><td>Lake</td><td>F</td><td>FHC</td><td>200m</td><td>200</td><td>27.9</td></tr><tr><td>A008</td><td>Kemar</td><td>Brooks</td><td>M</td><td>STC</td><td>400m</td><td>400</td><td>55.75</td></tr><tr><td>A009</td><td>Aaliyah</td><td>York</td><td>F</td><td>MGH</td><td>100m</td><td>100</td><td>13.28</td></tr><tr><td>A010</td><td>Dante</td><td>Brantley</td><td>M</td><td>FHA</td><td>200m</td><td>200</td><td>24.02</td></tr><tr><td>A011</td><td>Precious</td><td>Hazel</td><td>F</td><td>SAS</td><td>200m</td><td>200</td><td>26.35</td></tr><tr><td>A012</td><td>Zion</td><td>Peterson</td><td>M</td><td>SBS</td><td>100m</td><td>100</td><td>11.89</td></tr><tr><td>A013</td><td>Rhea</td><td>Hanley</td><td>F</td><td>BVS</td><td>400m</td><td>400</td><td>61.2</td></tr><tr><td>A014</td><td>Jaylen</td><td>Singh</td><td>M</td><td>MGH</td><td>100m</td><td>100</td><td>12.05</td></tr></tbody></table></div><a class=\"btn ghost\" download=\"Athletes.csv\" href=\"data:text/csv;charset=utf-8,AthleteID%2CFirstName%2CLastName%2CGender%2CSchoolCode%2CEvent%2CDistance%2CBestTime%0D%0AA001%2CKayla%2CLiburd%2CF%2CSAS%2C100m%2C100%2C12.71%0D%0AA002%2CJaden%2CRichardson%2CM%2CSAS%2C400m%2C400%2C52.4%0D%0AA003%2CTia%2CCarty%2CF%2CHRH%2C100m%2C100%2C13.62%0D%0AA004%2CMalik%2CHodge%2CM%2CHRH%2C200m%2C200%2C23.18%0D%0AA005%2CShanice%2CDuzant%2CF%2CBVS%2C100m%2C100%2C13.05%0D%0AA006%2CTariq%2CNisbett%2CM%2CBVS%2C100m%2C100%2C11.36%0D%0AA007%2CImani%2CLake%2CF%2CFHC%2C200m%2C200%2C27.9%0D%0AA008%2CKemar%2CBrooks%2CM%2CSTC%2C400m%2C400%2C55.75%0D%0AA009%2CAaliyah%2CYork%2CF%2CMGH%2C100m%2C100%2C13.28%0D%0AA010%2CDante%2CBrantley%2CM%2CFHA%2C200m%2C200%2C24.02%0D%0AA011%2CPrecious%2CHazel%2CF%2CSAS%2C200m%2C200%2C26.35%0D%0AA012%2CZion%2CPeterson%2CM%2CSBS%2C100m%2C100%2C11.89%0D%0AA013%2CRhea%2CHanley%2CF%2CBVS%2C400m%2C400%2C61.2%0D%0AA014%2CJaylen%2CSingh%2CM%2CMGH%2C100m%2C100%2C12.05%0D%0A\">⬇ Athletes.csv</a><h4>Programme headings (for Question 1)</h4><ol><li>Welcome from the President (Heading 1)</li><li>Order of Events (Heading 1) · Track Events (Heading 2) · Field Events (Heading 2)</li><li>Participating Schools (Heading 1)</li><li>Championship Records (Heading 1)</li><li>Rules and Safety (Heading 1)</li></ol><ol type=\"a\"><li>Create a primary document <code>CoachLetter</code> on ISSF letterhead inviting each coach to the technical meeting on <b>Monday 9 March 2026 at 4:00 p.m.</b> in the Sea-Almond auditorium. Use <b>Schools.csv</b> as the data source and insert the merge fields <b>Coach</b>, <b>SchoolName</b> and <b>CoachEmail</b> (in a line that reads <i>Your confirmation will be sent to «CoachEmail»</i>). <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li>Merge only the letters for schools in the <b>Basseterre</b> district, sorted by SchoolName. Save as <code>BasseterreCoaches</code>. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li>Create <code>ChampionshipProgramme</code> using the programme headings in the case study with Heading 1 and Heading 2 styles, a short paragraph under each, each Heading 1 on a new page, a header containing <i>ISSF Championship 2026</i>, page numbers in the footer and an automatic table of contents on page 1. Under Participating Schools insert a table (copied from Schools.csv) of SchoolName and Coach. <i>(4 marks)</i></li></ol><p>Describe your steps, show the letter with «merge fields», list the merged letters, and give the final table of contents.</p><p class=\"row\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"btn ghost\" data-gx=\"opengame\" data-page=\"games/wordsim.html?level=3\" data-id=\"wordsim\">📘 Open the Word Lab</button></p>","points":8,"fb":"<h4>Marking guide</h4><p><b>Mark scheme</b></p><ul><li>(a) Complete letter on letterhead (1); three merge fields from Schools.csv (1).</li><li>(b) Filter on Basseterre (1); sorted by SchoolName, three letters (1).</li><li>(c) Heading styles and page breaks (1); header and footer page numbers (1); automatic TOC (1); table of schools and coaches (1).</li></ul><h4>Model answer</h4><p><b>(a) CoachLetter</b></p><div class=\"passage\"><p style=\"text-align:center\"><b>ST. KITTS AND NEVIS INTER-SCHOOLS SPORTS FEDERATION</b><br>Warner Park, Basseterre · info@issf.kn</p><p>1 March 2026</p><p>«Coach»<br>«SchoolName»</p><p>Dear «Coach»,</p><p><b>Technical meeting: 2026 Inter-Schools Athletics Championship</b></p><p>The Federation invites you, as coach of «SchoolName», to the technical meeting on <b>Monday 9 March 2026 at 4:00 p.m.</b> in the Sea-Almond Secondary School auditorium. We will confirm entries, heat draws and safety arrangements. Please bring your final entry list.</p><p>Your confirmation will be sent to «CoachEmail».</p><p>Yours in sport,<br><br>Hyacinth Liburd-Romney<br>Secretary, ISSF</p></div><p><b>(b)</b> Mailings ▸ Edit Recipient List ▸ Filter: <b>District</b> Equal to <b>Basseterre</b>; Sort Records: <b>SchoolName</b> ascending ▸ Finish &amp; Merge ▸ Edit Individual Documents. Three letters, in order: Mr. Trevor Hazel (Breadfruit Valley Secondary); Mr. Glen Hanley (Mahoe Grove High School); Mr. Desmond Lake (Sea-Almond Secondary School).</p><p><b>(c)</b> Heading 1: Welcome from the President, Order of Events, Participating Schools, Championship Records, Rules and Safety; Heading 2: Track Events, Field Events. Page break before each Heading 1. Insert ▸ Header ▸ Blank: <i>ISSF Championship 2026</i>; Insert ▸ Page Number ▸ Bottom of Page. Under Participating Schools: Insert ▸ Table (2 columns × 9 rows) with SchoolName and Coach copied from Schools.csv, header row bold. References ▸ Table of Contents ▸ Automatic Table 1 on page 1:</p><div class=\"passage\"><pre>Welcome from the President ........... 2\nOrder of Events ...................... 3\n   Track Events ...................... 3\n   Field Events ...................... 3\nParticipating Schools ................ 4\nChampionship Records ................. 5\nRules and Safety ..................... 6</pre></div>"},{"t":"essay","title":"Question 2 · Web page design: championship results page","q":"<p>Create <code>results.html</code> for the ISSF website:</p><ol type=\"a\"><li>Logo <code>issf-logo.png</code> with alternative text, and the heading <b>ISSF Championship 2026 · Results</b>. <i>(1 mark)</i></li><li>A navigation bar linking to <code>index.html</code>, <code>schedule.html</code> and <code>records.html</code>. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li>A table showing the top three schools with their points (from Question 3). <i>(1 mark)</i></li><li>A link that downloads <code>ChampionshipProgramme.docx</code> from Question 1. <i>(1 mark)</i></li><li>A link that opens an e-mail to <code>results@issf.kn</code>. <i>(1 mark)</i></li><li>A <i>Back to top</i> link at the bottom of the page that jumps to the heading. <i>(1 mark)</i></li></ol><p>Paste your HTML or describe each element.</p><p class=\"row\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"btn ghost\" data-gx=\"opengame\" data-page=\"games/websim.html?level=3\" data-id=\"websim\">🌐 Open the Web Page Builder</button></p>","points":7,"fb":"<h4>Marking guide</h4><p><b>Mark scheme</b></p><ul><li>(a) Logo with alt and heading (1).</li><li>(b) Three page links in a navigation area (2); one or two links (1).</li><li>(c) Table with the correct top three and points (1).</li><li>(d) File link (1).</li><li>(e) mailto link (1).</li><li>(f) Back-to-top bookmark with target (1).</li></ul><h4>Model answer</h4><pre>&lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt;\n&lt;html lang=\"en\"&gt;\n&lt;head&gt;\n  &lt;meta charset=\"utf-8\"&gt;\n  &lt;meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1\"&gt;\n  &lt;title&gt;ISSF Championship 2026 | Results&lt;/title&gt;\n  &lt;style&gt; body{font-family:Arial,sans-serif;margin:0} header{background:#123a7a;color:#fff;padding:12px 20px} nav a{color:#ffd23f;margin-right:14px} th,td{border:1px solid #888;padding:6px 10px} table{border-collapse:collapse} &lt;/style&gt;\n&lt;/head&gt;\n&lt;body&gt;\n  &lt;header&gt;\n    &lt;img src=\"issf-logo.png\" alt=\"Antigua Inter-Schools Sports Federation logo\" width=\"90\"&gt;\n    &lt;h1 id=\"top\"&gt;ISSF Championship 2026 · Results&lt;/h1&gt;\n    &lt;nav&gt;&lt;a href=\"index.html\"&gt;Home&lt;/a&gt; &lt;a href=\"schedule.html\"&gt;Schedule&lt;/a&gt; &lt;a href=\"records.html\"&gt;Records&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/nav&gt;\n  &lt;/header&gt;\n  &lt;main&gt;\n    &lt;table&gt;\n      &lt;caption&gt;Top three schools&lt;/caption&gt;\n      &lt;tr&gt;&lt;th&gt;Place&lt;/th&gt;&lt;th&gt;School&lt;/th&gt;&lt;th&gt;Points&lt;/th&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;\n      &lt;tr&gt;&lt;td&gt;1&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td&gt;Sea-Almond Secondary School&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td&gt;54&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;\n      &lt;tr&gt;&lt;td&gt;2&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td&gt;Hummingbird Ridge High School&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td&gt;54&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;\n      &lt;tr&gt;&lt;td&gt;3&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td&gt;Breadfruit Valley Secondary&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td&gt;46&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;\n    &lt;/table&gt;\n    &lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=\"ChampionshipProgramme.docx\"&gt;Download the championship programme&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;\n    &lt;p&gt;Query a result? &lt;a href=\"mailto:results@issf.kn\"&gt;E-mail the results team&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;\n    &lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=\"#top\"&gt;Back to top&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;\n  &lt;/main&gt;\n  &lt;footer&gt;© 2026 ISSF&lt;/footer&gt;\n&lt;/body&gt;\n&lt;/html&gt;</pre>"},{"t":"essay","title":"Question 3 · Spreadsheet: medal table and points","q":"<p>Open <b>MedalTable.csv</b> (headings row 1, data rows 2–9) and save as <code>MedalPoints</code>. In K1:L4 enter: K1 <i>Medal</i>, L1 <i>Points</i>; K2 Gold, L2 5; K3 Silver, L3 3; K4 Bronze, L4 1.</p><ol type=\"a\"><li>In F1:I1 type TotalMedals, Points, Rank, Status. In F2 use a function to total the medals. In G2 calculate the points using the values in L2:L4 as absolute references. Copy to row 9. <i>(3 marks)</i></li><li>In H2 rank each school's points (highest = 1). <i>(1 mark)</i></li><li>In I2 display <b>Qualifies</b> if Points are 40 or more, otherwise <b>Does not qualify</b>. <i>(1 mark)</i></li><li>In row 10 total the Gold, Silver, Bronze and TotalMedals columns; in G11 find the average points to 1 decimal place; in I11 count the schools that qualify. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li>Sort by Points (highest first) and then by Gold (highest first). <i>(1 mark)</i></li><li>Create a bar chart of Points by School titled <b>ISSF 2026 Championship Points by School</b>, with axis titles and data labels, on a sheet named <b>PointsChart</b>. <i>(1 mark)</i></li><li>On a copy named <b>ThreeGold</b>, filter to show schools with 3 or more gold medals. <i>(1 mark)</i></li></ol><p>Record formulas, results and steps.</p><p class=\"row\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"btn ghost\" data-gx=\"opengame\" data-page=\"games/excelsim.html?level=3\" data-id=\"excelsim\">🟩 Open the Excel Lab</button> <span class=\"hint\">Import MedalTable.csv with File ▸ Open.</span></p>","points":10,"fb":"<h4>Marking guide</h4><p><b>Mark scheme</b></p><ul><li>(a) SUM (1); Points formula correct (1) with absolute references to L2, L3, L4 (1).</li><li>(b) RANK with absolute range (1).</li><li>(c) IF (1).</li><li>(d) Row 10 SUMs (1); rounded AVERAGE and COUNTIF (1).</li><li>(e) Two-level sort (1).</li><li>(f) Bar chart with exact title, axis titles and data labels on PointsChart (1).</li><li>(g) Number filter Gold ≥ 3 (1).</li></ul><h4>Model answer</h4><p><b>(a)</b> F2: <code>=SUM(C2:E2)</code> &nbsp; G2: <code>=C2*$L$2+D2*$L$3+E2*$L$4</code>. <b>(b)</b> H2: <code>=RANK(G2,$G$2:$G$9)</code>. <b>(c)</b> I2: <code>=IF(G2&gt;=40,\"Qualifies\",\"Does not qualify\")</code>. All filled down to row 9.</p><div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>School</th><th>TotalMedals (F)</th><th>Points (G)</th><th>Rank (H)</th><th>Status (I)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Sea-Almond Secondary School</td><td>16</td><td>54</td><td>1</td><td>Qualifies</td></tr><tr><td>Hummingbird Ridge High School</td><td>16</td><td>54</td><td>1</td><td>Qualifies</td></tr><tr><td>Breadfruit Valley Secondary</td><td>18</td><td>46</td><td>3</td><td>Qualifies</td></tr><tr><td>Flamboyant Heights College</td><td>10</td><td>26</td><td>5</td><td>Does not qualify</td></tr><tr><td>Sandbox Tree College</td><td>10</td><td>36</td><td>4</td><td>Does not qualify</td></tr><tr><td>Frangipani Hill Academy</td><td>11</td><td>23</td><td>6</td><td>Does not qualify</td></tr><tr><td>Mahoe Grove High School</td><td>6</td><td>16</td><td>7</td><td>Does not qualify</td></tr><tr><td>Seagrape Bay Secondary School</td><td>7</td><td>13</td><td>8</td><td>Does not qualify</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p><b>(d)</b> C10 <code>=SUM(C2:C9)</code> = 28; D10 = 31; E10 = 35; F10 = 94. G11: <code>=ROUND(AVERAGE(G2:G9),1)</code> = 33.5. I11: <code>=COUNTIF(I2:I9,\"Qualifies\")</code> = 3.</p><p><b>(e)</b> Select A1:I9 ▸ Sort by <b>Points</b> Largest to Smallest ▸ Add Level ▸ then by <b>Gold</b> Largest to Smallest:</p><div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>School</th><th>Points</th><th>Gold</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Sea-Almond Secondary School</td><td>54</td><td>7</td></tr><tr><td>Hummingbird Ridge High School</td><td>54</td><td>6</td></tr><tr><td>Breadfruit Valley Secondary</td><td>46</td><td>4</td></tr><tr><td>Sandbox Tree College</td><td>36</td><td>5</td></tr><tr><td>Flamboyant Heights College</td><td>26</td><td>3</td></tr><tr><td>Frangipani Hill Academy</td><td>23</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>Mahoe Grove High School</td><td>16</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>Seagrape Bay Secondary School</td><td>13</td><td>0</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p><b>(f)</b> Select School (B1:B9) and Points (G1:G9) ▸ Insert ▸ Clustered Bar ▸ title <b>ISSF 2026 Championship Points by School</b> ▸ axis titles <b>School</b> and <b>Points</b> ▸ Data Labels ▸ Outside End ▸ Move Chart ▸ New sheet <b>PointsChart</b>.</p><p><b>(g)</b> Copy the sheet ▸ rename <b>ThreeGold</b> ▸ Data ▸ Filter ▸ Gold ▸ Number Filters ▸ Greater Than Or Equal To ▸ 3:</p><div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>School</th><th>Gold</th><th>Points</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Sea-Almond Secondary School</td><td>7</td><td>54</td></tr><tr><td>Hummingbird Ridge High School</td><td>6</td><td>54</td></tr><tr><td>Breadfruit Valley Secondary</td><td>4</td><td>46</td></tr><tr><td>Flamboyant Heights College</td><td>3</td><td>26</td></tr><tr><td>Sandbox Tree College</td><td>5</td><td>36</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},{"t":"essay","title":"Question 4 · Database: championship entries","q":"<p>Create a database <code>ISSF2026</code> and import <b>Schools.csv</b> and <b>Athletes.csv</b> as tables named <b>Schools</b> and <b>Athletes</b>.</p><ol type=\"a\"><li>Set primary keys; set BestTime to Number (Double) with 2 decimal places and Gender to accept only M or F; create a one-to-many relationship enforcing referential integrity. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li>Create a query <b>BasseterreGirls100</b> showing SchoolName, FirstName, LastName and BestTime for <b>female</b> athletes in the <b>100m</b> from schools in the <b>Basseterre</b> district, fastest first. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li>Create a query <b>Speeds</b> with a calculated field <b>Speed</b> = Distance ÷ BestTime, rounded to 2 decimal places, sorted with the fastest speed first. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li>Create a form <b>SchoolAthletes</b> with each school in the main form and its athletes in a subform. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li>Create a report showing FirstName, LastName, Event and BestTime <b>grouped by SchoolName</b>, sorted by Event, with a count of athletes for each school. Title on two lines:<br><b>Inter-Schools Sports Federation</b><br><b>2026 Athletics Championship Entries by School</b> <i>(2 marks)</i></li></ol><p>Describe your designs and list the records returned.</p><p class=\"row\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"btn ghost\" data-gx=\"opengame\" data-page=\"games/accesssim.html?level=3\" data-id=\"accesssim\">🗃️ Open the Access Lab</button> <span class=\"hint\">Import both CSV files with File ▸ Open.</span></p>","points":10,"fb":"<h4>Marking guide</h4><p><b>Mark scheme</b></p><ul><li>(a) Keys and data types/validation rule (1); relationship with referential integrity (1).</li><li>(b) Both tables with three criteria on one row (1); sorted ascending with the three correct records (1).</li><li>(c) Calculated field Speed (1); rounded and sorted descending (1).</li><li>(d) Main form Schools (1); subform Athletes (1).</li><li>(e) Grouped by SchoolName with Count (1); exact two-line title (1).</li></ul><h4>Model answer</h4><p><b>(a)</b> Schools: PK <b>SchoolCode</b>. Athletes: PK <b>AthleteID</b>; Distance Number (Integer); BestTime Number, Field Size <b>Double</b>, Decimal Places 2; Gender Validation Rule <code>\"M\" Or \"F\"</code>, Validation Text <i>Enter M or F</i>. Relationship Schools.SchoolCode (one) → Athletes.SchoolCode (many), Enforce Referential Integrity.</p><p><b>(b) BasseterreGirls100</b></p><div class=\"tablewrap\"><table class=\"qbe\"><tr><th>Field</th><td>SchoolName</td><td>FirstName</td><td>LastName</td><td>BestTime</td><td>Gender</td><td>Event</td><td>District</td></tr><tr><th>Table</th><td>Schools</td><td>Athletes</td><td>Athletes</td><td>Athletes</td><td>Athletes</td><td>Athletes</td><td>Schools</td></tr><tr><th>Sort</th><td></td><td></td><td></td><td>Ascending</td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><th>Show</th><td>✔</td><td>✔</td><td>✔</td><td>✔</td><td>☐</td><td>☐</td><td>☐</td></tr><tr><th>Criteria</th><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td>\"F\"</td><td>\"100m\"</td><td>\"Basseterre\"</td></tr></table></div><p>Results: Sea-Almond Secondary School, Kayla Liburd, 12.71; Breadfruit Valley Secondary, Shanice Duzant, 13.05; Mahoe Grove High School, Aaliyah York, 13.28. (Tia Carty, 13.62, is excluded because Hummingbird Ridge High School is in Cotton Ground.)</p><p><b>(c) Speeds</b></p><div class=\"tablewrap\"><table class=\"qbe\"><tr><th>Field</th><td>FirstName</td><td>LastName</td><td>Event</td><td>Speed: Round([Distance]/[BestTime],2)</td></tr><tr><th>Table</th><td>Athletes</td><td>Athletes</td><td>Athletes</td><td></td></tr><tr><th>Sort</th><td></td><td></td><td></td><td>Descending</td></tr><tr><th>Show</th><td>✔</td><td>✔</td><td>✔</td><td>✔</td></tr><tr><th>Criteria</th><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr></table></div><p>Fastest three: Tariq Nisbett (100m) 8.80 m/s; Malik Hodge (200m) 8.63 m/s; Zion Peterson (100m) 8.41 m/s; slowest: Rhea Hanley 6.54 m/s.</p><p><b>(d)</b> Form Wizard ▸ Schools: SchoolCode, SchoolName, District, Coach ▸ Athletes: AthleteID, FirstName, LastName, Gender, Event, BestTime ▸ view by Schools ▸ Form with subform(s) ▸ name <b>SchoolAthletes</b>.</p><p><b>(e)</b> Report Wizard ▸ SchoolName (Schools), FirstName, LastName, Event, BestTime (Athletes) ▸ view by Schools (grouping <b>SchoolName</b>) ▸ sort <b>Event</b> then LastName ▸ in Design View add a text box <code>=Count(*)</code> labelled <i>Athletes entered</i> in the SchoolName footer ▸ title on two lines:</p><div class=\"passage\"><b>Inter-Schools Sports Federation</b><br><b>2026 Athletics Championship Entries by School</b></div><p>Counts: Sea-Almond Secondary School 3; Hummingbird Ridge High School 2; Breadfruit Valley Secondary 3; Flamboyant Heights College 1; Sandbox Tree College 1; Mahoe Grove High School 2; Frangipani Hill Academy 1; Seagrape Bay Secondary School 1; total 14.</p>"},{"t":"essay","title":"Question 5 · Problem-solving and programming: 100 m heat times","q":"<p>A 100 m heat has a <b>maximum of 8</b> runners. The timekeeper enters each runner's time in seconds (e.g. 12.84); entering <b>-1</b> ends the heat early. The program must output the number of runners, the fastest time, the average time (2 decimal places) and the number of runners who qualify for the final with a time <b>under 13.00</b> seconds.</p><ol type=\"a\"><li>Write a problem statement and identify ONE variable and ONE constant with suitable data types. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li>Write pseudocode (or draw a flowchart) using a WHILE loop that stops at -1 OR after 8 runners. <i>(5 marks)</i></li><li>Complete a trace table for the times <b>12.84, 13.40, 12.95, 14.10, 12.61, -1</b>. <i>(3 marks)</i></li><li>Write the program in Python (Pascal or VBA accepted); save as <code>HeatTimes</code>. <i>(4 marks)</i></li><li>Run it with the data in (c) and record the output. <i>(1 mark)</i></li></ol><p class=\"row\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"btn ghost\" data-gx=\"opengame\" data-page=\"games/codelab.html?level=3\" data-id=\"codelab\">🐍 Open the Code Lab</button></p>","points":15,"fb":"<h4>Marking guide</h4><p><b>Mark scheme</b></p><ul><li>(a) Statement (1); variable and constant with types (1).</li><li>(b) Initialisation including fastest (1); WHILE with both conditions and READ inside (1); accumulator and counter (1); fastest and qualifier selections (1); average and output after the loop (1).</li><li>(c) Rows correct (2); final output (1).</li><li>(d) Input with float() and loop (1); selections (1); output formatted (1); runs correctly (1).</li><li>(e) Output recorded (1).</li></ul><h4>Model answer</h4><p><b>(a)</b> <b>Problem statement:</b> The ISSF needs a program that reads the times of up to eight runners in a 100 m heat (stopping early at -1) and reports the number of runners, the fastest time, the average time and how many runners qualify with a time under 13.00 seconds. <b>Variable:</b> time, real (float). <b>Constant:</b> QUALIFY_TIME = 13.00, real (also MAX_RUNNERS = 8, integer).</p><p><b>(b)</b></p><pre>START\n   MAX_RUNNERS ← 8\n   QUALIFY_TIME ← 13.00\n   count ← 0\n   total ← 0\n   fastest ← 99.99\n   qualifiers ← 0\n   PRINT \"Enter time (-1 to end)\"\n   READ time\n   WHILE (time &lt;&gt; -1) AND (count &lt; MAX_RUNNERS) DO\n      count ← count + 1\n      total ← total + time\n      IF time &lt; fastest THEN\n         fastest ← time\n      ENDIF\n      IF time &lt; QUALIFY_TIME THEN\n         qualifiers ← qualifiers + 1\n      ENDIF\n      IF count &lt; MAX_RUNNERS THEN\n         READ time\n      ENDIF\n   ENDWHILE\n   IF count &gt; 0 THEN\n      average ← total / count\n      PRINT \"Runners: \", count, \" Fastest: \", fastest\n      PRINT \"Average: \", average, \" Qualifiers: \", qualifiers\n   ELSE\n      PRINT \"No times entered\"\n   ENDIF\nSTOP</pre><p><b>(c)</b> The loop ran 5 times (below the maximum of 8) because -1 was entered.</p><div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>time</th><th>Loop runs?</th><th>count</th><th>total</th><th>fastest</th><th>qualifiers</th><th>Output</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>–</td><td>–</td><td>0</td><td>0.00</td><td>99.99</td><td>0</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>12.84</td><td>Yes</td><td>1</td><td>12.84</td><td>12.84</td><td>1</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>13.40</td><td>Yes</td><td>2</td><td>26.24</td><td>12.84</td><td>1</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>12.95</td><td>Yes</td><td>3</td><td>39.19</td><td>12.84</td><td>2</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>14.10</td><td>Yes</td><td>4</td><td>53.29</td><td>12.84</td><td>2</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>12.61</td><td>Yes</td><td>5</td><td>65.90</td><td>12.61</td><td>3</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>-1</td><td>No (time = -1)</td><td>5</td><td>65.90</td><td>12.61</td><td>3</td><td>Runners: 5, Fastest: 12.61, Average: 13.18, Qualifiers: 3</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p><b>(d)</b></p><pre># HeatTimes: ISSF 100 m heat summary\nMAX_RUNNERS = 8\nQUALIFY_TIME = 13.00\ncount = 0\ntotal = 0.0\nfastest = 99.99\nqualifiers = 0\ntime = float(input(\"Enter time (-1 to end): \"))\nwhile time != -1 and count &lt; MAX_RUNNERS:\n    count = count + 1\n    total = total + time\n    if time &lt; fastest:\n        fastest = time\n    if time &lt; QUALIFY_TIME:\n        qualifiers = qualifiers + 1\n    if count &lt; MAX_RUNNERS:\n        time = float(input(\"Enter time (-1 to end): \"))\nif count &gt; 0:\n    average = total / count\n    print(\"Runners:\", count, \"Fastest:\", format(fastest, \".2f\"))\n    print(\"Average:\", format(average, \".2f\"), \"Qualifiers:\", qualifiers)\nelse:\n    print(\"No times entered\")</pre><p><b>(e)</b></p><pre>Runners: 5 Fastest: 12.61\nAverage: 13.18 Qualifiers: 3</pre>"}]}]}];
